WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:02.068
We bought all the chicken sandwiches

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.237
from every fast food
chain in the US and the UK

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:05.638
to compare the differences.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.107
This is Food Wars.

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.914
At a UK McDonald's, chicken
sandwiches come in three sizes.

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:18.551
The mayo chicken, a McChicken
sandwich, and a McCrispy.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:22.188
McDonald's in the US has four
chicken sandwich options.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:26.493
The McChicken, the McCrispy,
the McCrispy deluxe,

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:31.498
and the McCrispy spicy deluxe.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:38.004
The UK KFC options are a
regular chicken sandwich,

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:40.940
a Tower burger, which is the
same but with a hash brown

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.876
and some cheese, and a Stacker burger,

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.779
which is two chicken patties
with some cheese as well.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.614
KFC options, we currently have two.

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:51.384
The Chicken Little - look at
this little guy, so little -

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.287
and the KFC chicken sandwich.

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:04.097
This is part of their Gourmet Kings range.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.537
Whoops.

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.039
We have two types of chicken
sandwich at a UK Burger King,

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:16.676
the Chicken Royale or the
steakhouse crispy chicken.

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.378
Burger King in the US

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:21.648
also has two chicken sandwich options,

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.116
and they are as follows.

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:24.717
I am taking this off.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:29.322
The Chicken Junior, this guy.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.257
I mean, this looks
exactly like a McChicken.

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:32.759
And the original chicken sandwich,

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:36.162
the only chicken sandwich
we're going to see today

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:37.897
that is shaped like a smartphone.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.401
Popeyes chicken sandwiches in the UK

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:44.104
only come in one size, the
classic chicken sandwich.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:47.807
Popeyes in the US also has
but one chicken sandwich.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.343
Shake Shack in the UK only has
one size of chicken burger,

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:51.377
the Chicken Shack.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.146
Shake Shack, we haven't done
anything with Shake Shack

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:54.948
in years it feels like, but they're back.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:56.249
They got a chicken sandwich.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:02:01.221
Just on looks, whoa, this
looks ridiculously good.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:03.890
Jollibee's chicken burgers
come in two sizes in the UK

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.992
as a standard or as a double.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.629
Jollibee. I love Jollibee,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.831
and their chicken
sandwiches are very good.

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:16.569
We have their regular chicken
sandwich and the spicy.

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:22.308
Wendy's in the US went all
in on chicken sandwiches.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:25.678
As you can see, there are nine varieties.

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.147
Two, four, six, eight, yeah.

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:30.683
Wendy's only has one size of
chicken sandwich in the UK.

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:34.053
OK, I'm sorry, what sandwich is it again?

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.956
And the crispy chicken
sandwich, chicken BLT.

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:41.961
OK, OK, OK, Asiago club,
spicy chicken sandwich.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.597
Wendy's, all right, we get it.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:45.765
BLT or club?

00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:47.567
Spicy ghost pepper.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:51.704
Bacon. Pretzel. Pub.
Wendy's spicy pretzel.

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.442
Then we've got some options
that you won't find in the US.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.344
Two things from a chain called Morley's,

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.513
a strip burger and a fillet burger,

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:03.616
and one thing from Nando's,
the grilled chicken burger.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.518
Arby's, which is only available in the US,

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.922
has three chicken sandwiches,
starting over here.

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:10.657
They have their crispy chicken sandwich,

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:12.792
they have their bacon Swiss
crispy chicken sandwich,

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:15.628
and they got the Buffalo chicken sandwich.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.398
Chick-fil-A, of course, the original.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:21.267
You can also get the same
sandwich spicy version.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.469
Raising Cane's, they have, of course,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:26.639
one chicken sandwich option
on the menu. This is that.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.808
Depending where you are
in the United States,

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:32.545
these are the five
Carl's Jr.-slash-Hardee's

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:34.113
chicken sandwiches.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:38.218
So here are almost all
of the chicken burgers

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:40.220
you'll find in the UK arranged by price.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.422
The cheapest at this end is
the McDonald's mayo chicken

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:43.990
at just 1 pound 19.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.693
The most expensive is the
Shake Shack Chicken Shack,

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.861
coming in at 9 pounds and 35 pence.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.863
For the price of one
Shake Shack Chicken Shack,

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.800
you could buy 7.8
McDonald's mayo chickens.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.401
In general, I think it seems like the size

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:56.302
is tracking with the price.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:57.804
So they do seem a little
bit smaller down this end

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.405
and a little bit bigger down this end,

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:00.673
with a couple of exceptions.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.309
I do think the Nando's one
looks a little bit small.

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:04.978
Even the Chicken Shack
does look a bit small,

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:06.079
and that's very expensive.

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:09.649
OK, prices in the US
starting from least expensive

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:10.683
to most expensive.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.719
The least expensive
sandwich, to no surprise,

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:16.823
is the KFC Chicken Little,
which comes in at $2.39.

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:18.958
But let's be honest, this
isn't actually a full sandwich.

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:20.560
I mean, it's almost like a little slider.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.663
The most expensive is the
Carl's Jr. bacon Swiss

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:27.100
chicken sandwich, coming in,
just the sandwich, $11.49.

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:33.539
I think it's interesting that
McDonald's takes up this part.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.075
Wendy's is almost all exactly right here.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.278
Arby's is almost exactly right over here.

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:40.146
I do enjoy Raising Cane's,

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:45.151
but this sandwich should not
be in this percentage of price.

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.821
First of all, it's
chicken tender sandwiches.

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.723
It's not even like a full chicken patty.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:55.461
I mean, the Jollibee spicy and regular are

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:57.597
in the lower third.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:58.631
This is a great sandwich.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:01.334
Popeyes is here, KFC is right here.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.036
Popeyes and KFC positioned

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:05.171
almost right next to each other, price.

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:08.941
A lot of people, myself included,
would say these might be

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:13.079
the best fast food crispy
chicken sandwiches on the market,

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:13.946
and they're down here, man.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.115
They're like hovering
around McChicken prices.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.751
Here are all the UK chicken
sandwiches ordered by weight.

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:22.288
The lightest option is the
mayo chicken at 120 grams,

00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:24.424
and the heaviest is the
KFC Zinger Stacker burger

00:05:24.424 --> 00:05:26.125
at 325 grams.

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:28.661
Key takeaways there, a few
of them really dropped down.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.363
For example, the Shake Shack Burger.

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:31.931
The Nando's burger has really plummeted

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:33.399
in terms of price versus weight.

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:35.368
Not a lot of movement at
the bottom end of the table,

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:36.302
which is interesting.

00:05:36.302 --> 00:05:38.004
I think even just
visually, like the colors

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:38.971
are quite interesting.

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:42.008
Down the mayo chicken end, the
buns are kind of very dull,

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:43.276
quite a matte finish.

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:44.944
Then the sesame seeds
kind of start to disappear

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:45.778
the higher up you go,

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.714
and you head more towards
the kind of brioche bun,

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:49.782
which just looks a little
bit fancier and shinier.

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:54.320
OK, now this is all the
US sandwiches by weight.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.023
Least weight down here,
most weight down there.

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.459
OK, once again, the Chicken Little

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:02.428
and the Wendy's crispy
chicken is at the very end

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:05.231
of the smallest. And all the
way down here at the end,

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:10.236
the Wendy's classic Pretzel
Bacon Pub at a hefty

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:13.806
284 grams on this one. Heavy sandwich.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:15.775
I don't know what you want to
do with this information really.

00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:18.444
The one thing I will notice
is the most expensive one,

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:19.812
or the one of the most expensive ones,

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.248
is the weight right in the middle.

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:23.750
So certainly not charging by volume.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.651
Shame on you, Carl's Jr.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:26.619
Popeyes, right?

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:28.187
Popeyes was over here.

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.355
Move this way all the way up.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:32.125
So less in price, more in weight.

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:33.259
Let's go.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.660
And here are all the chicken sandwiches

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.629
arranged in order of price by weight.

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:38.731
So the best value in
terms of price by weight

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:41.768
is the McDonald's mayo chicken.
Per 100 grams of burger,

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.536
that will cost you just 99 pence.

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:44.704
The most expensive by weight

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:47.640
is the Shake Shack Chicken
Shack. Per 100 grams of burger,

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.009
that one will cost you 4 pounds 25.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:52.979
The Shake Shack burger
is 329% more expensive

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:54.080
than the mayo chicken.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.882
I guess ultimately it comes
down to personal preference

00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:57.517
as to whether you want
to pay a little bit more

00:06:57.517 --> 00:06:59.519
for what you would hope
to be higher quality

00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:01.154
or if you kind of just
want value for money

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:02.588
and quantity over quality.

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:03.756
I think when it comes to fast food,

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.158
I kind of just want value for money.

00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:07.627
I just want to leave feeling
satisfied with my food

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:09.429
for as little price as possible.

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:12.098
I'm kind of surprised how
low down in the value scale

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:13.833
the Chicken Royale from Burger King

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:16.002
and the standard fillet
burger from KFC ended up.

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:17.537
You're really not getting
that much value for money

00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:19.138
when compared to some of these options,

00:07:19.138 --> 00:07:21.040
and I don't think the quality
of either of those chains

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.276
is necessarily high enough
to justify that price point.

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:25.745
For example, I think the
Jollibee double chicken sandwich,

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:27.480
as well as the Zinger Stacker burger,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.215
you're getting a lot of
food for your money there.

00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:30.950
And honestly, these are
really tasty burgers.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.019
So the quality is good, price is good.

00:07:33.019 --> 00:07:35.354
I think these ones are what I
would lean towards in general

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.589
if I were to order.

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:39.258
This is the order from
best value to worst value.

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:41.794
Coming in at No. 1 ...

00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.604
... is a Wendy's BLT.

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:52.872
Look at this little guy.

00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:54.006
What a deal.

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:56.476
I love the fact that both the Popeyes

00:07:56.476 --> 00:08:00.913
and the KFC chicken sandwiches
are arguably the best deal

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:02.748
you can get in the US.

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:03.583
Look at this.

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:05.718
If you're going per 100
grams, the Chicken Little

00:08:05.718 --> 00:08:09.088
isn't as good of a deal as
a full chicken sandwich.

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:10.690
I love it.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.358
Glad to see the pretzel
buns are down here.

00:08:12.358 --> 00:08:13.626
Because these were the heaviest.

00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:15.361
But also comparing it with the price,

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:16.195
you're getting a pretty good deal.

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:19.232
I think from here on, it's like, yeah, OK.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.135
Back here, Jollibee spicy chicken.

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:24.070
Glad to see it.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:25.204
Great deal.

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:26.639
McChicken's over here, huh?

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:27.473
Raising Cane's.

00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:28.541
All right.

00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:29.842
I was a little tough on
Raising Cane's before.

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.077
Everyone's favorite, Chick-fil-A.

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:32.311
Chick-fil-A here and here.

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:33.145
Really?

00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:34.213
Really?

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:35.047
This is your favorite restaurant.

00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:35.915
We're closed on Sundays for this?

00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:36.749
Give me a break.

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:37.950
Shake Shack's over here.

00:08:39.218 --> 00:08:42.622
I don't know. I was enticed by its size,

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:43.990
but after running the numbers,

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:45.057
it's not that good of a deal.

00:08:45.057 --> 00:08:48.160
Shame on you, Shake Shack.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.197
McDonald's. Even McDonald's isn't safe.

00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:53.699
Their McCrispy, all the
way over here for this one?

00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:57.303
That's surprising and annoying.

00:08:57.303 --> 00:08:59.338
Look at this. Look at this.

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:02.842
Every Carl's Jr., most expensive, right?

00:09:02.842 --> 00:09:05.444
They weren't the heaviest. Worst value.

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:07.780
The Carl's Jr. hand-breaded.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:09.916
Not even the ones with
all the fun stuff on it.

00:09:09.916 --> 00:09:12.118
Just a regular hand-breaded sandwich.

00:09:12.118 --> 00:09:12.952
Here you go.

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:17.957
It was $10.99 and it weighed 221 grams.

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:21.661
So it's $4.97 per 100 grams.

00:09:21.661 --> 00:09:23.262
I know it's a lot of data to throw at you.

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:27.099
Let me just give you the
TL;DR. Too expensive.

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:31.904
So here are all the UK
standard chicken sandwiches.

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:33.172
I'm going to taste them all back to back

00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:35.107
and provide you with
my definitive ranking.

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:38.811
Here are the standard chicken sandwiches

00:09:38.811 --> 00:09:42.448
at several US fast food chains.

00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:44.083
I'm going to try and rank them right now.

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:46.452
McDonald's McCrispy.

00:09:46.452 --> 00:09:47.286
I'll give this one a try.

00:09:47.286 --> 00:09:48.554
They've gone really heavy on the mayo.

00:09:50.323 --> 00:09:51.324
The bun is quite fancy.

00:09:51.324 --> 00:09:52.925
It's fancier than you'd normally find

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:53.960
on a McDonald's burger.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.261
It's kind of like a pretzel bun effect.

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:57.563
Nice shiny, decent texture on it.

00:09:57.563 --> 00:09:58.397
Not too doughy.

00:09:58.397 --> 00:10:00.466
Chicken patty, maybe a little bit thin.

00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.034
It's a good place to start,
because I think there is room

00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:03.903
for improvement there, but
it could be a lot worse.

00:10:03.903 --> 00:10:07.440
So starting with down
here, the chicken classic.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.741
I mean, I feel like I'm a bit biased,

00:10:08.741 --> 00:10:11.911
because I've been eating these
as long as I can remember.

00:10:13.245 --> 00:10:14.747
I'll put it here for now.

00:10:14.747 --> 00:10:17.083
I feel like, I mean,
it's good for its value,

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:20.686
but I don't know, it's a little basic.

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:21.721
Now, I don't think I've
had this one before,

00:10:21.721 --> 00:10:23.389
the Burger King one.

00:10:26.792 --> 00:10:28.661
Similar, I think I like that better.

00:10:28.661 --> 00:10:29.595
Yeah, BK.

00:10:29.595 --> 00:10:30.863
All right.

00:10:30.863 --> 00:10:33.299
Next up we've got the KFC fillet burger.

00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:34.100
By the looks of things,

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.370
also just mayo and lettuce and chicken.

00:10:39.138 --> 00:10:40.973
Overall, it's just lacking
a little bit of something.

00:10:40.973 --> 00:10:42.008
It's quite bland.

00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:45.044
I think so far, whether it's the chicken,

00:10:45.044 --> 00:10:46.912
the breading, or the mayo,

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:48.814
not quite as flavorful as the McCrispy.

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:50.950
So slightly below it.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:51.784
Come on.

00:10:51.784 --> 00:10:55.287
Popeyes chicken sandwiches
has been sitting for a while.

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:57.723
This is the one that everyone
lost their minds about

00:10:57.723 --> 00:10:58.557
on the internet.

00:11:01.127 --> 00:11:03.596
Everything I'm looking for. Still crispy,

00:11:03.596 --> 00:11:04.864
amazing Popeyes flavor.

00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:05.865
Love the bun.

00:11:05.865 --> 00:11:07.800
Little bit of mayo or sauce on there.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.268
Not too overpowering.

00:11:09.268 --> 00:11:10.670
Wow, that's great.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.872
That'll stay in this region.

00:11:12.872 --> 00:11:14.940
Big competitor though, KFC.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.443
Ooh, and this one, this
is still really crispy.

00:11:17.443 --> 00:11:18.277
All right.

00:11:24.016 --> 00:11:25.685
You hear that crunch?

00:11:25.685 --> 00:11:26.652
Yeah.

00:11:26.652 --> 00:11:27.953
Going in for another bite.

00:11:32.158 --> 00:11:33.693
The Burger King Chicken Royale.

00:11:33.693 --> 00:11:34.827
I'm tempted to dock points

00:11:34.827 --> 00:11:36.929
just because of how
stupid this thing looks.

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:39.732
I've never understood
why they made it long.

00:11:41.667 --> 00:11:44.870
I think the ratio is slightly off.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:49.642
The bun is obviously a long
bun, but it's also quite thick.

00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:51.577
So, if you can see that,
you're getting quite a lot

00:11:51.577 --> 00:11:53.412
of bread-to-meat ratio.

00:11:53.412 --> 00:11:55.114
Yeah, that's the worst one so far.

00:11:57.183 --> 00:11:58.718
Wendy's we saw, we'll see later,

00:11:58.718 --> 00:12:02.922
but Wendy's had a hundred
freaking sandwiches.

00:12:08.461 --> 00:12:09.895
Dry, barely seasoned.

00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:12.064
No, that's the worst one.

00:12:12.064 --> 00:12:13.466
Was it cheese on this?

00:12:13.466 --> 00:12:15.634
No, not very good.

00:12:15.634 --> 00:12:17.069
Popeyes chicken sandwich.

00:12:17.069 --> 00:12:19.405
Popeyes is a relatively
recent addition to the UK.

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:22.074
We didn't have this until
maybe a year or two ago,

00:12:22.074 --> 00:12:24.777
and I will say so far,
people seem to be liking it.

00:12:24.777 --> 00:12:27.947
On this one, you get
the chicken, the mayo,

00:12:27.947 --> 00:12:31.550
and instead of the
lettuce, you get pickles,

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.853
which I think is more
of an American thing,

00:12:33.853 --> 00:12:37.590
and one that I enjoy,
because I love pickles.

00:12:40.926 --> 00:12:41.761
This is good.

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:43.295
I think we have a leader so far.

00:12:44.196 --> 00:12:47.266
The chicken I would say
is thicker and crispier

00:12:47.266 --> 00:12:49.168
than any of the three I've tried so far.

00:12:49.168 --> 00:12:53.739
Get little, like, crunchy
bits that flake off the edge.

00:12:54.807 --> 00:12:57.743
So yeah, I think my
favorite so far, Popeyes.

00:12:57.743 --> 00:13:01.180
Arby's, I mean, they got the meats,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:02.548
and I guess chicken's a meat,

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:05.518
but I don't think about getting
chicken when I go to Arby's.

00:13:05.518 --> 00:13:07.453
It's roast beef, right?

00:13:13.492 --> 00:13:14.727
No.

00:13:14.727 --> 00:13:15.728
Flavorless.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.498
Shake Shack Chicken Shack is up next.

00:13:19.498 --> 00:13:20.599
This is the most expensive

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:23.135
of any of the burgers we've tried today.

00:13:23.135 --> 00:13:25.237
It looks pretty fancy.

00:13:25.237 --> 00:13:27.406
The bun is that classic Shake Shack bun.

00:13:27.406 --> 00:13:28.808
I think it's a potato roll.

00:13:30.409 --> 00:13:33.646
We have chicken, mayo, and lettuce.

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:35.548
No pickles in this one.

00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:39.852
I was wrong.

00:13:39.852 --> 00:13:41.353
There are pickles in this one.

00:13:42.288 --> 00:13:43.856
The breading on the chicken,

00:13:43.856 --> 00:13:45.691
it looked like it was going to be tasty,

00:13:46.559 --> 00:13:49.228
but in actuality doesn't
have that much flavor.

00:13:49.228 --> 00:13:50.729
And if you actually look at the chicken,

00:13:50.729 --> 00:13:52.898
it's quite a thick piece of chicken,

00:13:52.898 --> 00:13:56.135
but it's very dry and quite tough.

00:13:56.135 --> 00:13:57.903
I think it's probably
worse than the McSpicy.

00:13:57.903 --> 00:13:59.638
So I'll tuck it in there.

00:13:59.638 --> 00:14:00.573
Here we go.

00:14:00.573 --> 00:14:02.241
Let's get everyone
fighting in the comments

00:14:02.241 --> 00:14:03.609
with Chick-fil-A.

00:14:03.609 --> 00:14:06.745
Never been a Chick-fil-A fan,
and I hope everyone watching

00:14:06.745 --> 00:14:09.148
can see what varieties
of chicken sandwiches

00:14:09.148 --> 00:14:11.483
you can get at any fast food chain.

00:14:11.483 --> 00:14:12.785
Why are you picking this?

00:14:12.785 --> 00:14:14.019
What are you doing?

00:14:14.019 --> 00:14:15.321
What are you guys doing?

00:14:15.321 --> 00:14:18.591
I've never really cared for Chick-fil-A.

00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:20.659
It's just the epitome of fine.

00:14:20.659 --> 00:14:23.629
I mean this is looking
closer to these ones

00:14:23.629 --> 00:14:25.164
than it is to these over here.

00:14:26.632 --> 00:14:28.033
Looks really dry.

00:14:33.539 --> 00:14:35.307
Got to get the sauce,
got to put sauce on it.

00:14:35.307 --> 00:14:37.810
So I got Chick-fil-A sauce.

00:14:38.677 --> 00:14:40.746
But I mean the fact that it's like, no,

00:14:40.746 --> 00:14:42.281
you have to put sauce on it to enjoy it.

00:14:42.281 --> 00:14:43.549
Well, then put the sauce on it.

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:44.583
Why do I got to do it?

00:14:46.585 --> 00:14:47.519
And I do really like
the Chick-fil-A sauce.

00:14:47.519 --> 00:14:48.988
It's a very good sauce.

00:14:48.988 --> 00:14:52.791
I will say what this has
going for it other than these

00:14:52.791 --> 00:14:57.463
is that its simplicity makes
it, to me, a little better,

00:14:57.463 --> 00:14:58.497
because it's not bogged down

00:14:58.497 --> 00:15:01.800
with a lot of, like, soggy
vegetables or sauce or whatever.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:05.504
When you add in this instance
the Chick-fil-A sauce,

00:15:05.504 --> 00:15:06.705
it is better.

00:15:06.705 --> 00:15:10.342
So for that, I'm going to put
it, let me put it down here.

00:15:11.644 --> 00:15:13.812
It's definitely not as
good as a McChicken.

00:15:15.014 --> 00:15:16.782
I just don't see what the big deal is.

00:15:16.782 --> 00:15:17.650
I don't.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.852
And everyone's going to flood my comments

00:15:19.852 --> 00:15:22.721
with the specialty orders I
have to get with the sauces

00:15:22.721 --> 00:15:24.456
and then, no. This is the
sandwiches on the menu.

00:15:24.456 --> 00:15:25.291
I got it.

00:15:25.291 --> 00:15:26.659
I tasted it. Boring and bland.

00:15:26.659 --> 00:15:30.195
Up next we got Jollibee, their
classic chicken sandwich.

00:15:30.195 --> 00:15:32.464
Also a relatively new addition to the UK.

00:15:33.632 --> 00:15:35.301
Let's give it a try.

00:15:40.005 --> 00:15:41.173
Wow.

00:15:41.173 --> 00:15:43.108
I think it's the crispiest
chicken we've had so far.

00:15:43.108 --> 00:15:45.711
I think it's even crispier
than the Popeyes one.

00:15:45.711 --> 00:15:48.647
Could maybe benefit from
a little bit more mayo.

00:15:48.647 --> 00:15:51.650
It's just slightly dry is
my main criticism so far.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:53.285
There's not much to
separate this from Popeyes.

00:15:53.285 --> 00:15:56.722
But I think I slightly
prefer the taste of Popeyes,

00:15:56.722 --> 00:15:58.590
so I'll tuck it in there.

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:00.893
Yo, Raising Cane's, though.

00:16:00.893 --> 00:16:02.962
Chicken tender sandwiches.

00:16:02.962 --> 00:16:06.098
Someone's head chef is a 12-year-old boy.

00:16:07.032 --> 00:16:08.968
Not a bad idea. Just
stick tenders in a bun,

00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:10.803
throw some sauce on here, let's go.

00:16:12.805 --> 00:16:14.373
This is good.

00:16:18.777 --> 00:16:19.979
Here's why this is smart.

00:16:19.979 --> 00:16:23.282
With the tenders, there's
more surface space,

00:16:23.282 --> 00:16:26.585
so the crispy-skin-to-chicken ratio

00:16:26.585 --> 00:16:28.787
leans more towards the crispy chicken

00:16:28.787 --> 00:16:30.689
or the crispy skin,

00:16:30.689 --> 00:16:32.591
which just gives it a lot more flavor.

00:16:35.127 --> 00:16:36.996
I really like Raising Cane's.

00:16:36.996 --> 00:16:40.532
I know I was disappointed when
we put it in the value matrix

00:16:40.532 --> 00:16:41.667
that Harry came up with.

00:16:41.667 --> 00:16:43.869
But, you don't want to discount taste,

00:16:43.869 --> 00:16:45.838
is a very tasty chicken sandwich.

00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:48.807
Oh, Raising Cane's,
another one that's famous

00:16:48.807 --> 00:16:52.077
for having this Cane sauce
you're supposed to dip it in,

00:16:52.077 --> 00:16:53.979
and I didn't even put any
on here. It's still good.

00:16:53.979 --> 00:16:57.149
So Chick-fil-A, get out of
here with your extra sauce.

00:16:57.149 --> 00:16:59.418
Then we're onto the Wendy's
classic chicken sandwich.

00:16:59.418 --> 00:17:03.989
Well, it looks like quite a dry
bun, chicken patty, lettuce,

00:17:03.989 --> 00:17:06.058
tomato, and pickles.

00:17:11.797 --> 00:17:13.599
Just visually, I had
quite low expectations,

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:16.101
but that's a pretty tasty sandwich.

00:17:16.101 --> 00:17:16.935
Not bad.

00:17:16.935 --> 00:17:19.571
I'm going to put it
slightly below the McCrispy.

00:17:19.571 --> 00:17:22.141
All right, last two.

00:17:24.076 --> 00:17:26.645
Shake Shack is great.

00:17:26.645 --> 00:17:28.447
I don't usually go there that often,

00:17:28.447 --> 00:17:29.548
because I always feel it's a little more

00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:32.584
on the expensive side.
And aesthetics alone,

00:17:32.584 --> 00:17:36.655
this looks like it would've
been maybe the best one.

00:17:36.655 --> 00:17:38.957
It looks like a big piece of chicken.

00:17:38.957 --> 00:17:41.060
Yeah, good amount of sauce.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.561
I like the bun.

00:17:42.561 --> 00:17:46.398
This looks like a big
chicken sandwich, right?

00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:53.138
This is really good.

00:17:54.406 --> 00:17:55.240
Yeah.

00:17:56.875 --> 00:18:01.113
The flavor of that chicken
is Popeyes- and KFC-level.

00:18:02.081 --> 00:18:05.317
I mean, Cane's is cool.

00:18:05.317 --> 00:18:06.752
Everything this way has more

00:18:06.752 --> 00:18:09.588
of a generic fried chicken flavor,

00:18:09.588 --> 00:18:12.691
but from here on, the flavors
are getting more specific,

00:18:12.691 --> 00:18:13.559
which I like.

00:18:13.559 --> 00:18:15.127
And Shake Shack's, wow.

00:18:15.127 --> 00:18:17.796
Then we've got two UK
exclusives to rank as well.

00:18:17.796 --> 00:18:20.899
I'm going to start with
the Morley's fillet burger.

00:18:20.899 --> 00:18:23.802
This kind of looks like the
platonic ideal chicken burger

00:18:23.802 --> 00:18:24.803
that you might find.

00:18:26.071 --> 00:18:27.973
Just the lettuce, quite a lot of mayo.

00:18:28.974 --> 00:18:29.875
Chicken looks good.

00:18:29.875 --> 00:18:31.110
Not the thickest piece of chicken,

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:33.679
but looks pretty well seasoned.

00:18:35.681 --> 00:18:38.884
The chicken, I would describe
the texture as leathery.

00:18:38.884 --> 00:18:42.221
Quality-wise, you can kind of
tell that the chicken in here

00:18:42.221 --> 00:18:44.456
is maybe not quite as good
as some of the other ones.

00:18:44.456 --> 00:18:45.557
I think it's down here actually.

00:18:45.557 --> 00:18:47.226
Not as bad as the long chicken.

00:18:47.226 --> 00:18:51.130
And last, what we've determined
arguably the worst value

00:18:52.364 --> 00:18:53.332
for your money,

00:18:53.332 --> 00:18:56.201
Carl's Jr. Oh man.

00:18:56.201 --> 00:19:01.073
But they're really emphasizing
that this is a hand-breaded.

00:19:01.073 --> 00:19:02.774
They're doing it by hand back there.

00:19:02.774 --> 00:19:04.776
Let's see how it tastes.

00:19:09.781 --> 00:19:10.682
Hello.

00:19:12.117 --> 00:19:14.086
This is actually pretty good.

00:19:14.086 --> 00:19:16.855
Oh man, I've been dogging
on Carl's Jr. all day.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:19.191
OK.

00:19:37.142 --> 00:19:37.976
All right.

00:19:37.976 --> 00:19:39.144
Raising Cane's is better
than this one, but not,

00:19:39.144 --> 00:19:41.880
I mean, it's close.

00:19:42.915 --> 00:19:45.050
They both have a unique flavor.

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:48.420
Cane's overall has a
better-lasting flavor.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.722
They don't taste exactly
the same, but Carl's Jr.,

00:19:50.722 --> 00:19:52.958
your hand-breaded chicken
sandwich is actually pretty good.

00:19:52.958 --> 00:19:56.094
Then our final option is the
Nando's grilled chicken burger.

00:19:56.094 --> 00:19:58.664
It's some of their
signature grilled chicken

00:19:58.664 --> 00:20:00.465
with your chosen spice level.

00:20:00.465 --> 00:20:02.467
Leon chose the spiciest one for me.

00:20:02.467 --> 00:20:03.302
Thank you.

00:20:03.302 --> 00:20:04.770
And it's served on these kind of,

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:06.305
I think it's a Portuguese roll,

00:20:07.372 --> 00:20:10.709
which have quite a firm texture to them.

00:20:13.679 --> 00:20:16.048
I will say, you can taste
the quality of the chicken

00:20:16.048 --> 00:20:17.916
with this one. The ratio is slightly off.

00:20:17.916 --> 00:20:21.420
I've always thought this
bun is, like, quite a lot

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.288
for the rest of the ingredients.

00:20:23.288 --> 00:20:25.290
It's good, but where does it rank?

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:26.692
I think this may upset people,

00:20:26.692 --> 00:20:29.895
but I'm actually going to put
it slightly below the McCrispy.

00:20:29.895 --> 00:20:31.096
And that is my ranking.

00:20:31.096 --> 00:20:33.665
Coming in dead last,
Burger King Chicken Royale.

00:20:33.665 --> 00:20:36.935
And I think my favorite is
the Popeyes chicken sandwich.

00:20:36.935 --> 00:20:40.472
Going down the ranking from least to best,

00:20:40.472 --> 00:20:44.009
says me, in last place we
have, was this Wendy's?

00:20:44.009 --> 00:20:44.810
Yeah.

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:49.348
But, of course, the top
two, Popeyes and KFC.

00:20:49.348 --> 00:20:52.618
And I want to say, at any given day,

00:20:52.618 --> 00:20:54.920
it could be Popeyes one, KFC two.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:57.422
Those guys are neck and neck,

00:20:57.422 --> 00:20:59.758
depending on the KFC
and Popeyes you go to.

00:20:59.758 --> 00:21:02.127
Oh my gosh, I almost forgot Jollibee.

00:21:02.127 --> 00:21:05.364
This is the standard Jollibee sandwich.

00:21:07.833 --> 00:21:09.701
I'll put that between the
McChicken and the Chick-fil-A.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:13.038
Yeah, not impressed.

00:21:13.038 --> 00:21:16.541
It's a lot of chicken I can keep track of.

00:21:22.781 --> 00:21:24.750
Now I'm going to talk
about all the US sandwiches

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:26.551
you cannot get in the UK.

00:21:26.551 --> 00:21:28.287
Here are all the chicken
sandwiches from the UK

00:21:28.287 --> 00:21:29.554
that you won't find in the US,

00:21:29.554 --> 00:21:31.456
only we call them chicken burgers.

00:21:31.456 --> 00:21:33.692
Firstly, it's the return
of the value king,

00:21:33.692 --> 00:21:34.826
it's the mayo chicken.

00:21:36.161 --> 00:21:39.431
This is as simple, cheap,
and cheerful as it gets.

00:21:39.431 --> 00:21:43.435
It's a slightly oddly shaped,
usually square chicken patty

00:21:43.435 --> 00:21:45.837
with some mayo and some lettuce on a bun.

00:21:45.837 --> 00:21:48.006
And for a few extra pounds,
you can also add bacon

00:21:48.006 --> 00:21:50.309
and have a bacon mayo chicken.

00:21:50.309 --> 00:21:52.711
Then we have a linguistic
difference. In the UK,

00:21:52.711 --> 00:21:54.680
our spicy version of the chicken sandwich

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:56.148
is called a McSpicy.

00:21:56.148 --> 00:21:59.918
You guys call yours a Spicy McCrispy,

00:21:59.918 --> 00:22:02.654
which to me seems like an
unnecessary amount of words.

00:22:03.622 --> 00:22:05.190
Whatever.

00:22:13.832 --> 00:22:15.033
Yarrr.

00:22:17.235 --> 00:22:18.837
Then we're onto KFC. In the UK,

00:22:18.837 --> 00:22:21.139
instead of having a
spicy chicken sandwich,

00:22:21.139 --> 00:22:23.008
we have a Zinger burger.

00:22:23.008 --> 00:22:24.810
It's basically the same thing, same patty,

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:27.212
but the breading is a bit more spicy.

00:22:27.212 --> 00:22:28.046
I quite like this.

00:22:28.046 --> 00:22:30.215
I think generally speaking,
I'll opt for a Zinger burger

00:22:30.215 --> 00:22:31.850
over just the classic fillet burger.

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:33.085
Also, do Americans find it weird

00:22:33.085 --> 00:22:35.787
that we pronounce it "FILL-it"
burger and not "fill-LAY"?

00:22:35.787 --> 00:22:37.656
Because I feel like you
guys will find that weird.

00:22:37.656 --> 00:22:39.825
Unless you're talking
about the Filet-O-Fish

00:22:39.825 --> 00:22:42.394
from McDonald's, in which
case we say "fill-LAY."

00:22:42.394 --> 00:22:43.729
Figure it out.

00:22:43.729 --> 00:22:47.399
Our next exclusive from KFC
in the UK is the Tower burger.

00:22:47.399 --> 00:22:51.403
So this is the chicken, but
then they add some cheese to it

00:22:51.403 --> 00:22:52.671
and a hash brown.

00:22:52.671 --> 00:22:55.173
We've also gone for the
Zinger Tower burger here,

00:22:55.173 --> 00:22:57.809
which means that you get
this spicy salsa-y sauce

00:22:59.177 --> 00:23:00.011
on top as well.

00:23:00.011 --> 00:23:03.548
And then we have the exclusive
Zinger Stacker burger.

00:23:04.549 --> 00:23:06.418
Now this is our largest
burger that you can find

00:23:06.418 --> 00:23:11.289
on a KFC UK menu, and it's,
if I can separate them,

00:23:11.289 --> 00:23:14.893
which apparently I can't,
it's just peeled instead.

00:23:14.893 --> 00:23:18.964
It's two chicken patties
with cheese between,

00:23:18.964 --> 00:23:22.267
and on top, lettuce, spicy mayo,

00:23:23.268 --> 00:23:25.470
and some more spicy sauce on top as well.

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:26.638
While we're talking about the KFC ones,

00:23:26.638 --> 00:23:29.241
you'll note that on the UK
menu, they're all named a burger

00:23:29.241 --> 00:23:30.709
rather than a sandwich.

00:23:30.709 --> 00:23:31.710
It's hard to explain exactly

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:33.378
where the linguistic
difference comes from.

00:23:33.378 --> 00:23:35.781
I think a lot of it comes
to do with the bread

00:23:35.781 --> 00:23:37.015
that your sandwich comes in.

00:23:37.015 --> 00:23:39.184
I think generally speaking,
if you have a round bun,

00:23:39.184 --> 00:23:41.453
particularly with sesame
seeds on it, in the UK,

00:23:41.453 --> 00:23:43.822
what's inside it is just
going to be called a burger.

00:23:43.822 --> 00:23:45.490
We actually have quite
a few chicken exclusives

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:47.459
on a UK Burger King menu.

00:23:47.459 --> 00:23:48.293
We'll start with this one,

00:23:48.293 --> 00:23:50.228
which is the steakhouse crispy chicken.

00:23:51.263 --> 00:23:54.733
This is part of their Gourmet Kings menu.

00:23:54.733 --> 00:23:56.168
That's how I describe myself.

00:23:58.103 --> 00:23:59.805
There's a tomatoey sauce on the bottom,

00:23:59.805 --> 00:24:02.607
a crispy chicken patty,
big slice of cheese,

00:24:02.607 --> 00:24:05.544
bacon, and then what looks
to be some rocket on top.

00:24:06.378 --> 00:24:09.648
For the American audience,
we call arugula rocket.

00:24:09.648 --> 00:24:10.515
I've not had this one before.

00:24:10.515 --> 00:24:11.650
It was quite expensive.

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:13.852
I think it's got gourmet in the name,

00:24:13.852 --> 00:24:14.786
and I think they're using that

00:24:14.786 --> 00:24:16.855
to put this at quite a high price point.

00:24:18.757 --> 00:24:19.624
It's not bad.

00:24:19.624 --> 00:24:22.160
I do think to get this as part of a meal,

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.095
you're pushing 10 pounds in the UK.

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:27.098
And I feel like once you've
hit that price point,

00:24:27.098 --> 00:24:27.933
just go somewhere else.

00:24:27.933 --> 00:24:31.036
Just go to a restaurant, because
you'll get better quality,

00:24:31.036 --> 00:24:35.474
fresher food for probably the
same, if not even less, money.

00:24:35.474 --> 00:24:36.975
There's a linguistic
difference to point out,

00:24:36.975 --> 00:24:38.243
which is that in the UK,

00:24:38.243 --> 00:24:41.480
we call our weird long
Burger King chicken burger

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:42.914
a Chicken Royale.

00:24:42.914 --> 00:24:44.916
In the UK, we can also
get our Chicken Royale

00:24:44.916 --> 00:24:46.117
with bacon and cheese,

00:24:48.453 --> 00:24:51.189
or we can get a Californian
barbecue Royale.

00:24:53.425 --> 00:24:56.761
So that is the standard
Royale with the chicken,

00:24:57.662 --> 00:24:59.764
the lettuce, and the mayo,

00:24:59.764 --> 00:25:02.234
but also quite a lot of barbecue sauce,

00:25:02.234 --> 00:25:04.102
and also some tomato on there.

00:25:04.102 --> 00:25:05.871
Surprisingly, we actually
have a couple of Popeyes

00:25:05.871 --> 00:25:08.340
exclusive sandwiches that
you don't have in the US.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:10.575
Here we have the deluxe chicken sandwich

00:25:10.575 --> 00:25:12.944
and the spicy deluxe chicken sandwich.

00:25:12.944 --> 00:25:17.382
Now, while a regular Popeye
sandwich is just sauce, chicken,

00:25:17.382 --> 00:25:20.719
and pickles, these ones have
sauce, chicken, pickles,

00:25:20.719 --> 00:25:23.188
but also lettuce and cheese.

00:25:23.188 --> 00:25:25.924
With the standard one, the sauce is mayo,

00:25:25.924 --> 00:25:29.394
and on the spicy one,
it's actually the sauce

00:25:29.394 --> 00:25:31.796
that changes rather than the chicken,

00:25:31.796 --> 00:25:34.499
this one having a spicy mayo instead.

00:25:34.499 --> 00:25:37.202
I don't know if adding
a little bit of lettuce

00:25:37.202 --> 00:25:41.239
and cheese to something is
enough to qualify it as deluxe.

00:25:42.207 --> 00:25:43.542
I think if you're
calling something deluxe,

00:25:43.542 --> 00:25:45.610
I'm going to need a little
bit more than that, guys,

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:47.012
but I'll give it a try.

00:25:48.813 --> 00:25:51.983
You know what, I'm going to say it.

00:25:51.983 --> 00:25:54.052
I think the deluxe sandwiches are worse

00:25:54.052 --> 00:25:55.687
than the originals from Popeyes.

00:25:55.687 --> 00:25:57.389
I think for the couple of extra pounds

00:25:57.389 --> 00:25:59.558
that it's costing you to
add a little bit of cheese

00:25:59.558 --> 00:26:01.326
and lettuce to your sandwich,

00:26:01.326 --> 00:26:03.395
you're better off without it, honestly.

00:26:03.395 --> 00:26:05.463
The next exclusive is actually
a bit surprising to me.

00:26:05.463 --> 00:26:08.199
It's this one from Jollibee,
the double chicken sandwich.

00:26:08.199 --> 00:26:10.802
It's surprising in the sense
that the UK is the country

00:26:10.802 --> 00:26:12.037
of excess here for once.

00:26:13.238 --> 00:26:17.275
We're the ones with the
much larger portion.

00:26:17.275 --> 00:26:19.511
This is a hefty, hefty sandwich.

00:26:19.511 --> 00:26:22.581
We have one exclusive chicken
sandwich from the UK Wendy's,

00:26:22.581 --> 00:26:24.783
which is the avocado chicken club.

00:26:24.783 --> 00:26:28.453
So you get the chicken patty
with some cheese on top,

00:26:28.453 --> 00:26:32.524
and then you have some
mashed avocado, bacon,

00:26:32.524 --> 00:26:34.459
lettuce, and tomato.

00:26:34.459 --> 00:26:35.727
Not to be outdone, the UK also has

00:26:35.727 --> 00:26:38.463
quite a few chicken chains
that you won't find in the US.

00:26:38.463 --> 00:26:40.231
And interestingly, our
chicken sandwich culture

00:26:40.231 --> 00:26:41.466
is quite different to yours.

00:26:41.466 --> 00:26:42.767
Now, in terms of chains,

00:26:42.767 --> 00:26:45.503
Nando's is obviously a
pretty iconic chicken joint

00:26:45.503 --> 00:26:46.738
over here in the UK.

00:26:46.738 --> 00:26:48.540
Now, while you can find some
of these on the East Coast

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:51.109
of the US, over here, they're
in pretty much every city

00:26:51.109 --> 00:26:53.211
and town and are kind of
like a foundational part

00:26:53.211 --> 00:26:55.013
of the UK culture at this point.

00:26:55.013 --> 00:26:57.215
We're now so obsessed
with peri-peri as a flavor

00:26:57.215 --> 00:26:58.883
that almost every restaurant
that sells chicken

00:26:58.883 --> 00:27:00.318
will have a peri-peri option.

00:27:00.318 --> 00:27:02.354
And you've seen lots of other
chains trying to spring up

00:27:02.354 --> 00:27:05.624
to capitalize on the popularity,
like Roosters and Pepe's.

00:27:05.624 --> 00:27:07.392
Now we can't talk about
British chicken sandwiches

00:27:07.392 --> 00:27:09.194
without talking about chicken shops.

00:27:09.194 --> 00:27:11.262
Chicken shops are an integral part of UK,

00:27:11.262 --> 00:27:13.098
and more specifically London, culture.

00:27:13.098 --> 00:27:15.266
The big players are Morley's
and Chicken Cottage,

00:27:15.266 --> 00:27:17.302
but there are literally
thousands of independent ones

00:27:17.302 --> 00:27:18.670
scattered across the UK.

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:20.338
They're so popular that
people are ripping off

00:27:20.338 --> 00:27:21.272
the big chains now.

00:27:21.272 --> 00:27:24.409
So you'll see things like
Monley's and Mowley's opening up

00:27:24.409 --> 00:27:27.012
with deceptively similar
branding and decor

00:27:27.012 --> 00:27:28.713
to the classic Morley's chain.

00:27:28.713 --> 00:27:31.049
To put into context just how
popular chicken shops are,

00:27:31.049 --> 00:27:33.585
there are currently around
8,000 of them in London.

00:27:33.585 --> 00:27:36.054
McDonald's has just 200 locations.

00:27:36.054 --> 00:27:37.989
A funny thing is how some of
the independent chicken shops

00:27:37.989 --> 00:27:39.958
aren't just ripping off
other ones like Morley's,

00:27:39.958 --> 00:27:42.193
but they're trying to rip
off the big chains like KFC.

00:27:42.193 --> 00:27:44.429
I think in the UK, people
don't really understand

00:27:44.429 --> 00:27:46.297
what fried chicken means when you refer it

00:27:46.297 --> 00:27:47.866
to a specific US state.

00:27:47.866 --> 00:27:49.701
So I'm sure to Americans
they will know the difference

00:27:49.701 --> 00:27:50.969
between Kentucky fried chicken

00:27:50.969 --> 00:27:52.871
versus something that was fried elsewhere.

00:27:52.871 --> 00:27:55.006
But in the UK, we don't
really have that context.

00:27:55.006 --> 00:27:56.408
In the UK, you might walk down the street

00:27:56.408 --> 00:27:59.911
and see Ohio fried chicken,
Maryland fried chicken,

00:27:59.911 --> 00:28:01.746
or Alaska fried chicken.

00:28:01.746 --> 00:28:04.683
As an American, yes, Kentucky
does have a distinct type

00:28:04.683 --> 00:28:05.750
of fried chicken.

00:28:07.085 --> 00:28:10.689
Popeyes is Louisiana,
other southern states

00:28:10.689 --> 00:28:13.258
have their own ways of
making fried chicken.

00:28:13.258 --> 00:28:14.959
Yeah, Nashville, hot fried chicken.

00:28:14.959 --> 00:28:15.794
That's another one,

00:28:15.794 --> 00:28:16.628
which is a city and not a state.

00:28:16.628 --> 00:28:17.729
Yes, I realized.

00:28:17.729 --> 00:28:22.734
But yeah, Alaska, no, Ohio, no.

00:28:22.901 --> 00:28:27.038
Montana, no, no. Montana has no cuisine.

00:28:27.038 --> 00:28:28.006
Another thing to point out

00:28:28.006 --> 00:28:29.874
is that when it comes to
the big chains like Morley's

00:28:29.874 --> 00:28:31.743
and Chicken Cottage, the individual shops

00:28:31.743 --> 00:28:32.977
are often franchised.

00:28:32.977 --> 00:28:35.580
Leon swears by the
Thornton Heath Morley's.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:38.249
He's a South London guy, and
he says that's the best one.

00:28:38.249 --> 00:28:39.084
If you've been to a better one,

00:28:39.084 --> 00:28:40.085
let us know.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:44.255
Now I'm going to talk
about all the US sandwiches

00:28:44.255 --> 00:28:48.093
you cannot get in the
UK. Going to go by chain.

00:28:48.093 --> 00:28:49.160
So McDonald's, for instance,

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:51.529
you can get a deluxe
crispy chicken sandwich,

00:28:51.529 --> 00:28:53.398
which is just the regular chicken sandwich

00:28:53.398 --> 00:28:56.401
with lettuce and tomato, mayo on it.

00:28:57.368 --> 00:29:00.004
And then you can also
get the spicy version.

00:29:00.004 --> 00:29:01.840
Sauce is a little bit oranger,

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:03.541
which I guess makes it spicier.

00:29:03.541 --> 00:29:06.111
Burger King. I saw it
in there. What was it?

00:29:06.111 --> 00:29:09.981
You guys called this
the the Burger Royale,

00:29:09.981 --> 00:29:13.251
and ours is just the original
crispy chicken sandwich,

00:29:14.486 --> 00:29:16.488
sub, sandwich, shaped like that

00:29:16.488 --> 00:29:19.090
for seemingly no specific reason.

00:29:19.090 --> 00:29:20.191
Yep.

00:29:20.191 --> 00:29:23.394
KFC, the Chicken Little,
that's a little guy.

00:29:23.394 --> 00:29:26.231
You can also get your Chick-fil-A
sandwich spicy version.

00:29:27.298 --> 00:29:28.666
That looks spicier?

00:29:28.666 --> 00:29:33.671
It comes in a red bag, because
red is hot, and spicy is hot.

00:29:33.972 --> 00:29:36.641
I mean everything at Carl's
Jr. was an exclusive, I think,

00:29:36.641 --> 00:29:38.409
because you guys don't even have one.

00:29:39.577 --> 00:29:42.647
It just seems like we're kind
of just throwing everything

00:29:42.647 --> 00:29:43.782
at the wall and seeing what sticks.

00:29:43.782 --> 00:29:45.283
Crispy chicken with bacon.

00:29:46.217 --> 00:29:49.154
I feel that Wendy's especially,
Wendy's and Carl's Jr.,

00:29:49.154 --> 00:29:54.159
they just are trying to
do variations on a theme,

00:29:54.359 --> 00:29:56.294
hoping that will kind of get everyone.

00:29:56.294 --> 00:29:59.564
I understand why, but it's
a bit overwhelmed, right?

00:29:59.564 --> 00:30:00.465
I mean I don't even know which one to get.

00:30:00.465 --> 00:30:01.599
I don't want to bite any of these.

00:30:01.599 --> 00:30:03.234
These all look bad.

00:30:03.234 --> 00:30:04.702
This whole section here is just like,

00:30:04.702 --> 00:30:06.337
throw everything at the
wall and see what sticks.

00:30:06.337 --> 00:30:09.774
I don't agree with that strategy, guys.

00:30:09.774 --> 00:30:12.510
Just by the looks of all these sandwiches,

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:15.747
the flagship chicken sandwich
at each one of the chains,

00:30:15.747 --> 00:30:17.649
I would more go for than any of these.

00:30:19.384 --> 00:30:20.819
I don't want any of these.

00:30:20.952 --> 00:30:22.921
Harry: From exclusive
items to portion sizes,

00:30:22.921 --> 00:30:25.190
we wanted to find out all the
differences between Ikea food

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:26.624
in the US and the UK.

00:30:26.624 --> 00:30:27.859
This is "Food Wars."

00:30:30.195 --> 00:30:31.996
Quick note: It turns
out Ikea does not offer

00:30:31.996 --> 00:30:33.965
a takeout option, but
fortunately they do sell

00:30:33.965 --> 00:30:35.333
lots of Tupperware containers.

00:30:35.333 --> 00:30:36.534
So in case you're wondering why the food

00:30:36.534 --> 00:30:37.602
looks a little bit sad,

00:30:37.602 --> 00:30:39.437
it's because we had to
improvise. On with the video.

00:30:39.437 --> 00:30:42.574
Let's start portion sizes
with Ikea's claim to fame

00:30:42.574 --> 00:30:45.443
when it comes to food,
the Swedish meatballs.

00:30:45.443 --> 00:30:48.813
In the US, Ikea's restaurant
offer only one portion size

00:30:48.813 --> 00:30:50.448
for meatballs, eight pieces,

00:30:50.448 --> 00:30:53.585
but after that you can get
them in increments of four.

00:30:53.585 --> 00:30:58.089
For instance, 12 meatballs.

00:30:58.089 --> 00:30:59.791
In the UK, Ikea's iconic meatballs

00:30:59.791 --> 00:31:03.561
come in two portion sizes, eight or 12.

00:31:03.561 --> 00:31:05.663
You can also add four extra
meatballs to any order

00:31:05.663 --> 00:31:07.999
for an extra £1.75, and there's seemingly

00:31:07.999 --> 00:31:10.034
no limit to how many
times you can do this.

00:31:10.034 --> 00:31:12.270
In the UK, the meatballs come
with either mashed potatoes

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:15.206
or chips on the side, as
well as some garden peas,

00:31:15.206 --> 00:31:18.309
lingonberry jam, and of
course their famous gravy.

00:31:18.309 --> 00:31:20.545
And of course the meatball
options all come with a side

00:31:20.545 --> 00:31:23.982
of mashed potatoes and
peas. Get a load of that.

00:31:23.982 --> 00:31:25.650
Also a little bit of cranberry sauce.

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:27.452
We're going to weigh eight
meatballs in both countries

00:31:27.452 --> 00:31:28.953
to see whose meatballs are bigger.

00:31:28.953 --> 00:31:31.522
One, two,

00:31:31.522 --> 00:31:33.925
three, four,

00:31:33.925 --> 00:31:35.860
five, six,

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:37.962
seven, and eight.

00:31:40.498 --> 00:31:42.100
Let's also try and weigh our mash.

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:44.335
Now, I do feel like the stats
might be skewed slightly

00:31:44.335 --> 00:31:46.170
because there's some
peas embedded in this.

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:48.239
I will try and free it where I can.

00:31:51.142 --> 00:31:55.380
We got way more mash than
the UK, like almost double!

00:31:56.714 --> 00:31:58.583
They're feeding us at our Ikeas.

00:31:58.583 --> 00:32:01.486
Vegans and vegetarians,
rejoice. Ikea also offers

00:32:01.486 --> 00:32:04.622
vegetarian-vegan versions
of its meatballs right here.

00:32:04.622 --> 00:32:07.992
We're doing a blind taste
test, plant versus meat.

00:32:10.261 --> 00:32:11.663
That's definitely plant-based.

00:32:11.663 --> 00:32:12.497
What's the next one?

00:32:12.497 --> 00:32:13.731
Peter: No.

00:32:13.731 --> 00:32:14.666
Oh, was that wrong?

00:32:14.666 --> 00:32:16.200
Wait, OK, give me another one.

00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:18.269
OK, this is the other one?

00:32:20.939 --> 00:32:22.840
Yeah, that's meat. Not even close.

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:25.043
Not even close. Thank you, Peter.

00:32:25.043 --> 00:32:26.544
Neither were bad.

00:32:26.544 --> 00:32:28.947
And if I was a vegetarian or vegan,

00:32:28.947 --> 00:32:31.749
I would be perfectly happy
with those plant balls.

00:32:31.749 --> 00:32:36.220
Tasted perfectly fine, but
the difference is noticeable.

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:38.056
We share a couple of
dessert options with the US,

00:32:38.056 --> 00:32:39.390
notably the Daim cake.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:41.626
Over here, we call that
the caramel almond cake.

00:32:41.626 --> 00:32:42.794
Let's weigh a slice.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.799
Another dessert option
we share with the UK

00:32:47.799 --> 00:32:49.133
is the soft-serve ice cream,

00:32:49.133 --> 00:32:51.469
which you get in vanilla or strawberry.

00:32:51.469 --> 00:32:54.005
OK, first of all, the
gentleman who worked there

00:32:54.005 --> 00:32:56.107
was adamant that's actually frozen yogurt.

00:32:56.107 --> 00:32:57.175
I don't know if that's true or not.

00:32:57.175 --> 00:32:59.944
Regardless, they didn't have to-go cups

00:32:59.944 --> 00:33:03.247
like Harry was able to get,
so all they would give us

00:33:03.247 --> 00:33:06.551
is the small cup where
it's kind of poking out

00:33:06.551 --> 00:33:07.552
like it's ready to eat.

00:33:07.552 --> 00:33:10.421
It is 97 degrees in Los Angeles today.

00:33:10.421 --> 00:33:13.524
There was absolutely no way
that was making it from Ikea

00:33:13.524 --> 00:33:15.660
to the car, let alone the studio.

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.930
So here's footage of me enjoying
it in the lobby of Ikea.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:20.965
So, I'm sorry I didn't bring the scale.

00:33:20.965 --> 00:33:22.200
I don't know how much it weighs.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.168
We have soft-serve ice
cream in the UK too,

00:33:24.168 --> 00:33:26.504
either as vanilla or vanilla
with chocolate sauce.

00:33:26.504 --> 00:33:28.973
Impressively, they've
given us exactly 100 grams

00:33:28.973 --> 00:33:30.108
of soft-serve ice cream.

00:33:30.108 --> 00:33:31.809
Ikea is also known for its hot dogs.

00:33:31.809 --> 00:33:33.978
In the UK you can get
either a classic hot dog

00:33:33.978 --> 00:33:35.880
or a veggie hot dog from the bistro stand.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.582
We're going to measure and
weigh the classic hot dog

00:33:37.582 --> 00:33:39.517
to compare with the US.

00:33:39.517 --> 00:33:41.753
The hot dog in the UK
is 7 inches in length,

00:33:41.753 --> 00:33:43.187
and with the toppings
of pickles and onions,

00:33:43.187 --> 00:33:45.123
it weighs 114 grams.

00:33:45.123 --> 00:33:46.824
Hot dog.

00:33:46.824 --> 00:33:50.461
Our hot dog is 5 ¼ inches long,

00:33:50.461 --> 00:33:53.898
and our hot dog weighs 75 grams.

00:33:57.835 --> 00:33:59.804
Here are all the menu items from a UK Ikea

00:33:59.804 --> 00:34:01.239
that you won't find in the US.

00:34:01.239 --> 00:34:03.875
Here's all the food
you'll find in a US Ikea

00:34:03.875 --> 00:34:05.576
you won't find in the UK.

00:34:05.576 --> 00:34:08.813
Starting out down here, hot entrées.

00:34:08.813 --> 00:34:12.016
This single piece of chicken
is the Havana chicken.

00:34:12.016 --> 00:34:14.919
Now, it doesn't come alone.
It comes with plantains,

00:34:14.919 --> 00:34:17.221
rice, and beans, which we
will see in a little bit.

00:34:17.221 --> 00:34:19.390
But just isolated, the chicken,

00:34:19.390 --> 00:34:21.259
has been sitting for a minute, sorry.

00:34:21.259 --> 00:34:23.694
Havana chicken. I wonder
what makes it so Havana.

00:34:25.163 --> 00:34:27.198
Yeah, I'm definitely
getting some spices in here.

00:34:27.198 --> 00:34:30.935
Very flavorful, not just, like,
a standard piece of chicken.

00:34:30.935 --> 00:34:31.969
It's actually really good.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:33.871
We'll start down this end
with some main courses.

00:34:33.871 --> 00:34:36.741
We have two different
nonmeat-meatball options in the UK.

00:34:36.741 --> 00:34:38.242
Firstly, we have the veggie balls.

00:34:38.242 --> 00:34:39.510
These are meatballs which are formed

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:40.678
of ground-up vegetables,

00:34:40.678 --> 00:34:43.281
and they're also served with
a vegetable couscous as well.

00:34:43.281 --> 00:34:44.949
But for vegetarians who
prefer something a bit more

00:34:44.949 --> 00:34:47.652
reminiscent of meat, they
also have plant balls.

00:34:47.652 --> 00:34:48.653
Now, I'm kind of intrigued by these

00:34:48.653 --> 00:34:49.687
because they look really good.

00:34:49.687 --> 00:34:53.057
These are a fake-meat version
of Ikea's classic meatballs.

00:34:53.057 --> 00:34:54.759
Now, compared to a classic meatball,

00:34:54.759 --> 00:34:56.694
they're actually almost
more visually appealing,

00:34:56.694 --> 00:34:59.630
I would say. They're a nice,
dark, golden brown color.

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:01.833
They look like they've been
cooked in a similar way,

00:35:01.833 --> 00:35:03.468
maybe even kind of fried

00:35:03.468 --> 00:35:06.337
or cooked for longer than
you would expect from these.

00:35:06.337 --> 00:35:08.840
Let's do a very quick taste
test. Classic meatball.

00:35:10.975 --> 00:35:12.977
We all know and love it.
These things are delicious.

00:35:12.977 --> 00:35:14.579
And then one of the plant balls.

00:35:16.414 --> 00:35:18.683
I will say the plant-based
one doesn't have that

00:35:18.683 --> 00:35:21.185
distinct Ikea meatball taste to it.

00:35:21.185 --> 00:35:22.887
That said, still pretty tasty.

00:35:22.887 --> 00:35:25.623
I think texture-wise,
it's almost identical

00:35:25.623 --> 00:35:26.657
to the classic meatball.

00:35:26.657 --> 00:35:29.227
You can also get a salmon
filet with pea pesto,

00:35:29.227 --> 00:35:32.063
vegetable medallions, and a corn medley,

00:35:32.063 --> 00:35:33.865
which you can see right here.

00:35:33.865 --> 00:35:35.900
I mean, salmon's OK, I guess.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:39.403
OK. This isn't, it's
been sitting for a while.

00:35:39.403 --> 00:35:42.373
If you like salmon, sure.

00:35:42.373 --> 00:35:44.375
I don't know, you go to
Ikea, you get salmon?

00:35:44.375 --> 00:35:45.710
Pea pesto's interesting though.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:48.379
Still in the mood for fish

00:35:48.379 --> 00:35:50.481
before you go furniture shopping?

00:35:50.481 --> 00:35:53.651
You can also get the garlic
lemon cod with, of course,

00:35:53.651 --> 00:35:55.253
mashed potatoes and peas.

00:35:55.253 --> 00:35:57.221
Actually, that's a pretty
good piece of fish.

00:35:57.221 --> 00:35:58.990
Yeah, I actually really like that.

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:00.758
OK, I stand corrected.

00:36:00.758 --> 00:36:05.196
I was dissing the fish.
It's actually pretty good.

00:36:05.196 --> 00:36:08.633
We have a British classic dish,
and that's fish and chips.

00:36:08.633 --> 00:36:11.369
Now, the fish they serve at
Ikea in the UK is pollack.

00:36:11.369 --> 00:36:15.106
It comes with chips, peas,
and some tartar sauce.

00:36:15.106 --> 00:36:16.941
Now, unlike fish and
chips that you might find

00:36:16.941 --> 00:36:18.643
from a classic chippy in the UK,

00:36:18.643 --> 00:36:20.211
they've actually breaded their fish

00:36:20.211 --> 00:36:22.013
rather than battered and then fried it.

00:36:22.013 --> 00:36:24.115
I generally prefer the
battered fish texture.

00:36:24.115 --> 00:36:25.783
I like the kind of crispy
crunch on the outside

00:36:25.783 --> 00:36:26.884
that you get from batter.

00:36:26.884 --> 00:36:30.121
I'm going to have a little try of this.

00:36:30.121 --> 00:36:32.190
It's very weird tasting
things on "Food Wars"

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:34.659
using a knife and fork. I don't like it.

00:36:34.659 --> 00:36:37.762
I respect that they have
attempted to localize their menu

00:36:37.762 --> 00:36:39.430
to the UK market.

00:36:39.430 --> 00:36:43.367
But maybe put a bit more
thought into your R&amp;D there.

00:36:43.367 --> 00:36:45.002
If you want to see what proper
fish and chips looks like,

00:36:45.002 --> 00:36:46.404
go and check out our "Food Tours"

00:36:46.404 --> 00:36:48.372
best fish and chips in London video.

00:36:48.372 --> 00:36:51.943
Next up we have another fish
option. It's a salmon filet.

00:36:51.943 --> 00:36:53.544
Charlie: Fillet.

00:36:53.544 --> 00:36:55.213
Leon: Come on.

00:36:56.347 --> 00:36:59.383
Next up we have another fish
option. It's a salmon fillet.

00:36:59.383 --> 00:37:02.153
In the UK, we generally
lean more towards "fillet"

00:37:02.153 --> 00:37:04.755
as the wording, and also we
spell it slightly differently.

00:37:04.755 --> 00:37:07.158
Often we spell it with two L's,
whereas I believe in the US

00:37:07.158 --> 00:37:08.559
it tends to be spelled with one.

00:37:08.559 --> 00:37:10.561
I think you can get a
salmon fillet in the US.

00:37:10.561 --> 00:37:11.696
However, like we say,

00:37:11.696 --> 00:37:12.964
we pronounce ours slightly differently,

00:37:12.964 --> 00:37:16.133
and also in the UK ours comes
with the veggie couscous.

00:37:16.133 --> 00:37:18.002
Chicken tenders. I mean, come on.

00:37:20.605 --> 00:37:22.440
Comes with fries and broccoli.

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:26.677
Hm, wait, these tenders
are actually very good.

00:37:26.677 --> 00:37:29.480
Like, the breading,
they're really breaded.

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:31.015
Decent fries. Hearty.

00:37:31.015 --> 00:37:33.551
Ikea also has a few things on the menu

00:37:33.551 --> 00:37:35.786
that were not available
at the one we went to.

00:37:35.786 --> 00:37:38.189
A chicken thigh with
garlic chipotle sauce,

00:37:38.189 --> 00:37:40.057
broccoli, and rice. Sounds
actually pretty good.

00:37:40.057 --> 00:37:42.827
Lingonberry barbecue
ribs with mac 'n' cheese.

00:37:42.827 --> 00:37:44.996
Ah! I was so mad they didn't have those.

00:37:44.996 --> 00:37:46.631
And a plant-based burger with fries,

00:37:46.631 --> 00:37:48.666
also not available at
time of filming this.

00:37:48.666 --> 00:37:52.603
Now, lingonberries are majorly
popular in Swedish cuisine,

00:37:52.603 --> 00:37:54.138
and you're going to see
them pop up a few more times

00:37:54.138 --> 00:37:55.006
in the Ikea menu.

00:37:55.006 --> 00:37:57.475
Next up is penne pasta with tomato sauce.

00:37:57.475 --> 00:37:59.744
Penne, of course, refers
to the shape of pasta

00:37:59.744 --> 00:38:01.612
that they use here in Ikea.

00:38:01.612 --> 00:38:03.781
[Charlie laughing]

00:38:03.781 --> 00:38:04.649
Charlie's loved that.

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:16.360
I think penne is maybe my
least favorite pasta shape.

00:38:17.628 --> 00:38:19.163
I do not respect penne.

00:38:19.163 --> 00:38:20.298
Moving swiftly on from the penne,

00:38:20.298 --> 00:38:22.199
we have a chicken katsu curry.

00:38:22.199 --> 00:38:24.001
I think this is a relatively new addition

00:38:24.001 --> 00:38:25.202
to the UK Ikea menu.

00:38:25.202 --> 00:38:28.039
So, katsu refers to the
chicken katsu fillet,

00:38:28.039 --> 00:38:30.107
which is a breaded and
fried piece of chicken,

00:38:30.107 --> 00:38:32.476
and then it's served with
rice and katsu sauce.

00:38:32.476 --> 00:38:34.045
I've not tried this before.

00:38:34.045 --> 00:38:36.714
That looks like one just massive
piece of chicken, you know?

00:38:39.083 --> 00:38:39.984
Science.

00:38:39.984 --> 00:38:42.720
Other than a couple of
slightly disconcerting

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:44.955
dark patches inside the chicken breast,

00:38:44.955 --> 00:38:46.724
it's actually not bad. I
think because of the size

00:38:46.724 --> 00:38:48.793
of the breast it's dried out quite a lot,

00:38:48.793 --> 00:38:50.795
but the katsu sauce is really nice.

00:38:50.795 --> 00:38:51.862
I like it with the rice.

00:38:51.862 --> 00:38:53.497
I think particularly
if we'd eaten this hot,

00:38:53.497 --> 00:38:54.565
this actually would be pretty good.

00:38:54.565 --> 00:38:56.200
Next up on the UK menu you can find

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:59.136
a potato and cauliflower rogan josh curry.

00:38:59.136 --> 00:39:01.372
A rogan josh is a curry
that you'll find on the menu

00:39:01.372 --> 00:39:02.773
of a lot of UK curry houses.

00:39:02.773 --> 00:39:04.842
It's not super spicy,
generally quite mild.

00:39:04.842 --> 00:39:07.178
I'd say traditionally you
would see this made with lamb,

00:39:07.178 --> 00:39:08.746
but they've made a
vegetarian version here,

00:39:08.746 --> 00:39:10.247
obviously with potato and cauliflower

00:39:10.247 --> 00:39:11.482
as the main bulk of it.

00:39:11.482 --> 00:39:13.751
The rogan josh from Ikea
is served with rice,

00:39:13.751 --> 00:39:15.853
and then you can also
get some side options

00:39:15.853 --> 00:39:18.789
which are designed to go with
it, including some pakoras,

00:39:18.789 --> 00:39:20.925
samosas, and a garlic naan.

00:39:20.925 --> 00:39:23.461
Obviously the UK has a large
British Indian population,

00:39:23.461 --> 00:39:24.829
so I do think this is another attempt to

00:39:24.829 --> 00:39:26.130
kind of localize the menu.

00:39:28.366 --> 00:39:30.201
That's pretty good.
That's pretty flavorful.

00:39:30.201 --> 00:39:32.236
There are some other main-course
options from UK Ikeas

00:39:32.236 --> 00:39:34.004
which unfortunately we
couldn't find today.

00:39:34.004 --> 00:39:36.474
These include such things
as butternut squash

00:39:36.474 --> 00:39:39.877
and sweet potato soup;
Mangalore chicken curry;

00:39:39.877 --> 00:39:43.347
butternut squash, blue cheese,
and caramelized onion pie;

00:39:43.347 --> 00:39:46.884
chili sin carne; garlic
butter chicken pie;

00:39:46.884 --> 00:39:48.252
and leek and potato soup.

00:39:48.252 --> 00:39:49.854
I would like to move on to the sides.

00:39:49.854 --> 00:39:52.189
Right here we have the

00:39:52.189 --> 00:39:55.159
vegetable medallions.

00:39:55.159 --> 00:39:56.460
I like veggies presented like this,

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:58.796
just like in a little
puck. Just bite into it.

00:39:58.796 --> 00:40:00.698
Looks like it has some broccoli,

00:40:00.698 --> 00:40:04.001
mostly potato friendly, little onion.

00:40:04.001 --> 00:40:05.870
Yeah, not bad. Decent side.

00:40:05.870 --> 00:40:09.707
It's like broccoli potatoes,
like broccoli mashed potatoes.

00:40:09.707 --> 00:40:12.109
That Havana chicken that
I mentioned earlier,

00:40:12.109 --> 00:40:14.145
this is the beans and rice it comes with.

00:40:14.145 --> 00:40:16.013
I don't know if they mix them
up or it just got mixed up

00:40:16.013 --> 00:40:19.116
in transit, but, you know,
beans and rice. Let's go.

00:40:19.116 --> 00:40:21.585
This is just white rice and black beans.

00:40:21.585 --> 00:40:25.256
I don't taste any additional
flavor to this at all.

00:40:25.256 --> 00:40:27.324
They do sometimes offer plantains.

00:40:27.324 --> 00:40:30.494
Again, the Ikea we went to
today did not have plantains,

00:40:30.494 --> 00:40:32.663
but they did have a garlic bread.

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:37.334
I think that's an exclusive.

00:40:37.334 --> 00:40:39.637
On to the Ikea cold dishes.

00:40:39.637 --> 00:40:42.840
They have a veggie dog.

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:46.043
Well, the veggie dog wrap
is under the cold dishes

00:40:46.043 --> 00:40:48.412
according to the script,
and this veggie dog is cold.

00:40:48.412 --> 00:40:49.313
Maybe it's been sitting a while.

00:40:49.313 --> 00:40:50.881
Either way, it's not very good.

00:40:50.881 --> 00:40:54.385
Also, they have a Greek
salad, also the Caesar salad,

00:40:54.385 --> 00:40:56.754
which, once again, I mean,
this is pretty low effort.

00:40:56.754 --> 00:41:00.090
A Stockholm salad,

00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:02.426
not to be confused with
Stockholm syndrome,

00:41:02.426 --> 00:41:04.562
which I'm starting to feel
right now eating this food.

00:41:04.562 --> 00:41:07.565
Salmon, lettuce, lemon.

00:41:08.799 --> 00:41:10.768
Sure.

00:41:10.768 --> 00:41:12.336
That's not bad, actually.

00:41:12.336 --> 00:41:16.974
I like this sauce that's
turning color under these lights

00:41:16.974 --> 00:41:18.576
and this salmon.

00:41:18.576 --> 00:41:21.212
We're not quite sure
what is in this salad,

00:41:21.212 --> 00:41:25.149
what the sauce is right now,
but due the miracle of editing,

00:41:25.149 --> 00:41:27.718
we can put that on the screen right,

00:41:27.718 --> 00:41:29.086
and that's what it is.

00:41:29.086 --> 00:41:30.254
Tastes pretty good.

00:41:30.254 --> 00:41:34.558
Also, you can get a BLT chicken wrap.

00:41:34.558 --> 00:41:37.695
Not bad if you like wraps
and BLT and chicken.

00:41:37.695 --> 00:41:40.197
Also at a US Ikea, which was not available

00:41:40.197 --> 00:41:43.000
at time of filming this
at our Burbank Ikea,

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:46.937
is a Greek veggie ball
wrap and marinated salmon.

00:41:46.937 --> 00:41:49.607
The UK's cold-plate menu is
pretty bland in comparison.

00:41:49.607 --> 00:41:52.343
The only one they had today was
this marinated salmon plate,

00:41:52.343 --> 00:41:55.346
but we can also get an egg
and shrimp sandwich with mayo,

00:41:55.346 --> 00:41:58.082
marinated salmon wrap, small salad bowl,

00:41:58.082 --> 00:41:59.850
and a tomato and mozzarella salad.

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:01.619
I guess I'll give the salmon a quick try.

00:42:01.619 --> 00:42:03.354
I do like smoked salmon.

00:42:03.354 --> 00:42:04.989
Here, of course, they call it gravlax

00:42:04.989 --> 00:42:06.991
because it's the traditional Swedish way,

00:42:06.991 --> 00:42:09.560
and it comes with a dill
sauce and some lemon.

00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:10.394
Fairly mild flavor.

00:42:10.394 --> 00:42:12.763
I like the kind of herb
crusting that you get.

00:42:12.763 --> 00:42:15.399
The dill sauce is pretty dilly.

00:42:15.399 --> 00:42:16.734
And now on to desserts.

00:42:16.734 --> 00:42:19.670
There are three exclusive
Ikea US desserts.

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:21.071
I was only able to get one of them.

00:42:21.071 --> 00:42:22.973
So, the first would be
the strawberry shortcake,

00:42:22.973 --> 00:42:24.708
not available at time of shooting this.

00:42:24.708 --> 00:42:27.011
The second is this, the
caramel almond cake.

00:42:27.011 --> 00:42:31.348
And the third is the infamous
chocolate layered cake,

00:42:31.348 --> 00:42:33.450
also known as the conspiracy cake.

00:42:33.450 --> 00:42:35.819
I asked everyone there,

00:42:35.819 --> 00:42:38.422
"Please tell me you have chocolate cake."

00:42:38.422 --> 00:42:40.024
And they all said no.

00:42:40.024 --> 00:42:44.194
No chocolate cake at the
Burbank Ikea, today, at least.

00:42:44.194 --> 00:42:45.796
Tragically, we don't
have the conspiracy cake

00:42:45.796 --> 00:42:47.364
here in the UK, but we do have some other

00:42:47.364 --> 00:42:48.732
exclusive dessert options.

00:42:48.732 --> 00:42:51.802
For example, this
delightful-looking strawberry tart.

00:42:51.802 --> 00:42:53.671
There's some real pieces
of strawberry in there.

00:42:53.671 --> 00:42:55.372
I like the pastry, I like the cream,

00:42:55.372 --> 00:42:56.540
I like the fresh strawberries.

00:42:56.540 --> 00:43:00.077
Whatever the kind of
strawberry syrup jelly stuff

00:43:00.077 --> 00:43:02.446
that they've put around
the outside is, it's OK,

00:43:02.446 --> 00:43:05.583
but it has a slightly, almost
medicinal quality to it

00:43:05.583 --> 00:43:09.119
that I don't really love. It
almost reminds me of Calpol.

00:43:09.119 --> 00:43:10.220
I don't know if you have that in the US,

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:11.622
but it's a medicine

00:43:11.622 --> 00:43:13.857
that's marketed towards
children in the UK.

00:43:13.857 --> 00:43:16.760
Next up, we have Ikea's famous Daim cake.

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:18.796
Now, you do have this on
the menu in the US, however,

00:43:18.796 --> 00:43:20.531
I believe you guys call it
something slightly different.

00:43:20.531 --> 00:43:21.966
I think caramel almond cake.

00:43:21.966 --> 00:43:24.101
Is Daim not a particularly
big brand name in the US?

00:43:24.101 --> 00:43:26.303
Because over here in the
UK I'd say most people

00:43:26.303 --> 00:43:29.340
are familiar with Daim
as a chocolate brand.

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:32.076
Harry was just talking a
minute ago about the Daim cake,

00:43:32.076 --> 00:43:34.311
the diam cake, D-I-A-M cake?

00:43:34.311 --> 00:43:36.313
I've never heard of it. I've
never heard of that brand.

00:43:36.313 --> 00:43:39.183
I have no idea what it is, I'm
sorry, but we do have this,

00:43:39.183 --> 00:43:41.185
which I think is supposed to be similar.

00:43:44.188 --> 00:43:46.724
Oh, yeah. This is fantastic.

00:43:46.724 --> 00:43:47.658
I'm getting one of these every time I go.

00:43:47.658 --> 00:43:48.993
I'm going to get one of these,
and I'm going to get that

00:43:48.993 --> 00:43:50.828
chocolate cake. I'm going to
be on the lookout for it now.

00:43:50.828 --> 00:43:53.430
It's maybe not quite as popular
in the UK as it used to be.

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:54.598
It reminds me of my grandad a lot

00:43:54.598 --> 00:43:56.100
because he used to really love a Daim bar.

00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:58.502
I wouldn't see many people
my age going for it,

00:43:58.502 --> 00:44:00.771
but still very tasty if
you're in the mood for it.

00:44:00.771 --> 00:44:03.107
And this cake is sensational.

00:44:03.107 --> 00:44:05.876
You get these layers of chocolate,

00:44:05.876 --> 00:44:08.512
this kind of biscuity crumb,
and these kind of, like,

00:44:08.512 --> 00:44:10.948
almost pancakes in the middle.

00:44:10.948 --> 00:44:13.150
I think these dividing
lines might almost be

00:44:13.150 --> 00:44:15.986
kind of cold, chilled pancakes.

00:44:15.986 --> 00:44:18.055
This might be my favorite
thing on the menu.

00:44:18.055 --> 00:44:20.424
This is why I'm making
excuses to go to Ikea.

00:44:22.426 --> 00:44:23.260
Mm.

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:25.929
They did have these at the register,

00:44:25.929 --> 00:44:29.133
and this also says Daim
on it. Have we seen that?

00:44:29.133 --> 00:44:31.835
Yeah. OK, this tastes like this.

00:44:31.835 --> 00:44:33.237
Never heard of this, but it's very good,

00:44:33.237 --> 00:44:34.405
and this is fantastic.

00:44:34.405 --> 00:44:36.473
Maybe they just rebranded
it because no one in the US

00:44:36.473 --> 00:44:39.243
knows what ... Daim cake? Diam cake is?

00:44:39.243 --> 00:44:41.145
Our next dessert option is a cheesecake

00:44:41.145 --> 00:44:42.746
with blueberries and raspberries.

00:44:42.746 --> 00:44:45.282
It's a very soft cheesecake,
really easy to cut.

00:44:45.282 --> 00:44:48.085
There's a nice baked crumb on top.

00:44:48.085 --> 00:44:49.319
There's a layer of the berry stuff,

00:44:49.319 --> 00:44:51.321
so there's some blueberries
in there, some raspberries.

00:44:51.321 --> 00:44:52.156
Then finally we have these

00:44:52.156 --> 00:44:54.458
Under the Sea rocky road biscuits.

00:44:54.458 --> 00:44:56.627
Obviously, rocky road
in the UK is a dessert,

00:44:56.627 --> 00:44:58.595
which is usually a no-bake
dessert made of things

00:44:58.595 --> 00:45:01.765
like melted chocolate,
golden syrup, marshmallows,

00:45:01.765 --> 00:45:04.134
even things like cornflakes,
pieces of biscuit,

00:45:04.134 --> 00:45:06.136
that sort of thing, all
just kind of mixed together,

00:45:06.136 --> 00:45:08.305
left to set, and then you
have a nice easy dessert.

00:45:08.305 --> 00:45:09.940
It turns out what makes this Under the Sea

00:45:09.940 --> 00:45:12.076
is that they have a kind of animal stamp

00:45:12.076 --> 00:45:15.145
in icing on top of it. Here
we have this nice octopus,

00:45:15.145 --> 00:45:17.181
and here we have a couple of dolphins.

00:45:19.216 --> 00:45:20.851
I think when I eat a rocky road

00:45:20.851 --> 00:45:23.620
I kind of want some
more distinct elements.

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:25.989
I like being able to bite
into it and crunch through

00:45:25.989 --> 00:45:28.525
a bit of biscuit or chew
through a raisin or something.

00:45:28.525 --> 00:45:30.894
Whereas with this one, it's
kind of all blended together,

00:45:30.894 --> 00:45:32.863
so you don't really get
the texture contrasts.

00:45:32.863 --> 00:45:34.665
The flavor's very much there.
It's still all those elements

00:45:34.665 --> 00:45:36.033
that I'm used to in terms of the flavor.

00:45:36.033 --> 00:45:37.968
You may also be able to find
some other dessert options

00:45:37.968 --> 00:45:39.937
on a UK menu which we couldn't find today.

00:45:39.937 --> 00:45:42.606
These include things like a
caramelized biscuit cheesecake,

00:45:42.606 --> 00:45:46.410
a chocolate clementine bomb,
an elderflower and lemon cake,

00:45:46.410 --> 00:45:49.279
a gooey chocolatey cake,
a lemon cheesecake,

00:45:49.279 --> 00:45:51.615
rhubarb and custard cake,
and a strawberry jelly.

00:45:51.615 --> 00:45:53.617
Aside from its restaurant, Ikea also has

00:45:53.617 --> 00:45:55.919
the Swedish bistro for quick bites.

00:45:55.919 --> 00:45:59.123
The bistro serves quick bites
like hot dogs and pizza slices

00:45:59.123 --> 00:46:01.525
that you can generally grab
on your way out of the store.

00:46:01.525 --> 00:46:04.528
A few exclusives we had,
of course, was pizza slice.

00:46:04.528 --> 00:46:05.996
There it is.

00:46:05.996 --> 00:46:07.364
I went with cheese.

00:46:07.364 --> 00:46:11.201
Is this like Costco where, like, surprise,

00:46:11.201 --> 00:46:12.736
Ikea has amazing pizza?

00:46:14.371 --> 00:46:16.006
This is a really good slice of pizza.

00:46:16.006 --> 00:46:18.776
I can't tell, but I think
this was oven-cooked,

00:46:18.776 --> 00:46:20.744
fire-oven-cooked, not
just like the pizza oven.

00:46:20.744 --> 00:46:23.847
This has, like, a,
yeah, like a brick oven.

00:46:25.015 --> 00:46:27.584
It's not as good as Costco,
but it's pretty good.

00:46:27.584 --> 00:46:30.521
Wow, weird. Ikea's got good
pizza. Good on you, Ikea.

00:46:30.521 --> 00:46:34.091
You can also get this
giant bistro pretzel.

00:46:34.091 --> 00:46:36.426
Look at this little,
like, a little braid here.

00:46:36.426 --> 00:46:37.461
Look at me, I'm a Jedi.

00:46:37.461 --> 00:46:39.596
But you are not a Jedi yet.

00:46:39.596 --> 00:46:40.798
And it comes with mustard.

00:46:40.798 --> 00:46:42.332
They didn't have the mustard either, man.

00:46:42.332 --> 00:46:44.701
I don't know, is it because I
went at 10:30 in the morning

00:46:44.701 --> 00:46:46.737
and the stuff hadn't shown up yet?

00:46:46.737 --> 00:46:48.038
They didn't have anything.

00:46:50.507 --> 00:46:52.109
A pretzel with no mustard.

00:46:53.777 --> 00:46:55.412
No, no.

00:46:57.414 --> 00:46:58.882
You need to dip in something.

00:46:58.882 --> 00:47:00.417
Also at the US Ikea bistro,

00:47:00.417 --> 00:47:02.986
you can get a marinated salmon baguette.

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:03.821
They didn't have it.

00:47:03.821 --> 00:47:06.490
We have a few exclusive bistro
options of our own in the UK.

00:47:06.490 --> 00:47:08.892
Firstly, if you like the
onions on the hot dog,

00:47:08.892 --> 00:47:11.128
you can just get a side of fried onions.

00:47:11.128 --> 00:47:14.498
Next up we have these,
which are chick'less strips.

00:47:14.498 --> 00:47:18.035
So, these are a plant-based
version of a chicken tender.

00:47:18.035 --> 00:47:19.102
I'd say they look pretty good.

00:47:19.102 --> 00:47:20.938
They kind of passed the visual test.

00:47:20.938 --> 00:47:22.873
Yeah, when I tore this apart I was worried

00:47:22.873 --> 00:47:24.474
that the texture wasn't going to be right,

00:47:24.474 --> 00:47:25.742
and unfortunately it's not.

00:47:25.742 --> 00:47:27.311
It's very spongy, it's very mushy.

00:47:27.311 --> 00:47:28.478
It just needs a little bit more texture.

00:47:28.478 --> 00:47:29.713
Just something to make it resemble

00:47:29.713 --> 00:47:31.248
a chicken tender a little bit more.

00:47:31.248 --> 00:47:33.150
Obviously when you get an
actual piece of chicken tender

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:34.718
or chicken breast, you
can kind of tear it,

00:47:34.718 --> 00:47:36.553
and you see those strips and the fibers.

00:47:36.553 --> 00:47:37.821
They just really haven't got that here.

00:47:37.821 --> 00:47:38.722
Flavor's not too bad, though.

00:47:38.722 --> 00:47:40.958
We can also get chips
from the bistro in the UK.

00:47:40.958 --> 00:47:42.893
It doesn't seem like you
guys can do this in the US.

00:47:42.893 --> 00:47:44.995
We have our veggie dog option,
which comes with the same

00:47:44.995 --> 00:47:46.630
toppings as the regular hot dog.

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:47.664
So, here we've got fried onions.

00:47:47.664 --> 00:47:49.132
You can also get some with pickles.

00:47:49.132 --> 00:47:52.236
The veggie dog in the UK isn't
a plant-based-meat thing,

00:47:52.236 --> 00:47:53.704
it is just vegetables.

00:47:53.704 --> 00:47:56.740
So it looks like there's some
kale in there, some carrot,

00:47:56.740 --> 00:47:58.175
maybe, like, some mustard seeds.

00:47:58.175 --> 00:47:59.943
I think that, with the fried onions,

00:47:59.943 --> 00:48:01.411
with the pickles and some sauce,

00:48:01.411 --> 00:48:03.146
actually a pretty good veggie option.

00:48:03.146 --> 00:48:05.849
Then we have some drink
options, notably milkshakes.

00:48:05.849 --> 00:48:08.185
In the UK you can get
milkshakes in four flavors,

00:48:08.185 --> 00:48:10.254
vanilla, chocolate,
strawberry, and banana.

00:48:10.254 --> 00:48:12.623
We got a banana one
today. Have a little sip.

00:48:14.558 --> 00:48:16.793
That's not bad. Pretty
gentle banana flavor.

00:48:16.793 --> 00:48:18.595
It's mostly just ice cream
but with a little bit

00:48:18.595 --> 00:48:21.832
of flavoring in that,
which, who could say no?

00:48:21.832 --> 00:48:23.967
Ikea in the UK does serve
breakfast, although unfortunately

00:48:23.967 --> 00:48:25.969
when we arrived today
they weren't serving it.

00:48:25.969 --> 00:48:27.437
The options that you'll
find there are pretty much

00:48:27.437 --> 00:48:28.538
everything that you would normally find

00:48:28.538 --> 00:48:31.041
on a full English breakfast,
so it includes things like

00:48:31.041 --> 00:48:33.277
bacon, sausages, hash browns,

00:48:33.277 --> 00:48:36.747
beans, tomatoes, mushrooms, black pudding.

00:48:36.747 --> 00:48:39.216
Sometimes you'll find things
like tattie scones as well,

00:48:39.216 --> 00:48:41.318
and you can also get
white or wholemeal toast.

00:48:41.318 --> 00:48:42.486
If breakfast platters aren't your thing,

00:48:42.486 --> 00:48:45.055
then you can also just go for
some fruit or some porridge.

00:48:45.055 --> 00:48:46.723
Breakfast!

00:48:46.723 --> 00:48:49.993
The breakfast platter in the
US also comes in both regular

00:48:49.993 --> 00:48:53.664
and small sizes and is made
up of eggs, potatoes, bacon,

00:48:53.664 --> 00:48:56.033
Swedish pancakes, and now
it's supposed to be topped

00:48:56.033 --> 00:48:58.201
with the lingonberry jam, and
they didn't give it to us!

00:48:58.201 --> 00:49:00.604
The US breakfast menu also offers

00:49:00.604 --> 00:49:04.041
a skagen croissant sandwich,
which is a croissant

00:49:04.041 --> 00:49:07.711
filled with traditional
Swedish shrimp salad.

00:49:07.711 --> 00:49:08.779
They didn't have it.

00:49:08.779 --> 00:49:12.015
I think Ikea has its own
line of drinks available

00:49:12.015 --> 00:49:14.851
in its restaurants. The US
Ikeas, you'll find cola,

00:49:14.851 --> 00:49:17.754
sugar-free cola,
sparkling raspberry juice,

00:49:17.754 --> 00:49:20.457
lingonberry juice, and sparkling lemonade.

00:49:20.457 --> 00:49:23.160
I went ahead and got
the lingonberry juice.

00:49:24.027 --> 00:49:25.362
Straight up, no idea
what a lingonberry is,

00:49:25.362 --> 00:49:26.663
but it's fantastic.

00:49:26.663 --> 00:49:28.265
There's no difference in the size cups.

00:49:28.265 --> 00:49:30.167
You just have glasses
that are the same size

00:49:30.167 --> 00:49:32.002
or these to-go cups or whatever.

00:49:32.002 --> 00:49:33.637
We have some drink options
at the restaurant in the UK

00:49:33.637 --> 00:49:35.272
that you guys might not have in the US.

00:49:35.272 --> 00:49:36.740
There are a couple of options for kids,

00:49:36.740 --> 00:49:38.976
which are these Pip Organic juices.

00:49:38.976 --> 00:49:41.111
We have a couple of
Robinsons options in the UK.

00:49:41.111 --> 00:49:43.380
Robinsons is probably the
most popular brand of squash

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:45.182
in the UK, and this is basically just

00:49:45.182 --> 00:49:47.017
a diluted version of their squash.

00:49:47.017 --> 00:49:49.419
Squash, for those who don't
know, is a concentrated,

00:49:49.419 --> 00:49:51.621
fruity drink that you kind of water down

00:49:51.621 --> 00:49:53.290
to have a nice refreshing beverage.

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:55.025
You can get a peach and mango version

00:49:55.025 --> 00:49:56.827
or a raspberry and apple version.

00:49:56.827 --> 00:49:58.128
And if you feel like celebrating

00:49:58.128 --> 00:49:59.563
any of your purchases
that you've just made

00:49:59.563 --> 00:50:02.065
from the Ikea, you can also get alcohol.

00:50:02.065 --> 00:50:05.669
We have three alcoholic options
on the menu at a UK Ikea,

00:50:05.669 --> 00:50:09.873
a rosé wine, a red wine, or a dark lager.

00:50:09.873 --> 00:50:12.175
Finally, Ikea has a Swedish
market where you can buy

00:50:12.175 --> 00:50:14.378
prepackaged food items to take home.

00:50:14.378 --> 00:50:16.279
The items on offer include
some of their classic foods,

00:50:16.279 --> 00:50:18.415
like the meatballs,
which you can buy frozen,

00:50:18.415 --> 00:50:20.584
or some gravy sachets,
which you can make at home.

00:50:20.584 --> 00:50:22.419
In the UK, you can also
find the plant-based balls

00:50:22.419 --> 00:50:23.453
on offer in there.

00:50:23.453 --> 00:50:25.188
You can get a whole frozen Daim cake,

00:50:25.188 --> 00:50:27.324
which I'll be taking home
and eating all by myself.

00:50:27.324 --> 00:50:29.026
And speaking of Daim, you
can also get the chocolate

00:50:29.026 --> 00:50:30.327
which the cake is based on.

00:50:30.327 --> 00:50:32.429
Here it's in bite-sized form rather than

00:50:32.429 --> 00:50:34.631
as actual chocolate
bars, but as you can see,

00:50:34.631 --> 00:50:37.100
it's chocolate with a kind
of hard caramel center.

00:50:37.100 --> 00:50:38.168
Charlie has dibs on these.

00:50:38.168 --> 00:50:42.539
Another thing at the US Ikea,

00:50:43.473 --> 00:50:45.308
maybe not exclusives per se,

00:50:45.308 --> 00:50:49.579
but of course they have a
whole thing of frozen stuff.

00:50:49.579 --> 00:50:51.114
I just grabbed stuff that was interesting.

00:50:51.114 --> 00:50:53.884
Come on, the Cinnabons, huh?

00:50:53.884 --> 00:50:55.252
The cinnamon buns.

00:50:55.252 --> 00:50:56.286
Meatballs.

00:50:56.286 --> 00:50:59.790
And, you know we were talking about

00:50:59.790 --> 00:51:01.525
the Daim cake,

00:51:01.525 --> 00:51:06.463
but they do have, it's
frozen to take home,

00:51:06.463 --> 00:51:08.465
and of course it is the Daim cake.

00:51:11.301 --> 00:51:13.236
Let's compare the nutritional
information of some iconic

00:51:13.236 --> 00:51:15.305
Ikea dishes in the US and the UK.

00:51:15.305 --> 00:51:18.208
In the UK, eight Ikea meatballs
contains the following:

00:51:18.208 --> 00:51:19.976
342 calories,

00:51:19.976 --> 00:51:21.978
25.1 grams of fat,

00:51:21.978 --> 00:51:23.447
8.2 grams of carbs,

00:51:23.447 --> 00:51:26.283
and 856 milligrams of sodium.

00:51:26.283 --> 00:51:28.852
In the US, the same plate
of Swedish meatballs contain

00:51:28.852 --> 00:51:30.253
304 calories,

00:51:30.253 --> 00:51:32.322
22.4 grams of fat,

00:51:32.322 --> 00:51:34.391
6.4 grams of carbs,

00:51:34.391 --> 00:51:37.194
592 milligrams of sodium.

00:51:37.194 --> 00:51:39.863
The UK takes this round in every category.

00:51:39.863 --> 00:51:41.131
What about the Ikea hot dog?

00:51:41.131 --> 00:51:44.968
In the US it contains 260
calories, 15 grams of fat,

00:51:44.968 --> 00:51:48.939
24 grams of carbs, and
780 milligrams of sodium.

00:51:48.939 --> 00:51:52.709
And in the UK, the hot
dog contains 293 calories,

00:51:52.709 --> 00:51:56.379
15.2 grams of fat, 29.5 grams of carbs,

00:51:56.379 --> 00:51:58.381
and 708 milligrams of sodium.

00:51:58.381 --> 00:52:00.684
Looks like the UK version
is higher in calories

00:52:00.684 --> 00:52:03.086
and fat once again. USA, baby.

00:52:05.856 --> 00:52:08.125
What's actually in the
iconic Ikea meatballs?

00:52:08.125 --> 00:52:09.860
According to the Ikea
website, if you want to make

00:52:09.860 --> 00:52:12.596
their meatballs at home, use
the following ingredients:

00:52:12.596 --> 00:52:17.067
beef mince, pork mince, onion,
garlic, bread crumbs, milk,

00:52:17.067 --> 00:52:19.336
egg, and salt and pepper to taste.

00:52:19.336 --> 00:52:20.837
But is that actually the ingredients list

00:52:20.837 --> 00:52:22.239
for proper Ikea meatballs?

00:52:22.239 --> 00:52:25.075
Well, on the back of the frozen packet,

00:52:25.075 --> 00:52:26.443
you'll find an ingredients list.

00:52:26.443 --> 00:52:30.614
According to this packet, they
contain beef, pork, water,

00:52:30.614 --> 00:52:33.850
bread crumbs, egg, dried onion,

00:52:33.850 --> 00:52:36.219
salt, egg powder,

00:52:36.219 --> 00:52:38.555
black pepper extract, and allspice.

00:52:38.555 --> 00:52:40.190
Overall, it seems like it's
pretty close to the list

00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:42.159
that they gave you on the
website, with a few more things

00:52:42.159 --> 00:52:44.427
like powdered ingredients
versus fresh ingredients,

00:52:44.427 --> 00:52:45.962
and a couple of spice differences too.

00:52:45.962 --> 00:52:49.933
I wasn't able to find it online

00:52:49.933 --> 00:52:51.968
or in the cafeteria,

00:52:51.968 --> 00:52:54.204
the ingredients list, but I got here

00:52:55.672 --> 00:52:59.409
the frozen precooked meatballs,

00:52:59.409 --> 00:53:00.710
and those ingredients ...

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:02.245
hm, actually not bad.

00:53:02.245 --> 00:53:04.347
Assuming these are the ones
that they have in the cafeteria

00:53:04.347 --> 00:53:08.118
in the US, the ingredients
for this is beef, pork,

00:53:08.118 --> 00:53:10.854
water, bread crumbs made
of unbleached wheat flour,

00:53:10.854 --> 00:53:12.055
salt, and yeast,

00:53:12.055 --> 00:53:15.759
whole eggs, salt,
dehydrated onion, egg white,

00:53:15.759 --> 00:53:18.628
that's not plural. Egg white, singular.

00:53:18.628 --> 00:53:19.930
Spices.

00:53:21.264 --> 00:53:24.134
That's not bad, I think, for
a prepackaged product, right?

00:53:24.134 --> 00:53:26.570
It's like, well, that's, you
know, what's in them, right?

00:53:26.570 --> 00:53:28.338
While we're on the subject
of Ikea ingredients,

00:53:28.338 --> 00:53:29.806
one thing that UK viewers might remember

00:53:29.806 --> 00:53:31.908
was our infamous horsemeat scandal.

00:53:34.377 --> 00:53:36.313
Back in 2013, a number of foods

00:53:36.313 --> 00:53:38.682
in the UK claiming to be beef or pork

00:53:38.682 --> 00:53:41.418
were actually found to
contain traces of horsemeat.

00:53:41.418 --> 00:53:43.420
While this wasn't
necessarily a health issue,

00:53:43.420 --> 00:53:45.388
it did raise some major
questions concerning

00:53:45.388 --> 00:53:47.857
the traceability of foods
throughout supply chains,

00:53:47.857 --> 00:53:50.627
and unfortunately Ikea was
also caught up in this scandal.

00:53:50.627 --> 00:53:52.629
Czech authorities found
traces of horsemeat

00:53:52.629 --> 00:53:54.998
in some Ikea meatballs that
had been produced in Sweden

00:53:54.998 --> 00:53:56.433
and shipped all around Europe.

00:53:56.433 --> 00:53:58.168
Thankfully, the horsemeat
meatballs were found

00:53:58.168 --> 00:53:59.536
before they reached consumers.

00:53:59.536 --> 00:54:03.006
In total, 760 kilograms of
meatballs were destroyed.

00:54:03.006 --> 00:54:04.507
Given the fact that
there's no health concerns

00:54:04.507 --> 00:54:06.076
related to the consumption of horsemeat,

00:54:06.076 --> 00:54:07.244
I think they should have
just leaned into it.

00:54:07.244 --> 00:54:08.111
Just passed them off as, like,

00:54:08.111 --> 00:54:09.980
limited-edition horse meatballs,

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:11.781
and just go with it, lads, it's fine.

00:54:14.351 --> 00:54:16.987
Ikea's food is almost suspiciously cheap,

00:54:16.987 --> 00:54:18.755
and it's actually one of
the ways it tricks you into

00:54:18.755 --> 00:54:21.324
spending more time and
money in its stores.

00:54:21.324 --> 00:54:24.060
Ikea is basically willing
to take its losses

00:54:24.060 --> 00:54:25.528
with its cheap food prices

00:54:25.528 --> 00:54:28.365
because it's counting on you
to spend a bunch of money

00:54:28.365 --> 00:54:30.500
on furniture and other household items.

00:54:30.500 --> 00:54:33.770
A former Ikea executive even
referred to the meatballs

00:54:33.770 --> 00:54:35.772
as the "best sofa seller."

00:54:35.772 --> 00:54:38.742
In the UK, a plate of
eight meatballs, peas,

00:54:38.742 --> 00:54:41.478
mash or chips, gravy, and lingonberry jam

00:54:41.478 --> 00:54:43.480
will set you back just £4.95.

00:54:43.480 --> 00:54:46.449
At the time of recording,
that's around $6.49.

00:54:46.449 --> 00:54:47.751
I do have to say, that's really cheap.

00:54:47.751 --> 00:54:49.085
I don't think the quality's that bad.

00:54:49.085 --> 00:54:51.554
It's really tasty food and,
yeah, it's a real bargain.

00:54:51.554 --> 00:54:53.823
I will make an effort
to go via the restaurant

00:54:53.823 --> 00:54:55.025
if I'm just going through Ikea.

00:54:55.025 --> 00:54:57.093
Even if I'm just popping in
for, like, one or two bits,

00:54:57.093 --> 00:54:58.862
I'm going to sit down,
eat a plate of meatballs.

00:54:58.862 --> 00:55:00.730
It's good value, it's tasty, I love it.

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:04.034
An eight-meatball meal in the US is $9.99,

00:55:04.034 --> 00:55:06.169
which in the UK is £7.80.

00:55:06.169 --> 00:55:09.639
That's a 54% increase in the States.

00:55:12.242 --> 00:55:14.611
Overall I would say Ikea is
pretty popular in the UK.

00:55:14.611 --> 00:55:17.180
It's most people's go-to
for basic furniture

00:55:17.347 --> 00:55:18.548
and household needs, I would say,

00:55:18.548 --> 00:55:20.583
especially when people are buying a house

00:55:20.583 --> 00:55:22.419
or moving house or something like that.

00:55:22.419 --> 00:55:25.255
Personally, I love Ikea.
I love going there.

00:55:25.255 --> 00:55:28.458
I do enjoy a trip. It's
fun. I like the food.

00:55:28.458 --> 00:55:30.160
I like walking around
and pretending, like,

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:31.528
"Ooh, that could be my apartment

00:55:31.528 --> 00:55:32.929
or my bedroom or my kitchen."

00:55:32.929 --> 00:55:34.531
I wouldn't say Ikea is
somewhere I go often,

00:55:34.531 --> 00:55:36.666
particularly because I live
in quite central London,

00:55:36.666 --> 00:55:38.068
and there aren't many in Central London.

00:55:38.068 --> 00:55:39.669
I think they're trying to
open one on Oxford Street

00:55:39.669 --> 00:55:42.138
right now. At the time of
recording this, it wasn't open,

00:55:42.138 --> 00:55:43.606
but they're kind of trying to get there.

00:55:43.606 --> 00:55:45.608
But so, like I say, it's more
of a place where you'll go

00:55:45.608 --> 00:55:47.811
specifically when you
need to do an Ikea shop.

00:55:47.811 --> 00:55:50.146
Ikea is so popular here.

00:55:50.146 --> 00:55:52.549
I think it's popular in the whole country.

00:55:52.549 --> 00:55:55.118
The one in Burbank, we went
there at 10:30 in the morning.

00:55:55.118 --> 00:55:59.422
It opens at 10, and at 10:30,
parking lot was pretty full.

00:55:59.422 --> 00:56:01.024
The cafeteria line was full.

00:56:01.024 --> 00:56:03.126
So I'd say Ikea's pretty popular.

00:56:03.126 --> 00:56:05.362
I personally wouldn't go
to Ikea just for the food.

00:56:05.362 --> 00:56:07.497
Again, it might be because
I don't live close enough

00:56:07.497 --> 00:56:09.032
to one to make that worthwhile.

00:56:09.032 --> 00:56:10.767
I do love the food. The
meatballs are great.

00:56:10.767 --> 00:56:12.435
Like I say, the desserts are fantastic.

00:56:12.435 --> 00:56:14.504
But I don't like it to
the extent that I would

00:56:14.504 --> 00:56:17.240
make the effort to travel
to one just to eat there.

00:56:17.240 --> 00:56:19.876
I'm not going to say I
go to Ikea for the food,

00:56:19.876 --> 00:56:24.114
but the food definitely is a
good motivator to go to Ikea.

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:26.116
I will say overall I
do enjoy the experience

00:56:26.116 --> 00:56:27.484
of going to an Ikea.

00:56:27.484 --> 00:56:29.219
I am aware that it's kind of designed

00:56:29.219 --> 00:56:30.286
to make us spend money.

00:56:30.320 --> 00:56:32.255
Obviously the restaurant
plays a part of this as well,

00:56:32.255 --> 00:56:33.923
because it's kind of
encouraging you to make a day

00:56:33.923 --> 00:56:36.126
of your trip to Ikea, really
spend some time there,

00:56:36.126 --> 00:56:38.228
sit down, take a break in
the middle of the day, eat,

00:56:38.228 --> 00:56:40.263
re-energize yourself for more shopping.

00:56:40.263 --> 00:56:41.798
And as you can guess by
my friends on the table

00:56:41.798 --> 00:56:44.000
with me here, I do think that
their marketing is a really

00:56:44.000 --> 00:56:46.169
clever ploy to make us spend
more money there as well.

00:56:46.169 --> 00:56:48.805
I'm joined here by my friend Djungelskog.

00:56:48.838 --> 00:56:51.374
I'm really sorry, I'm going to
butcher these pronunciations.

00:56:51.374 --> 00:56:55.111
And my friend Blåhaj, who
is the adorable little shark

00:56:55.111 --> 00:56:57.847
who is also, I think, a
trans icon. Love that.

00:56:57.847 --> 00:56:58.848
You'll often see things like this

00:56:58.848 --> 00:57:01.017
really getting a
reputation on the internet,

00:57:01.017 --> 00:57:03.219
driving more commerce to Ikea stores.

00:57:03.219 --> 00:57:04.888
I don't know if they're
doing that deliberately

00:57:04.888 --> 00:57:07.857
or if it's just kind of a
side effect of making some

00:57:07.857 --> 00:57:09.325
very, very cute plush toys.

00:57:09.325 --> 00:57:11.261
But everyone, I think, will generally have

00:57:11.261 --> 00:57:12.762
at least one thing from
Ikea in their home.

00:57:12.762 --> 00:57:15.098
So whatever they're doing,
it's kind of working.

00:57:15.098 --> 00:57:16.299
I'm not mad about it either.

00:57:16.299 --> 00:57:18.701
I support Ikea. Keep
up the good work, guys.

00:57:57.607 --> 00:57:59.142
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

00:57:59.142 --> 00:58:01.478
we wanted to find out all the
differences between Pop-Tarts

00:58:01.478 --> 00:58:03.046
in the US and the UK.

00:58:03.046 --> 00:58:04.614
This is "Food Wars."

00:58:08.518 --> 00:58:11.621
In the UK, Pop-Tarts come in
one main size, an eight-pack.

00:58:11.621 --> 00:58:13.756
You might also be able
to find a 36-pack online,

00:58:13.756 --> 00:58:15.458
but these seem more
geared towards retailers

00:58:15.458 --> 00:58:16.392
rather than consumers,

00:58:16.392 --> 00:58:18.728
so for the purposes of "Food
Wars," we're not counting them.

00:58:18.728 --> 00:58:21.898
In the US, our Pop-Tarts
come in seven size varieties.

00:58:21.898 --> 00:58:24.334
Down here with the two-pack; eight-pack;

00:58:24.334 --> 00:58:27.203
12-pack; 16-pack, the value pack;

00:58:27.203 --> 00:58:28.805
32-pack, the family pack,

00:58:28.805 --> 00:58:30.073
I don't know if you can see
it says "family" on there;

00:58:30.073 --> 00:58:33.142
and the biggest amount, the 48-pack.

00:58:33.142 --> 00:58:35.378
They probably also come in one box.

00:58:35.378 --> 00:58:39.449
That means our biggest Pop-Tart
variety is 33.3% bigger

00:58:39.449 --> 00:58:41.217
than what you can get in the UK.

00:58:41.217 --> 00:58:43.686
But are the Pop-Tarts the
same size in both countries?

00:58:43.686 --> 00:58:44.954
Let's measure them to find out.

00:58:44.954 --> 00:58:47.290
So, our UK Pop-Tart was 8 centimeters wide

00:58:47.290 --> 00:58:48.625
and 11 centimeters long,

00:58:48.625 --> 00:58:51.861
for a surface area of
88 centimeters squared.

00:58:51.861 --> 00:58:54.497
We will do the same.

00:58:54.497 --> 00:58:57.467
3 by 4.

00:58:57.467 --> 00:58:58.968
And of course, we're going to weigh it.

00:58:58.968 --> 00:59:03.039
Joe: And a Pop-Tart in
the US weighs 48 grams.

00:59:03.039 --> 00:59:05.542
Harry: Our UK Pop-Tart
was bang on 50 grams,

00:59:05.542 --> 00:59:07.110
which I think is actually slightly more

00:59:07.110 --> 00:59:08.111
than it's supposed to weigh.

00:59:08.111 --> 00:59:10.947
Kellogg's woke up feeling
a little generous today.

00:59:10.947 --> 00:59:12.882
"But what about the
pastry-to-filling ratio?"

00:59:12.882 --> 00:59:13.716
I hear you cry.

00:59:13.716 --> 00:59:15.251
Well, don't worry, we're
going to look at that too.

00:59:15.251 --> 00:59:18.521
You can sort of chisel
off layers of pastry.

00:59:18.521 --> 00:59:20.256
I mean, there's really
not much jam in there.

00:59:20.256 --> 00:59:22.258
That's a very thin layer
of jam right there.

00:59:22.258 --> 00:59:24.427
I think we might have to abandon this.

00:59:24.427 --> 00:59:25.728
It's gone real bad.

00:59:25.728 --> 00:59:27.463
So, unfortunately, we had
to abandon the scrape test

00:59:27.463 --> 00:59:28.631
because that was not working.

00:59:28.631 --> 00:59:30.233
Joe, if you pulled that
off, you're a genius.

00:59:30.233 --> 00:59:31.167
I don't know how you've done it.

00:59:31.167 --> 00:59:33.870
However, on the back of
the box of the Pop-Tarts,

00:59:33.870 --> 00:59:36.439
it says that the fruit
filling should be 34%

00:59:36.439 --> 00:59:37.774
of the Pop-Tart by weight.

00:59:37.774 --> 00:59:40.510
If our maths are correct,
this was a 50-gram Pop-Tart.

00:59:40.510 --> 00:59:44.147
34% of that should be 17 grams of jam.

00:59:44.147 --> 00:59:45.615
Joe: Yeah, all right.

00:59:45.615 --> 00:59:46.916
Hot! Hot! Hot!

00:59:47.917 --> 00:59:49.819
Hot. Ow! Mm!

00:59:49.819 --> 00:59:51.087
Ow. Mm!

00:59:52.388 --> 00:59:54.490
I'm wiping it on here. Right?

00:59:54.490 --> 00:59:57.327
We'll round up to 10 grams.

00:59:57.327 --> 01:00:02.332
So that is this percentage of jam.

01:00:05.234 --> 01:00:07.270
Here's all the Pop-Tarts
you can get in the US

01:00:07.270 --> 01:00:08.705
you can't get in the UK.

01:00:08.705 --> 01:00:10.206
And here are all the Pop-Tarts from the UK

01:00:10.206 --> 01:00:11.808
that you can't find in the US.

01:00:11.808 --> 01:00:13.343
Yes, it's just the one.

01:00:13.343 --> 01:00:15.912
[laughing]

01:00:15.912 --> 01:00:16.913
We got all of them.

01:00:16.913 --> 01:00:18.247
Wanted to get this out
of the way real quick.

01:00:18.247 --> 01:00:21.684
There are currently two
limited-edition Pop-Tarts

01:00:21.684 --> 01:00:24.120
out right now, the red,
white, and blueberry

01:00:24.120 --> 01:00:26.589
and the frosted strawberry throwback.

01:00:26.589 --> 01:00:29.225
I went to Walmart. The guy
said they didn't have them.

01:00:29.225 --> 01:00:30.760
Also, newer ones,

01:00:30.760 --> 01:00:33.463
there is an Apple Jacks
frosted apple cinnamon one,

01:00:33.463 --> 01:00:35.898
which is supposed to be
delivered to my house tomorrow.

01:00:35.898 --> 01:00:37.000
I'm sorry.

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:40.269
But one new one we do have
is frosted banana bread,

01:00:40.269 --> 01:00:42.238
which is ... looks like a Pop-Tart.

01:00:42.238 --> 01:00:43.973
Gave us some extra frosting up top there.

01:00:45.942 --> 01:00:47.110
Yeah, it tastes like banana bread.

01:00:47.110 --> 01:00:48.344
Actually, it's very good.

01:00:48.344 --> 01:00:50.179
You can only find three Pop-Tart varieties

01:00:50.179 --> 01:00:53.349
on sale in the UK, including
strawberry sensation,

01:00:53.349 --> 01:00:56.352
frosted chocotastic, and hot fudge sundae.

01:00:56.352 --> 01:00:57.920
These two you can also find in the US,

01:00:57.920 --> 01:00:59.022
so they're not exclusives,

01:00:59.022 --> 01:01:00.757
but this one is. The frosted chocotastic.

01:01:00.757 --> 01:01:02.592
While it has its own name, it
does seem a little bit similar

01:01:02.592 --> 01:01:04.027
to the US chocolate fudge one,

01:01:04.027 --> 01:01:05.428
so we'll take it out and have a look.

01:01:05.428 --> 01:01:08.531
So, this is a UK frosted
chocotastic Pop-Tart.

01:01:08.531 --> 01:01:09.832
It's got the chocolate icing,

01:01:09.832 --> 01:01:11.868
the pastry itself is chocolate-flavored,

01:01:11.868 --> 01:01:14.637
and it's got these little
flecks of white stuff on it.

01:01:14.637 --> 01:01:16.606
Ambitiously, in my mind, I
was hoping it might be, like,

01:01:16.606 --> 01:01:18.708
flaky sea salt, but it's a Pop-Tart.

01:01:18.708 --> 01:01:20.376
I think it's more likely
to just be some icing.

01:01:22.845 --> 01:01:23.713
Not too bad.

01:01:23.713 --> 01:01:26.382
You also have a chocolaty
filling on that as well,

01:01:26.382 --> 01:01:27.817
so it's kind of like three levels

01:01:27.817 --> 01:01:29.085
of chocolate going on here.

01:01:29.085 --> 01:01:30.787
Not too sweet either, actually.

01:01:30.787 --> 01:01:34.590
All right, starting with
the frosted chocolate fudge.

01:01:34.590 --> 01:01:36.693
All right, let's compare
this to what Harry got.

01:01:38.661 --> 01:01:39.996
Chocolate on chocolate.

01:01:42.031 --> 01:01:43.032
I know I'm just going
to state the obvious,

01:01:43.032 --> 01:01:46.169
but this is, like, really
chocolaty and fudgy.

01:01:46.169 --> 01:01:47.503
Seeing as we only have three Pop-Tarts,

01:01:47.503 --> 01:01:49.038
I figured I'd try the
other ones too just to see

01:01:49.038 --> 01:01:51.774
how they stack up against
the exclusive chocotastic.

01:01:51.774 --> 01:01:53.776
So, this is our version
of hot fudge sundae.

01:01:53.776 --> 01:01:55.712
Pastry-wise, looks kind of similar

01:01:55.712 --> 01:01:58.014
to the chocolate one with
the chocolate pastry,

01:01:58.014 --> 01:01:59.716
but then it has something more resembling

01:01:59.716 --> 01:02:01.417
the strawberry icing.

01:02:01.417 --> 01:02:04.120
I feel like this is probably
a slightly lazy attempt

01:02:04.120 --> 01:02:06.489
to just kind of rehash
things they already have

01:02:06.489 --> 01:02:09.692
into a new product. The
filling looks pretty similar

01:02:09.692 --> 01:02:12.462
to the filling found on
the frosted chocotastic.

01:02:12.462 --> 01:02:14.163
Slightly lighter in color,
so maybe they've gone for

01:02:14.163 --> 01:02:16.733
more of a fudge vibe rather
than just straight chocolate.

01:02:18.267 --> 01:02:20.503
I think with the combination
of the standard icing

01:02:20.503 --> 01:02:22.972
and the slightly different
filling, this has more of a

01:02:22.972 --> 01:02:25.208
well-rounded, balanced sweet flavor to it.

01:02:26.075 --> 01:02:28.544
And, of course, the classic
strawberry Pop-Tart.

01:02:28.544 --> 01:02:29.812
Obviously, visually this stands out

01:02:29.812 --> 01:02:32.048
because they have not gone
for the chocolate pastry.

01:02:32.048 --> 01:02:35.351
It's that very, kind of almost
alarmingly pale pastry color.

01:02:35.351 --> 01:02:36.519
Pretty much the same icing, I think,

01:02:36.519 --> 01:02:38.121
that they've used on the hot fudge sundae.

01:02:39.922 --> 01:02:41.758
It's the classic for a reason.

01:02:41.758 --> 01:02:42.725
It's just really good.

01:02:42.725 --> 01:02:44.560
It's the blueberry. All
right, getting some fruit.

01:02:44.560 --> 01:02:45.928
I wouldn't be able to
guess what fruit this was

01:02:45.928 --> 01:02:47.497
just by looking at the outside. I mean ...

01:02:49.065 --> 01:02:50.566
Here in the US so far,
one thing I've noticed,

01:02:50.566 --> 01:02:52.101
they're really skimping on the filling.

01:02:52.101 --> 01:02:55.304
I know it's, like, dusty, but
brown sugar cinnamon, beat up.

01:02:55.304 --> 01:02:57.340
Ooh, this is, like, really smooth.

01:02:57.340 --> 01:02:58.508
Also very dusty.

01:03:00.643 --> 01:03:01.978
I honestly can't tell if the flavor

01:03:01.978 --> 01:03:04.113
is the frosting or the jam.

01:03:04.113 --> 01:03:07.183
Cherry. I mean, I feel like
this is a flagship one.

01:03:07.183 --> 01:03:08.951
Aim's not so great over
there at the factory.

01:03:08.951 --> 01:03:11.254
Already though, both on
this side and this side,

01:03:11.254 --> 01:03:13.156
you can almost see the filling
coming out of it, right?

01:03:15.024 --> 01:03:16.793
I'm starting to think that a
lot of the sweet fruit flavor

01:03:16.793 --> 01:03:19.262
is coming from the frosting
and not the inside.

01:03:20.329 --> 01:03:21.597
Chocolate chip.

01:03:21.597 --> 01:03:22.832
All right. Already, this.

01:03:22.832 --> 01:03:24.934
Could we get that sad trombone please?

01:03:24.934 --> 01:03:26.669
It's like, so — it's
like a doctor's signature.

01:03:26.669 --> 01:03:28.037
It's just, whoop! Right across it.

01:03:28.037 --> 01:03:29.839
I can't read this. What does this say?

01:03:29.839 --> 01:03:32.542
Oh, come — ugh, Pop-Tart.

01:03:33.576 --> 01:03:35.244
Ooh, this really irritates me.

01:03:35.244 --> 01:03:36.612
This really winds me up.

01:03:36.612 --> 01:03:38.514
Oh, you gotta be kidding me with this.

01:03:39.515 --> 01:03:41.951
Your mom or your school
or your work, whatever,

01:03:41.951 --> 01:03:43.019
doesn't get Oreos,

01:03:43.019 --> 01:03:46.556
they get the knockoff-brand
sandwich cookies,

01:03:46.556 --> 01:03:47.723
and it's like, you'll eat them,

01:03:47.723 --> 01:03:50.059
but you're like, "Ah, man.
Kind of wanted Oreos."

01:03:50.059 --> 01:03:50.893
That's what this tastes like.

01:03:50.893 --> 01:03:52.528
This tastes like a knockoff of an Oreo.

01:03:53.496 --> 01:03:54.530
Frosted confetti.

01:03:54.530 --> 01:03:58.301
Ugh. No, don't make me eat this.

01:03:58.301 --> 01:03:59.836
Please don't make me eat this.

01:04:01.938 --> 01:04:03.639
It's like just eating straight frosting.

01:04:03.639 --> 01:04:07.243
Next up, frosted hot fudge sundae.

01:04:07.243 --> 01:04:09.245
Breakfast is served.

01:04:09.245 --> 01:04:11.747
This looks like an absolute nightmare.

01:04:11.747 --> 01:04:14.383
Imagine eating this and going to school.

01:04:14.383 --> 01:04:17.353
Totally phoned in, like, blah! Drizzle.

01:04:18.788 --> 01:04:20.289
It's very sweet.

01:04:22.091 --> 01:04:22.992
Oh, man.

01:04:22.992 --> 01:04:25.761
A Pop-Tart s'mores. Yuelei's choice.

01:04:27.430 --> 01:04:28.865
OK. It tastes like a s'mores.

01:04:28.865 --> 01:04:31.767
You can see a little pocket
of marshmallow there.

01:04:31.767 --> 01:04:33.536
This is the first dual
filling I think I've had,

01:04:33.536 --> 01:04:35.705
or at least I've noticed.
And you know what?

01:04:35.705 --> 01:04:37.273
I don't really like it.

01:04:37.273 --> 01:04:38.774
I can't believe we only had one exclusive.

01:04:38.774 --> 01:04:39.876
I feel like I'm really missing out

01:04:39.876 --> 01:04:41.611
on some of those tasty American Pop-Tarts.

01:04:41.611 --> 01:04:43.145
Joe, can I get some of those?

01:04:43.145 --> 01:04:44.981
No worries, man. I got you.

01:04:44.981 --> 01:04:45.815
Ah!

01:04:51.320 --> 01:04:52.188
Thank you!

01:04:52.188 --> 01:04:53.990
So, it looks like Joe
has kindly shared with me

01:04:53.990 --> 01:04:56.058
some confetti cupcake Pop-Tarts,

01:04:56.058 --> 01:04:58.694
the American version of the
hot fudge sundae Pop-Tarts,

01:04:58.694 --> 01:05:00.830
and some frosted s'mores Pop-Tarts.

01:05:00.830 --> 01:05:02.965
Frosted confetti.

01:05:02.965 --> 01:05:04.300
I feel like Americans are just obsessed

01:05:04.300 --> 01:05:05.134
with eating sprinkles

01:05:05.134 --> 01:05:07.003
because it seems like every kind of brand,

01:05:07.003 --> 01:05:08.604
whether it's chocolate, pastry,

01:05:08.604 --> 01:05:11.507
will have a kind of confetti
cupcake, birthday cake flavor,

01:05:11.507 --> 01:05:12.875
and Pop-Tarts are no exception.

01:05:12.875 --> 01:05:14.977
American sprinkles look so much better

01:05:14.977 --> 01:05:15.945
than British sprinkles.

01:05:15.945 --> 01:05:18.214
I know it's because they
are using banned chemicals,

01:05:18.214 --> 01:05:19.048
which we don't have over here.

01:05:19.048 --> 01:05:21.050
We have to opt for natural alternatives.

01:05:21.050 --> 01:05:22.151
But when it comes to baking,

01:05:22.151 --> 01:05:23.519
they just hold their color so much better.

01:05:23.519 --> 01:05:24.954
They look a lot more vibrant.

01:05:24.954 --> 01:05:26.656
I kind of get why you guys are using it.

01:05:28.858 --> 01:05:30.493
It doesn't give me any
pleasure to admit it,

01:05:30.493 --> 01:05:32.495
but that is really tasty.

01:05:32.495 --> 01:05:33.763
It's got almost a sort of

01:05:33.763 --> 01:05:35.564
cake-batter taste to it, that filling.

01:05:35.564 --> 01:05:37.833
So it kind of is like you're
eating a confetti cupcake,

01:05:37.833 --> 01:05:40.236
or at least the batter
of a confetti cupcake.

01:05:40.236 --> 01:05:41.570
In my left hand, I have a

01:05:41.570 --> 01:05:43.572
British strawberry sensation Pop-Tart.

01:05:43.572 --> 01:05:44.807
In my right hand, I have the American

01:05:44.807 --> 01:05:46.442
frosted confetti cupcake one.

01:05:46.442 --> 01:05:47.977
Just quickly wanted to point
out the color difference

01:05:47.977 --> 01:05:49.645
because that is wild.

01:05:49.645 --> 01:05:51.547
Next up is the American version

01:05:51.547 --> 01:05:53.149
of the hot fudge sundae Pop-Tarts.

01:05:53.149 --> 01:05:54.216
The American version of these

01:05:54.216 --> 01:05:55.718
looks so much more fun than ours.

01:05:55.718 --> 01:05:57.620
Size-wise, they look pretty much the same,

01:05:57.620 --> 01:05:59.388
but the American one looks thicker.

01:06:00.589 --> 01:06:03.192
The American one has
this white icing filling,

01:06:03.192 --> 01:06:04.493
whereas in the UK I think they've gone for

01:06:04.493 --> 01:06:06.862
more of a kind of fudgy chocolate filling.

01:06:06.862 --> 01:06:08.831
The difference between these
two is kind of night and day.

01:06:08.831 --> 01:06:11.968
The American one, better than
ours in pretty much every way.

01:06:11.968 --> 01:06:12.802
I tasted this at first,

01:06:12.802 --> 01:06:14.603
and I wasn't disappointed by it, per se.

01:06:14.603 --> 01:06:15.871
But now that I've had the comparison,

01:06:15.871 --> 01:06:17.606
direct comparison with the US,

01:06:17.606 --> 01:06:19.275
this tastes like eating cardboard.

01:06:19.275 --> 01:06:21.410
Finally, it's the
frosted s'mores Pop-Tart.

01:06:21.410 --> 01:06:23.913
I gotta say, s'mores aren't
really a thing in the UK.

01:06:23.913 --> 01:06:26.082
In the UK, we don't have graham crackers,

01:06:26.082 --> 01:06:28.084
or, as we would pronounce
it, gray-ham crackers.

01:06:28.084 --> 01:06:29.485
Shout out to my dad, Graham.

01:06:31.220 --> 01:06:33.990
Yeah, there we go. There we go.

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:35.992
It's almost like a digestive biscuit.

01:06:35.992 --> 01:06:36.826
These are pretty good.

01:06:36.826 --> 01:06:38.961
This one looks maybe the scariest.

01:06:38.961 --> 01:06:40.363
It's cookies and cream.

01:06:42.131 --> 01:06:44.767
Whenever it's frosting
inside, I know I'm in trouble.

01:06:47.703 --> 01:06:48.871
Yuck.

01:06:48.871 --> 01:06:50.906
Don't give this to a child ever.

01:06:50.906 --> 01:06:52.942
And most adults should also not eat this.

01:06:52.942 --> 01:06:54.710
Grape. Grape?!

01:06:55.578 --> 01:06:58.814
Fruit ones look how I think a
Pop-Tart is supposed to look.

01:06:58.814 --> 01:07:01.183
They don't look that intimidating.

01:07:02.885 --> 01:07:06.455
Grape is a really
popular flavor in the US,

01:07:06.455 --> 01:07:08.057
but anyone in the US will tell you

01:07:08.057 --> 01:07:11.360
that grape-flavored candy
doesn't really taste like grapes.

01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:15.164
They somehow invented a grapelike flavor,

01:07:15.164 --> 01:07:17.033
probably because grapes have
a weaker flavor, I think.

01:07:17.033 --> 01:07:18.534
I'm not quite sure
exactly how it came to be,

01:07:18.534 --> 01:07:21.137
but this tastes exactly like that

01:07:21.137 --> 01:07:24.340
fake, imitation Bubblicious
bubble gum grape.

01:07:24.340 --> 01:07:25.508
You know what I'm talking about.

01:07:25.508 --> 01:07:26.609
A grape soda.

01:07:26.609 --> 01:07:28.177
It's not bad. So far, all the fruit ones

01:07:28.177 --> 01:07:29.879
are better than any of the other ones, so.

01:07:29.879 --> 01:07:31.414
Yeah, Eggo.

01:07:31.414 --> 01:07:32.982
How unnatural looking is this?

01:07:32.982 --> 01:07:34.316
You know how you can take those classes,

01:07:34.316 --> 01:07:37.053
I think they're called,
like, sip and paint,

01:07:37.053 --> 01:07:40.056
where everyone paints the same
picture, the same still life

01:07:40.056 --> 01:07:42.391
while they're sipping
wine with their friends?

01:07:42.391 --> 01:07:44.493
This was the image they were painting,

01:07:44.493 --> 01:07:46.295
and this is what they painted drunk.

01:07:49.031 --> 01:07:51.767
This does not in any way
taste like an Eggo waffle.

01:07:51.767 --> 01:07:53.235
I don't think it tastes
like waffles at all.

01:07:53.235 --> 01:07:56.038
What ruins it is this frosting on here.

01:07:56.038 --> 01:07:57.540
This is so sweet.

01:07:57.540 --> 01:07:58.607
Raspberry.

01:07:58.607 --> 01:08:00.443
I have a feeling this is
going to be a good one.

01:08:00.443 --> 01:08:01.277
Yeah.

01:08:02.478 --> 01:08:03.813
Top tier right here.

01:08:03.813 --> 01:08:05.614
These were optimized for fruit filling.

01:08:05.614 --> 01:08:07.116
Wild berry.

01:08:07.116 --> 01:08:08.918
So is just the combination wild?

01:08:08.918 --> 01:08:10.986
Or these were found in the wild?

01:08:10.986 --> 01:08:13.355
Oh, no!

01:08:13.355 --> 01:08:17.726
Alarm bells in my
caveman DNA is going off.

01:08:17.726 --> 01:08:19.662
Do not eat. This is poisonous.

01:08:19.662 --> 01:08:22.431
Do not eat. Warning. Warning.

01:08:22.431 --> 01:08:24.166
Ancestors who ate this died.

01:08:24.166 --> 01:08:26.802
Let's go against millions
of years of evolution

01:08:26.802 --> 01:08:31.173
and bite into this woefully
bright-colored piece of,

01:08:31.173 --> 01:08:32.608
I'm assuming, food.

01:08:32.608 --> 01:08:33.809
We'll find out together.

01:08:35.010 --> 01:08:35.911
With all the frosting that's on there,

01:08:35.911 --> 01:08:36.846
it's actually not that sweet,

01:08:36.846 --> 01:08:38.013
compared to the other fruit ones.

01:08:40.449 --> 01:08:42.251
Play this part of the video

01:08:42.251 --> 01:08:46.889
to the ambulance driver
when he asks what happened.

01:08:46.889 --> 01:08:48.023
Boston cream.

01:08:48.023 --> 01:08:49.158
Boston cream doughnut

01:08:49.158 --> 01:08:51.861
is this doughnut right here,
if you haven't seen it.

01:08:51.861 --> 01:08:54.096
You can't even tell where the cream is

01:08:54.096 --> 01:08:55.931
compared to the color of the pastry.

01:08:57.166 --> 01:08:59.869
What I'm about to say
will come as no surprise

01:08:59.869 --> 01:09:00.970
to anyone watching this video,

01:09:00.970 --> 01:09:04.607
but this one tastes completely artificial.

01:09:04.607 --> 01:09:06.375
I know, right? Can you believe it?

01:09:06.375 --> 01:09:08.711
On to the next, smaller pyramid.

01:09:09.745 --> 01:09:10.913
Snickerdoodle.

01:09:12.248 --> 01:09:13.315
Bland.

01:09:13.315 --> 01:09:15.384
All right, let me look
at the unfrosted ones.

01:09:15.384 --> 01:09:16.352
Unfrosted?

01:09:16.352 --> 01:09:18.787
Unfrosted, we got strawberry,
brown sugar cinnamon,

01:09:18.787 --> 01:09:20.189
and blueberry.

01:09:20.189 --> 01:09:22.258
Unfrosted v. frosted.

01:09:22.258 --> 01:09:23.592
I was complaining how sweet they were,

01:09:23.592 --> 01:09:25.094
so maybe I'm more of an unfrosted guy

01:09:25.094 --> 01:09:27.763
and I don't realize it,
but this looks weird.

01:09:27.763 --> 01:09:29.732
This looks like an army ration.

01:09:34.670 --> 01:09:35.971
This one is sweeter,

01:09:35.971 --> 01:09:37.840
but not that much sweeter.

01:09:37.840 --> 01:09:40.543
Don't you think with the
frosting it'd be night and day?

01:09:40.543 --> 01:09:41.544
They're pretty similar.

01:09:41.544 --> 01:09:45.114
Simply frosted harvest strawberry.

01:09:45.114 --> 01:09:47.016
There you go. No artificial colors.

01:09:47.016 --> 01:09:48.918
Color from natural sources.

01:09:49.952 --> 01:09:54.123
Once again, regular strawberry Pop-Tarts.

01:09:54.123 --> 01:09:55.858
They look pretty similar, right?

01:09:55.858 --> 01:09:57.726
This one maybe has more frosting on it.

01:10:01.397 --> 01:10:05.067
Wow. They taste exactly the
same as the artificial ones.

01:10:05.067 --> 01:10:06.502
One other thing that
we wanted to talk about

01:10:06.502 --> 01:10:09.104
was the
toasted-versus-untoasted-Pop-Tarts debate.

01:10:09.104 --> 01:10:10.773
Now, people have been debating for years

01:10:10.773 --> 01:10:12.775
whether it's better to
eat your Pop-Tarts toasted

01:10:12.775 --> 01:10:14.043
or straight out of the packet.

01:10:14.043 --> 01:10:16.212
I will say, in the UK,
this is less of a debate

01:10:16.212 --> 01:10:17.947
because I feel like most
people are more likely

01:10:17.947 --> 01:10:19.982
to toast their Pop-Tarts,
even though they are

01:10:19.982 --> 01:10:21.350
safe to eat straight out of the packet.

01:10:21.350 --> 01:10:22.284
However, we did notice that

01:10:22.284 --> 01:10:23.786
on the American ones that Joe sent over,

01:10:23.786 --> 01:10:25.154
there are some varying instructions,

01:10:25.154 --> 01:10:26.222
which kind of confused us.

01:10:26.222 --> 01:10:28.390
Apparently in the US,
you can microwave them,

01:10:28.390 --> 01:10:30.659
which you're supposed to do
in bursts of three seconds

01:10:30.659 --> 01:10:31.894
until they're hot enough to eat,

01:10:31.894 --> 01:10:33.696
despite the fact that the British ones

01:10:33.696 --> 01:10:36.131
explicitly tell you to not microwave them.

01:10:36.131 --> 01:10:38.500
I'm assuming at some point
someone tried to microwave them

01:10:38.500 --> 01:10:40.669
in the foil and was met
with a nasty surprise.

01:10:40.669 --> 01:10:41.804
And also the American ones say

01:10:41.804 --> 01:10:44.373
that you can freeze them
and eat them frozen.

01:10:44.373 --> 01:10:45.608
Don't know why you would do that.

01:10:45.608 --> 01:10:47.509
Frozen or semi-frozen pastry

01:10:47.509 --> 01:10:48.711
does not sound like it would be nice.

01:10:48.711 --> 01:10:50.579
But we're not here for
microwaved or frozen Pop-Tarts.

01:10:50.579 --> 01:10:51.647
We're just going to heat a couple up

01:10:51.647 --> 01:10:53.182
in the toaster oven and then compare them

01:10:53.182 --> 01:10:55.851
to some untoasted Pop-Tarts
to see which ones I prefer.

01:10:55.851 --> 01:10:58.687
All right. Toasty boy going in.

01:10:58.687 --> 01:11:00.956
Let's see if we can rectify that.

01:11:04.126 --> 01:11:07.196
Also, did you notice?

01:11:07.196 --> 01:11:10.266
What is with Harry's giant toaster oven?

01:11:10.266 --> 01:11:11.500
Look at the size of that thing!

01:11:11.500 --> 01:11:13.002
Just get a regular toaster.

01:11:14.069 --> 01:11:16.372
Ta-da!

01:11:16.372 --> 01:11:17.940
Smells like toasted pastry.

01:11:19.208 --> 01:11:20.042
Ah!

01:11:20.976 --> 01:11:22.211
Hey!

01:11:22.211 --> 01:11:23.579
Ooh, it is falling apart.

01:11:23.579 --> 01:11:26.048
They're just breaking apart already.

01:11:26.048 --> 01:11:27.082
The untoasted.

01:11:29.918 --> 01:11:31.220
Yeah, toasted's way better.

01:11:31.220 --> 01:11:32.921
The filling's more flavorful.

01:11:32.921 --> 01:11:34.156
The crispiness of the pastry.

01:11:34.156 --> 01:11:36.492
I just am too lazy to toast them.

01:11:36.492 --> 01:11:39.028
So, this one on my right
is the untoasted version,

01:11:39.028 --> 01:11:40.663
and on my left is the toasted version.

01:11:40.663 --> 01:11:43.198
I gotta say, visually,
not a lot has changed.

01:11:43.198 --> 01:11:45.000
I was hoping for a
little bit more browning

01:11:45.000 --> 01:11:46.135
while it was in the oven.

01:11:46.135 --> 01:11:48.704
Still not much of a snap from
the pastry, even when toasted.

01:11:48.704 --> 01:11:51.774
But you can see that the
filling has slightly liquefied.

01:11:51.774 --> 01:11:53.175
Really impressive that
they've actually managed

01:11:53.175 --> 01:11:55.678
to create a pastry that doesn't just burst

01:11:55.678 --> 01:11:57.980
and leak all of the filling
when it does get toasted.

01:11:57.980 --> 01:12:00.482
I say impressive; probably
just uses a lot of chemicals.

01:12:00.482 --> 01:12:02.518
Whereas when you compare
it to the untoasted one,

01:12:02.518 --> 01:12:04.119
even when you kind of squish it down,

01:12:04.119 --> 01:12:05.854
that filling is not going anywhere.

01:12:08.791 --> 01:12:11.627
I do prefer the toasted
version, as I sort of expected.

01:12:11.627 --> 01:12:12.561
I think the main difference for me

01:12:12.561 --> 01:12:13.829
is actually in the filling.

01:12:13.829 --> 01:12:15.197
Obviously, the flavor doesn't change a lot

01:12:15.197 --> 01:12:17.266
when you toast it. It's
more the texture, I think.

01:12:17.266 --> 01:12:19.101
Just the fact that it
does liquefy slightly

01:12:19.101 --> 01:12:20.536
gives it a much more pleasant texture

01:12:20.536 --> 01:12:22.104
when you actually bite into this thing.

01:12:22.104 --> 01:12:23.472
Also in the US,

01:12:23.472 --> 01:12:25.574
you can get something
called Pop-Tarts bites.

01:12:25.574 --> 01:12:26.842
These guys right here.

01:12:26.842 --> 01:12:28.844
I have strawberry, of course,

01:12:28.844 --> 01:12:30.546
and confetti cake.

01:12:30.546 --> 01:12:32.981
But you can also get brown sugar cinnamon,

01:12:32.981 --> 01:12:35.217
frosted blueberry,
frosted chocolate fudge,

01:12:35.217 --> 01:12:36.585
and strawberry banana.

01:12:36.585 --> 01:12:37.853
I have no idea what these are.

01:12:41.457 --> 01:12:42.558
No, these are not good.

01:12:42.558 --> 01:12:44.827
Pop-Tarts also does
collabs with other brands.

01:12:44.827 --> 01:12:47.563
For instance, there's a
Pop-Tarts Jeni's ice cream,

01:12:47.563 --> 01:12:49.765
Coffee Mate has its own Pop-Tarts flavor,

01:12:49.765 --> 01:12:51.633
and, of course, Pop-Tarts cereal.

01:12:51.633 --> 01:12:54.670
It was originally called
Pop-Tarts Crunch, discontinued,

01:12:54.670 --> 01:12:57.139
but they renamed it after
they brought it back.

01:13:00.309 --> 01:13:02.411
In the UK, an eight-pack
of strawberry Pop-Tarts

01:13:02.411 --> 01:13:04.313
costs you £2.99,

01:13:04.313 --> 01:13:06.949
which is around $3.86.

01:13:06.949 --> 01:13:11.653
That same box in the
US is $4.49, or £3.47,

01:13:11.653 --> 01:13:14.623
a 16.3% increase in the US.

01:13:14.623 --> 01:13:16.425
Oof! With the gouging!

01:13:16.425 --> 01:13:17.893
Come on, guys.

01:13:17.893 --> 01:13:18.761
It's not surprising to me

01:13:18.761 --> 01:13:20.796
that Pop-Tarts are
really popular in the US.

01:13:20.796 --> 01:13:23.165
We love a quick and convenient breakfast.

01:13:23.165 --> 01:13:26.368
They have positioned
themselves in their advertising

01:13:26.368 --> 01:13:29.204
as a more convenient
breakfast than cereal,

01:13:29.204 --> 01:13:30.973
because you know how
inconvenient cereal is.

01:13:30.973 --> 01:13:34.243
You gotta get the box and
the bowl and the spoon

01:13:34.243 --> 01:13:36.311
and the milk and sit down and eat it.

01:13:36.311 --> 01:13:38.580
Not Pop-Tarts. Cereal, too much work.

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:40.182
Pop-Tarts, way easier.

01:13:40.182 --> 01:13:41.950
For me, the price increase
per portion makes sense

01:13:41.950 --> 01:13:43.852
because I've always seen
Pop-Tarts as more of a treat

01:13:43.852 --> 01:13:45.220
than an everyday breakfast food.

01:13:45.220 --> 01:13:46.088
It was the sort of thing that

01:13:46.088 --> 01:13:47.556
my mom might have let me had as, like,

01:13:47.556 --> 01:13:49.691
a weekend-treat breakfast
if I had accompanied

01:13:49.691 --> 01:13:52.060
on her weekly shop and
been very well behaved.

01:13:52.060 --> 01:13:53.796
I will say that the marketing in the UK

01:13:53.796 --> 01:13:56.265
explicitly doesn't brand
these as a breakfast food,

01:13:56.265 --> 01:13:57.733
compared to something like cereal.

01:13:57.733 --> 01:13:58.567
There is no mention

01:13:58.567 --> 01:14:00.335
of the word "breakfast" anywhere on here.

01:14:00.335 --> 01:14:02.471
They're just referred to
as "toaster pastries."

01:14:02.471 --> 01:14:04.973
So I guess they could be
construed as more of a snack.

01:14:04.973 --> 01:14:07.376
Is the lack of breakfast
talk the same in the US, Joe,

01:14:07.376 --> 01:14:08.944
or are you guys a bit less shameless

01:14:08.944 --> 01:14:11.413
about marketing desserts as
breakfast foods for children?

01:14:11.413 --> 01:14:12.781
So, I originally thought
this was supposed to be

01:14:12.781 --> 01:14:14.383
breakfast food, but
Harry makes a good point.

01:14:14.383 --> 01:14:16.718
It doesn't say breakfast anywhere on here.

01:14:16.718 --> 01:14:18.253
Even the box says you can get them

01:14:18.253 --> 01:14:21.256
straight from the foil,
toasted, stacked, or frozen.

01:14:21.256 --> 01:14:23.926
But they really are not saying
anything about breakfast.

01:14:23.926 --> 01:14:25.661
So I went back and looked online.

01:14:25.661 --> 01:14:27.596
In the '90s — I looked on YouTube.

01:14:27.596 --> 01:14:29.164
I found a bunch of commercials

01:14:29.164 --> 01:14:31.333
where they said it was
specifically for breakfast,

01:14:31.333 --> 01:14:34.036
they even quote "part of
your complete breakfast."

01:14:34.036 --> 01:14:35.971
So they did sometimes market them

01:14:35.971 --> 01:14:38.474
as breakfast foods and then sometimes not.

01:14:38.474 --> 01:14:39.775
And just for me, it sunk in that, yeah,

01:14:39.775 --> 01:14:41.243
you have these for
breakfast in the morning

01:14:41.243 --> 01:14:43.512
when you're in a hurry
to get to school or work.

01:14:46.114 --> 01:14:47.382
Here is the nutritional information

01:14:47.382 --> 01:14:49.618
for one strawberry Pop-Tart in the UK.

01:14:50.786 --> 01:14:52.254
Let's be honest. They
come in packs of two.

01:14:52.254 --> 01:14:53.489
So we're going to double those numbers

01:14:53.489 --> 01:14:55.757
for a more accurate serving-size reading.

01:14:55.757 --> 01:14:57.426
Thank you, Harry. Because in the US,

01:14:57.426 --> 01:14:59.695
we go straight for the
two-tart serving size,

01:14:59.695 --> 01:15:02.030
and here, our strawberry Pop-Tarts are ...

01:15:03.031 --> 01:15:04.032
It looks like the UK numbers

01:15:04.032 --> 01:15:05.934
are actually slightly
higher than those in the US,

01:15:05.934 --> 01:15:08.036
so I guess that means we're winning?

01:15:08.036 --> 01:15:10.506
Now, if calories are
your thing, in the US,

01:15:10.506 --> 01:15:13.242
our cinnamon brown sugar
Pop-Tarts have the most.

01:15:13.242 --> 01:15:14.776
400 per serving.

01:15:14.776 --> 01:15:16.211
But wait!

01:15:17.513 --> 01:15:19.515
The unfrosted

01:15:19.515 --> 01:15:21.683
have even more calories.

01:15:21.683 --> 01:15:24.253
410 per serving.

01:15:24.253 --> 01:15:25.954
I don't know how that works either.

01:15:25.954 --> 01:15:28.123
In 2021, somebody sued Kellogg's,

01:15:28.123 --> 01:15:30.492
claiming that Pop-Tarts
contained way less strawberry

01:15:30.492 --> 01:15:32.427
than what was presented on the packaging.

01:15:32.427 --> 01:15:34.930
This case was ultimately
dismissed, as the judge said,

01:15:34.930 --> 01:15:37.499
quote, "No reasonable
consumer could believe

01:15:37.499 --> 01:15:40.068
from Kellogg's packaging
that the breakfast staple" —

01:15:40.068 --> 01:15:42.271
Hey, breakfast! Perfect. OK.

01:15:42.271 --> 01:15:44.740
"Contained only strawberries,
or more strawberries

01:15:44.740 --> 01:15:47.242
than other ingredients
such as pears and apples."

01:15:47.242 --> 01:15:49.177
The US packaging actually states that

01:15:49.177 --> 01:15:52.514
the strawberry Pop-Tarts
contain 2% or less

01:15:52.514 --> 01:15:53.849
of dried strawberries.

01:15:56.852 --> 01:15:57.819
Here are all the ingredients

01:15:57.819 --> 01:16:00.889
of a UK strawberry
sensation frosted Pop-Tart.

01:16:00.889 --> 01:16:03.425
One thing to note is that the
gelatin used in a UK Pop-Tart

01:16:03.425 --> 01:16:05.661
is explicitly labeled as "beef gelatin."

01:16:05.661 --> 01:16:07.462
So unfortunately, this
means that British Pop-Tarts

01:16:07.462 --> 01:16:09.231
are not suitable for vegetarians.

01:16:09.231 --> 01:16:10.933
And here are all the ingredients

01:16:10.933 --> 01:16:13.435
in a US strawberry Pop-Tart.

01:16:13.435 --> 01:16:14.937
Doo, doo, doo, doo.

01:16:14.937 --> 01:16:16.538
Of the many differences
between these two lists,

01:16:16.538 --> 01:16:18.640
there are a few ingredients
we wanted to highlight here.

01:16:18.640 --> 01:16:21.443
The first is TBHQ, or
tert-butylhydroquinone.

01:16:21.443 --> 01:16:22.277
[beeping]

01:16:22.277 --> 01:16:24.646
Leon: Tuberculosis headquarters, isn't it?

01:16:24.646 --> 01:16:26.815
To be honest, quite.

01:16:26.815 --> 01:16:27.649
[beeping]

01:16:27.649 --> 01:16:30.118
This is a preservative which
is used to extend shelf life

01:16:30.118 --> 01:16:32.421
or for added freshness,
as the US label states.

01:16:32.421 --> 01:16:35.424
The FDA has put limits on
how much it can be used.

01:16:35.424 --> 01:16:39.461
"TBHQ can't account for
more than 0.02 percent

01:16:39.461 --> 01:16:40.729
of the oils in a food

01:16:40.729 --> 01:16:43.365
because the FDA doesn't have evidence

01:16:43.365 --> 01:16:45.000
that greater amounts are safe.

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:46.568
While that doesn't mean

01:16:46.568 --> 01:16:49.371
that more than 0.02 percent is dangerous,

01:16:49.371 --> 01:16:51.907
it does indicate that higher safety levels

01:16:51.907 --> 01:16:53.141
haven't been determined."

01:16:53.141 --> 01:16:55.477
Research has linked
TBHQ to health problems.

01:16:55.477 --> 01:16:58.080
The National Library of Medicine
found vision disturbances

01:16:58.080 --> 01:16:59.514
when it was consumed by humans,

01:16:59.514 --> 01:17:01.283
as well as "liver enlargement,

01:17:01.283 --> 01:17:03.452
neurotoxic effects, convulsions,

01:17:03.452 --> 01:17:05.621
and paralysis in laboratory animals."

01:17:05.621 --> 01:17:08.523
Pop-Tarts in the US also
contain synthetic food coloring

01:17:08.523 --> 01:17:10.392
which are restricted in the UK,

01:17:10.392 --> 01:17:15.364
specifically yellow 5, yellow 6, red 40,

01:17:15.364 --> 01:17:17.232
because of their link to hyperactivity

01:17:17.232 --> 01:17:18.767
and inattention in children.

01:17:18.767 --> 01:17:20.769
Golly gee, I can't imagine why.

01:17:20.769 --> 01:17:23.071
My brain is just going all over the place.

01:17:23.071 --> 01:17:25.007
Another thing to note is that
the Pop-Tarts Joe sent me

01:17:25.007 --> 01:17:26.675
are technically contraband items.

01:17:26.675 --> 01:17:28.176
If you're working with the
police or the government

01:17:28.176 --> 01:17:30.012
and you watch this, please
do not extradite Joe.

01:17:30.012 --> 01:17:31.046
We need him for filming.

01:17:31.046 --> 01:17:33.749
That's because the flour used
in US Pop-Tarts is bleached.

01:17:33.749 --> 01:17:36.752
Bleaching flour has been banned
in the UK since the 1990s

01:17:36.752 --> 01:17:38.720
over concerns about
potential health effects.

01:17:38.720 --> 01:17:40.188
It also just seems unnecessary to me,

01:17:40.188 --> 01:17:41.723
especially for something
that's going to be toasted

01:17:41.723 --> 01:17:42.991
and darken in color anyway.

01:17:42.991 --> 01:17:44.860
You guys just love putting
chemicals in your food

01:17:44.860 --> 01:17:47.129
and seem to seize any
opportunity you can to do it.

01:17:47.129 --> 01:17:49.931
Yeah, don't arrest me, but
he did ask me to send it.

01:17:49.931 --> 01:17:53.201
So I will flip on the UK team in a second.

01:17:53.201 --> 01:17:55.404
FBI, if you're watching,
I will wear a wire.

01:17:55.404 --> 01:17:57.072
It was all Harry's idea.

01:17:57.072 --> 01:17:59.174
I will help you take him down.

01:17:59.408 --> 01:18:00.776
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

01:18:00.776 --> 01:18:02.310
we wanted to find out all the differences

01:18:02.310 --> 01:18:05.147
between Hard Rock Cafe
in the US and the UK.

01:18:05.147 --> 01:18:06.314
This is "Food Wars."

01:18:08.183 --> 01:18:10.986
Let's break down "the
burger that started it all,"

01:18:10.986 --> 01:18:12.621
the original legendary burger.

01:18:12.621 --> 01:18:14.589
Let's weigh the US burger.

01:18:14.589 --> 01:18:16.124
318 grams.

01:18:16.124 --> 01:18:18.326
Here's the original
legendary burger in the UK.

01:18:18.326 --> 01:18:19.161
Let's weigh it.

01:18:19.161 --> 01:18:20.896
Our burger weighed 280 grams.

01:18:20.896 --> 01:18:22.030
That's a pretty big difference,

01:18:22.030 --> 01:18:25.567
from 280 to 318 grams.

01:18:25.567 --> 01:18:27.335
This is a heavy, heavy burger.

01:18:27.335 --> 01:18:28.370
And, of course, all their burgers

01:18:28.370 --> 01:18:29.905
come with a side of seasoned fries.

01:18:29.905 --> 01:18:31.807
We're going to weigh ours to
see how much we're getting.

01:18:31.807 --> 01:18:34.643
So, our side of fries came to 178 grams.

01:18:34.643 --> 01:18:37.546
They feel so soggy right now in my hands.

01:18:37.546 --> 01:18:41.116
So, that is 126 grams of french fries.

01:18:41.116 --> 01:18:43.218
Another iconic menu
item is baby back ribs.

01:18:43.218 --> 01:18:44.386
We're going to weigh our UK portion

01:18:44.386 --> 01:18:45.654
to find out how much we're getting.

01:18:48.090 --> 01:18:50.926
Is that too heavy?
Charlie: That's too heavy.

01:18:50.926 --> 01:18:54.296
335 plus ...

01:18:54.296 --> 01:18:55.564
oh, God.

01:18:55.564 --> 01:18:56.765
300.

01:18:56.765 --> 01:18:59.034
635 grams.

01:18:59.034 --> 01:19:00.402
This is heavy.

01:19:00.402 --> 01:19:05.407
This is 802 grams of ribs.

01:19:05.607 --> 01:19:09.277
That sounds like too much
ribs for one sit-down dinner.

01:19:09.277 --> 01:19:10.679
That's a lot of ribs.

01:19:10.679 --> 01:19:13.949
The cowboy rib eye is
supposedly 16 ounces of beef.

01:19:13.949 --> 01:19:15.917
Let's see if that's actually true.

01:19:15.917 --> 01:19:19.454
This is 12.8 ounces of meat.

01:19:19.454 --> 01:19:20.388
Meat does shrink,

01:19:20.388 --> 01:19:23.925
so a little wiggle room is to be expected,

01:19:23.925 --> 01:19:26.061
but that's

01:19:26.061 --> 01:19:27.696
a pretty decent chunk.
Crew: Boo!

01:19:27.696 --> 01:19:28.563
We booed.

01:19:28.563 --> 01:19:30.198
Everyone's booing.

01:19:30.198 --> 01:19:31.333
Everyone's booing me.

01:19:31.333 --> 01:19:33.068
So, after being booed by everyone,

01:19:33.068 --> 01:19:35.203
I'll be more upset about losing out

01:19:35.203 --> 01:19:36.438
on some of this meat.

01:19:36.438 --> 01:19:38.173
It just looks like such a big portion

01:19:38.173 --> 01:19:40.242
that I'm like, it's fine.

01:19:40.242 --> 01:19:43.178
But it is 4 ounces that we're missing

01:19:43.178 --> 01:19:45.914
that was advertised. So I understand.

01:19:45.914 --> 01:19:48.617
I understand why everyone's upset.

01:19:48.617 --> 01:19:49.718
Strangely, the cowboy rib eye

01:19:49.718 --> 01:19:52.254
is advertised as being
14 ounces here in the UK.

01:19:52.254 --> 01:19:53.655
I'm not sure why we're
getting shortchanged,

01:19:53.655 --> 01:19:55.724
but we're going to measure
it to keep them honest.

01:19:55.724 --> 01:20:00.095
So, our UK cowboy rib eye
has actually only come to

01:20:00.095 --> 01:20:01.530
10 ½ ounces.

01:20:01.530 --> 01:20:03.732
I understand that steaks
do lose some of their mass

01:20:03.732 --> 01:20:07.502
while cooking, but 3 ½
ounces? That seems like a lot.

01:20:07.502 --> 01:20:09.037
Let's get into some apps.

01:20:09.037 --> 01:20:11.840
We got the classic nachos, no meat.

01:20:11.840 --> 01:20:13.175
This is "Food Wars,"

01:20:13.175 --> 01:20:15.210
so you know we gotta weigh these nachos.

01:20:15.210 --> 01:20:17.746
602 grams.

01:20:17.746 --> 01:20:19.114
Here are the UK nachos.

01:20:19.114 --> 01:20:21.416
I'm going to weigh them
with the paper because

01:20:21.416 --> 01:20:24.753
our scale has had enough trauma already.

01:20:24.753 --> 01:20:27.255
Our UK nachos weighed 510 grams.

01:20:27.255 --> 01:20:28.423
I'm interested to see which country

01:20:28.423 --> 01:20:30.659
has bigger wing portions,
so let's count them out.

01:20:30.659 --> 01:20:32.594
Count them out with me. We have one,

01:20:33.728 --> 01:20:34.863
two.

01:20:34.863 --> 01:20:36.298
Three,

01:20:36.298 --> 01:20:37.699
four.

01:20:37.699 --> 01:20:39.401
Harry: 10,

01:20:39.401 --> 01:20:40.635
11,

01:20:40.635 --> 01:20:43.338
and 12.

01:20:43.338 --> 01:20:44.973
Nico: Seven,

01:20:44.973 --> 01:20:45.974
eight.

01:20:45.974 --> 01:20:47.742
I'm going to let them have this one.

01:20:47.742 --> 01:20:49.377
I'm going to let them have this one

01:20:49.377 --> 01:20:51.847
because we've literally
been destroying them

01:20:51.847 --> 01:20:54.182
in these portion sizes this entire time.

01:20:54.182 --> 01:20:57.752
So you know what? You deserve
the extra amount of wings.

01:20:57.752 --> 01:20:58.954
Hard Rock Cafe is also known

01:20:58.954 --> 01:21:00.488
for its famous onion ring tower.

01:21:00.488 --> 01:21:01.656
Now, on the menu, they don't specify

01:21:01.656 --> 01:21:03.558
how many onion rings you get in a tower.

01:21:03.558 --> 01:21:05.727
However, in a promotional
photo, I counted eight.

01:21:05.727 --> 01:21:07.696
So we're going to count ours
out now and see if that's true

01:21:07.696 --> 01:21:09.464
or if we get more or less
than we were promised.

01:21:09.464 --> 01:21:12.400
I'm going to try my hardest to beat Harry

01:21:12.400 --> 01:21:14.736
in the onion-ring-tower-making.

01:21:14.736 --> 01:21:17.939
Somehow built this the wrong way round,

01:21:17.939 --> 01:21:19.741
so it's getting wider near the top.

01:21:19.741 --> 01:21:21.776
Six,

01:21:21.776 --> 01:21:22.644
seven.

01:21:26.548 --> 01:21:28.283
Oh, God, no!

01:21:28.283 --> 01:21:29.584
I'm not doing that again.

01:21:30.585 --> 01:21:31.419
Eight,

01:21:32.354 --> 01:21:33.355
nine,

01:21:33.355 --> 01:21:34.456
10.

01:21:36.892 --> 01:21:38.193
Girls in STEM.

01:21:38.193 --> 01:21:40.562
So, while my tower design failed horribly,

01:21:40.562 --> 01:21:41.963
we did get 11 onion rings

01:21:41.963 --> 01:21:43.598
in our onion ring tower as a side.

01:21:43.598 --> 01:21:44.499
What about dessert?

01:21:44.499 --> 01:21:46.067
Let's compare Hard Rock's apple cobbler

01:21:46.067 --> 01:21:47.435
in the US and the UK.

01:21:47.435 --> 01:21:48.870
It smells so good.

01:21:48.870 --> 01:21:50.572
I'm resisting every urge I have

01:21:50.572 --> 01:21:52.274
to bite into it right now.

01:21:52.274 --> 01:21:56.544
We've got 432 grams of apple cobbler.

01:21:56.544 --> 01:21:59.881
So, the apple cobbler in
the UK came to 376 grams.

01:22:01.783 --> 01:22:03.084
Here are all the menu items you'll find

01:22:03.084 --> 01:22:05.587
at a Hard Rock in the UK that
you won't find in the US.

01:22:05.587 --> 01:22:07.389
Here's all the food that you'll find

01:22:07.389 --> 01:22:10.425
at Hard Rock Cafe in the US
that you won't find in the UK.

01:22:10.425 --> 01:22:11.993
There are a couple of burgers you'll find

01:22:11.993 --> 01:22:13.728
in the US but not in the UK,

01:22:13.728 --> 01:22:16.398
the turkey burger and
the Impossible burger.

01:22:16.398 --> 01:22:19.167
I think Impossible beef isn't
available in the UK yet.

01:22:19.167 --> 01:22:22.170
There's an ingredient in it
called soy leghemoglobin,

01:22:22.170 --> 01:22:24.839
which is approved in the
US but not in the UK.

01:22:24.839 --> 01:22:26.408
The Impossible burger.

01:22:31.379 --> 01:22:35.483
That soy leghemoglobin is
doing what it needs to do.

01:22:35.483 --> 01:22:37.786
That's the ingredient
that makes this taste

01:22:37.786 --> 01:22:42.023
like actual beef, and it's
working. It's working for me.

01:22:42.023 --> 01:22:44.659
I can tell texture-wise,
in the back of my head,

01:22:44.659 --> 01:22:45.860
this is not beef,

01:22:45.860 --> 01:22:48.263
but it's doing enough
where I don't really care.

01:22:48.263 --> 01:22:49.698
It tastes pretty good.

01:22:49.698 --> 01:22:50.999
There's also a small difference

01:22:50.999 --> 01:22:53.568
in the classic legendary
burger in the two countries.

01:22:53.568 --> 01:22:56.071
The UK uses just regular cheddar cheese,

01:22:56.071 --> 01:22:58.974
while the US specifically
uses Tillamook cheddar.

01:22:58.974 --> 01:23:01.676
Tillamook is a popular
creamery based in Oregon.

01:23:01.676 --> 01:23:03.011
I'm not going to bite
into everything here today

01:23:03.011 --> 01:23:04.646
because we are going to
give some of this away,

01:23:04.646 --> 01:23:06.314
but if there's anything that
catches my eye particularly,

01:23:06.314 --> 01:23:07.148
then I'll give it a try.

01:23:07.148 --> 01:23:09.117
We actually have quite a
few exclusives in the UK,

01:23:09.117 --> 01:23:10.986
so I'll start at this
end and work my way down.

01:23:10.986 --> 01:23:13.054
First off, we have cauliflower tacos.

01:23:13.054 --> 01:23:14.756
I think they are pretty
much as the name suggests.

01:23:14.756 --> 01:23:16.725
It's a taco, which is a,

01:23:16.725 --> 01:23:18.593
I think is a flour tortilla

01:23:18.593 --> 01:23:21.196
with some pieces of seasoned
cauliflower in there

01:23:21.196 --> 01:23:24.265
as well as some slaw, some
sauce, and some jalapeños.

01:23:24.265 --> 01:23:25.867
Next up, we've got barbecue chicken.

01:23:25.867 --> 01:23:27.736
This one really just does
what it says on the tin.

01:23:27.736 --> 01:23:29.037
It's just half of a chicken,

01:23:29.037 --> 01:23:31.339
which has been grilled and
covered in barbecue sauce.

01:23:31.339 --> 01:23:32.674
It's almost unnerving

01:23:32.674 --> 01:23:35.643
how accurately they have
halved this chicken.

01:23:37.112 --> 01:23:38.346
It's literally like they've just gone

01:23:38.346 --> 01:23:40.382
straight through the middle
of it with, like, a laser beam

01:23:40.382 --> 01:23:41.783
and sliced the thing in two.

01:23:41.783 --> 01:23:44.319
The next exclusive menu
item is fish and chips.

01:23:44.319 --> 01:23:45.620
You get this a lot. You
might have a company

01:23:45.620 --> 01:23:48.556
which branding-wise seems
like it's not from the UK,

01:23:48.556 --> 01:23:49.858
but when they launch in the UK,

01:23:49.858 --> 01:23:52.327
they feel compelled to have
fish and chips on the menu.

01:23:52.327 --> 01:23:54.162
I will have to try this one.

01:23:54.162 --> 01:23:55.597
I can't let them off the hook

01:23:55.597 --> 01:23:57.265
if they've really
butchered fish and chips.

01:23:57.265 --> 01:23:59.968
Although I will give them,
again, some credit for the fact

01:23:59.968 --> 01:24:02.337
that this has been
sitting around for a while

01:24:02.337 --> 01:24:04.105
and therefore has lost all of the crisp

01:24:04.105 --> 01:24:05.407
which I think it once had.

01:24:08.443 --> 01:24:09.511
It's not too bad.

01:24:09.511 --> 01:24:12.547
I do think had we eaten
this hot and fresh,

01:24:12.547 --> 01:24:14.516
it would've been a bit crispier.

01:24:14.516 --> 01:24:18.386
You can definitely see some
of the flakes of batter

01:24:18.386 --> 01:24:19.521
which would've crisped up.

01:24:19.521 --> 01:24:21.456
Next up, we have the
Moving Mountains burger.

01:24:21.456 --> 01:24:22.724
Now, we don't have the Impossible burger

01:24:22.724 --> 01:24:24.359
on the UK menu because as of right now,

01:24:24.359 --> 01:24:26.928
Impossible beef still
cannot be sold in the UK.

01:24:26.928 --> 01:24:28.329
Moving Mountains is a company

01:24:28.329 --> 01:24:29.497
which does very similar things.

01:24:29.497 --> 01:24:31.599
It's making plant-based meat alternatives,

01:24:31.599 --> 01:24:33.435
including a beef burger, which is approved

01:24:33.435 --> 01:24:35.670
for sale in the UK and is on offer here.

01:24:35.670 --> 01:24:38.173
I mean, visually, I gotta say

01:24:38.173 --> 01:24:40.041
the Moving Mountains burger
does look pretty good.

01:24:40.041 --> 01:24:42.977
It's got a nice brown exterior.

01:24:42.977 --> 01:24:45.046
They've got the kind of grainy,

01:24:45.046 --> 01:24:47.015
like, meat fibers, almost, in the middle,

01:24:47.015 --> 01:24:48.883
and it definitely looks a bit pink, even.

01:24:51.519 --> 01:24:52.687
I'm quite impressed by that.

01:24:52.687 --> 01:24:53.521
That's really not bad.

01:24:53.521 --> 01:24:54.989
Obviously it doesn't
have the exact beef taste

01:24:54.989 --> 01:24:56.424
that you're used to, but

01:24:56.424 --> 01:24:57.992
there's definitely a meatiness to it.

01:24:57.992 --> 01:25:01.062
Every Hard Rock Cafe location
also has a local burger

01:25:01.062 --> 01:25:03.531
which is inspired by
ingredients found in that area.

01:25:03.531 --> 01:25:05.433
So this is the London burger.

01:25:05.433 --> 01:25:07.435
On here, you'll find a beef patty

01:25:07.435 --> 01:25:09.471
with some layers of roast beef,

01:25:09.471 --> 01:25:11.906
a horseradish mayo, rocket,

01:25:11.906 --> 01:25:14.008
some tomato, and some lettuce.

01:25:14.008 --> 01:25:15.777
Now, the London burger is loosely inspired

01:25:15.777 --> 01:25:16.978
by a British Sunday roast,

01:25:16.978 --> 01:25:18.580
specifically the roast beef.

01:25:18.580 --> 01:25:19.647
I do think I need to taste this

01:25:19.647 --> 01:25:22.784
to see if it's giving an
accurate representation

01:25:22.784 --> 01:25:23.952
of British cuisine.

01:25:23.952 --> 01:25:25.186
They've been fairly generous

01:25:25.186 --> 01:25:27.255
with the roast beef slices, I have to say.

01:25:28.456 --> 01:25:29.524
Cheers.

01:25:32.794 --> 01:25:35.430
You definitely do get
that roast beef flavor

01:25:35.430 --> 01:25:37.165
from these pieces of beef.

01:25:37.165 --> 01:25:38.066
I think it could maybe have done

01:25:38.066 --> 01:25:40.168
with even more horseradish mayo,

01:25:40.168 --> 01:25:41.836
because the flavor of
that is really lovely.

01:25:41.836 --> 01:25:42.670
It's pretty good.

01:25:42.670 --> 01:25:45.006
Whether it's a accurate
representation of London

01:25:45.006 --> 01:25:48.576
and London cuisine, I'm
going to go with no.

01:25:48.576 --> 01:25:50.812
But is it a tasty burger?

01:25:50.812 --> 01:25:51.813
Kind of, yes.

01:25:51.813 --> 01:25:54.816
New York City doesn't have
a region-specific burger,

01:25:54.816 --> 01:25:58.953
but they do offer a French
dip on garlic bread.

01:25:58.953 --> 01:26:00.955
Joe made me aware that the French dip

01:26:00.955 --> 01:26:03.558
was not created in New
York, but rather LA.

01:26:03.558 --> 01:26:07.028
But I can kind of see
what they were going for

01:26:07.028 --> 01:26:09.764
because roast beef sandwiches in general

01:26:09.764 --> 01:26:12.901
are popular here. That
includes French dips.

01:26:12.901 --> 01:26:14.936
And they might have
chosen the garlic bread

01:26:14.936 --> 01:26:17.739
because New York City has so much Italian

01:26:17.739 --> 01:26:20.408
and Italian American
culture ingrained in it.

01:26:20.408 --> 01:26:23.444
I feel like I'm kind
of reaching right now,

01:26:23.444 --> 01:26:25.880
but I'm just trying to
make sense of it all.

01:26:25.880 --> 01:26:28.483
This is the garlic bread
that they speak of.

01:26:28.483 --> 01:26:32.353
Um, I think we need to have
some words with Hard Rock Cafe.

01:26:32.353 --> 01:26:33.488
What is the sauce called?

01:26:34.556 --> 01:26:35.623
Bless you.

01:26:37.659 --> 01:26:38.493
Got her.

01:26:38.493 --> 01:26:40.128
Got both of them.

01:26:40.128 --> 01:26:42.030
I'm taking a dip. I'm taking a dip.

01:26:42.030 --> 01:26:43.298
You can do it! You can do it!

01:26:43.298 --> 01:26:46.634
This is the coldest au jus

01:26:46.634 --> 01:26:48.603
and the coldest French dip.

01:26:54.943 --> 01:26:56.477
This is just not it.

01:26:56.477 --> 01:26:59.647
Go somewhere else. Go somewhere else!

01:26:59.647 --> 01:27:01.149
Hard Rock Cafe has also partnered

01:27:01.149 --> 01:27:02.717
with the greatest footballer of all time,

01:27:02.717 --> 01:27:03.918
Lionel Messi.

01:27:03.918 --> 01:27:05.820
Now, I'm not sure if
Messi's diet allows him

01:27:05.820 --> 01:27:07.622
to regularly eat at Hard Rock Cafe.

01:27:07.622 --> 01:27:09.857
However, he's partnered with
them to release two burgers.

01:27:09.857 --> 01:27:11.092
You can find the Messi beef burger

01:27:11.092 --> 01:27:12.527
in both the US and the UK,

01:27:12.527 --> 01:27:14.195
but in the UK we have an exclusive one,

01:27:14.195 --> 01:27:15.363
the Messi chicken burger.

01:27:15.363 --> 01:27:17.599
Let's just take a look
and see what we can find.

01:27:17.599 --> 01:27:21.736
So, we have what looks to
be maybe a potato roll.

01:27:21.736 --> 01:27:24.706
We've got some bacon, some cheese,

01:27:25.707 --> 01:27:28.042
a big grilled chicken breast.

01:27:28.042 --> 01:27:29.844
There is some sauce on here as well.

01:27:30.945 --> 01:27:33.081
Mayonnaise.

01:27:33.081 --> 01:27:36.317
As well as tomato and lettuce.

01:27:36.317 --> 01:27:37.285
If I take a bite out of this,

01:27:37.285 --> 01:27:39.287
do you think I will gain
the footballing ability

01:27:39.287 --> 01:27:40.421
of Lionel Messi?

01:27:43.791 --> 01:27:47.395
Whether that is a sandwich
fitting for the GOAT,

01:27:47.395 --> 01:27:48.229
I'm not sure.

01:27:48.229 --> 01:27:49.430
Speaking of Messi,

01:27:49.430 --> 01:27:52.166
I think now's a great time to quiz Joe

01:27:52.166 --> 01:27:54.769
and see if he can pick him
out in a group of photos.

01:27:54.769 --> 01:27:57.238
He is one of the most famous
men in the entire world,

01:27:57.238 --> 01:27:59.974
so it shouldn't be hard. Right, Joe?

01:27:59.974 --> 01:28:03.611
So, one of these persons is Lionel Messi,

01:28:03.611 --> 01:28:07.749
and I don't know which one he is.

01:28:07.749 --> 01:28:09.150
Well, this guy's from "Ted Lasso,"

01:28:09.150 --> 01:28:10.285
so I'm assuming it's not him,

01:28:10.285 --> 01:28:12.086
or someone would've said at some point,

01:28:12.086 --> 01:28:13.221
"Can you believe that guy

01:28:13.221 --> 01:28:15.189
is also a famous soccer player?"

01:28:15.189 --> 01:28:17.425
This looks like a person
who works in a café.

01:28:17.425 --> 01:28:19.694
So I'm going to assume that's not him.

01:28:19.694 --> 01:28:22.096
Tough times for football
players, apparently.

01:28:22.096 --> 01:28:23.031
I mean, this just looks like,

01:28:23.031 --> 01:28:25.033
I feel like I've seen this guy somewhere.

01:28:25.033 --> 01:28:26.567
I feel like this guy works at Insider.

01:28:26.567 --> 01:28:27.635
Who's this?

01:28:27.635 --> 01:28:28.870
This can't be him.

01:28:28.870 --> 01:28:30.271
This looks like a stock photo.

01:28:30.271 --> 01:28:33.041
And then this looks like a guy

01:28:33.041 --> 01:28:37.412
from, like, a background
extra in "The Matrix," so.

01:28:37.412 --> 01:28:39.647
So I'm going to think that it's this guy.

01:28:39.647 --> 01:28:40.481
Oh, it's not?

01:28:40.481 --> 01:28:42.150
This guy?
Yuelei: Yeah.

01:28:42.150 --> 01:28:46.854
This guy is this super-famous
football soccer player?

01:28:46.854 --> 01:28:48.956
So, I think I was right.
This is Shutterstock, though.

01:29:00.001 --> 01:29:01.736
Hey, what do you want from me?

01:29:01.736 --> 01:29:03.871
None of this means anything to me.

01:29:05.440 --> 01:29:09.844
A legendary burger in the
US contains 1,610 calories.

01:29:09.844 --> 01:29:14.315
Now, I don't know if
they mean 1,610 calories

01:29:14.315 --> 01:29:15.783
with the fries or without,

01:29:15.783 --> 01:29:17.251
so just keep that in mind.

01:29:17.251 --> 01:29:20.555
According to the FDA, that is 81%

01:29:20.555 --> 01:29:23.191
of my daily recommended caloric intake.

01:29:23.191 --> 01:29:24.992
Here in the UK, the
original legendary burger

01:29:24.992 --> 01:29:28.062
contains 1,375 calories,

01:29:28.062 --> 01:29:29.697
which, according to FDA guidelines,

01:29:29.697 --> 01:29:32.500
is around 69% of my daily
recommended allowance.

01:29:32.500 --> 01:29:35.069
A lot of food items on the Hard Rock menu

01:29:35.069 --> 01:29:37.572
are actually super high in calories,

01:29:37.572 --> 01:29:39.640
including the baby back ribs,

01:29:39.640 --> 01:29:43.845
which clock in at 2,433 calories.

01:29:43.845 --> 01:29:45.246
Here in the UK, the baby back ribs

01:29:45.246 --> 01:29:48.216
contain around 1,796 calories,

01:29:48.216 --> 01:29:49.484
which makes the US version

01:29:49.484 --> 01:29:51.753
around 35% more calorific.

01:29:51.753 --> 01:29:54.088
More detailed nutritional
info for Hard Rock Cafe

01:29:54.088 --> 01:29:56.224
doesn't seem to be available online,

01:29:56.224 --> 01:29:59.660
but I did notice this
little tiny salt symbol

01:29:59.660 --> 01:30:02.663
that was next to a whole bunch of dishes.

01:30:02.663 --> 01:30:05.199
This indicates that the
sodium content of that item

01:30:05.199 --> 01:30:07.735
is higher than the total
daily recommended limit,

01:30:07.735 --> 01:30:10.671
which is 2,300 milligrams.

01:30:10.671 --> 01:30:12.340
There's something about a fudge brownie

01:30:12.340 --> 01:30:13.374
with vanilla ice cream

01:30:13.374 --> 01:30:16.377
that feels very American food chain.

01:30:16.377 --> 01:30:18.513
In the US, this dessert contains

01:30:18.513 --> 01:30:21.682
1,122 cals.

01:30:21.682 --> 01:30:22.884
I think it's worth it, though.

01:30:22.884 --> 01:30:24.652
This thing looks really good.

01:30:24.652 --> 01:30:26.421
In the UK, the fudge
brownie with ice cream

01:30:26.421 --> 01:30:28.489
comes to 2,009 calories.

01:30:28.489 --> 01:30:30.124
What we have here is the takeaway version,

01:30:30.124 --> 01:30:31.426
which I personally do not think

01:30:31.426 --> 01:30:32.693
contains 2,000 calories.

01:30:32.693 --> 01:30:33.961
I think what they're referring to

01:30:33.961 --> 01:30:35.363
is the in-restaurant version,

01:30:35.363 --> 01:30:37.532
which is an enormous
chocolate fudge sundae

01:30:37.532 --> 01:30:40.001
with loads of ice cream, sauces, and more.

01:30:40.001 --> 01:30:42.637
In front of me now, I have a
starter of 12 chicken wings

01:30:42.637 --> 01:30:44.338
with Buffalo sauce; I have the main,

01:30:44.338 --> 01:30:46.340
which is the legendary burger and fries;

01:30:46.340 --> 01:30:48.276
and I have dessert, which
is an apple cobbler.

01:30:48.276 --> 01:30:50.311
Now, I would say this is
a fairly standard dinner.

01:30:50.311 --> 01:30:51.879
I think it would be on the heavier side

01:30:51.879 --> 01:30:53.214
for a dinner, but if I was hungry,

01:30:53.214 --> 01:30:55.516
I could definitely polish off
all three courses, no problem.

01:30:55.516 --> 01:30:56.884
However, if I ate all of this,

01:30:56.884 --> 01:31:00.455
I would be consuming 4,819 calories.

01:31:00.455 --> 01:31:02.423
I think those daily-caloric-intake values

01:31:02.423 --> 01:31:05.626
might be outdated and vary
depending on the person.

01:31:05.626 --> 01:31:06.994
I'm not a nutritionist.

01:31:06.994 --> 01:31:07.962
I'm just a girl.

01:31:10.097 --> 01:31:13.434
In the US, an order of Buffalo
wings, a legendary burger,

01:31:13.434 --> 01:31:18.105
and a hot fudge brownie
would cost around $52.97.

01:31:18.105 --> 01:31:22.877
Why in God's green earth
is this burger $20?

01:31:22.877 --> 01:31:25.780
It looks good. Just not $20 good.

01:31:25.780 --> 01:31:28.115
Hard Rock Cafe is a theme restaurant,

01:31:28.115 --> 01:31:30.518
and I feel like with a theme restaurant

01:31:30.518 --> 01:31:33.721
also comes pretty high prices.

01:31:33.721 --> 01:31:37.625
I think that's because they're
kind of tourist-trap-y.

01:31:37.625 --> 01:31:39.827
They think people are willing to pay more

01:31:39.827 --> 01:31:42.964
because it's an experience
and not just the food.

01:31:42.964 --> 01:31:46.267
In the UK, the same meal
would cost you £43.85,

01:31:46.267 --> 01:31:48.569
which is $56.56.

01:31:48.569 --> 01:31:52.773
All of this food for £43
is kind of a lot of food,

01:31:52.773 --> 01:31:54.942
and, as we've discussed,
a lot of calories,

01:31:54.942 --> 01:31:56.844
but also, I don't think I'm getting

01:31:56.844 --> 01:31:58.279
that much value for my money.

01:31:58.279 --> 01:31:59.647
I feel like even in London,

01:31:59.647 --> 01:32:01.582
I can find better value elsewhere,

01:32:01.582 --> 01:32:03.484
and I do think it's kind of being marketed

01:32:03.484 --> 01:32:05.052
as kind of a tourist trap

01:32:05.052 --> 01:32:07.421
or maybe kind of a
special-occasion treat meal.

01:32:09.390 --> 01:32:10.925
Did you know that the first Hard Rock Cafe

01:32:10.925 --> 01:32:12.527
was actually opened in London?

01:32:12.527 --> 01:32:15.396
It was the Hyde Park branch,
and it opened in 1971.

01:32:15.396 --> 01:32:18.065
It's pretty rare to come
across a Hard Rock Cafe

01:32:18.065 --> 01:32:20.268
if you don't live in a major city

01:32:20.268 --> 01:32:21.936
or tourist destination.

01:32:21.936 --> 01:32:23.404
I think because of that reason,

01:32:23.404 --> 01:32:25.907
they're not super popular here,

01:32:25.907 --> 01:32:27.408
even though they are popular

01:32:27.408 --> 01:32:30.845
in the sense that we know what it is.

01:32:30.845 --> 01:32:33.681
There are only 34
locations in the entire US,

01:32:33.681 --> 01:32:35.583
and six of those are in Florida.

01:32:35.583 --> 01:32:39.720
18% of the Hard Rock Cafes
in America are in Florida.

01:32:39.720 --> 01:32:41.489
But this is an international chain,

01:32:41.489 --> 01:32:42.924
so you can find Hard Rock Cafes

01:32:42.924 --> 01:32:45.893
in over 50 countries, including Armenia,

01:32:45.893 --> 01:32:48.296
Nepal, Nigeria, and Poland.

01:32:48.296 --> 01:32:50.831
Hard Rock Cafe does have
a very dedicated fan base.

01:32:50.831 --> 01:32:52.099
There's a community of collectors

01:32:52.099 --> 01:32:53.901
who will buy and swap commemorative merch

01:32:53.901 --> 01:32:54.969
from the different locations,

01:32:54.969 --> 01:32:57.038
including things like
the pins and, of course,

01:32:57.038 --> 01:32:59.240
the iconic T-shirts and jumpers.

01:32:59.240 --> 01:33:01.742
Some superfans even celebrate
Hard Rock milestones

01:33:01.742 --> 01:33:04.512
like visiting your 25th location.

01:33:04.512 --> 01:33:07.081
I think I've got maybe 24 to go.

01:33:07.081 --> 01:33:09.183
Same. All we have to do is go to Florida,

01:33:09.183 --> 01:33:11.285
and we can cross six more off our list.

01:33:11.285 --> 01:33:12.587
They sell normal American fare,

01:33:12.587 --> 01:33:13.754
which I can find anywhere,

01:33:13.754 --> 01:33:15.723
like at a Chili's or an Applebee's,

01:33:15.723 --> 01:33:17.858
chains that are much more available.

01:33:17.858 --> 01:33:20.628
The thing that makes Hard
Rock Cafe special, though,

01:33:20.628 --> 01:33:22.663
isn't the food, it's the memorabilia.

01:33:22.663 --> 01:33:25.366
The first piece of memorabilia
donated to Hard Rock Cafe

01:33:25.366 --> 01:33:28.603
was Eric Clapton's Lead II Fender in 1979.

01:33:28.603 --> 01:33:31.172
Since then, they have
amassed quite the collection,

01:33:31.172 --> 01:33:34.508
with 86,000 pieces worldwide.

01:33:34.508 --> 01:33:37.278
Supposedly, it is the
largest private collection

01:33:37.278 --> 01:33:39.480
of music memorabilia in the entire world.

01:33:39.480 --> 01:33:41.882
These days, the memorabilia is
still either donated to them

01:33:41.882 --> 01:33:43.684
or sometimes purchased through auctions.

01:33:43.684 --> 01:33:44.885
There are some pretty
cool pieces out there,

01:33:44.885 --> 01:33:48.055
including Elvis Presley's
24-karat gold leaf piano,

01:33:48.055 --> 01:33:50.358
handwritten lyrics for
John Lennon's "Imagine,"

01:33:50.358 --> 01:33:52.660
and, of course, One
Direction's iconic phone box,

01:33:52.660 --> 01:33:54.762
which they used in
their album cover shoot.

01:33:56.464 --> 01:33:59.400
I am so jealous that Harry
got to take a picture

01:33:59.400 --> 01:34:01.102
with the One Direction phone booth.

01:34:01.102 --> 01:34:03.371
I don't care if something
good happened to you.

01:34:03.371 --> 01:34:05.473
It should have happened to me instead.

01:34:05.473 --> 01:34:07.108
I did manage to get a picture

01:34:07.108 --> 01:34:08.943
with one of Beyoncé's outfits

01:34:08.943 --> 01:34:11.145
from her "Green Light" music video.

01:34:11.145 --> 01:34:14.482
It's not as iconic as the
"One Direction" phone booth,

01:34:14.482 --> 01:34:15.916
but it's something.

01:34:15.916 --> 01:34:16.851
We have a quote here from one of the

01:34:16.851 --> 01:34:19.387
Hard Rock Cafe founders which
we think is quite interesting.

01:34:19.387 --> 01:34:21.389
It says, "The Hard Rock
Cafe Mayfair's grace

01:34:21.389 --> 01:34:23.891
was becoming London's
first classless restaurant

01:34:23.891 --> 01:34:25.359
at a time when a baker and a banker

01:34:25.359 --> 01:34:28.262
could not commune, live,
shop, or dine together."

01:34:28.262 --> 01:34:30.064
I think they might be
reaching a little bit here.

01:34:30.064 --> 01:34:31.666
I do think that London in the '70s

01:34:31.666 --> 01:34:33.367
was kind of in its punk era.

01:34:33.367 --> 01:34:35.970
I think social barriers were
already being torn down.

01:34:35.970 --> 01:34:38.339
We'd already had the kind of
hippie movement in the '60s.

01:34:38.339 --> 01:34:40.741
I don't think Hard Rock
Cafe can really claim

01:34:40.741 --> 01:34:43.144
that they were the only ones
to break down social barriers.

01:34:43.144 --> 01:34:46.180
And there is nothing more
punk than the Hard Rock Cafe.

01:34:46.414 --> 01:34:48.416
Harry: From calorie
count to portion sizes,

01:34:48.416 --> 01:34:50.418
we wanted to find the
differences between MREs

01:34:50.418 --> 01:34:52.853
and ration packs in the US and the UK.

01:34:52.853 --> 01:34:53.988
This is "Food Wars."

01:34:56.957 --> 01:35:00.361
A US MRE is manufactured and packaged

01:35:00.361 --> 01:35:04.932
by Wornick, AmeriQual, and Sopakco

01:35:04.932 --> 01:35:06.901
and comes in one standard pack size.

01:35:06.901 --> 01:35:09.370
UK MREs, or ration packs, as we call them,

01:35:09.370 --> 01:35:10.938
are produced by Vestey Foods

01:35:10.938 --> 01:35:12.473
and come in three portion sizes.

01:35:12.473 --> 01:35:15.509
The standard portion is
this, a 24-hour ration pack.

01:35:15.509 --> 01:35:17.178
You can also get a smaller version,

01:35:17.178 --> 01:35:18.713
which is a 12-hour ration pack,

01:35:18.713 --> 01:35:21.048
and our biggest portion

01:35:21.048 --> 01:35:22.149
is this.

01:35:22.149 --> 01:35:24.385
This is a 10-man operational pack,

01:35:24.385 --> 01:35:27.521
which contains 10 of these
24-hour ration packs.

01:35:27.521 --> 01:35:29.890
According to the Defense
Logistics Agency website,

01:35:29.890 --> 01:35:31.792
a case of MREs, which is 12 meals,

01:35:31.792 --> 01:35:35.362
weighs around 22 pounds,
or 9.98 kilograms.

01:35:35.362 --> 01:35:40.234
So on average, one meal
would be roughly 831.6 grams.

01:35:40.234 --> 01:35:42.103
The approximate weight of
one of these ration packs

01:35:42.103 --> 01:35:43.804
is 1.75 kilograms.

01:35:43.804 --> 01:35:45.506
We're going to weigh it
to see if that's accurate.

01:35:49.510 --> 01:35:51.278
So, the weight of our 24-hour ration pack

01:35:51.278 --> 01:35:54.215
here in the UK was
actually 1.49 kilograms.

01:35:54.215 --> 01:35:56.751
This is a bit lower
than the promised 1.75.

01:35:56.751 --> 01:35:58.252
I don't know if you're
a soldier in the field

01:35:58.252 --> 01:35:59.653
if this is actually a positive thing

01:35:59.653 --> 01:36:01.589
because you've got less
weight to carry or if you're

01:36:01.589 --> 01:36:03.357
actually getting slightly less
food than you were promised.

01:36:03.357 --> 01:36:05.226
In the UK, our ration pack menu No. 2

01:36:05.226 --> 01:36:06.660
contains a chili con carne.

01:36:06.660 --> 01:36:08.395
We're going to compare
ours to the American chili

01:36:08.395 --> 01:36:09.697
to see who's getting more.

01:36:13.801 --> 01:36:16.370
I do think I've lost a little bit in here.

01:36:16.370 --> 01:36:18.939
And if I were eating this
straight out of the packet,

01:36:18.939 --> 01:36:21.208
maybe tearing the packet
open, getting better access,

01:36:21.208 --> 01:36:24.111
I could probably get that
closer to the 300 mark.

01:36:24.111 --> 01:36:26.180
So, here are the contents
of a 24-hour ration pack

01:36:26.180 --> 01:36:27.014
in the UK.

01:36:27.014 --> 01:36:29.350
In total, we counted 29 edible items

01:36:29.350 --> 01:36:31.552
as well as a few extra
bits, including tissues,

01:36:31.552 --> 01:36:33.954
sanitary wipes, matches,
and other stuff like that.

01:36:33.954 --> 01:36:37.124
Joe: We counted 11 edible
items in our US ration pack.

01:36:39.794 --> 01:36:42.263
Here are all the military MREs for the US

01:36:42.263 --> 01:36:43.798
not available in the UK.

01:36:43.798 --> 01:36:45.399
And here are all the
ration packs in the UK

01:36:45.399 --> 01:36:47.001
that you can't find in the US.

01:36:47.001 --> 01:36:48.803
There's actually very few overlap

01:36:48.803 --> 01:36:52.573
of specific meals in US MREs
and the UK ration packs.

01:36:52.573 --> 01:36:55.976
Also, depending on which MRE meal you get,

01:36:55.976 --> 01:36:58.779
they contain different
combinations of snacks and sides.

01:36:58.779 --> 01:37:01.081
In both countries, official
MREs are not available

01:37:01.081 --> 01:37:02.817
for sale directly to consumers.

01:37:02.817 --> 01:37:04.518
Basically, when you get
a ration pack in the UK,

01:37:04.518 --> 01:37:06.620
it will come with a
corresponding menu number.

01:37:06.620 --> 01:37:08.255
The menu number will give
you a rough indication

01:37:08.255 --> 01:37:09.323
of what you're going to find inside,

01:37:09.323 --> 01:37:10.791
although there is a bit of variation.

01:37:10.791 --> 01:37:12.593
So, what we've done here
is lay out the breakfast

01:37:12.593 --> 01:37:15.229
and the main-course options
in order of the menus.

01:37:15.229 --> 01:37:16.363
In the UK, we're currently able

01:37:16.363 --> 01:37:18.532
to get hold of menus one to 10.

01:37:18.532 --> 01:37:20.801
All right. We got the US MREs.

01:37:20.801 --> 01:37:22.870
I think we got every menu, one through 24.

01:37:22.870 --> 01:37:24.438
We're going to go through the entrées

01:37:24.438 --> 01:37:25.940
and the breakfasts now.

01:37:25.940 --> 01:37:28.609
I want to point out that for the US MREs,

01:37:28.609 --> 01:37:31.879
not every single one came
with a definitive breakfast,

01:37:31.879 --> 01:37:33.214
and some of them just had something

01:37:33.214 --> 01:37:35.149
that was fruit or fruit-related,

01:37:35.149 --> 01:37:37.418
which we assumed was supposed to be eaten

01:37:37.418 --> 01:37:38.819
first thing in the morning.

01:37:38.819 --> 01:37:40.955
Now, all of these can be
eaten either hot or cold.

01:37:40.955 --> 01:37:42.656
I guess ideally, you'd
have them heated up.

01:37:42.656 --> 01:37:45.526
We actually got sent this
flameless heating bag.

01:37:45.526 --> 01:37:47.094
Instructions for use.

01:37:47.094 --> 01:37:49.430
This is either going to go really well

01:37:49.430 --> 01:37:50.564
or I'm going to give myself

01:37:50.564 --> 01:37:53.234
and the "Food Wars" table
some chemical burns.

01:37:53.234 --> 01:37:54.501
I'm going to do a chili
because we've already

01:37:54.501 --> 01:37:56.403
got a chili opened and I kind
of know what they taste like.

01:37:56.403 --> 01:37:57.905
Filter line only.

01:37:57.905 --> 01:38:00.507
And then leave that for 10 minutes.

01:38:00.507 --> 01:38:01.342
Oh.

01:38:05.713 --> 01:38:08.015
Well, it works. [laughs]

01:38:08.015 --> 01:38:09.450
I hope I've sealed that well enough,

01:38:09.450 --> 01:38:11.886
because that steamed
up very quickly there.

01:38:11.886 --> 01:38:13.487
So, while that does its thing,
I guess all that remains

01:38:13.487 --> 01:38:15.022
is for me to try the rest of them.

01:38:15.022 --> 01:38:17.858
Starting down here, remember
our good friend the chili?

01:38:17.858 --> 01:38:21.428
Not bad. I would say as
average as chili can be.

01:38:21.428 --> 01:38:22.496
I think, honestly,

01:38:22.496 --> 01:38:25.399
I've never been in the military,
I haven't done any service,

01:38:25.399 --> 01:38:27.735
but I used to camp a lot when I was a kid,

01:38:27.735 --> 01:38:28.969
and I do remember going on

01:38:28.969 --> 01:38:32.706
sometimes two-, three-week-long
canoe trips in Canada.

01:38:32.706 --> 01:38:35.175
Couple days in, this is, like, gourmet.

01:38:35.175 --> 01:38:36.410
You know what I'm talking about.

01:38:36.410 --> 01:38:38.612
Like, your standards change quickly

01:38:38.612 --> 01:38:40.114
when you're outside all day.

01:38:40.114 --> 01:38:41.749
It comes with a cornbread.

01:38:41.749 --> 01:38:44.218
This cornbread muffin's
actually really good.

01:38:44.218 --> 01:38:45.619
Let's try it with the chili.

01:38:46.520 --> 01:38:48.055
So, the breakfast that we got for menu one

01:38:48.055 --> 01:38:50.791
was pork sausage and
beans in a tomato sauce.

01:38:50.791 --> 01:38:53.560
This is kind of an iconic
British meal. I can't lie.

01:38:53.560 --> 01:38:55.195
It's kind of good.

01:38:55.195 --> 01:38:56.897
We're starting off strong. I like that.

01:38:56.897 --> 01:38:58.899
Next up, we have a pindi chana aloo,

01:38:58.899 --> 01:39:01.101
which seems to be a
potato and chickpea curry.

01:39:01.101 --> 01:39:03.804
And then the final option
from menu one in the UK,

01:39:03.804 --> 01:39:05.039
meatballs and pasta.

01:39:05.039 --> 01:39:08.175
Do I have high or low expectations
for meatballs and pasta?

01:39:08.175 --> 01:39:09.343
Yeah, I mean,

01:39:09.343 --> 01:39:11.345
I think my fear was that,
like, pasta doesn't hold up

01:39:11.345 --> 01:39:13.480
very well when it's kind of submerged

01:39:13.480 --> 01:39:15.082
in a sauce and cooked for a while,

01:39:15.082 --> 01:39:16.917
and I think that's kind of come true.

01:39:16.917 --> 01:39:17.785
Meatballs are all right, though.

01:39:17.785 --> 01:39:19.553
Menu No. 2, shredded
beef in barbecue sauce,

01:39:19.553 --> 01:39:22.856
but also came with seasoned
black beans and tortillas.

01:39:22.856 --> 01:39:24.158
So, I mean,

01:39:24.158 --> 01:39:25.292
I don't know how you would do this

01:39:25.292 --> 01:39:30.030
out in the field with no
plates and not a table.

01:39:30.030 --> 01:39:32.266
And then the beans.

01:39:32.266 --> 01:39:34.501
A military taco. Here we go.

01:39:34.501 --> 01:39:36.804
These beans are rock solid.

01:39:36.804 --> 01:39:38.272
The consistency's a little weird.

01:39:38.272 --> 01:39:41.709
This one is the chicken and noodles

01:39:41.709 --> 01:39:44.278
with vegetables in sauce.

01:39:44.278 --> 01:39:47.014
Yeah, this looks like a

01:39:47.014 --> 01:39:49.583
can of, like, chunky soup

01:39:49.583 --> 01:39:51.418
or chunky stew before you heat it up.

01:39:51.418 --> 01:39:52.920
This definitely should
be heated up before.

01:39:52.920 --> 01:39:54.822
And this smell — oh,
my God, this is brutal.

01:39:54.822 --> 01:39:55.656
If you had this

01:39:55.656 --> 01:39:57.558
and you couldn't heat it
up for some reason ...

01:39:57.558 --> 01:39:58.826
This is very bad.

01:39:58.826 --> 01:40:00.127
The first potential breakfast,

01:40:00.127 --> 01:40:02.062
the applesauce with raspberry puree.

01:40:02.062 --> 01:40:03.097
Now we're talking here.

01:40:05.265 --> 01:40:07.401
Oh, my God, that does
not taste like fruit.

01:40:07.401 --> 01:40:09.103
That tastes like pure sugar.

01:40:09.103 --> 01:40:10.270
There is fruit in it,

01:40:10.270 --> 01:40:14.108
but it tastes more like
fruit-flavored soda.

01:40:14.108 --> 01:40:15.709
This will definitely wake
you up in the morning.

01:40:15.709 --> 01:40:16.543
Then we're on to menu two,

01:40:16.543 --> 01:40:19.847
and the breakfast is a
raspberry muesli with milk.

01:40:19.847 --> 01:40:21.982
Now, it says "with milk," but
there is no liquid in here.

01:40:21.982 --> 01:40:23.283
So I think what they've
done is actually put

01:40:23.283 --> 01:40:25.652
powdered milk in here,
and you add some water,

01:40:25.652 --> 01:40:26.720
and then hopefully that kind of

01:40:26.720 --> 01:40:28.722
turns into something resembling milk.

01:40:28.722 --> 01:40:31.492
It looks very chalky and powdery

01:40:31.492 --> 01:40:33.193
at the moment, without the water in it.

01:40:33.193 --> 01:40:35.963
It says to add around 100 mils of water,

01:40:35.963 --> 01:40:39.033
mix it, and then leave it
to stand for a minute or so.

01:40:39.033 --> 01:40:42.202
But yeah. I mean, now that's
looking a lot more like muesli.

01:40:42.202 --> 01:40:45.472
That's actually not bad.
I'm quite impressed by that.

01:40:45.472 --> 01:40:47.041
There is a slightly noticeable difference

01:40:47.041 --> 01:40:48.575
between that and real milk.

01:40:48.575 --> 01:40:50.044
Then the mains for menu two,

01:40:50.044 --> 01:40:52.446
obviously the chili con carne,

01:40:52.446 --> 01:40:54.681
which we opened for the
portion-sizes section.

01:40:54.681 --> 01:40:56.183
Not the best visuals,

01:40:56.183 --> 01:41:00.120
but it's quite hard to make a
nice-looking chili anyway, so,

01:41:00.120 --> 01:41:01.822
it's more about the taste.

01:41:01.822 --> 01:41:03.857
It's not spicy. Pretty well seasoned.

01:41:03.857 --> 01:41:06.326
And then menu two also has a rajma masala.

01:41:06.326 --> 01:41:07.461
The longer I leave this one,

01:41:07.461 --> 01:41:08.362
I think this is the first one

01:41:08.362 --> 01:41:10.431
that has actually a bit of heat to it,

01:41:10.431 --> 01:41:13.400
but not unpleasant at all,
just a nice warmth there.

01:41:13.400 --> 01:41:14.401
This hits close to home.

01:41:14.401 --> 01:41:17.738
Spaghetti with beef and sauce.

01:41:17.738 --> 01:41:21.442
Oh, and it even came
with Italian breadsticks.

01:41:21.442 --> 01:41:23.277
What do we think? It's not meatballs.

01:41:23.277 --> 01:41:26.046
They had meatballs. We
only have meat sauce.

01:41:26.046 --> 01:41:27.214
Breadsticks.

01:41:27.214 --> 01:41:28.682
They're a bit dry.

01:41:28.682 --> 01:41:31.218
They're the most driest
thing I've ever eaten.

01:41:31.218 --> 01:41:32.419
These are so dry.

01:41:32.419 --> 01:41:33.754
White chicken chunks.

01:41:33.754 --> 01:41:36.623
Oh, no! It's just, look.

01:41:37.558 --> 01:41:39.726
Yeah, just canned white chicken.

01:41:42.629 --> 01:41:45.232
No, I would be so bummed.

01:41:45.232 --> 01:41:46.533
Yeah, this is the worst one.

01:41:46.533 --> 01:41:50.604
So, we got a beef taco
filling with Santa Fe rice.

01:41:52.873 --> 01:41:55.943
Um, not horrible. Decent
seasoning, but yeah,

01:41:55.943 --> 01:41:58.212
I think what's missing
for all these is cheese.

01:41:58.212 --> 01:41:59.079
Then we're on to menu three,

01:41:59.079 --> 01:42:00.948
and we have our first duplicate item.

01:42:00.948 --> 01:42:02.483
It's another muesli for breakfast.

01:42:02.483 --> 01:42:03.484
Now, this one, I'm intrigued by.

01:42:03.484 --> 01:42:05.152
One of the main options in menu three,

01:42:05.152 --> 01:42:07.554
steak and vegetables with dumplings.

01:42:07.554 --> 01:42:09.623
A classic British beef stew

01:42:09.623 --> 01:42:11.391
is kind of one of my favorite foods.

01:42:11.391 --> 01:42:13.093
Having said that, I've opened this,

01:42:13.093 --> 01:42:14.795
and it looks and smells like cat food.

01:42:14.795 --> 01:42:17.064
Now, I've been eating most of
these straight out of the bag

01:42:17.064 --> 01:42:19.433
because they've had a kind of
texture which permits that,

01:42:19.433 --> 01:42:21.568
but this one I think we're
going to have to plate up.

01:42:21.568 --> 01:42:26.473
This apparently is steak and
vegetables with dumplings.

01:42:26.473 --> 01:42:27.708
Now, I started this section by saying

01:42:27.708 --> 01:42:29.543
that all of these can
be eaten hot or cold,

01:42:29.543 --> 01:42:30.711
and I do think that's true.

01:42:30.711 --> 01:42:32.513
I guess the question is
whether or not all of them

01:42:32.513 --> 01:42:34.581
should be eaten hot or cold.

01:42:34.581 --> 01:42:37.317
And I think this one, just
immediately from the look of it,

01:42:37.317 --> 01:42:39.086
I think probably needs to be heated up

01:42:39.086 --> 01:42:43.824
because we've had our first
womp-womp moment of the episode.

01:42:45.592 --> 01:42:46.927
[womp womp]

01:42:46.927 --> 01:42:47.761
That's not good.

01:42:47.761 --> 01:42:50.364
Beef strips in a savory tomato sauce.

01:42:50.364 --> 01:42:52.799
Marinara sauce with meatballs.

01:42:52.799 --> 01:42:56.036
Wait, so there's no noodles?
Just sauce and meatballs?

01:42:56.970 --> 01:42:58.906
Oh, my goodness. Look how
little these meatballs are.

01:42:58.906 --> 01:42:59.740
Look at these little guys.

01:42:59.740 --> 01:43:01.909
There's no pasta in
there, just the meatballs.

01:43:01.909 --> 01:43:04.444
Yeah, ooh. SpaghettiO
level of meat flavor.

01:43:04.444 --> 01:43:07.281
Beef stew. Oh, at least
this one has noodles.

01:43:07.281 --> 01:43:11.084
And this one came with a "breakfast"

01:43:11.084 --> 01:43:12.686
that was grape jelly.

01:43:12.686 --> 01:43:14.054
We're going to jump ahead
slightly to menu eight

01:43:14.054 --> 01:43:16.523
because that's where I've
taken our hot main from.

01:43:16.523 --> 01:43:17.457
And the alarm has just gone off,

01:43:17.457 --> 01:43:18.659
which means it's ready to eat.

01:43:18.659 --> 01:43:21.395
Once it's cooked, just tear it along here,

01:43:21.395 --> 01:43:23.130
and then it's at the perfect height

01:43:23.130 --> 01:43:25.999
that you can then just also tear this

01:43:25.999 --> 01:43:27.167
and eat the whole thing.

01:43:27.167 --> 01:43:29.570
It's changed the consistency
of this a little bit.

01:43:29.570 --> 01:43:31.905
I think with the steak and
vegetables, for example,

01:43:31.905 --> 01:43:34.107
I think that would change
the consistency a lot,

01:43:34.107 --> 01:43:35.409
whereas this has just made it

01:43:35.409 --> 01:43:37.644
a little bit more runny, but not too bad.

01:43:37.644 --> 01:43:39.446
That was very hot, but
that's still really good.

01:43:39.446 --> 01:43:41.415
I think this one I do
prefer heated up, actually.

01:43:41.415 --> 01:43:43.083
It brings out a few more of the flavors,

01:43:43.083 --> 01:43:44.751
a little bit more depth of flavor to it.

01:43:44.751 --> 01:43:45.919
That's really good, and I'm so impressed

01:43:45.919 --> 01:43:47.588
with this heating-pad thing.

01:43:47.588 --> 01:43:51.892
The heating pad, as well as
just, I guess, warmed-up meals.

01:43:51.892 --> 01:43:53.227
Thumbs up from me so far.

01:43:53.227 --> 01:43:55.696
Macaroni in a beef
sauce. Well, that's good.

01:43:55.696 --> 01:43:58.398
We got noodles! And the chili.

01:43:58.398 --> 01:44:00.767
Oh, that's terrible. It
doesn't taste like anything.

01:44:00.767 --> 01:44:02.269
Vegetarian taco pasta,

01:44:02.269 --> 01:44:06.240
which is a vegetable crumble
with pasta in taco-style sauce.

01:44:08.141 --> 01:44:10.077
Is this supposed to be "Zapplesauce"?

01:44:10.077 --> 01:44:11.979
Who doesn't love a little applesauce?

01:44:13.480 --> 01:44:15.249
Again, wow, super sweet.

01:44:15.249 --> 01:44:17.818
With sugar, not high-fructose
corn syrup, so that's a plus.

01:44:17.818 --> 01:44:19.286
And the last item from menu three

01:44:19.286 --> 01:44:21.788
is a beef burrito-style filling with rice.

01:44:21.788 --> 01:44:25.025
Sadly, this does not
come with any tortillas,

01:44:25.025 --> 01:44:26.793
so you can't make an
actual burrito with it.

01:44:26.793 --> 01:44:27.861
There's a little bit more spice

01:44:27.861 --> 01:44:29.496
than anything else that I've had so far.

01:44:29.496 --> 01:44:30.597
The texture of the rice

01:44:30.597 --> 01:44:32.299
would be improved by heating this one up

01:44:32.299 --> 01:44:34.935
because the rice is still
a little bit al dente.

01:44:34.935 --> 01:44:36.870
Elbow macaroni in tomato sauce.

01:44:36.870 --> 01:44:38.939
Granted, I mean, I know
I gotta heat them up.

01:44:38.939 --> 01:44:40.307
Yeah, this one's all right.

01:44:40.307 --> 01:44:43.043
Mango peach applesauce.
I'm liking the applesauce.

01:44:43.043 --> 01:44:44.711
This would be considered,
I guess, a breakfast,

01:44:44.711 --> 01:44:45.979
or ... it's fruity,

01:44:45.979 --> 01:44:48.282
so maybe this you would
have first thing in the day.

01:44:48.282 --> 01:44:50.017
Ooh, yeah. Ooh, this is really good.

01:44:50.017 --> 01:44:51.084
Next up, we've got menu four,

01:44:51.084 --> 01:44:53.020
and we're starting with
an all-day breakfast.

01:44:53.020 --> 01:44:54.554
Apparently it differs
from the sausage and beans

01:44:54.554 --> 01:44:56.189
because this contains some omelet

01:44:56.189 --> 01:44:57.858
as well as some bacon pieces.

01:44:57.858 --> 01:45:00.827
I think my only criticism
of this is that the sauce

01:45:00.827 --> 01:45:02.996
that they put the beans
in is exceptionally sweet.

01:45:02.996 --> 01:45:04.131
Carrying on through menu four,

01:45:04.131 --> 01:45:06.500
we have a pasta Bolognese main.

01:45:06.500 --> 01:45:09.069
I have a similar concern
to the meatballs and pasta.

01:45:09.069 --> 01:45:10.537
I think that is roughly the color

01:45:10.537 --> 01:45:13.206
that you want your Bolognese
sauce to look like.

01:45:13.206 --> 01:45:15.742
I think when I looked at this
one, I was expecting worse.

01:45:15.742 --> 01:45:17.477
Again, the vibe I'm getting,

01:45:17.477 --> 01:45:19.980
particularly from the pasta dishes,

01:45:19.980 --> 01:45:21.682
is very much tinned-pasta vibe,

01:45:21.682 --> 01:45:22.783
like a tinned Bolognese.

01:45:22.783 --> 01:45:23.950
And the last menu-four item

01:45:23.950 --> 01:45:26.553
is a chicken curry with potatoes and rice.

01:45:26.553 --> 01:45:28.622
There's actual decent-sized pieces

01:45:28.622 --> 01:45:30.557
of what I hope is chicken.

01:45:30.557 --> 01:45:32.793
Less flavorful than
some of the other ones.

01:45:32.793 --> 01:45:34.261
I want to try heating some of these up.

01:45:34.261 --> 01:45:38.332
You do this. And then
you put your MRE inside.

01:45:38.332 --> 01:45:40.367
Sorry, if you can't tell,
we're adding water to this.

01:45:40.367 --> 01:45:41.335
Here we go.

01:45:41.335 --> 01:45:43.136
Real exciting stuff here in the studio!

01:45:43.136 --> 01:45:44.705
While we're moving on,

01:45:44.705 --> 01:45:47.140
so, we got one, two, three heating up.

01:45:47.140 --> 01:45:50.043
The staff was able to make
this thing work. I was not.

01:45:50.043 --> 01:45:51.845
I don't know why. We're moving on.

01:45:51.845 --> 01:45:56.483
We have a cheese tortellini
in a tomato sauce.

01:45:56.483 --> 01:45:57.284
Oh, yeah, look at this.

01:45:57.284 --> 01:45:59.986
They got the whole tortellini right there.

01:45:59.986 --> 01:46:01.221
Then we're on to menu five,

01:46:01.221 --> 01:46:03.023
and we're starting off
with another muesli.

01:46:03.023 --> 01:46:05.158
It's the third muesli
we've had in five meals.

01:46:05.158 --> 01:46:06.360
The variation, I think, is going to

01:46:06.360 --> 01:46:07.761
start to kind of run dry.

01:46:07.761 --> 01:46:09.162
Speaking of a lack of variation,

01:46:09.162 --> 01:46:11.698
meal five also has
another pasta Bolognese.

01:46:11.698 --> 01:46:13.367
The different option from meal five

01:46:13.367 --> 01:46:15.769
is a sweet and sour chicken with rice.

01:46:15.769 --> 01:46:19.639
Good color on it. Nice
familiar orange color.

01:46:19.639 --> 01:46:21.942
There's pineapple in
this. And water chestnuts.

01:46:21.942 --> 01:46:23.877
I'm kind of impressed.
This is pretty good.

01:46:23.877 --> 01:46:25.178
I'd say this leans more on the balance

01:46:25.178 --> 01:46:27.381
of sweet rather than sweet and sour.

01:46:27.381 --> 01:46:28.615
I think with the water chestnut

01:46:28.615 --> 01:46:30.450
and also the pineapple chunks,

01:46:30.450 --> 01:46:31.885
there's still some things
in there with bite,

01:46:31.885 --> 01:46:32.719
which I appreciate.

01:46:32.719 --> 01:46:35.322
Creamy spinach fettuccine
with egg noodles,

01:46:35.322 --> 01:46:37.991
spinach, and mushroom in a cream sauce.

01:46:37.991 --> 01:46:40.360
Ugh.

01:46:40.360 --> 01:46:43.296
it feels like they're going a little,

01:46:43.296 --> 01:46:44.297
I don't want to say overboard

01:46:44.297 --> 01:46:46.099
because I figure you gotta
have a variety of meals,

01:46:46.099 --> 01:46:49.102
but doesn't a fettuccine with
mushrooms in a cream sauce,

01:46:49.102 --> 01:46:51.538
like, after a long day of

01:46:51.538 --> 01:46:53.473
hauling stuff around and soldiering

01:46:53.473 --> 01:46:56.710
and exhausting job and
carrying all this stuff,

01:46:56.710 --> 01:46:58.078
just being like, "Ah, let's kick back

01:46:58.078 --> 01:47:01.615
with some spinach, mushrooms
in a cream sauce," like.

01:47:01.615 --> 01:47:03.683
The most colorful one so far.

01:47:03.683 --> 01:47:06.553
I don't feel great. I do not feel good.

01:47:06.553 --> 01:47:07.454
Next up is menu six,

01:47:07.454 --> 01:47:09.289
which we've actually
covered all of already.

01:47:09.289 --> 01:47:11.591
Starts with an all-day
breakfast, which I've had,

01:47:11.591 --> 01:47:14.161
then moves on to another pasta Bolognese,

01:47:14.161 --> 01:47:17.564
which I've also had, and
finishes with my old friend

01:47:17.564 --> 01:47:19.933
steak vegetables with dumplings,

01:47:19.933 --> 01:47:21.935
which I will absolutely
not be trying again.

01:47:21.935 --> 01:47:25.372
This should be the
Mexican-style chicken stew.

01:47:25.372 --> 01:47:26.540
Oh, and then they're saying

01:47:26.540 --> 01:47:29.075
there's another "Tear
here to remove the MRE."

01:47:29.075 --> 01:47:31.978
Very smart. So you got
a second spot to tear.

01:47:31.978 --> 01:47:34.481
This is really smart. Second spot to tear.

01:47:34.481 --> 01:47:38.518
So it's easier just to grab the MRE out.

01:47:39.753 --> 01:47:42.289
All right, so the package is hot,

01:47:42.289 --> 01:47:44.157
so it feels like this is much ...

01:47:44.157 --> 01:47:45.992
kind of smooshing around a little bit.

01:47:45.992 --> 01:47:48.562
So, they are still flimsy tortillas,

01:47:48.562 --> 01:47:51.097
but it looks like they're
chipotle flavored.

01:47:51.097 --> 01:47:53.033
Not as gelatinous, I guess.

01:47:54.367 --> 01:47:56.036
Definitely better heated up.

01:47:56.036 --> 01:47:57.904
Yeah, I like this. It's pretty good.

01:47:57.904 --> 01:47:59.005
Next up is menu seven,

01:47:59.005 --> 01:48:00.640
which, I don't know if
this was deliberate or not,

01:48:00.640 --> 01:48:02.876
but appears to be a fully vegetarian menu.

01:48:02.876 --> 01:48:05.979
For starters, we have a
vegetarian all-day breakfast.

01:48:05.979 --> 01:48:08.315
I think the main difference
is that they've subbed out

01:48:08.315 --> 01:48:11.318
the meat sausages for vegetarian sausages.

01:48:11.318 --> 01:48:14.221
It's another one that
seems to be mostly beans.

01:48:14.221 --> 01:48:16.122
It seems very fitting
that the British army

01:48:16.122 --> 01:48:17.524
is powered by baked beans.

01:48:18.592 --> 01:48:19.926
Makes you proud to be British.

01:48:19.926 --> 01:48:21.127
It's got the same issue as before,

01:48:21.127 --> 01:48:23.396
that the beans are just I think too sweet.

01:48:23.396 --> 01:48:25.732
One of the main-course
options in menu seven is

01:48:25.732 --> 01:48:29.002
the rajma masala with rice,
which I've again also had,

01:48:29.002 --> 01:48:30.504
but one that I have not had is

01:48:30.504 --> 01:48:32.606
a vegetable and chickpea biryani.

01:48:32.606 --> 01:48:33.807
I like a biryani.

01:48:33.807 --> 01:48:37.711
For those who don't know,
it's just various things,

01:48:37.711 --> 01:48:39.846
sometimes meat, in this case vegetables,

01:48:39.846 --> 01:48:42.949
cooked down with rice and
a bunch of Indian spices.

01:48:42.949 --> 01:48:45.151
I've had much better biryani in the past.

01:48:45.151 --> 01:48:47.153
I'm sure there are lots
of people watching at home

01:48:47.153 --> 01:48:48.688
and recoiling in horror
at the thought of this

01:48:48.688 --> 01:48:50.323
being a biryani, but it's OK,

01:48:50.323 --> 01:48:52.959
and I think on the whole,
vegetarian menu, pretty good.

01:48:52.959 --> 01:48:55.762
Moving on, we have the
chicken burrito bowl,

01:48:55.762 --> 01:48:57.597
which comes in a bag and not a bowl.

01:48:57.597 --> 01:48:59.533
Sure, a lot of it's just, like ... eh, no.

01:48:59.533 --> 01:49:00.934
We got some heated up, so
you can see the difference

01:49:00.934 --> 01:49:04.337
when it's not heated up. It's
just this weird gel cube.

01:49:04.337 --> 01:49:07.607
And that comes with what
essentially would be considered

01:49:07.607 --> 01:49:11.511
a breakfast, I guess, apple
pieces in a spiced sauce.

01:49:11.511 --> 01:49:13.680
So, the entrée appears to be

01:49:13.680 --> 01:49:16.483
a maple-flavored pork sausage patty.

01:49:16.483 --> 01:49:19.619
OK, so just one block of — yep.

01:49:21.121 --> 01:49:25.025
Ah, it is of course just this.

01:49:25.025 --> 01:49:27.761
It's like a Clif Bar, but pork.

01:49:27.761 --> 01:49:29.663
Not good. Really weird.

01:49:29.663 --> 01:49:31.698
It tastes surprisingly bland.

01:49:31.698 --> 01:49:35.602
A hash brown potato with
bacon, pepper, and onions.

01:49:35.602 --> 01:49:40.507
Ooh, no! This is the most
cat-food-y-looking one.

01:49:40.507 --> 01:49:41.908
Menu eight is another duplicate one.

01:49:41.908 --> 01:49:43.610
We start with another all-day breakfast.

01:49:43.610 --> 01:49:44.978
Then there's another chili con carne,

01:49:44.978 --> 01:49:47.147
which I've actually already
heated up and eaten.

01:49:47.147 --> 01:49:50.283
And we have another beef
burrito-style filling with rice.

01:49:50.283 --> 01:49:54.321
A cooked beef ravioli, once again.

01:49:54.321 --> 01:49:56.356
It, like, heated the bottom of it,

01:49:56.356 --> 01:49:59.526
so you still gotta
massage it a little bit.

01:49:59.526 --> 01:50:01.127
Like, this side is heated,

01:50:01.127 --> 01:50:02.963
and then this middle
part didn't get heated.

01:50:02.963 --> 01:50:04.998
It also came with
tortillas, which is funny.

01:50:04.998 --> 01:50:06.700
This is what this looks
like, and it looks gross.

01:50:06.700 --> 01:50:08.668
Menu nine starts with more muesli,

01:50:08.668 --> 01:50:11.438
then moves to a new dish,
which is sausage casserole.

01:50:11.438 --> 01:50:13.273
This is another classic British dish,

01:50:13.273 --> 01:50:14.507
a sausage casserole.

01:50:14.507 --> 01:50:16.743
Slow-cooked stewed sausages

01:50:16.743 --> 01:50:20.113
with tomato, potatoes, other vegetables.

01:50:20.113 --> 01:50:21.514
On the whole, the sausages
have actually been,

01:50:21.514 --> 01:50:22.882
like, surprisingly good.

01:50:22.882 --> 01:50:24.484
It might just be because I quite like

01:50:24.484 --> 01:50:27.988
those slightly cheap,
like, prepackaged sausages.

01:50:27.988 --> 01:50:29.055
And then for your other main course,

01:50:29.055 --> 01:50:31.191
we have another pindi chana aloo.

01:50:31.191 --> 01:50:32.826
This one, another hot one.

01:50:32.826 --> 01:50:35.562
Oh, so it's just going to be a patty?

01:50:35.562 --> 01:50:36.896
Ugh!

01:50:36.896 --> 01:50:37.731
Yuelei: Oh!

01:50:38.898 --> 01:50:40.734
Joe: It has a sauce, apparently.

01:50:40.734 --> 01:50:43.770
It's grilled jalapeño
pepper jack flavored.

01:50:43.770 --> 01:50:45.705
Comes with au gratin potatoes.

01:50:47.073 --> 01:50:49.909
Au gratin potatoes? What
do you think of that?

01:50:49.909 --> 01:50:51.277
This looks really rough.

01:50:51.277 --> 01:50:54.748
Italian sausage with peppers,
onions in a marinara sauce.

01:50:54.748 --> 01:50:55.582
Yeah, OK.

01:50:55.582 --> 01:50:59.052
Menu No. 21 should be lemon pepper tuna

01:50:59.052 --> 01:51:00.654
or tuna chunks in a bag.

01:51:00.654 --> 01:51:02.322
We've just been informed that this bag

01:51:02.322 --> 01:51:04.758
actually says white chicken
chunks, which we saw before.

01:51:04.758 --> 01:51:06.926
There is a lemon pepper
tuna that we don't have.

01:51:06.926 --> 01:51:08.995
And finally, last but not
least, we have menu 10,

01:51:08.995 --> 01:51:11.231
which starts with an instant porridge.

01:51:11.231 --> 01:51:12.832
The cooking instructions actually specify

01:51:12.832 --> 01:51:14.334
that you need to put boiling water in it,

01:51:14.334 --> 01:51:16.603
leave it for three minutes
to soak, and then eat it,

01:51:16.603 --> 01:51:17.771
whereas all the other ones

01:51:17.771 --> 01:51:19.539
are technically able to be eaten cold.

01:51:19.539 --> 01:51:21.307
Menu 10 has a couple of mains,

01:51:21.307 --> 01:51:22.542
one of which is another

01:51:22.542 --> 01:51:24.811
beef burrito-style filling with rice.

01:51:24.811 --> 01:51:27.080
And our final main course
is hunters chicken.

01:51:27.080 --> 01:51:29.816
It's another plate one,

01:51:29.816 --> 01:51:32.452
which I don't think is a compliment.

01:51:32.452 --> 01:51:35.355
Doesn't look great, does
it? This is hunters chicken.

01:51:35.355 --> 01:51:37.824
Traditionally, this is chicken
breast with some bacon,

01:51:37.824 --> 01:51:39.759
some cheese, some barbecue sauce.

01:51:39.759 --> 01:51:41.361
I think bits of chicken in here,

01:51:41.361 --> 01:51:44.764
although I'm kind of
struggling to see where.

01:51:45.932 --> 01:51:47.634
I think it's these small bits

01:51:47.634 --> 01:51:49.736
rather than, like, a chicken breast.

01:51:49.736 --> 01:51:51.805
Nah, that's really bad.

01:51:51.805 --> 01:51:54.007
Beef goulash. Beef with brown rice,

01:51:54.007 --> 01:51:56.876
spinach, carrots, tomatoes,
and smoked paprika.

01:51:56.876 --> 01:51:58.778
A pepperoni pizza and cheese slice.

01:52:00.547 --> 01:52:02.582
[groans]

01:52:02.582 --> 01:52:03.783
Army pizza.

01:52:06.152 --> 01:52:07.954
Oh, God!

01:52:07.954 --> 01:52:10.323
That's so bad.

01:52:10.323 --> 01:52:11.624
This has been brutal.

01:52:11.624 --> 01:52:12.892
I don't know what it is about this food.

01:52:12.892 --> 01:52:14.661
Like, yeah, some things tasted bad,

01:52:14.661 --> 01:52:16.796
but most things just tasted OK,

01:52:16.796 --> 01:52:19.966
but I have a headache and my
stomach doesn't feel good.

01:52:19.966 --> 01:52:22.302
I noticed there's a
lot of sodium in these.

01:52:22.302 --> 01:52:23.837
So that might have
something to do with it.

01:52:23.837 --> 01:52:24.938
Tortillas came with this one,

01:52:24.938 --> 01:52:28.508
the Southwest-style beef
and black beans with sauce.

01:52:28.508 --> 01:52:30.643
Again, we have very few "breakfasts,"

01:52:30.643 --> 01:52:34.614
but this one is mixed
fruit, which appears to be,

01:52:34.614 --> 01:52:35.749
there's some chunks in there.

01:52:35.749 --> 01:52:38.017
So you got a bag of mixed fruit.

01:52:38.017 --> 01:52:39.619
On top of the mains and breakfast options,

01:52:39.619 --> 01:52:41.654
you also get a dessert
in a British ration pack.

01:52:41.654 --> 01:52:44.257
The options we got today
included a chocolate brownie,

01:52:44.257 --> 01:52:46.860
a cinnamon cake, a vanilla cake,

01:52:46.860 --> 01:52:48.561
a chocolate cake, or an orange cake.

01:52:48.561 --> 01:52:51.831
The US MREs have an
assortment of desserts,

01:52:51.831 --> 01:52:55.034
which looks all right: muffin
tops, cookies, puddings,

01:52:55.034 --> 01:53:00.006
cakes, a bun, a toaster
pastry, cookies, and cobblers.

01:53:00.006 --> 01:53:01.541
I'll try a couple of these.
I want to try the brownie

01:53:01.541 --> 01:53:04.110
because it's packaged
differently to the others

01:53:04.110 --> 01:53:05.411
and because I like brownie.

01:53:05.411 --> 01:53:07.881
It looks a little bit more
cakey than brownie-ish,

01:53:07.881 --> 01:53:10.683
but, uh, we'll see.

01:53:10.683 --> 01:53:12.418
No. That's so dry.

01:53:12.418 --> 01:53:13.987
Starting down here,

01:53:13.987 --> 01:53:15.955
get a maple muffin top

01:53:15.955 --> 01:53:19.192
or a chocolate banana nut muffin top.

01:53:20.093 --> 01:53:21.327
Three different types of cookies.

01:53:21.327 --> 01:53:23.963
You get a oatmeal chocolate chip cookie,

01:53:23.963 --> 01:53:26.633
chocolate chip cookie, and
oatmeal cookie. That's funny.

01:53:26.633 --> 01:53:27.534
And actually, now that I think about it,

01:53:27.534 --> 01:53:29.102
we've got patriotic sugar cookies.

01:53:29.102 --> 01:53:30.503
Could be in the cookie category.

01:53:30.503 --> 01:53:32.305
The packaging was different,
so I separated them,

01:53:32.305 --> 01:53:37.043
but I'm curious what a patriotic
sugar cookie looks like.

01:53:37.043 --> 01:53:38.778
Oh, no, nothing.

01:53:38.778 --> 01:53:41.581
These look like broken-up
Girl Scout cookies.

01:53:41.581 --> 01:53:43.149
Is that a rocket? What is that?

01:53:44.284 --> 01:53:46.019
I don't know what that is.
Do you know what that is?

01:53:49.756 --> 01:53:50.590
Joe: Ah!

01:53:50.590 --> 01:53:51.724
It's the Liberty Torch.
[eagle calling]

01:53:51.724 --> 01:53:52.792
Oh, it's a bunch of different ones,

01:53:52.792 --> 01:53:54.427
but they're all broken up.

01:53:54.427 --> 01:53:56.629
Oh, well. These are pretty good.

01:53:56.629 --> 01:53:58.631
Then I wanted to try
one of the cake options.

01:53:58.631 --> 01:53:59.465
It's actually probably the cinnamon

01:53:59.465 --> 01:54:00.500
that appeals to me the most, so.

01:54:00.500 --> 01:54:01.968
I mean, this looks better.

01:54:03.770 --> 01:54:06.072
It could be, again, slightly more moist,

01:54:06.072 --> 01:54:07.740
but it's got a pretty nice texture to it.

01:54:07.740 --> 01:54:09.375
There's definitely a
cinnamon flavor there.

01:54:09.375 --> 01:54:12.879
In terms of, like,
shelf-stable baked goods,

01:54:12.879 --> 01:54:14.514
this is a much better
effort than the brownie was.

01:54:14.514 --> 01:54:16.449
This is actually perfectly edible.

01:54:16.449 --> 01:54:18.318
I have an assortment of pound cakes.

01:54:18.318 --> 01:54:21.588
Applesauce poundcake, lemon
poppy seed pound cake,

01:54:21.588 --> 01:54:24.324
vanilla pound cake, marble pound cake.

01:54:25.191 --> 01:54:27.493
Cinnamon bun

01:54:27.493 --> 01:54:30.263
and a toaster pastry

01:54:30.263 --> 01:54:32.131
that just feels completely
destroyed in here.

01:54:32.131 --> 01:54:34.534
So, I don't know what toaster

01:54:34.534 --> 01:54:36.202
you're using out in the
field, but all right.

01:54:36.202 --> 01:54:38.905
And last, this package that
looks like all the other ones

01:54:38.905 --> 01:54:40.506
is a cherry blueberry cobbler.

01:54:40.506 --> 01:54:41.741
Eh, I think this'll just be fine.

01:54:41.741 --> 01:54:43.276
Oh, that's colorful.

01:54:43.276 --> 01:54:46.112
But it just looks like a
whole thing of, like, jelly.

01:54:46.112 --> 01:54:47.213
Every ration pack in the UK

01:54:47.213 --> 01:54:49.449
also comes with four
to five snack options.

01:54:49.449 --> 01:54:50.583
We've taken all of the ones we got

01:54:50.583 --> 01:54:52.518
from menus one to 10
and laid them out here.

01:54:52.518 --> 01:54:56.356
Snacks. Our US MREs come
with a lot of snacks.

01:54:56.356 --> 01:54:58.958
You usually find three or four of these

01:54:58.958 --> 01:55:02.762
in each MRE in various combinations.

01:55:02.762 --> 01:55:05.665
So, here we have a mixed-fruit snack,

01:55:05.665 --> 01:55:07.600
which is, like, a fruit puree

01:55:07.600 --> 01:55:09.369
in one of these handy little pouches.

01:55:09.369 --> 01:55:13.072
First of all, I mean,
how bright do these look,

01:55:13.072 --> 01:55:14.274
just compared to all the packs

01:55:14.274 --> 01:55:15.642
you've been dealing with this whole video?

01:55:15.642 --> 01:55:16.542
Like, I'm looking at these,

01:55:16.542 --> 01:55:19.245
I almost want to, like,
turn the saturation down.

01:55:19.245 --> 01:55:22.382
Peppermint candy rings,
and then you got M&amp;M's,

01:55:22.382 --> 01:55:24.217
Twizzler nibs, more M&amp;M's,

01:55:24.217 --> 01:55:26.920
Skittles, Skittles, Reese's Pieces.

01:55:26.920 --> 01:55:28.521
Very nice. Thank you.

01:55:28.521 --> 01:55:29.989
There are a few different peanut options

01:55:29.989 --> 01:55:31.324
on the menus in the UK,

01:55:31.324 --> 01:55:34.727
starting with barbecue
peanuts, some chili peanuts,

01:55:34.727 --> 01:55:36.562
and the classic salted peanuts.

01:55:36.562 --> 01:55:41.034
Dried fruit, dried cranberries, raisins,

01:55:41.034 --> 01:55:44.103
and a trail mix that has ...

01:55:44.103 --> 01:55:46.139
a nut raisin mix. For the trail.

01:55:46.139 --> 01:55:48.541
Here we have cheddar cheese flavor spread.

01:55:48.541 --> 01:55:49.709
In the UK, if you're having cheese,

01:55:49.709 --> 01:55:51.277
you have cheese and crackers.

01:55:51.277 --> 01:55:53.813
So I feel like maybe this
is supposed to be enjoyed

01:55:53.813 --> 01:55:55.648
with some of these crackers down this end.

01:55:55.648 --> 01:55:58.384
Down here, three different types
of energy bars you can get.

01:55:58.384 --> 01:56:00.954
I've never heard of
the brand First Strike.

01:56:00.954 --> 01:56:02.155
Not sure if that is a brand

01:56:02.155 --> 01:56:04.190
that exists outside the military,

01:56:04.190 --> 01:56:06.526
but you can get an apple cinnamon,

01:56:06.526 --> 01:56:11.464
chocolate, or cran-raspberry energy bar.

01:56:11.464 --> 01:56:13.066
Oh, geez, look at this thing.

01:56:15.168 --> 01:56:16.703
A bunch of junk.

01:56:18.104 --> 01:56:18.972
It tastes OK.

01:56:18.972 --> 01:56:21.040
Next, we have a fruit flapjack.

01:56:21.040 --> 01:56:22.942
Flapjack in the UK is a mixture

01:56:22.942 --> 01:56:26.012
of oats, syrup, butter, and often raisins,

01:56:26.012 --> 01:56:27.714
which are kind of packed together

01:56:27.714 --> 01:56:29.782
to make a nice nutritious sweet treat.

01:56:29.782 --> 01:56:33.119
This is an oatmeal block. I'm
not really sure what this is.

01:56:33.119 --> 01:56:34.287
I'm actually going to open this one

01:56:34.287 --> 01:56:36.222
just out of curiosity more than anything.

01:56:36.222 --> 01:56:39.792
Looks like a sort of shortbread
biscuit on the outside.

01:56:41.761 --> 01:56:44.097
Yeah, somewhere between
a digestive biscuit

01:56:44.097 --> 01:56:47.800
and a rusk, which, for the uninitiated,

01:56:47.800 --> 01:56:49.635
is I think an oaty biscuit

01:56:49.635 --> 01:56:51.304
that's basically made for babies.

01:56:51.304 --> 01:56:52.605
I feel like I used to steal rusks

01:56:52.605 --> 01:56:54.874
from my baby sister when she was little.

01:56:54.874 --> 01:56:55.708
Sorry, Annie.

01:56:55.708 --> 01:56:58.911
Trail mix, granola, nuts. You get it.

01:56:58.911 --> 01:57:01.180
Packages of either dry-roasted nuts.

01:57:01.180 --> 01:57:03.883
Man, they really sucked this
thing dry. Look at that.

01:57:03.883 --> 01:57:06.519
Same with this one, the smoked almonds

01:57:06.519 --> 01:57:09.322
and jalapeño cashews.

01:57:09.322 --> 01:57:12.759
Recovery trail mix with pretzels,

01:57:12.759 --> 01:57:14.861
granola,

01:57:14.861 --> 01:57:17.130
and this trail mix that
has beef jerky in it.

01:57:17.130 --> 01:57:18.598
Wanna mainline your jerky?

01:57:18.598 --> 01:57:20.933
US government has you covered.

01:57:20.933 --> 01:57:23.202
Beef sticks.

01:57:23.202 --> 01:57:24.904
Here we have a sachet of peanut butter.

01:57:24.904 --> 01:57:27.306
We have three different
types of jam that we found.

01:57:27.306 --> 01:57:30.309
We have apricot, we have plum,
and we have strawberry jam.

01:57:30.309 --> 01:57:33.146
This is an exotic-fruits nougat bar.

01:57:33.146 --> 01:57:34.380
Here we have a sesame bar,

01:57:34.380 --> 01:57:35.681
which is basically just sesame seeds

01:57:35.681 --> 01:57:37.383
held together with melted sugar.

01:57:37.383 --> 01:57:39.052
Apricot oat biscuits.

01:57:39.052 --> 01:57:41.254
A tropical fruit and nut mix,

01:57:41.254 --> 01:57:45.725
as well as just nuts and just fruit.

01:57:45.725 --> 01:57:47.927
Cheese-filled cracker, pepperoni flavor;

01:57:49.228 --> 01:57:51.197
cheese-filled pretzel, cheddar flavor;

01:57:52.465 --> 01:57:54.367
pretzel nuggets;

01:57:54.367 --> 01:57:55.535
barbecue corn nuggets;

01:57:55.535 --> 01:57:56.903
barbecue-flavored corn kernels.

01:57:56.903 --> 01:57:58.438
So like corn nuts, right?

01:57:58.438 --> 01:58:00.373
Baked snack crackers, cheddar flavor;

01:58:00.373 --> 01:58:04.343
and the protein puffs,
ring-shaped, barbecue.

01:58:04.343 --> 01:58:06.045
I feel like if you get these
ones, you've done quite well.

01:58:06.045 --> 01:58:09.248
These sound fancy. We have
chocolate orange mini cookies

01:58:09.248 --> 01:58:11.517
and coconut biscuit bites.

01:58:11.517 --> 01:58:13.319
These sound like a more gourmet approach

01:58:13.319 --> 01:58:15.488
to a ration-pack snack.

01:58:15.488 --> 01:58:18.291
On to the breads and
spreads, we'll call it.

01:58:18.291 --> 01:58:21.327
Multigrain bread, white wheat snack bread,

01:58:21.327 --> 01:58:22.161
wheat snack bread.

01:58:22.161 --> 01:58:23.563
I mean, how good are the
shots we have to get of these?

01:58:23.563 --> 01:58:24.397
Everything looks the same.

01:58:24.397 --> 01:58:26.432
And then this one, Italian
bread blobbity blob.

01:58:26.432 --> 01:58:27.733
Got it. OK, great.

01:58:27.733 --> 01:58:30.770
You have a vegetable cracker
or a regular cracker.

01:58:30.770 --> 01:58:32.405
What do you want to put on
those breads or crackers?

01:58:32.405 --> 01:58:35.308
You got chunky peanut
butter, peanut butter,

01:58:35.308 --> 01:58:37.210
chocolate peanut spread,

01:58:37.210 --> 01:58:39.479
cheese bread, cheese
spread with jalapeños,

01:58:40.446 --> 01:58:41.781
cheese spread with bacon.

01:58:41.781 --> 01:58:43.583
Here we have cheese and oat biscuits,

01:58:43.583 --> 01:58:45.051
which I think are probably
designed, like I say,

01:58:45.051 --> 01:58:46.552
to be eaten with the cheesy spread.

01:58:46.552 --> 01:58:48.354
Next up, our biscuit browns.

01:58:48.354 --> 01:58:50.123
I wasn't sure what a biscuit brown was,

01:58:50.123 --> 01:58:52.859
and after tasting it, I'm
still kind of equally unsure.

01:58:52.859 --> 01:58:54.360
It's, like, a sort of brown, like,

01:58:54.360 --> 01:58:56.629
wheaty cracker-type thing.

01:58:56.629 --> 01:59:00.666
For the fruit, you got
apple jelly, strawberry jam,

01:59:00.666 --> 01:59:02.268
and I know there was a
grape jelly from earlier.

01:59:02.268 --> 01:59:03.102
I don't know where it went.

01:59:03.102 --> 01:59:05.238
But it looks exactly like this package.

01:59:05.238 --> 01:59:07.907
And finally, we have a
cookies and cream bar.

01:59:07.907 --> 01:59:10.009
[laughs] That's not what
I was expecting at all.

01:59:10.009 --> 01:59:11.711
When you hear cookies and cream,

01:59:11.711 --> 01:59:13.713
I'm right in thinking that
you expect Oreo, right?

01:59:13.713 --> 01:59:17.750
Whereas this is more like a
rice crispy type of treat.

01:59:17.750 --> 01:59:19.552
Doesn't smell of Oreo at all.

01:59:19.552 --> 01:59:21.921
It literally smells of
a rice crispy treat,

01:59:21.921 --> 01:59:23.589
maybe dark chocolate.

01:59:23.589 --> 01:59:24.790
Doesn't taste great either.

01:59:24.790 --> 01:59:27.693
The US MREs have an assortment
of different drinks,

01:59:27.693 --> 01:59:30.863
both I guess regular temperature and warm.

01:59:30.863 --> 01:59:32.598
So, the first thing I want
to do before we get into that

01:59:32.598 --> 01:59:34.667
is point out that we have four different,

01:59:34.667 --> 01:59:36.669
in the middle here, coffee drinks,

01:59:36.669 --> 01:59:38.905
hot drinks, as you will have it.

01:59:38.905 --> 01:59:41.340
This is a French vanilla cappuccino. Mm.

01:59:41.340 --> 01:59:43.342
And I want to make it real quick.

01:59:43.342 --> 01:59:45.378
And we saw how we heated
up the drinks before, so.

01:59:45.378 --> 01:59:47.313
Here it also comes with
a hot beverage bag.

01:59:47.313 --> 01:59:48.681
Pre-added the water.

01:59:48.681 --> 01:59:51.551
So now I'm going to go ahead and put

01:59:53.152 --> 01:59:54.654
powder in here.

01:59:55.721 --> 01:59:57.957
This looks all right.
Mix it up a little bit.

01:59:58.958 --> 02:00:02.595
♪ I have me cappuccino ♪

02:00:06.432 --> 02:00:08.301
It smells like when the
VCR gets really hot,

02:00:08.301 --> 02:00:10.036
when you watch, like,
three movies in a row.

02:00:10.036 --> 02:00:11.571
Now we're on to the drink
options, which, again,

02:00:11.571 --> 02:00:14.540
I think you get four of in a
24-hour British ration pack.

02:00:14.540 --> 02:00:16.042
These all come in the form of powders,

02:00:16.042 --> 02:00:17.843
which you mix with water and then consume.

02:00:17.843 --> 02:00:19.679
They're also split into
four different categories.

02:00:19.679 --> 02:00:21.981
We have just a standard drink powder,

02:00:21.981 --> 02:00:23.749
we have an energy drink powder,

02:00:23.749 --> 02:00:25.651
a fortified energy drink powder,

02:00:25.651 --> 02:00:28.120
and we have an isotonic drink powder.

02:00:28.120 --> 02:00:30.256
Having quickly scanned the
ingredients of both of these,

02:00:30.256 --> 02:00:31.824
I'm actually not sure what
the difference is between,

02:00:31.824 --> 02:00:33.492
say, an energy drink powder

02:00:33.492 --> 02:00:35.728
and a standard fruit-flavor powder.

02:00:35.728 --> 02:00:38.331
There's no caffeine in the
energy powders, for example.

02:00:38.331 --> 02:00:39.599
I think most of the energy is coming from

02:00:39.599 --> 02:00:41.100
the sugar content in these.

02:00:41.100 --> 02:00:45.605
While that's cooking, let's
see what other drinks we have.

02:00:45.605 --> 02:00:47.873
We got orange, lemon-lime, and grape.

02:00:47.873 --> 02:00:51.544
That's definitely not grape
colored. It says grape on here.

02:00:53.246 --> 02:00:55.314
I think it tastes like grape,
but also because it's blue,

02:00:55.314 --> 02:00:56.782
I'm thinking blue raspberry.

02:00:56.782 --> 02:00:58.484
While my coffee is cooking,

02:00:58.484 --> 02:01:00.653
I also have different coffee flavors.

02:01:00.653 --> 02:01:04.924
I get a chocolate hazelnut protein drink,

02:01:04.924 --> 02:01:08.227
Irish cream cappuccino,
chocolate protein drink.

02:01:08.227 --> 02:01:10.029
Regardless of the type of drink
powder that you're getting,

02:01:10.029 --> 02:01:12.265
they do come in a bunch of
different flavor options.

02:01:12.265 --> 02:01:15.067
I'm not sure if any of these
particularly appeal to me.

02:01:15.067 --> 02:01:16.135
I'll try the tutti-frutti one.

02:01:16.135 --> 02:01:18.671
I feel like of all of
these, sounds pretty fun.

02:01:18.671 --> 02:01:20.373
Yeah, I was really hoping
that the color would be, like,

02:01:20.373 --> 02:01:24.043
a bright blue or pink or
something, but just normal.

02:01:26.412 --> 02:01:28.547
It's unlocking some kind of, like,

02:01:28.547 --> 02:01:31.083
repressed alcohol memory, I think.

02:01:31.083 --> 02:01:34.186
I'm fairly sure I've drunk a cocktail

02:01:34.186 --> 02:01:36.689
to the point of oblivion that
tasted pretty much like this.

02:01:36.689 --> 02:01:41.527
So, this is my French vanilla coffee.

02:01:47.033 --> 02:01:47.867
Oh, yeah.

02:01:47.867 --> 02:01:50.569
I'm not recognizing a lot of these words,

02:01:50.569 --> 02:01:51.771
but yeah, this is nice.

02:01:51.771 --> 02:01:56.242
And the last thing we have
here are beverage base powders.

02:01:56.242 --> 02:01:59.278
So, these had electrolytes in them.

02:01:59.278 --> 02:02:00.780
It's like energy drinks almost.

02:02:00.780 --> 02:02:02.848
And then these, I think, are just,

02:02:02.848 --> 02:02:04.483
these have lots of sugar in them.

02:02:04.483 --> 02:02:09.221
So, we have lemon-lime,
orange, and tropical punch,

02:02:09.221 --> 02:02:10.990
and then this little stick,

02:02:10.990 --> 02:02:13.259
beverage base, orange type three.

02:02:13.259 --> 02:02:14.960
Finally, every ration pack in the UK

02:02:14.960 --> 02:02:16.996
also contains a bag of common items.

02:02:19.732 --> 02:02:22.368
We'll start down this end,
where we have some matches.

02:02:22.368 --> 02:02:25.137
Then we have some
water-purification tablets.

02:02:25.137 --> 02:02:27.206
I think there are six of
these in here, actually.

02:02:27.206 --> 02:02:28.140
And according to this,

02:02:28.140 --> 02:02:30.509
you can purify a liter
of water per tablet.

02:02:30.509 --> 02:02:31.344
That's pretty good.

02:02:31.344 --> 02:02:33.279
I like that this is in
the essential-items thing.

02:02:33.279 --> 02:02:34.413
We have some hot sauce.

02:02:34.413 --> 02:02:36.349
We have some tissues,

02:02:36.349 --> 02:02:39.285
as well as two alcohol-free hand wipes.

02:02:39.285 --> 02:02:42.722
Then you have two tea bags,
two sachets of instant coffee,

02:02:42.722 --> 02:02:45.358
four sachets of beverage whitener.

02:02:45.358 --> 02:02:47.593
I think, again, this might be
something along the lines of

02:02:47.593 --> 02:02:49.695
they can't legally call
this powdered milk,

02:02:49.695 --> 02:02:51.997
but it is effectively a
powdered-milk substitute.

02:02:51.997 --> 02:02:54.400
And four sachets of sugar.

02:02:54.400 --> 02:02:57.370
Our US MREs have these
following accessories.

02:02:57.370 --> 02:02:58.404
First of all, over here,

02:02:58.404 --> 02:03:02.975
you've seen it a few times this
episode, the heating thing,

02:03:02.975 --> 02:03:05.578
the heating bags to heat up
your food and your drinks.

02:03:05.578 --> 02:03:07.446
Saw this the whole time, the spoon.

02:03:07.446 --> 02:03:09.849
Every MRE comes with a spoon.

02:03:09.849 --> 02:03:12.651
Now, did you notice I had a difficult time

02:03:12.651 --> 02:03:14.453
kind of cutting into some
stuff? And I thought,

02:03:14.453 --> 02:03:16.288
why don't they have anything
like a knife in here?

02:03:16.288 --> 02:03:17.189
Of course they have a knife.

02:03:17.189 --> 02:03:19.625
They're soldiers. They have pocket knives.

02:03:19.625 --> 02:03:22.762
They come with a
concentrated beverage base.

02:03:22.762 --> 02:03:25.064
This one is cranberry grape.

02:03:25.064 --> 02:03:27.500
Over here is the coffee
zone, for the most part.

02:03:27.500 --> 02:03:30.035
Bill's Brew freeze-dried coffee.

02:03:30.035 --> 02:03:32.271
You can add it to hot or cold water.

02:03:32.271 --> 02:03:33.572
6 ounces right here.

02:03:33.572 --> 02:03:36.075
And you have your choice
of either Splenda or sugar.

02:03:36.075 --> 02:03:39.111
Salt, if you need more
sodium on your food.

02:03:39.111 --> 02:03:40.746
This is a coffee creamer.

02:03:40.746 --> 02:03:42.982
Each pack of accessories
comes with two pieces of gum.

02:03:42.982 --> 02:03:45.117
These are waterproof matches.

02:03:45.117 --> 02:03:47.586
Spice up that food a little
bit, a little bit of Tabasco.

02:03:47.586 --> 02:03:51.624
A little Tabasco guy. Look
at this little guy. Right?

02:03:51.624 --> 02:03:55.194
A moist towelette and regular napkins.

02:03:58.531 --> 02:04:01.534
In the US, MREs were first
introduced in the 1980s

02:04:01.534 --> 02:04:03.769
and were designed to be
lightweight, portable,

02:04:03.769 --> 02:04:05.004
and self-contained meals

02:04:05.004 --> 02:04:07.239
that didn't require
cooking or refrigeration.

02:04:07.239 --> 02:04:10.276
They were also intended to
provide the necessary nutrition

02:04:10.276 --> 02:04:11.610
for soldiers in the field.

02:04:11.610 --> 02:04:13.212
Early iterations of British ration packs

02:04:13.212 --> 02:04:14.513
actually used cans.

02:04:14.513 --> 02:04:15.948
It wasn't until the 1990s

02:04:15.948 --> 02:04:17.483
that these were replaced with pouches.

02:04:17.483 --> 02:04:21.387
Each MRE typically contains
around 1,200 to 1,300 calories

02:04:21.387 --> 02:04:24.890
with balanced nutrition essential
to supporting the demands

02:04:24.890 --> 02:04:27.493
placed on soldiers during
military operations.

02:04:27.493 --> 02:04:29.328
Each MRE is said to have approximately

02:04:29.328 --> 02:04:31.263
170 grams of carbohydrates,

02:04:31.263 --> 02:04:34.333
45 grams of protein, and 50 grams of fat.

02:04:34.333 --> 02:04:36.669
As we've said, a British
ration pack is usually designed

02:04:36.669 --> 02:04:38.838
for 24 hours' worth of nutrition,

02:04:38.838 --> 02:04:40.906
whereas it seems as if
the American ones are not.

02:04:40.906 --> 02:04:43.342
We can assume this because
the average nutritional value

02:04:43.342 --> 02:04:44.844
of an entire British ration pack

02:04:44.844 --> 02:04:48.347
is somewhere between
3,800 and 4,200 calories.

02:04:48.347 --> 02:04:50.149
Now, here's a chili meal from before.

02:04:50.149 --> 02:04:52.151
We're going to go through
its specific nutrition.

02:04:52.151 --> 02:04:52.985
Now, I want to point out

02:04:52.985 --> 02:04:55.321
that the bag didn't have anything on it,

02:04:55.321 --> 02:04:59.959
so I am learning about the
nutrition of these in real time.

02:04:59.959 --> 02:05:02.628
The chili with the
beans, one serving size.

02:05:02.628 --> 02:05:05.231
All this is 260 calories.

02:05:05.231 --> 02:05:07.199
And this single serving of cornbread

02:05:07.199 --> 02:05:10.636
with your chili, also 260 calories.

02:05:10.636 --> 02:05:13.572
Your vegetable cracker is
going to be 170 calories,

02:05:13.572 --> 02:05:17.810
and your cheesy spread is 120 calories.

02:05:17.810 --> 02:05:20.579
Then the cheese-filled crackers,
the pepperoni pizza flavor,

02:05:20.579 --> 02:05:23.182
240 calories for this one.

02:05:23.182 --> 02:05:24.884
And if you're going to be sipping on your

02:05:24.884 --> 02:05:28.220
lemon-lime-flavored non-fruit-juice juice,

02:05:28.220 --> 02:05:31.257
that's going to be 130 calories
for the whole beverage.

02:05:31.257 --> 02:05:33.826
A grand total of this many calories.

02:05:33.826 --> 02:05:35.961
We've selected some items
which seem to match up roughly

02:05:35.961 --> 02:05:38.230
with what you'd expect
to find in a US MRE.

02:05:38.230 --> 02:05:40.366
Here we have the chili con carne main.

02:05:40.366 --> 02:05:42.501
We've got some crackers
with the cheese spread,

02:05:42.501 --> 02:05:45.671
some peanut butter, and
a fruit-flavored drink.

02:05:45.671 --> 02:05:49.241
The total calories for
these items comes to 1,379.

02:05:49.241 --> 02:05:50.876
The FDA recommends that adults consume

02:05:50.876 --> 02:05:54.246
around 75 to 90 milligrams
of vitamin C per day.

02:05:54.246 --> 02:05:55.514
Just two of these drink sachets

02:05:55.514 --> 02:05:57.583
is more than enough
vitamin C for that intake.

02:05:57.583 --> 02:05:58.918
We are not getting scurvy anymore.

02:05:58.918 --> 02:06:00.686
We learned our lessons
back in the navy days.

02:06:00.686 --> 02:06:01.687
Although it's also worth noting

02:06:01.687 --> 02:06:03.255
the amount of sugar
that is in one of these.

02:06:03.255 --> 02:06:06.759
In a 45-gram sachet of mango
and passion fruit drink,

02:06:06.759 --> 02:06:09.695
you will find 40.5 grams of sugar.

02:06:09.695 --> 02:06:12.131
That's 81% of your daily
recommended intake,

02:06:12.131 --> 02:06:13.232
just in one of these.

02:06:15.467 --> 02:06:16.468
Let's take a look at the ingredients

02:06:16.468 --> 02:06:18.337
of the chili con carne in the UK.

02:06:18.337 --> 02:06:22.942
Beef, red kidney beans,
tomato puree, water, onion,

02:06:22.942 --> 02:06:26.178
red and green peppers, wheat
flour, calcium carbonate,

02:06:26.178 --> 02:06:29.915
iron, thiamine, and nicotinamide,
modified maize starch,

02:06:29.915 --> 02:06:32.518
salt, spices, spice extracts,

02:06:32.518 --> 02:06:34.553
calcium carbonate, and smoke flavor.

02:06:34.553 --> 02:06:36.722
The ingredients in here are
actually all pretty natural,

02:06:36.722 --> 02:06:38.824
without any real artificial preservatives.

02:06:38.824 --> 02:06:39.892
Our eyebrows were raised

02:06:39.892 --> 02:06:42.127
when we saw nicotinamide
in the wheat flour.

02:06:42.127 --> 02:06:43.762
Thankfully, it's not
some kind of nicotine,

02:06:43.762 --> 02:06:46.932
it's just another type of
vitamin B3, aka niacin.

02:06:46.932 --> 02:06:50.469
This chili with beans contains all this.

02:06:50.469 --> 02:06:52.304
Looks like mostly normal stuff

02:06:52.304 --> 02:06:53.872
until you get the bottom
of the list there.

02:06:53.872 --> 02:06:57.743
Ethoxylated mono- diglycerides,
I have no idea what that is.

02:06:57.743 --> 02:06:59.311
I don't think we've ever had
that in "Food Wars" before.

02:06:59.311 --> 02:07:01.947
So if someone in editing can go ahead

02:07:01.947 --> 02:07:04.083
and say what it's used for ...

02:07:04.083 --> 02:07:06.552
These have a minimum
shelf life of three years,

02:07:06.552 --> 02:07:08.454
depending on storage conditions.

02:07:08.454 --> 02:07:10.155
The storage conditions for these MREs

02:07:10.155 --> 02:07:12.891
I'm assuming were room
temperature the whole time,

02:07:12.891 --> 02:07:17.429
so I'm feeling pretty confident
these are all not expired.

02:07:17.429 --> 02:07:19.965
Similarly, ours have a shelf
life of around three years,

02:07:19.965 --> 02:07:21.533
and after that, it's
down to the temperature

02:07:21.533 --> 02:07:22.434
at which they're stored.

02:07:22.434 --> 02:07:25.371
For example, this chili
was made in December 2021

02:07:25.371 --> 02:07:28.374
and apparently is shelf
stable until around 2025.

02:07:28.374 --> 02:07:29.708
"But how can these meals have

02:07:29.708 --> 02:07:32.244
such a long shelf life?" you ask.

02:07:32.244 --> 02:07:33.479
According to the USDA,

02:07:33.479 --> 02:07:36.448
MREs are shelf stable
because they have been

02:07:36.448 --> 02:07:39.184
commercially sterilized by heat

02:07:39.184 --> 02:07:41.387
in a sealed container to destroy bacteria

02:07:41.387 --> 02:07:44.089
that can make it unsafe or spoil the food.

02:07:44.089 --> 02:07:46.191
Like food in metal cans,

02:07:46.191 --> 02:07:50.062
MREs can be kept for a long
time, but not indefinitely.

02:07:50.062 --> 02:07:51.864
The shelf life of MREs and ration packs

02:07:51.864 --> 02:07:52.931
is pretty directly related

02:07:52.931 --> 02:07:54.800
to the temperature at
which they're stored.

02:07:54.800 --> 02:07:56.101
The cooler the storage temperature,

02:07:56.101 --> 02:07:57.202
the longer they'll last.

02:07:57.202 --> 02:07:58.570
Rations in a desert temperature

02:07:58.570 --> 02:08:00.005
might only last a few months,

02:08:00.005 --> 02:08:02.474
whereas rations at room
temperature or colder climates

02:08:02.474 --> 02:08:04.376
can be good up to five years.

02:08:04.376 --> 02:08:06.378
We're filming this
towards the end of 2023,

02:08:06.378 --> 02:08:08.280
which means that our chili
con carne, for example,

02:08:08.280 --> 02:08:10.215
is already around two years old.

02:08:10.215 --> 02:08:12.284
I've gotta say, I don't feel
great after eating them.

02:08:12.284 --> 02:08:14.019
However, I do think
that's probably more due

02:08:14.019 --> 02:08:15.421
to the quantity that I've eaten

02:08:15.421 --> 02:08:18.290
rather than due to the
storage quality of these.

02:08:18.290 --> 02:08:21.193
Since 1981, the US menu has
changed about five times,

02:08:21.193 --> 02:08:22.695
improving the quality and the taste.

02:08:22.695 --> 02:08:25.064
It also has expanded the menu variety

02:08:25.064 --> 02:08:28.701
and now includes vegetarian,
kosher, and halal options.

02:08:28.701 --> 02:08:30.502
There also aren't many changes in the UK.

02:08:30.502 --> 02:08:32.771
However, there was a recent
controversial change.

02:08:32.771 --> 02:08:34.440
Chocolate bars were taken
away from British troops

02:08:34.440 --> 02:08:36.008
serving in Iraq and Afghanistan

02:08:36.008 --> 02:08:37.743
because they kept melting
in the desert heat.

02:08:37.743 --> 02:08:40.612
They were replaced with the
much less popular peanut butter.

02:08:40.612 --> 02:08:42.247
I can see how people would
be annoyed about that.

02:08:42.581 --> 02:08:44.650
Harry: From portion sizes
to exclusive flavors,

02:08:44.650 --> 02:08:46.118
we wanted to find out all the differences

02:08:46.118 --> 02:08:48.654
between Prime in the US and the UK.

02:08:48.654 --> 02:08:49.955
This is "Food Wars."

02:08:53.325 --> 02:08:54.727
Let's start with Prime hydration.

02:08:54.727 --> 02:08:57.229
In the UK, it comes in
a 500-milliliter bottle.

02:08:57.229 --> 02:08:58.964
You can either buy this as a single bottle

02:08:58.964 --> 02:09:00.699
or as part of a 12-pack.

02:09:00.699 --> 02:09:03.402
In the US, our hydration
also comes in one size,

02:09:03.402 --> 02:09:07.473
the 16.9-fluid-ounce
bottle, or 500 milliliters.

02:09:07.473 --> 02:09:09.508
You can get it in options of one bottle;

02:09:09.508 --> 02:09:11.844
an eight-pack, which
we did not get, sorry;

02:09:11.844 --> 02:09:16.115
the 12-pack; and a 15-pack variety.

02:09:16.115 --> 02:09:17.416
We also have Prime energy,

02:09:17.416 --> 02:09:20.819
which in the UK comes
in a 330-milliliter can.

02:09:20.819 --> 02:09:23.322
Just like Prime hydration, this
either comes as a single can

02:09:23.322 --> 02:09:25.023
or as part of a 12-pack.

02:09:25.023 --> 02:09:28.360
In the US, Prime energy
is a 12-fluid-ounce can,

02:09:28.360 --> 02:09:30.262
or 355 milliliters,

02:09:30.262 --> 02:09:33.432
and it comes in a single can, the 12-pack,

02:09:33.432 --> 02:09:35.801
and a 24-pack.

02:09:35.801 --> 02:09:38.771
In the US, our biggest
Prime hydration single item

02:09:38.771 --> 02:09:42.274
by volume is this, the
15-drink variety pack.

02:09:42.274 --> 02:09:46.678
Total fluid volume, 253.6 fluid ounces,

02:09:46.678 --> 02:09:49.448
or 7,500 milliliters.

02:09:49.448 --> 02:09:51.316
Our largest size is a 12-pack of bottles,

02:09:51.316 --> 02:09:53.418
which adds up to 6 liters of Prime,

02:09:53.418 --> 02:09:56.555
but that's still 20% less than
the biggest size in America.

02:09:56.555 --> 02:09:57.790
And if you're going energy drink,

02:09:57.790 --> 02:10:00.092
[grunts]

02:10:00.092 --> 02:10:03.529
24 cans of these energy drinks.

02:10:03.529 --> 02:10:05.564
This is 288 fluid ounces,

02:10:05.564 --> 02:10:08.934
or 8,520 milliliters.

02:10:08.934 --> 02:10:11.170
Our biggest Prime energy
item is, again, the 12-pack,

02:10:11.170 --> 02:10:12.037
but remember that our cans

02:10:12.037 --> 02:10:13.672
are slightly smaller than America's.

02:10:13.672 --> 02:10:15.307
A 12-pack of Prime energy in the UK

02:10:15.307 --> 02:10:18.110
adds up to 3,960 milliliters.

02:10:18.110 --> 02:10:21.613
That's 53.5% smaller than
the biggest size in America.

02:10:23.782 --> 02:10:27.820
Here are almost all of the
exclusive Prime products

02:10:27.820 --> 02:10:30.222
you can get in the US
you can't get in the UK.

02:10:30.222 --> 02:10:31.290
And here are all the Prime drinks

02:10:31.290 --> 02:10:33.258
you'll find in the UK but not in the US.

02:10:33.258 --> 02:10:35.327
There aren't any. Sorry, guys.

02:10:35.327 --> 02:10:37.229
There are currently seven
flavors of Prime hydration

02:10:37.229 --> 02:10:38.230
available in the UK.

02:10:38.230 --> 02:10:40.532
However, all of them are
available in the US as well,

02:10:40.532 --> 02:10:42.234
so none of them are actually exclusive.

02:10:42.234 --> 02:10:44.436
That said, there are some subtle
differences in ingredients

02:10:44.436 --> 02:10:45.671
when it's compared to the US,

02:10:45.671 --> 02:10:47.372
and also, I've never had Prime before,

02:10:47.372 --> 02:10:49.641
so I want to try all of
these, give you my thoughts,

02:10:49.641 --> 02:10:51.076
and maybe rank them as well.

02:10:51.076 --> 02:10:53.846
Here are currently three of the four

02:10:53.846 --> 02:10:56.281
Prime hydration drinks
you can get in the US

02:10:56.281 --> 02:10:57.449
you can't get in the UK.

02:10:57.449 --> 02:11:00.385
So we got the lemonade, we got orange,

02:11:00.385 --> 02:11:02.387
we have this LA Dodgers flavor

02:11:02.387 --> 02:11:04.857
that might be just
specific to Los Angeles,

02:11:04.857 --> 02:11:06.158
and there's a grape flavor,

02:11:06.158 --> 02:11:08.126
couldn't get my hands on. Wah.

02:11:08.126 --> 02:11:10.262
And here are all the other
flavors you can get in the US.

02:11:10.262 --> 02:11:11.196
We'll start down here.

02:11:11.196 --> 02:11:13.498
Lemon lime. I mean,
this, they're just going

02:11:13.498 --> 02:11:14.533
after Gatorade on this one.

02:11:14.533 --> 02:11:17.436
Wasn't that the first
Gatorade flavor, lemon lime?

02:11:17.436 --> 02:11:19.638
Really sweet. Wow, that's really sweet.

02:11:19.638 --> 02:11:21.640
Ice pop. But also, this looks like --

02:11:21.640 --> 02:11:22.474
they have different names,

02:11:22.474 --> 02:11:25.077
but I grew up calling them bomb pops.

02:11:25.077 --> 02:11:26.845
I feel like I'm only tasting
the cherry in this one.

02:11:26.845 --> 02:11:28.547
I think everyone who's
tried these has said it.

02:11:28.547 --> 02:11:29.681
These are very sweet.

02:11:29.681 --> 02:11:30.749
But, I mean, I'm kind of not surprised.

02:11:30.749 --> 02:11:32.351
I mean, it's marketed towards kids.

02:11:32.351 --> 02:11:33.518
Another thing I wanted to point out

02:11:33.518 --> 02:11:35.854
is that I was able to find ice pop flavors

02:11:35.854 --> 02:11:38.790
both in the UK version and the US version.

02:11:38.790 --> 02:11:39.925
We're going to get more into ingredients

02:11:39.925 --> 02:11:41.960
a little bit later on, but
I'll do a very quick taste

02:11:41.960 --> 02:11:44.162
to see if there's any notable differences.

02:11:44.162 --> 02:11:46.031
We'll start with the UK one.

02:11:46.031 --> 02:11:48.634
And then we'll try the American version.

02:11:48.634 --> 02:11:50.302
The flavors are broadly the same,

02:11:50.302 --> 02:11:53.605
but the way that this one
kind of hits your taste buds,

02:11:53.605 --> 02:11:55.173
it's very different,
and it's really weird.

02:11:55.173 --> 02:11:57.409
When you consume a drink,
obviously you want to feel

02:11:57.409 --> 02:11:58.710
like your thirst is being quenched.

02:12:00.178 --> 02:12:01.713
You take a sip, and
there's something in here

02:12:01.713 --> 02:12:04.483
that's actually kind of
drying my mouth out, almost.

02:12:04.483 --> 02:12:06.451
I think if I had to pick
which one to prefer,

02:12:06.451 --> 02:12:07.619
it is going to be the UK's,

02:12:07.619 --> 02:12:09.488
and I'm not just saying that from bias.

02:12:09.488 --> 02:12:11.290
Tropical punch. [grunts]

02:12:11.290 --> 02:12:12.824
OK, I'm not ...

02:12:12.824 --> 02:12:14.126
What the?

02:12:14.993 --> 02:12:15.961
[groans]

02:12:15.961 --> 02:12:17.429
All right, well, that's
pretty embarrassing.

02:12:17.429 --> 02:12:18.730
Hope nobody's watching this.

02:12:19.898 --> 02:12:21.133
I do like that.

02:12:21.133 --> 02:12:22.734
No kidding, they're getting
progressively better

02:12:22.734 --> 02:12:23.669
as we go through.

02:12:23.669 --> 02:12:25.170
Can't screw up orange.

02:12:25.170 --> 02:12:26.738
Mm.

02:12:26.738 --> 02:12:28.206
Yeah, this might be my favorite one.

02:12:28.206 --> 02:12:30.242
This is really good, and
this doesn't feel as sweet

02:12:30.242 --> 02:12:32.411
as the other ones. Or am
I just getting used to it?

02:12:32.411 --> 02:12:34.212
Like how your eyes adjust to the dark,

02:12:34.212 --> 02:12:36.682
I think my tongue's
adjusting to the sweetness.

02:12:36.682 --> 02:12:38.684
Of the many sports teams in the world

02:12:38.684 --> 02:12:40.686
that have some sort of deal with Prime,

02:12:40.686 --> 02:12:42.521
LA Dodgers is one of them.

02:12:42.521 --> 02:12:44.589
Yeah, I've seen people on eBay

02:12:44.589 --> 02:12:46.325
bidding a lot for these, so ...

02:12:47.192 --> 02:12:48.493
Straight up, I don't
know what flavor this is,

02:12:48.493 --> 02:12:49.728
but it's good. Do you want to try it?

02:12:49.728 --> 02:12:51.229
My lips did touch it. Is it too weird?

02:12:53.098 --> 02:12:54.700
We have a professional relationship.

02:12:57.002 --> 02:12:58.403
Oh no, I've gotten used to it!

02:12:59.338 --> 02:13:01.807
It's a good lemonade. As
far as lemon-flavored,

02:13:01.807 --> 02:13:03.909
not-carbonated beverage, pretty good.

02:13:03.909 --> 02:13:05.277
I was also able to get my hands on

02:13:05.277 --> 02:13:07.846
one of the American exclusives,
the lemonade flavor.

02:13:07.846 --> 02:13:09.848
I'm interested to try this
because the US and the UK

02:13:09.848 --> 02:13:12.117
do have slightly differing
ideas of what lemonade is.

02:13:12.117 --> 02:13:14.252
Obviously, in the UK,
we consider, generally,

02:13:14.252 --> 02:13:18.223
lemonade to be a carbonated,
clear beverage, like a Sprite,

02:13:18.223 --> 02:13:20.392
whereas in America, it's
usually just kind of

02:13:20.392 --> 02:13:23.328
lemon juice, sugar,
and water combined, so.

02:13:23.328 --> 02:13:25.263
It does taste a bit like you've let, like,

02:13:25.263 --> 02:13:27.332
a Sprite kind of go flat.

02:13:27.332 --> 02:13:29.568
You can probably keep this
one for the time being, guys.

02:13:29.568 --> 02:13:32.170
Glowberry. It is supposed
to glow in the dark.

02:13:32.170 --> 02:13:33.972
The label, not the beverage.

02:13:36.641 --> 02:13:39.011
What the hell? Yeah, I
think that's the worst one.

02:13:39.011 --> 02:13:40.012
That's really weird.

02:13:40.012 --> 02:13:41.613
Meta moon. What is the vibe of this?

02:13:41.613 --> 02:13:44.516
What about this says, like,
oh, that's so refreshing?

02:13:44.516 --> 02:13:47.786
This tastes the way sunblock smells.

02:13:47.786 --> 02:13:48.754
Strawberry watermelon, all right,

02:13:48.754 --> 02:13:50.722
back to things I've heard of.

02:13:50.722 --> 02:13:52.224
No, this tastes exactly like gum.

02:13:52.224 --> 02:13:54.559
Bubblicious, like one of
those really fruity ones,

02:13:54.559 --> 02:13:55.394
yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:13:55.394 --> 02:13:56.361
We're going to start down this end

02:13:56.361 --> 02:13:57.629
with the glowberry flavor.

02:13:57.629 --> 02:13:59.831
Now, I did have to Google
this to see if glowberries

02:13:59.831 --> 02:14:01.266
were an actual berry or not.

02:14:01.266 --> 02:14:02.934
As far as I can tell, they are not.

02:14:02.934 --> 02:14:05.871
I think it generally
refers to a berry flavor

02:14:05.871 --> 02:14:07.305
and also the fact that the bottle

02:14:07.305 --> 02:14:08.807
is supposed to glow in the dark.

02:14:08.807 --> 02:14:10.208
I think we should put that to the test.

02:14:10.208 --> 02:14:11.510
Charlie, hit the lights.

02:14:13.779 --> 02:14:15.747
[laughing]

02:14:15.747 --> 02:14:18.083
Not really glowing, is it? [laughs]

02:14:19.151 --> 02:14:20.452
It's my first taste of Prime,

02:14:20.452 --> 02:14:22.854
and it's not blowing my socks off, to say.

02:14:22.854 --> 02:14:25.690
It's just a very artificial berry flavor.

02:14:25.690 --> 02:14:26.825
Got a bit of tang to it.

02:14:26.825 --> 02:14:28.293
Next up we've got strawberry watermelon.

02:14:32.230 --> 02:14:33.865
I like that. The flavor I get from that

02:14:33.865 --> 02:14:36.701
is almost more like a kind
of blue-Powerade-type flavor.

02:14:36.701 --> 02:14:38.236
According to the label, the ice pop flavor

02:14:38.236 --> 02:14:40.739
is supposed to taste like
cherry, lime, and blue raspberry.

02:14:41.706 --> 02:14:43.275
Yeah, I mean, so far, the vibe I'm getting

02:14:43.275 --> 02:14:45.911
is that they all just
taste of artificial fruit,

02:14:45.911 --> 02:14:48.280
but I think in terms of,
like, balancing the flavors,

02:14:48.280 --> 02:14:49.881
this one's done a pretty good job so far.

02:14:49.881 --> 02:14:52.751
Maybe the most delicately
balanced flavor of the lot.

02:14:52.751 --> 02:14:54.619
Next up, we've got meta moon.

02:14:54.619 --> 02:14:56.721
Obviously, I don't know
what the moon tastes like,

02:14:56.721 --> 02:14:58.890
and I don't know what the meta
tastes like right now either.

02:14:58.890 --> 02:15:00.392
If there were a "Food Wars" meta,

02:15:00.392 --> 02:15:03.628
yellow No. 5 would be S-tier right now.

02:15:06.331 --> 02:15:08.500
Label just says this is candy-flavored,

02:15:08.500 --> 02:15:10.001
which doesn't fill me with confidence,

02:15:10.001 --> 02:15:11.837
because, like, which candy?

02:15:11.837 --> 02:15:13.271
Tastes like Mark Zuckerberg.

02:15:17.042 --> 02:15:18.376
No?

02:15:18.376 --> 02:15:20.078
Like, at least these ones have had, like,

02:15:20.078 --> 02:15:22.180
a fairly identifiable fruity flavor,

02:15:22.180 --> 02:15:25.317
whereas this one is just
kind of generic sweet thing.

02:15:25.317 --> 02:15:26.985
Then we're on to blue
raspberry, and one thing

02:15:26.985 --> 02:15:28.787
that's immediately stuck
out to me is the color.

02:15:28.787 --> 02:15:31.490
I think I was actually
expecting this to be

02:15:31.490 --> 02:15:33.792
a more bright blue color,

02:15:33.792 --> 02:15:36.061
but it's actually just kind of white.

02:15:37.028 --> 02:15:38.497
It's quite a gentle blue raspberry.

02:15:38.497 --> 02:15:40.899
I want a bit more acidity
from that, I think,

02:15:40.899 --> 02:15:42.701
but it goes down very smoothly.

02:15:42.701 --> 02:15:44.736
I do feel like I'm giving
Prime way too much credit here.

02:15:44.736 --> 02:15:47.806
I'm swirling it around in a
glass like I'm a sommelier.

02:15:50.275 --> 02:15:52.177
Maybe if we had a decanter
I could pour it out,

02:15:52.177 --> 02:15:53.345
let it breathe a little bit.

02:15:53.345 --> 02:15:54.179
I'm drinking too much Prime.

02:15:54.179 --> 02:15:55.480
I'm going to be hydrated as hell.

02:15:56.515 --> 02:15:57.883
I'm getting hopped up on Prime.

02:15:57.883 --> 02:16:00.118
I need to go do a celebrity
boxing match or something.

02:16:03.021 --> 02:16:04.089
Yeah? Joe, you down?

02:16:04.089 --> 02:16:07.159
You and me in the ring,
the full 12 rounds?

02:16:07.159 --> 02:16:08.226
I actually don't know who'd win that.

02:16:08.226 --> 02:16:09.628
Who'd win the fight between me and Joe?

02:16:10.795 --> 02:16:12.330
You back me? Charlie backs me.

02:16:12.330 --> 02:16:14.166
Harry's vibe is very, like,

02:16:14.166 --> 02:16:16.468
they're really always just
calm and reserved or whatever,

02:16:16.468 --> 02:16:19.471
but that sarcasm that they
have over there, to me,

02:16:19.471 --> 02:16:20.739
says that they're, like,
something's going on

02:16:20.739 --> 02:16:23.141
underneath the surface
that they're suppressing,

02:16:23.141 --> 02:16:24.409
so Harry strikes me as the type of guy

02:16:24.409 --> 02:16:27.112
that if he got into a fight-fight,

02:16:27.112 --> 02:16:29.648
he would just, like, black out,

02:16:29.648 --> 02:16:32.684
and then come to, like,
in the middle of a field

02:16:32.684 --> 02:16:35.353
at midnight with a
shovel, covered in blood,

02:16:35.353 --> 02:16:36.655
with a corpse in his trunk.

02:16:36.655 --> 02:16:38.957
I'll box him as long as it's supervised.

02:16:40.025 --> 02:16:43.261
Moving swiftly on from
me and Joe fighting.

02:16:43.261 --> 02:16:45.297
Next up, we've got tropical punch Prime.

02:16:46.932 --> 02:16:49.501
Whoo! So, while I was
out in the US recently,

02:16:49.501 --> 02:16:51.503
I was able to try Hawaiian Punch,

02:16:51.503 --> 02:16:53.738
and that's basically exactly
what I'm getting from this.

02:16:53.738 --> 02:16:56.808
And then finally, we've
got the lemon lime flavor.

02:16:56.808 --> 02:16:58.643
Of all of these flavors, this is the one

02:16:58.643 --> 02:17:01.313
that tastes the most
like it's supposed to.

02:17:01.313 --> 02:17:02.647
If I had to rank them, I think I'd do it

02:17:02.647 --> 02:17:03.582
in the following order.

02:17:03.582 --> 02:17:06.284
I think my least favorite,
probably meta moon,

02:17:06.284 --> 02:17:07.185
right in the middle here.

02:17:07.185 --> 02:17:08.887
Meta moon is down there.

02:17:08.887 --> 02:17:10.755
I think with ice pop,
they've just tried to combine

02:17:10.755 --> 02:17:12.190
a bunch of flavors that don't necessarily

02:17:12.190 --> 02:17:13.291
work together very well,

02:17:13.291 --> 02:17:15.060
so I think that's actually
going to be my second to last.

02:17:15.060 --> 02:17:17.529
I think blue raspberry is
going to come next for me,

02:17:17.529 --> 02:17:18.363
and they lose points because

02:17:18.363 --> 02:17:19.764
they didn't even try and color it.

02:17:19.764 --> 02:17:21.266
I think next is going to be glowberry.

02:17:21.266 --> 02:17:22.667
The bottle didn't really glow,

02:17:22.667 --> 02:17:24.603
so maybe leave it in
the sun for a bit longer

02:17:24.603 --> 02:17:25.737
if you're going to try that at home.

02:17:25.737 --> 02:17:27.472
I think next up, we're going
to have tropical punch.

02:17:27.472 --> 02:17:29.140
My No. 2 is lemon lime,

02:17:29.140 --> 02:17:31.810
which means that my No.
1, strawberry watermelon.

02:17:31.810 --> 02:17:32.911
My favorite's the orange.

02:17:32.911 --> 02:17:35.013
In second place, I like
the Dodgers flavor.

02:17:35.013 --> 02:17:36.982
Actually, now that I think about it,

02:17:36.982 --> 02:17:38.650
I think I'm going to go here,
yeah. The lemonade's good.

02:17:38.650 --> 02:17:40.585
And from here on, ice pop, eh.

02:17:40.585 --> 02:17:42.587
I mean, yeah, lemon
lime, these are all like,

02:17:42.587 --> 02:17:45.257
I'd grab any one of these.
These three, I'd say skip.

02:17:49.060 --> 02:17:50.262
A little bit of flavor tweaking

02:17:50.262 --> 02:17:51.563
or balancing might go a long way.

02:17:51.563 --> 02:17:53.732
I think with some of
these, you wanted maybe

02:17:53.732 --> 02:17:56.468
a bit more tang to them, bit more acidity,

02:17:56.468 --> 02:17:59.070
maybe dial back the sweetness
on one or two as well.

02:17:59.070 --> 02:18:01.106
I don't know if I would
necessarily pick a Prime

02:18:01.106 --> 02:18:03.541
over a classic energy
drink, like a Powerade,

02:18:03.541 --> 02:18:06.811
but I do think, in general,
if I wanted a sports drink

02:18:06.811 --> 02:18:08.747
and I went into a store
and this was all they had,

02:18:08.747 --> 02:18:10.181
I think I'd still be
happy to buy one then.

02:18:10.181 --> 02:18:12.350
They're all fine, and they
kind of do that same job.

02:18:12.350 --> 02:18:14.319
Next up, let's take a
look at Prime energy.

02:18:14.319 --> 02:18:15.754
Now, while the Prime hydration drinks

02:18:15.754 --> 02:18:17.656
are similar to maybe a Powerade,

02:18:17.656 --> 02:18:19.891
these are much more similar
to a classic energy drink

02:18:19.891 --> 02:18:21.026
like a Red Bull.

02:18:21.026 --> 02:18:22.661
There is one exclusive energy drink flavor

02:18:22.661 --> 02:18:24.162
in the US you can't get in the UK.

02:18:24.162 --> 02:18:27.599
It is the original. That
just was announced this week.

02:18:27.599 --> 02:18:29.601
Couldn't get it. Anyway,
here are the rest.

02:18:29.601 --> 02:18:31.269
Yes, the UK also has them.

02:18:31.269 --> 02:18:33.138
Most of them are similar
to the hydration flavors,

02:18:33.138 --> 02:18:35.473
but there is a new one, orange mango.

02:18:35.473 --> 02:18:37.008
I'm a bit apprehensive about trying these

02:18:37.008 --> 02:18:38.410
because I don't drink energy drinks.

02:18:38.410 --> 02:18:40.512
I don't even drink
coffee, I only drink tea,

02:18:40.512 --> 02:18:42.080
and I think one of these contains

02:18:42.080 --> 02:18:44.783
about as much caffeine
as three cups of tea, so.

02:18:44.783 --> 02:18:46.351
We'll start with ice pop.

02:18:46.351 --> 02:18:48.019
Chin-chin.

02:18:48.019 --> 02:18:49.721
I think that does mirror the flavor

02:18:49.721 --> 02:18:51.723
of the hydration drink pretty closely,

02:18:51.723 --> 02:18:53.224
but I will say it tastes
better than, like,

02:18:53.224 --> 02:18:54.326
a standard Red Bull flavor.

02:18:54.326 --> 02:18:55.794
Strawberry watermelon.

02:18:55.794 --> 02:18:57.195
I think there might be a theme here.

02:18:57.195 --> 02:18:59.264
They just kind of taste
like fizzy versions of that.

02:18:59.264 --> 02:19:02.167
Here's the new one, orange mango.

02:19:02.167 --> 02:19:04.536
I get mostly mango. I'm not
getting much orange from that.

02:19:04.536 --> 02:19:06.137
Yeah, not far off, like, a Rubicon.

02:19:06.137 --> 02:19:08.640
Tropical punch is up next.

02:19:08.640 --> 02:19:09.974
Yeah, I think that one's actually improved

02:19:09.974 --> 02:19:11.743
by becoming an energy drink.

02:19:11.743 --> 02:19:13.178
Lemon lime.

02:19:13.178 --> 02:19:16.147
You could give me a glass of
that and a glass of Sprite,

02:19:16.147 --> 02:19:18.116
and I'd struggle to tell
the difference, I think,

02:19:18.116 --> 02:19:20.151
because it is just,
again, like, a carbonated

02:19:20.151 --> 02:19:21.086
lemon and lime beverage.

02:19:21.086 --> 02:19:22.320
Blue raspberry.

02:19:23.855 --> 02:19:25.490
I actually think for a few of these,

02:19:25.490 --> 02:19:28.360
just carbonating them has
actually made them better.

02:19:28.360 --> 02:19:31.463
I think notably that was the
case with the tropical punch,

02:19:31.463 --> 02:19:33.398
and also to a degree, I
think, blue raspberry.

02:19:33.398 --> 02:19:35.133
One I could not get for
the time of filming this

02:19:35.133 --> 02:19:36.835
was the ice pop.

02:19:36.835 --> 02:19:38.203
Strawberry watermelon, though.

02:19:39.871 --> 02:19:41.239
It's, like, sweet and tart.

02:19:41.239 --> 02:19:42.407
Orange mango.

02:19:43.341 --> 02:19:44.743
Somehow worse. Uh-oh.

02:19:44.743 --> 02:19:45.610
So far, not good.

02:19:45.610 --> 02:19:46.878
Tropical punch, OK.

02:19:48.446 --> 02:19:49.848
Yeah, this one's good.

02:19:49.848 --> 02:19:52.517
Lemon lime, ooh.

02:19:52.517 --> 02:19:53.518
Yeah, it's pretty good, too.

02:19:53.518 --> 02:19:54.586
Kind of tastes like Sprite.
Harry: Like some Sprite.

02:19:54.586 --> 02:19:57.021
Blue raspberry, ooh.

02:19:57.021 --> 02:20:00.358
Nah. Yeah, this one also has
a tart flavor to it that,

02:20:00.358 --> 02:20:02.227
not that into it. So I think
I'm going to rank it like this.

02:20:02.227 --> 02:20:03.395
Favorite was lemon lime,

02:20:03.395 --> 02:20:05.130
really enjoyed the fruit punch,

02:20:05.130 --> 02:20:06.831
blue raspberry is in the middle,

02:20:06.831 --> 02:20:07.899
and then these two I wouldn't get.

02:20:07.899 --> 02:20:10.034
I guess, wow, apparently the
strawberry watermelon flavor,

02:20:10.034 --> 02:20:10.869
I'm just not digging.

02:20:10.869 --> 02:20:12.737
Now, for the orange mango,
which was the worst,

02:20:12.737 --> 02:20:15.907
something about it just
tastes like an armpit.

02:20:15.907 --> 02:20:17.475
Ice pop, did not like
that one, I'm afraid.

02:20:17.475 --> 02:20:20.378
Orange mango, fine, but
a bit too artificial,

02:20:20.378 --> 02:20:21.613
and there are, I think, better

02:20:21.613 --> 02:20:23.381
fizzy mango drinks out there in the UK.

02:20:23.381 --> 02:20:25.784
Tropical punch and blue
raspberry were both improved,

02:20:25.784 --> 02:20:27.252
I think, by making them fizzy,

02:20:27.252 --> 02:20:29.854
but I think just in terms
of, like, flavor balance,

02:20:29.854 --> 02:20:31.556
strawberry watermelon, undefeated.

02:20:35.260 --> 02:20:37.162
One thing I will say about Prime energy

02:20:37.162 --> 02:20:38.930
is that so far, when
I've been tasting these,

02:20:38.930 --> 02:20:40.832
I feel like on the very
rare occasions when I drink,

02:20:40.832 --> 02:20:42.700
like, a Red Bull or a Monster,

02:20:42.700 --> 02:20:44.369
they have kind of a very
distinct flavor to them,

02:20:44.369 --> 02:20:46.438
and even if you add flavorings to that,

02:20:46.438 --> 02:20:47.939
it's hard to mask it completely,

02:20:47.939 --> 02:20:50.308
whereas I don't really get a distinct

02:20:50.308 --> 02:20:52.944
energy-drink flavor
throughout all of these.

02:20:52.944 --> 02:20:54.546
Most of them actually
do just kind of taste

02:20:54.546 --> 02:20:56.414
like fizzy versions of
the hydration drink.

02:20:56.414 --> 02:20:57.649
I think it's actually quite impressive.

02:20:57.649 --> 02:21:01.119
I think you probably could
drink them almost as a soda.

02:21:01.119 --> 02:21:03.755
I don't know if that's a
good thing or a bad thing.

02:21:03.755 --> 02:21:05.523
I do think there's still
some room for improvement

02:21:05.523 --> 02:21:06.724
with the flavors themselves,

02:21:06.724 --> 02:21:08.560
I think particularly
down this end, of course,

02:21:08.560 --> 02:21:10.395
but in general, I think they've done

02:21:10.395 --> 02:21:11.596
a pretty good job with the flavors.

02:21:11.596 --> 02:21:13.565
One product that we have
exclusively here in the US

02:21:13.565 --> 02:21:17.402
are these Prime Hydration+ sticks.

02:21:17.402 --> 02:21:20.038
These are like the
hydration flavors we have,

02:21:20.038 --> 02:21:21.172
but in powder form,

02:21:21.172 --> 02:21:25.109
and I think you can get it in
almost every single flavor.

02:21:25.109 --> 02:21:27.579
I got two, four, six of the crowd.

02:21:27.579 --> 02:21:29.814
Got six sticks.

02:21:29.814 --> 02:21:30.882
OK.

02:21:31.950 --> 02:21:34.385
Mm-hmm, OK. I mean, it
looks lemon-limey too.

02:21:34.385 --> 02:21:35.987
I guess probably you're
supposed to shake it up,

02:21:35.987 --> 02:21:37.622
and that's why you do a
water bottle and not a cup,

02:21:37.622 --> 02:21:39.023
because look, it's all, like,

02:21:39.023 --> 02:21:40.558
globulating at the top here.

02:21:41.559 --> 02:21:42.760
Ah, very strong.

02:21:42.760 --> 02:21:45.330
I think I could have cut
it with more water, but.

02:21:45.330 --> 02:21:46.164
Oh, God.

02:21:47.565 --> 02:21:48.633
Yeah, it's pretty close.

02:21:48.633 --> 02:21:49.634
I mean, this is obviously stronger

02:21:49.634 --> 02:21:50.902
because there's not as much water.

02:21:50.902 --> 02:21:52.837
Sadly, the Prime Hydration+ sachets

02:21:52.837 --> 02:21:54.806
have not hit UK shelves just yet,

02:21:54.806 --> 02:21:56.274
although I can't say
I'm too mad about that.

02:21:56.274 --> 02:21:58.142
I feel like, just buy
the drink, it's fine.

02:22:00.278 --> 02:22:02.013
The price of Prime can vary wildly

02:22:02.013 --> 02:22:03.948
depending on where or
when you are looking.

02:22:03.948 --> 02:22:06.818
On the official UK website,
a 12-pack of Prime hydration

02:22:06.818 --> 02:22:09.153
sells for £24.99.

02:22:09.153 --> 02:22:11.523
That's around $31.82.

02:22:11.523 --> 02:22:13.157
The per-bottle cost on
the website, therefore,

02:22:13.157 --> 02:22:14.559
is just over £2.

02:22:14.559 --> 02:22:18.162
In the US, that same pack is $29.99,

02:22:18.162 --> 02:22:19.964
or £23.56,

02:22:19.964 --> 02:22:21.866
so, not much of a price difference.

02:22:21.866 --> 02:22:24.702
If you're not aware, Prime
is a brand of hydration

02:22:24.702 --> 02:22:26.971
and energy drinks promoted
by the influencers

02:22:26.971 --> 02:22:28.673
Logan Paul and KSI.

02:22:28.673 --> 02:22:31.409
The two have amassed
a huge fan base online

02:22:31.409 --> 02:22:33.811
through various ventures
on YouTube and podcasting,

02:22:33.811 --> 02:22:35.113
music, and even wrestling.

02:22:35.113 --> 02:22:37.181
Now, Prime is manufactured
by Congo Brands,

02:22:37.181 --> 02:22:39.317
a company based out of
Louisville, Kentucky.

02:22:39.317 --> 02:22:42.520
Paul and KSI are the primary
spokespeople for the brand.

02:22:42.520 --> 02:22:44.322
When Prime launched over a year ago,

02:22:44.322 --> 02:22:47.258
with KSI and Logan Paul
putting their celebrity weight

02:22:47.258 --> 02:22:50.695
behind the promotion of the
brand, it was a huge success.

02:22:50.695 --> 02:22:52.497
However, due to the
popularity of the drink,

02:22:52.497 --> 02:22:54.532
a huge reselling market has emerged.

02:22:54.532 --> 02:22:56.901
Prime came to the UK in June 2022

02:22:56.901 --> 02:22:58.436
and was an immediate smash hit.

02:22:58.436 --> 02:23:00.371
On release, Britain saw
teenagers queuing up

02:23:00.371 --> 02:23:02.240
outside supermarkets all morning

02:23:02.240 --> 02:23:03.474
to try and get their hands on some.

02:23:03.474 --> 02:23:05.910
Places like Asda sold out
so quickly that they had

02:23:05.910 --> 02:23:08.413
to implement a
three-bottle-per-customer limit.

02:23:08.413 --> 02:23:10.715
Convenience stores realized
they could buy Prime in bulk

02:23:10.715 --> 02:23:13.051
and flip individual
bottles for a huge markup.

02:23:13.051 --> 02:23:15.386
Some, such as the
TikTok-famous Wakey Wines,

02:23:15.386 --> 02:23:16.854
achieved infamy when they were caught

02:23:16.854 --> 02:23:20.224
selling bottles or cans
for as much as £100 each.

02:23:20.224 --> 02:23:23.194
KSI even had to step in at
one point and scold resellers,

02:23:23.194 --> 02:23:24.963
saying that it was "not ethical."

02:23:24.963 --> 02:23:26.097
It wasn't as bad over here.

02:23:26.097 --> 02:23:28.032
It did fly off the
shelves for a little bit,

02:23:28.032 --> 02:23:29.200
but I think it might've
had something to do with

02:23:29.200 --> 02:23:32.770
the resale value of these
online was pretty good.

02:23:32.770 --> 02:23:35.440
I've never seen anyone
drinking it, and, to be honest,

02:23:35.440 --> 02:23:38.876
I got everything at Target
and online kind of no problem.

02:23:38.876 --> 02:23:41.312
Now, currently, the LA Dodgers
limited-edition bottles

02:23:41.312 --> 02:23:42.647
are pretty pricey on eBay.

02:23:42.647 --> 02:23:44.449
I'm assuming, once again,
that has more to do

02:23:44.449 --> 02:23:46.284
with its scarcity than its taste.

02:23:46.284 --> 02:23:48.620
Even now as we film this, in October 2023,

02:23:48.620 --> 02:23:51.189
the Prime market hasn't exactly
calmed all the way down.

02:23:51.189 --> 02:23:52.390
For some of the more standard flavors,

02:23:52.390 --> 02:23:54.158
the supply has now caught up with demand,

02:23:54.158 --> 02:23:56.094
and you're able to find
them in most supermarkets

02:23:56.094 --> 02:23:57.495
for the standard retail price.

02:23:57.495 --> 02:23:59.030
For example, I was able to find the bottle

02:23:59.030 --> 02:24:01.733
of strawberry watermelon
in Tesco for just £2.

02:24:01.733 --> 02:24:03.668
However, for some of
the more exotic flavors,

02:24:03.668 --> 02:24:07.338
the only places you can find
them are specialist candy shops

02:24:07.338 --> 02:24:08.806
or niche resellers.

02:24:08.806 --> 02:24:10.775
The only place I could
find my bottle of glowberry

02:24:10.775 --> 02:24:13.111
was at one of those weird
American candy stores

02:24:13.111 --> 02:24:14.512
which plague Central London.

02:24:14.512 --> 02:24:17.515
This one bottle cost me £14.99.

02:24:17.515 --> 02:24:20.618
I wouldn't have paid £18
for a bottle of Prime,

02:24:20.618 --> 02:24:23.621
but you did expense it, so
you didn't pay for it either.

02:24:23.621 --> 02:24:25.723
What is the most I would realistically pay

02:24:25.723 --> 02:24:27.291
for a bottle of Prime hydration?

02:24:27.291 --> 02:24:28.326
I don't know, 5 bucks?

02:24:28.326 --> 02:24:30.294
You think 5 is high?
How much would you pay?

02:24:30.294 --> 02:24:32.664
I've just been informed $5 is too high.

02:24:32.664 --> 02:24:35.033
$1.50?! You can't get anything for $1.50.

02:24:35.033 --> 02:24:36.067
What are you talking about?

02:24:36.067 --> 02:24:40.538
Fine! I would pay as much
as Gatorade and Powerade

02:24:40.538 --> 02:24:42.073
and all the other ades are for this.

02:24:42.073 --> 02:24:43.641
It's hard to find empirical evidence

02:24:43.641 --> 02:24:45.243
as to whether or not
the craze around Prime

02:24:45.243 --> 02:24:47.612
has kind of died down, but
just from anecdotal evidence,

02:24:47.612 --> 02:24:48.880
I really don't think it has.

02:24:48.880 --> 02:24:50.948
Even when I was out buying
the Prime for this episode,

02:24:50.948 --> 02:24:53.317
I would see kids in the
stores just taking selfies

02:24:53.317 --> 02:24:55.386
with bottles of Prime
like it was a celebrity.

02:24:55.386 --> 02:24:57.321
I think due to the
scarcity and the demand,

02:24:57.321 --> 02:24:58.856
it's almost become like a status symbol

02:24:58.856 --> 02:25:00.391
among certain generations of kids.

02:25:00.391 --> 02:25:02.260
It's a race to see who
can get the new flavors

02:25:02.260 --> 02:25:03.728
of Prime the fastest.

02:25:03.728 --> 02:25:05.296
People will, I guess, empty them out

02:25:05.296 --> 02:25:06.731
and just use them as water bottles

02:25:06.731 --> 02:25:08.266
when they go to school, and again,

02:25:08.266 --> 02:25:10.435
it's just kind of a way
to flex on your peers.

02:25:10.435 --> 02:25:12.270
Besides converting their massive fan bases

02:25:12.270 --> 02:25:13.404
into loyal customers,

02:25:13.404 --> 02:25:15.273
KSI and Logan Paul have also been able

02:25:15.273 --> 02:25:17.475
to translate this into
commercial partnerships.

02:25:17.475 --> 02:25:20.244
Prime is now an official
drink supplier of the UFC,

02:25:20.244 --> 02:25:21.979
the LA Dodgers, and Arsenal,

02:25:21.979 --> 02:25:23.715
of which I believe KSI is a fan,

02:25:23.715 --> 02:25:25.750
and they're also the official
sports-drink partners

02:25:25.750 --> 02:25:28.720
of FC Barcelona and FC Bayern Munich.

02:25:28.720 --> 02:25:31.022
We've seen other influencers capitalize

02:25:31.022 --> 02:25:33.491
on their success in the
food-and-beverage space.

02:25:33.491 --> 02:25:35.326
MrBeast, of course, has Beast Burger

02:25:35.326 --> 02:25:37.261
and his chocolate bars, Feastables.

02:25:37.261 --> 02:25:40.398
Emma Chamberlain has her coffee
brand, Chamberlain Coffee.

02:25:40.398 --> 02:25:42.667
Now, according to Paul, in the first year,

02:25:42.667 --> 02:25:47.171
the brand has cleared $250
million in retail sales.

02:25:47.171 --> 02:25:48.005
Goodness.

02:25:49.874 --> 02:25:51.809
Here are all the ingredients
for a Prime hydration

02:25:51.809 --> 02:25:53.945
ice pop flavor in the UK.

02:25:53.945 --> 02:25:57.582
These are the US hydration
ice pop ingredients.

02:25:57.582 --> 02:25:59.350
The ingredients are
pretty similar overall.

02:25:59.350 --> 02:26:01.352
The UK has slightly more coconut water,

02:26:01.352 --> 02:26:03.788
10.5% compared to America's 10%,

02:26:03.788 --> 02:26:06.324
but ours is actually
reconstituted from a powder,

02:26:06.324 --> 02:26:08.126
whereas America's is from concentrate.

02:26:08.126 --> 02:26:11.295
Prime hydration in both
countries, both zero added sugar,

02:26:11.295 --> 02:26:14.432
but does contain artificial
sweeteners sucralose

02:26:14.432 --> 02:26:17.001
and acelflame, no, that's not it,

02:26:17.001 --> 02:26:20.304
acesulfame K, or ace K.

02:26:20.304 --> 02:26:23.307
According to the Center for
Science in the Public Interest,

02:26:23.307 --> 02:26:25.810
ace K raises health issues such as cancer,

02:26:25.810 --> 02:26:28.980
hormone disruption, and
potential pregnancy risks.

02:26:28.980 --> 02:26:31.115
Per Healthline, there is
also evidence suggesting

02:26:31.115 --> 02:26:33.618
that acetoacetamide, a
compound formed in the body

02:26:33.618 --> 02:26:35.153
during the breakdown of ace K,

02:26:35.153 --> 02:26:37.588
may result in thyroid
damage in lab animals.

02:26:37.588 --> 02:26:39.490
Now, ironically enough, the lack of sugar

02:26:39.490 --> 02:26:43.127
may be an issue for the very
purpose of Prime hydration.

02:26:43.127 --> 02:26:45.296
Electrolytes play a
crucial role in making sure

02:26:45.296 --> 02:26:46.697
your body functions properly.

02:26:46.697 --> 02:26:49.500
When you exercise or play hard, you sweat.

02:26:49.500 --> 02:26:50.701
You lose not just water,

02:26:50.701 --> 02:26:53.104
but you also lose important electrolytes.

02:26:53.104 --> 02:26:55.540
Electrolytes are key to hydration,

02:26:55.540 --> 02:26:57.375
and Prime does contain electrolytes

02:26:57.375 --> 02:26:59.143
like magnesium and potassium.

02:26:59.143 --> 02:27:01.479
But the low amount of
glucose may impair your body

02:27:01.479 --> 02:27:03.247
from properly absorbing the electrolytes,

02:27:03.247 --> 02:27:05.850
as glucose is useful in
transporting them to your cells.

02:27:05.850 --> 02:27:07.251
Typically, you don't need glucose

02:27:07.251 --> 02:27:09.187
to absorb electrolytes, but it does help.

02:27:09.187 --> 02:27:11.222
Prime energy is currently
in hot water with the media

02:27:11.222 --> 02:27:12.623
as well as the US government

02:27:12.623 --> 02:27:15.193
because of its perceived
excessive levels of caffeine.

02:27:15.193 --> 02:27:16.861
Here in the UK, our Prime energy cans

02:27:16.861 --> 02:27:19.096
contain 140 milligrams of caffeine.

02:27:19.096 --> 02:27:21.833
To put that into perspective,
a grande coffee from Starbucks

02:27:21.833 --> 02:27:23.701
contains around 136.

02:27:23.701 --> 02:27:25.136
Energy cans in the US, however,

02:27:25.136 --> 02:27:27.004
contain 200 milligrams of caffeine,

02:27:27.004 --> 02:27:30.241
which is a 42.9% increase from the UK can.

02:27:30.241 --> 02:27:31.976
Because of this, Prime
is currently experiencing

02:27:31.976 --> 02:27:33.511
a significant amount of backlash,

02:27:33.511 --> 02:27:35.813
resulting in some shops
actually pulling it from shelves

02:27:35.813 --> 02:27:37.548
in order to protect kids' health.

02:27:37.548 --> 02:27:39.817
In July of this year, Sen. Chuck Schumer

02:27:39.817 --> 02:27:42.286
called for an FDA probe of Prime

02:27:42.286 --> 02:27:44.589
and said it was a "cauldron of caffeine"

02:27:44.589 --> 02:27:46.924
that posed "serious
health concerns" for kids.

02:27:46.924 --> 02:27:49.794
One Prime energy contains
200 milligrams of caffeine,

02:27:49.794 --> 02:27:52.330
which is about as much
caffeine in a six-pack of Coke.

02:27:52.330 --> 02:27:55.266
Says it right there on
legally the smallest font

02:27:55.266 --> 02:27:57.001
you could put it in.

02:27:57.001 --> 02:27:58.469
However, looking at the caffeine amounts

02:27:58.469 --> 02:28:00.938
of some other drinks currently
on the market in the US

02:28:00.938 --> 02:28:02.406
paints a slightly different picture.

02:28:02.406 --> 02:28:04.408
Let's be clear: 200 milligrams in this can

02:28:04.408 --> 02:28:05.509
is a lot of caffeine.

02:28:05.509 --> 02:28:07.278
Pound for pound, it contains more caffeine

02:28:07.278 --> 02:28:09.180
than Monster Energy or Red Bull,

02:28:09.180 --> 02:28:11.816
but other American drinks,
such as Bang Energy

02:28:11.816 --> 02:28:14.919
or Celsius Heat, contain
300 milligrams per can.

02:28:14.919 --> 02:28:16.954
That's 50% more than Prime.

02:28:16.954 --> 02:28:18.155
We don't have these in the UK.

02:28:18.155 --> 02:28:20.191
I've never heard of
Bang or Celsius energy,

02:28:20.191 --> 02:28:21.726
and they sound terrifying.

02:28:21.726 --> 02:28:24.028
One distinction that Sen.
Schumer is highlighting

02:28:24.028 --> 02:28:26.097
is that Prime is aimed at a kids market,

02:28:26.097 --> 02:28:27.965
despite the fact that the can says,

02:28:27.965 --> 02:28:30.034
"Not for children under the age of 18."

02:28:30.034 --> 02:28:31.269
Now, if that is the case,

02:28:31.269 --> 02:28:33.371
Prime still isn't the biggest offender.

02:28:33.371 --> 02:28:36.340
G Fuel, a brand that appears
to also appeal to kids,

02:28:36.340 --> 02:28:38.075
specifically in the gaming space,

02:28:38.075 --> 02:28:42.113
has several drinks with
300 milligrams of caffeine.

02:28:42.113 --> 02:28:44.849
They got flavors like
rainbow sherbet, snow cone,

02:28:44.849 --> 02:28:48.185
hype sauce that contain
more caffeine than a Prime,

02:28:48.185 --> 02:28:50.888
as well as celebrity-branded
flavors from PewDiePie

02:28:50.888 --> 02:28:53.758
and someone or something
called One_Shot_Gurl.

02:28:53.758 --> 02:28:58.529
Both have 300 milligrams
of caffeine in the can.

02:28:58.529 --> 02:28:59.997
Where's the outrage for that?

02:29:02.266 --> 02:29:05.036
A bottle of ice pop
Prime hydration in the US

02:29:05.036 --> 02:29:06.971
contains all of this.

02:29:06.971 --> 02:29:09.240
In the UK, a bottle of
ice pop Prime hydration

02:29:09.240 --> 02:29:10.908
contains the following.

02:29:10.908 --> 02:29:13.110
So in the UK, our Prime
has slightly more calories

02:29:13.110 --> 02:29:14.378
but less sodium.

02:29:14.378 --> 02:29:17.081
If you grab yourself a Prime energy,

02:29:17.081 --> 02:29:18.549
I have the lemon lime flavor,

02:29:18.549 --> 02:29:20.551
it contains all of this.

02:29:20.551 --> 02:29:21.585
In the UK, the same drink,

02:29:21.585 --> 02:29:24.689
albeit in a slightly smaller
can, contains the following.

02:29:24.689 --> 02:29:26.457
So it's almost identical to America's,

02:29:26.457 --> 02:29:28.192
although we have a little bit more sodium.

02:29:28.225 --> 02:29:30.127
Harry: From exclusive
items to portion sizes,

02:29:30.127 --> 02:29:31.562
we wanted to find out all the differences

02:29:31.562 --> 02:29:34.231
between Chinese takeout
in the US and the UK.

02:29:34.231 --> 02:29:35.933
This is "Food Wars."

02:29:38.936 --> 02:29:40.104
Here are all the menu items you'll find

02:29:40.104 --> 02:29:43.441
at a UK Chinese takeaway that
you might not find in the US.

02:29:43.441 --> 02:29:47.244
Here's all of the Chinese
takeout we got at our local spot

02:29:47.244 --> 02:29:49.647
that you might not be
able to find in the UK.

02:29:49.647 --> 02:29:50.681
We'll start with the appetizers.

02:29:50.681 --> 02:29:52.583
In the UK, it's pretty common
when you order a Chinese

02:29:52.583 --> 02:29:54.785
to order a few starters,
and you kind of eat them

02:29:54.785 --> 02:29:55.853
alongside your meal.

02:29:55.853 --> 02:29:58.122
One thing to point out,
almost all of these are beige,

02:29:58.122 --> 02:29:59.523
and I think that's going
to be a bit of a theme

02:29:59.523 --> 02:30:00.725
as we go throughout this episode.

02:30:00.725 --> 02:30:01.926
A lot of British Chinese food

02:30:01.926 --> 02:30:04.328
is just kind of beige fried
stuff, and we love it.

02:30:04.328 --> 02:30:07.264
We'll start with an
exception to the beige rule,

02:30:07.264 --> 02:30:08.366
with crispy seaweed.

02:30:08.366 --> 02:30:09.800
It's actually got a misleading name,

02:30:09.800 --> 02:30:11.035
because this isn't seaweed.

02:30:11.035 --> 02:30:13.704
It's shredded cabbage
which is then deep fried.

02:30:13.704 --> 02:30:15.806
It's always aggressively salty.

02:30:15.806 --> 02:30:16.907
Sometimes you get a little bit

02:30:16.907 --> 02:30:19.310
of fish flakes sprinkled on top.

02:30:19.310 --> 02:30:21.545
I think this one is
basically just fried cabbage

02:30:21.545 --> 02:30:22.947
with some salt powder on it.

02:30:24.715 --> 02:30:26.550
When we have a Chinese takeaway at home,

02:30:26.550 --> 02:30:29.653
it would pretty much be the
only green thing on our plates.

02:30:29.653 --> 02:30:32.590
And here's what we got for
our exclusive appetizers,

02:30:32.590 --> 02:30:34.859
starting with the steamed dumplings.

02:30:37.261 --> 02:30:38.662
That's so good. I love a dumpling.

02:30:38.662 --> 02:30:41.766
Another pretty iconic starter
is a chicken satay skewer.

02:30:41.766 --> 02:30:44.301
They will take a piece of
chicken, put it on a skewer,

02:30:44.301 --> 02:30:46.270
grill it up in a kind of satay sauce,

02:30:46.270 --> 02:30:48.272
which is mostly peanuts,

02:30:48.272 --> 02:30:49.373
and they'll serve it to you there.

02:30:49.373 --> 02:30:51.142
We also have a bit of
additional satay sauce

02:30:51.142 --> 02:30:54.245
for dipping as well. These are great.

02:30:54.245 --> 02:30:55.980
They're always a little bit dry.

02:30:55.980 --> 02:30:57.114
I think that's why you
have to have the sauce

02:30:57.114 --> 02:30:58.282
on the side as well.

02:30:58.282 --> 02:31:00.851
Next we have the scallion pancake,

02:31:00.851 --> 02:31:03.354
which literally looks like
a cut-up little pancake.

02:31:03.354 --> 02:31:04.488
I'm going to take a taste.

02:31:05.856 --> 02:31:07.024
Yeah, that's really tasty.

02:31:07.024 --> 02:31:08.559
I want to dip this in something, though.

02:31:08.559 --> 02:31:10.327
I'm getting the urge to dip.

02:31:10.327 --> 02:31:12.263
When I think of a
British Chinese takeaway,

02:31:12.263 --> 02:31:14.598
this might be one of the main
dishes that comes to mind.

02:31:14.598 --> 02:31:16.734
These are sweet and sour chicken balls.

02:31:16.734 --> 02:31:17.735
So, the sweet and sour bit

02:31:17.735 --> 02:31:19.103
actually just refers to the sauce

02:31:19.103 --> 02:31:19.937
that it's served with.

02:31:19.937 --> 02:31:21.806
It's basically just a piece of chicken

02:31:21.806 --> 02:31:23.707
which is then battered and deep-fried.

02:31:26.577 --> 02:31:27.411
Mm-hmm.

02:31:27.411 --> 02:31:28.779
Now, these vary in quality a lot

02:31:28.779 --> 02:31:29.980
from restaurant to restaurant.

02:31:29.980 --> 02:31:30.948
If chicken balls aren't your thing,

02:31:30.948 --> 02:31:33.150
then we also have the
option of a prawn ball.

02:31:33.150 --> 02:31:34.385
Now, instead of a piece of chicken,

02:31:34.385 --> 02:31:37.154
they take an entire prawn,
batter it, and deep-fry it.

02:31:37.154 --> 02:31:38.489
Kind of like a tempura prawn,

02:31:38.489 --> 02:31:40.124
but I think a lot heavier on the batter.

02:31:40.124 --> 02:31:41.692
We've been locked in a debate here as to

02:31:41.692 --> 02:31:43.994
if there's any difference
between prawn and shrimp.

02:31:43.994 --> 02:31:46.063
It seems like sometimes
they're used interchangeably,

02:31:46.063 --> 02:31:48.065
but maybe they are
actually different species.

02:31:48.065 --> 02:31:49.366
I don't know if Nico,
you have any thoughts

02:31:49.366 --> 02:31:51.202
on the differences
between prawn and shrimp.

02:31:51.202 --> 02:31:54.371
There is actually a difference
between shrimp and prawns.

02:31:54.371 --> 02:31:56.240
It's not just linguistic.

02:31:56.240 --> 02:31:57.641
Shrimp are more common in the US

02:31:57.641 --> 02:32:00.244
and tend to be smaller
with a more savory flavor

02:32:00.244 --> 02:32:01.745
when compared to prawns.

02:32:01.745 --> 02:32:03.347
Charlie's saying that when
he's had it in the past,

02:32:03.347 --> 02:32:05.382
it's been like a sort of prawn paste

02:32:05.382 --> 02:32:07.451
mashed up into a ball, whereas these ones

02:32:07.451 --> 02:32:09.353
are just whole prawns inside some batter,

02:32:09.353 --> 02:32:11.255
so I feel like I've
got a better deal here.

02:32:11.255 --> 02:32:12.957
This is a fried wonton.

02:32:12.957 --> 02:32:14.959
It's so cute. It looks
like a little flower.

02:32:16.494 --> 02:32:18.295
That is so good and crunchy.

02:32:18.295 --> 02:32:20.431
Next up, I did want to take
a look at British wontons.

02:32:20.431 --> 02:32:21.699
I know you can get these in America,

02:32:21.699 --> 02:32:23.267
but I thought there might
be some differences.

02:32:23.267 --> 02:32:25.703
So, these ones are not
crispy. These are steamed.

02:32:25.703 --> 02:32:28.506
Pretty tasty. Not something
that I would commonly order.

02:32:28.506 --> 02:32:31.075
Moving on, we have the crab rangoons.

02:32:31.075 --> 02:32:33.911
These are kind of a meme
now for some reason.

02:32:33.911 --> 02:32:37.081
I don't know why. These
had a spike in popularity

02:32:37.081 --> 02:32:40.217
just in general, but this
is what they look like.

02:32:40.217 --> 02:32:43.587
Kind of similar to a wonton, but bigger.

02:32:43.587 --> 02:32:46.056
Let's crack this bad boy open.

02:32:46.056 --> 02:32:47.691
Oh!

02:32:47.691 --> 02:32:48.659
Gorj.

02:32:48.659 --> 02:32:50.961
The filling is just crab and cream cheese.

02:32:52.863 --> 02:32:54.565
I get it, I think.

02:32:54.565 --> 02:32:56.367
I get why it's so popular.

02:32:56.367 --> 02:32:57.368
This is a banger.

02:32:57.368 --> 02:33:00.271
When you talk about your
beige-food Mount Rushmore,

02:33:00.271 --> 02:33:01.605
this has got to be up there.

02:33:01.605 --> 02:33:03.307
This is sesame prawn toast.

02:33:03.307 --> 02:33:06.644
We have four beige foods
immortalized forever in stone.

02:33:06.644 --> 02:33:08.345
Now, if you order a full
English breakfast in the UK,

02:33:08.345 --> 02:33:10.314
you're likely to find
some fried bread on there.

02:33:10.314 --> 02:33:12.216
Basically, what Chinese
restaurants in the UK have done

02:33:12.216 --> 02:33:14.418
is elevate fried bread by putting a layer

02:33:14.418 --> 02:33:15.786
of prawn paste in the middle

02:33:15.786 --> 02:33:17.688
and then topping it with
loads of sesame seeds.

02:33:17.688 --> 02:33:20.524
These are delicious. I love these.

02:33:20.524 --> 02:33:22.593
Moving on, we have the spareribs.

02:33:22.593 --> 02:33:25.196
I have to admit, I've
never had a sparerib.

02:33:25.196 --> 02:33:27.965
It's not something I've ever ordered.

02:33:27.965 --> 02:33:29.733
These have bones!

02:33:29.733 --> 02:33:31.535
That's literally a bone.

02:33:31.535 --> 02:33:33.370
I thought I ordered
the boneless spareribs,

02:33:33.370 --> 02:33:34.972
but I guess I ordered the spareribs

02:33:34.972 --> 02:33:36.440
with the Peking duck sauce,

02:33:36.440 --> 02:33:37.975
and they look and smell amazing,

02:33:37.975 --> 02:33:40.144
so I am going to take a bite anyway.

02:33:40.144 --> 02:33:42.179
Yeah, that's literally so good.

02:33:42.179 --> 02:33:45.249
That is so good! Why don't I order ribs?

02:33:45.249 --> 02:33:46.450
That's so yummy.

02:33:46.450 --> 02:33:47.718
Now, I was shocked when I saw these

02:33:47.718 --> 02:33:49.253
as an exclusive in this episode,

02:33:49.253 --> 02:33:51.322
but our next one is prawn crackers.

02:33:51.322 --> 02:33:52.289
These are another thing that you'll see

02:33:52.289 --> 02:33:54.458
on pretty much every UK
Chinese takeaway order.

02:33:54.458 --> 02:33:55.526
Even if you don't order them,

02:33:55.526 --> 02:33:57.294
often they'll just throw
you in a bag for free.

02:33:57.294 --> 02:33:58.729
They're just great for mopping up

02:33:58.729 --> 02:33:59.797
bits of sauce or something.

02:33:59.797 --> 02:34:02.666
You can also dip them in
the sweet and sour sauce,

02:34:02.666 --> 02:34:03.867
and you kind of get this crackling.

02:34:06.971 --> 02:34:09.073
I really almost hope
that we've made a mistake

02:34:09.073 --> 02:34:11.375
in the script and that
you can in fact find these

02:34:11.375 --> 02:34:12.776
on Chinese takeout menus in the US,

02:34:12.776 --> 02:34:14.245
because they're great, and
you guys are missing out.

02:34:14.245 --> 02:34:16.847
The last two items we wanted
to talk about were chips.

02:34:16.847 --> 02:34:18.082
We have two versions here that you'll find

02:34:18.082 --> 02:34:20.384
on pretty much every British
Chinese takeaway menu.

02:34:20.384 --> 02:34:23.153
We have regular chips, and we
have salt and pepper chips.

02:34:23.153 --> 02:34:24.355
As I'm sure some of you might know,

02:34:24.355 --> 02:34:25.422
British people don't tend to have

02:34:25.422 --> 02:34:27.524
the most adventurous palates in the world,

02:34:27.524 --> 02:34:29.393
and there's usually a
lot of demand for chips.

02:34:29.393 --> 02:34:31.795
Same with Indian restaurants
and Chinese restaurants.

02:34:31.795 --> 02:34:33.497
Basically, they're just
catering to the demand

02:34:33.497 --> 02:34:34.632
of the British public.

02:34:34.632 --> 02:34:36.367
Also, it's very common to pair your chips

02:34:36.367 --> 02:34:37.735
with curry sauce.

02:34:37.735 --> 02:34:39.670
So, curry sauce in this sense,

02:34:39.670 --> 02:34:41.839
it probably has some of
the similar flavorings

02:34:41.839 --> 02:34:44.642
that you would find in a
katsu-curry-type sauce.

02:34:44.642 --> 02:34:46.277
I would refer to this type of curry sauce

02:34:46.277 --> 02:34:48.145
as chip-shop curry sauce,

02:34:48.145 --> 02:34:50.247
and that kind of applies to
whether it's Chinese takeaways

02:34:50.247 --> 02:34:51.382
or fish and chip shops.

02:34:51.382 --> 02:34:52.783
Then we also have the
salt and pepper chips.

02:34:52.783 --> 02:34:54.652
Now, salt and pepper
seasoning is quite common

02:34:54.652 --> 02:34:56.120
at Chinese restaurants in the UK.

02:34:56.120 --> 02:34:57.388
That refers to a seasoning mix

02:34:57.388 --> 02:34:59.390
which is comprised of salt, white pepper,

02:34:59.390 --> 02:35:00.591
and Chinese five spice.

02:35:00.591 --> 02:35:02.293
They kind of take that, base it around it,

02:35:02.293 --> 02:35:04.628
they'll make chips, cook
them most of the way,

02:35:04.628 --> 02:35:06.163
and then finish them usually in a wok

02:35:06.163 --> 02:35:08.365
with some chili peppers, some onion,

02:35:08.365 --> 02:35:10.234
and some spring onion.
These are delicious.

02:35:10.234 --> 02:35:11.802
Now, I will defend salt and pepper chips,

02:35:11.802 --> 02:35:13.170
but they were kind of at the center

02:35:13.170 --> 02:35:15.839
of that TikTok controversy
around British Chinese food,

02:35:15.839 --> 02:35:17.875
which honestly is kind of
why we've made this episode.

02:35:17.875 --> 02:35:19.410
I also got an order of fries.

02:35:19.410 --> 02:35:21.378
Obviously these are not an exclusive

02:35:21.378 --> 02:35:23.781
just to US Chinese takeout places,

02:35:23.781 --> 02:35:26.183
but I just wanted to show
what they looked like here

02:35:26.183 --> 02:35:27.751
versus in the UK.

02:35:27.751 --> 02:35:30.254
Very bland. I don't even
think these have salt on them,

02:35:30.254 --> 02:35:32.389
which is kind of interesting.

02:35:32.389 --> 02:35:35.492
This is definitely one of those
Chinese takeout menu items

02:35:35.492 --> 02:35:39.596
that feels very catered to
a different kind of palate.

02:35:39.596 --> 02:35:41.965
I would say that I do not see people

02:35:41.965 --> 02:35:45.069
ordering french fries pretty much ever

02:35:45.069 --> 02:35:47.171
from a Chinese food place.

02:35:47.171 --> 02:35:49.039
Something like this is probably for kids

02:35:49.039 --> 02:35:51.375
who are picky eaters,
which I definitely was.

02:35:51.375 --> 02:35:53.677
I was ordering white
rice and chicken wings

02:35:53.677 --> 02:35:55.279
from Chinese takeout as a child

02:35:55.279 --> 02:35:57.448
because I didn't want to
try anything else, but,

02:35:57.448 --> 02:35:58.549
that's what this feels like.

02:35:58.549 --> 02:36:00.617
Something to please the children.

02:36:00.617 --> 02:36:03.420
I'm calling Harry a child right now.

02:36:03.420 --> 02:36:05.022
Something to please the child.

02:36:05.022 --> 02:36:06.824
Another British Chinese takeout staple

02:36:06.824 --> 02:36:08.959
is a crispy aromatic duck pancake.

02:36:08.959 --> 02:36:10.327
Now, it sits somewhere kind of between

02:36:10.327 --> 02:36:11.595
an appetizer and a main course,

02:36:11.595 --> 02:36:13.030
because, I mean, if you
gave me enough of this,

02:36:13.030 --> 02:36:14.631
I would just eat that as my main.

02:36:14.631 --> 02:36:16.166
It can be kind of
expensive, so I feel like

02:36:16.166 --> 02:36:17.968
some people just get
a little bit to share.

02:36:17.968 --> 02:36:19.103
So, it was neatly packaged for us,

02:36:19.103 --> 02:36:20.504
but it comes in a few
different components.

02:36:20.504 --> 02:36:23.107
Obviously, we have the
crispy aromatic duck itself.

02:36:23.107 --> 02:36:25.542
These little crispy bits
of skin are amazing.

02:36:25.542 --> 02:36:27.077
We have the pancakes.

02:36:27.077 --> 02:36:28.512
We have hoisin sauce.

02:36:28.512 --> 02:36:29.813
And then you have your vegetables.

02:36:29.813 --> 02:36:32.983
In this case, we have
some julienned cucumber

02:36:32.983 --> 02:36:34.151
and spring onion.

02:36:34.151 --> 02:36:36.320
I'll do a little demo assembly here.

02:36:36.320 --> 02:36:37.454
I feel like everyone's got different ways

02:36:37.454 --> 02:36:39.123
of assembling, so don't judge mine.

02:36:39.123 --> 02:36:40.190
Start with a pancake.

02:36:40.190 --> 02:36:42.259
Get as much of that
crispy skin as possible.

02:36:42.259 --> 02:36:43.927
Little bits of cuke.

02:36:43.927 --> 02:36:45.562
Some spronions.

02:36:45.562 --> 02:36:47.731
Hoisin is kind of a plum-based sauce.

02:36:47.731 --> 02:36:49.266
Just nice and sweet.

02:36:49.266 --> 02:36:50.968
Works really well with the savory.

02:36:50.968 --> 02:36:53.137
Have some fun with it, you know?

02:36:53.137 --> 02:36:54.972
Beautiful. These are sensational.

02:36:54.972 --> 02:36:56.707
I've not actually had
one of these in a while.

02:36:57.775 --> 02:37:01.011
Next, let's talk about the
exclusive soup options we have.

02:37:01.011 --> 02:37:02.980
This is vegetable bean curd soup.

02:37:02.980 --> 02:37:04.948
I should mention that I never order soup

02:37:04.948 --> 02:37:06.083
when I get Chinese takeout,

02:37:06.083 --> 02:37:08.752
but it's obviously going to
vary from person to person.

02:37:08.752 --> 02:37:10.721
Some people like a little soup appetizer.

02:37:10.721 --> 02:37:12.289
I'm not one of those people.

02:37:13.357 --> 02:37:15.225
Although I love soup in general.

02:37:15.225 --> 02:37:17.327
It mostly just smells like broccoli to me.

02:37:18.962 --> 02:37:21.465
It doesn't have a super-strong flavor.

02:37:21.465 --> 02:37:23.300
I feel like the broth isn't as rich

02:37:23.300 --> 02:37:26.203
as I wanted it to be, but
the tofu is really tasty.

02:37:26.203 --> 02:37:28.038
Next we have the hot and sour.

02:37:28.038 --> 02:37:30.140
I've never had hot and sour soup before,

02:37:30.140 --> 02:37:32.009
so I am interested to try it.

02:37:32.009 --> 02:37:33.944
This smells a lot like
sweet and sour sauce,

02:37:33.944 --> 02:37:35.579
so I assume that that's what's in here.

02:37:35.579 --> 02:37:38.115
I'm seeing vegetables,
what I think is tofu.

02:37:38.115 --> 02:37:41.718
It smells really good, so
let's take a little slurp.

02:37:42.619 --> 02:37:44.555
The texture is not my favorite.

02:37:44.555 --> 02:37:48.325
I think it's got the
texture of a sauce to me,

02:37:48.325 --> 02:37:51.762
so sipping it like a
soup doesn't feel right.

02:37:51.762 --> 02:37:53.130
But it does taste good.

02:37:53.130 --> 02:37:54.865
And lastly, we have the egg drop soup,

02:37:54.865 --> 02:37:57.301
which I feel like is a pretty popular soup

02:37:57.301 --> 02:37:59.069
when you're ordering Chinese takeout here.

02:37:59.069 --> 02:38:00.571
The one we got here is
chicken and sweet corn,

02:38:00.571 --> 02:38:02.306
which I think is kind
of like the archetypal

02:38:02.306 --> 02:38:04.074
Chinese takeaway soup here in the UK.

02:38:04.074 --> 02:38:05.642
I won't necessarily go out of my way

02:38:05.642 --> 02:38:06.510
to order one of these,

02:38:06.510 --> 02:38:08.745
although I do think they're pretty tasty.

02:38:08.745 --> 02:38:10.914
One thing to point out though
is the texture of these,

02:38:10.914 --> 02:38:11.815
because if you're not used to it,

02:38:11.815 --> 02:38:13.083
it can really take you by surprise.

02:38:13.083 --> 02:38:14.785
There's something about a
chicken and sweet corn soup

02:38:14.785 --> 02:38:16.453
in particular that's just so comforting.

02:38:16.453 --> 02:38:20.224
We actually do have a corn
and chicken soup here too,

02:38:20.224 --> 02:38:22.693
I just don't think it's as popular

02:38:22.693 --> 02:38:24.795
as something like a hot and sour soup.

02:38:24.795 --> 02:38:26.864
In the UK, we have a lot
of fried-rice dishes.

02:38:26.864 --> 02:38:28.432
These probably could be
ordered as your main meal,

02:38:28.432 --> 02:38:30.100
but I would say in the UK,
they're more traditionally

02:38:30.100 --> 02:38:32.503
ordered alongside a
meat or a vegetable dish

02:38:32.503 --> 02:38:33.737
and kind of enjoyed together.

02:38:33.737 --> 02:38:35.005
We picked out a couple of options.

02:38:35.005 --> 02:38:37.407
Here is the house-special fried rice,

02:38:37.407 --> 02:38:39.710
and then also we have
the Singapore fried rice.

02:38:39.710 --> 02:38:41.078
I just wanted to find out what makes this

02:38:41.078 --> 02:38:42.112
the house-special fried rice,

02:38:42.112 --> 02:38:44.848
because usually, it'll include a variation

02:38:44.848 --> 02:38:46.283
of kind of things mixed in with it.

02:38:46.283 --> 02:38:48.018
There's little pieces of chicken, pork,

02:38:48.018 --> 02:38:50.621
and prawn in here, as well
as some spring onions.

02:38:50.621 --> 02:38:52.155
The Singapore fried rice
seems to actually have

02:38:52.155 --> 02:38:53.223
the same fillings, because I'm seeing

02:38:53.223 --> 02:38:54.858
these little tiny shrimps in them.

02:38:54.858 --> 02:38:57.661
I'm seeing bits of
chicken and pork as well.

02:38:57.661 --> 02:38:58.896
I think this is supposed to be

02:38:58.896 --> 02:39:00.731
a slightly spicier fried rice,

02:39:00.731 --> 02:39:02.733
so let me give this one a quick try.

02:39:04.401 --> 02:39:05.969
Not overpoweringly hot,

02:39:05.969 --> 02:39:08.972
but definitely more of a
chili kick to that one.

02:39:08.972 --> 02:39:11.074
Moving on to our fried-rice option,

02:39:11.074 --> 02:39:14.845
we only had one, and this is
the young chow fried rice.

02:39:14.845 --> 02:39:17.147
It's basically made with a bunch

02:39:17.147 --> 02:39:18.682
of different protein options.

02:39:18.682 --> 02:39:21.018
Looking down right now,
I just see spareribs,

02:39:21.018 --> 02:39:23.153
I see chicken, I see shrimp.

02:39:23.153 --> 02:39:24.054
Looks kind of good.

02:39:24.054 --> 02:39:26.156
I've never thought of
eating multiple meats

02:39:26.156 --> 02:39:27.925
at one time like this.

02:39:27.925 --> 02:39:30.427
I would argue and say
that fried rice in general

02:39:30.427 --> 02:39:32.396
is a popular food here.

02:39:32.396 --> 02:39:33.597
I make it all the time.

02:39:33.597 --> 02:39:35.432
It's such an easy dish to make at home.

02:39:35.432 --> 02:39:38.035
And it's definitely popular
at Chinese takeout places,

02:39:38.035 --> 02:39:40.504
but for some reason, we only
had one exclusive option

02:39:40.504 --> 02:39:42.339
at this particular location.

02:39:42.339 --> 02:39:43.440
I'm going to try to get a bite

02:39:43.440 --> 02:39:45.509
with every single meat on it.

02:39:45.509 --> 02:39:47.077
Oh! There it is.

02:39:49.546 --> 02:39:52.749
It has a very odd taste when it's

02:39:52.749 --> 02:39:55.452
three different meats at
once, but it's not bad.

02:39:55.452 --> 02:39:56.620
It's actually pretty good.

02:39:56.620 --> 02:39:58.221
Now, chow mein has to be
one of the most popular

02:39:58.221 --> 02:39:59.156
dishes in the UK.

02:39:59.156 --> 02:40:01.692
I think surveys have actually
placed it as the No. 1 dish.

02:40:01.692 --> 02:40:03.393
It's pretty versatile.
I'd say usually, again,

02:40:03.393 --> 02:40:06.530
this would be an accompaniment
to maybe a meat dish as well,

02:40:06.530 --> 02:40:08.265
or you could kind of
just eat this on its own.

02:40:08.265 --> 02:40:10.067
Personally, chicken chow
mein is my favorite,

02:40:10.067 --> 02:40:11.935
and I think probably
most Brits' favorite too,

02:40:11.935 --> 02:40:13.270
but you can also just get plain,

02:40:13.270 --> 02:40:15.205
vegetable, or various other toppings.

02:40:15.205 --> 02:40:16.607
Here we have the chicken chow mein.

02:40:16.607 --> 02:40:18.542
It's effectively just a
stir-fried-noodle dish.

02:40:18.542 --> 02:40:20.644
Chow mein definitely is a thing here,

02:40:20.644 --> 02:40:24.281
it just isn't a thing at
this particular restaurant.

02:40:24.281 --> 02:40:26.216
I'm sorry I have to repeat myself so much.

02:40:26.216 --> 02:40:28.118
Let's talk noodles.

02:40:28.118 --> 02:40:29.953
This is the pan-fried noodle,

02:40:29.953 --> 02:40:31.188
this is the lo mein,

02:40:31.188 --> 02:40:33.423
and this is the Singapore mei fun.

02:40:33.423 --> 02:40:35.726
Opening this up, I knew
exactly what it smelled like.

02:40:35.726 --> 02:40:37.294
It smells like curry, so there has to be

02:40:37.294 --> 02:40:39.630
some sort of curry element in it.

02:40:39.630 --> 02:40:42.399
It smells really good, so I
think I'm going to take a bite.

02:40:42.399 --> 02:40:43.734
I've never had this before.

02:40:43.734 --> 02:40:45.569
And it also looks like they use

02:40:45.569 --> 02:40:48.672
a smaller noodle than in
either of these two dishes.

02:40:48.672 --> 02:40:51.608
It's like those thin, is it,
like, vermicelli noodles?

02:40:52.376 --> 02:40:54.044
Vermicelli noodles.

02:40:54.044 --> 02:40:55.879
That's my daughter's name.

02:40:55.879 --> 02:40:57.180
[crew laughs]

02:40:57.180 --> 02:40:59.816
This dish also has
multiple proteins going on.

02:40:59.816 --> 02:41:01.585
I'm seeing chicken, and I'm seeing shrimp.

02:41:01.585 --> 02:41:03.086
I don't know if there's
anything else underneath,

02:41:03.086 --> 02:41:04.888
but that's what I see right now.

02:41:04.888 --> 02:41:06.223
The flavor's pretty good.

02:41:06.223 --> 02:41:08.692
I don't know if I like vermicelli noodles.

02:41:08.692 --> 02:41:10.327
I think this is my first time having them.

02:41:10.327 --> 02:41:11.728
They're just such a thin noodle.

02:41:11.728 --> 02:41:13.096
I like something chewier,

02:41:13.096 --> 02:41:14.998
like the ones that come in the lo mein.

02:41:14.998 --> 02:41:17.167
Once you get past the rice
and the noodle portions,

02:41:17.167 --> 02:41:18.535
the Chinese takeaway menus in the UK

02:41:18.535 --> 02:41:20.303
are usually categorized by protein,

02:41:20.303 --> 02:41:22.372
so you'll choose either chicken, pork,

02:41:22.372 --> 02:41:23.874
beef, or anything else,

02:41:23.874 --> 02:41:25.008
and then you kind of choose a sauce

02:41:25.008 --> 02:41:25.876
that goes with that.

02:41:25.876 --> 02:41:27.744
For example, we can get
sweet and sour chicken,

02:41:27.744 --> 02:41:29.246
but you can also get sweet and sour pork

02:41:29.246 --> 02:41:30.280
and sweet and sour beef.

02:41:30.280 --> 02:41:31.748
From each protein category,
we're going to show you

02:41:31.748 --> 02:41:34.084
a couple of either interesting
or unique food items.

02:41:34.084 --> 02:41:37.020
Let's start with chicken.
Here I have lemon chicken.

02:41:37.020 --> 02:41:38.021
This is another one of those

02:41:38.021 --> 02:41:39.089
that's probably going to be found

02:41:39.089 --> 02:41:41.625
on pretty much every Chinese
takeaway menu in the UK.

02:41:41.625 --> 02:41:42.459
The sauce is pretty good, though.

02:41:42.459 --> 02:41:44.961
It's lemony. A little bit
sweet. A little bit tart.

02:41:44.961 --> 02:41:46.463
Then here we have the kung po chicken.

02:41:46.463 --> 02:41:48.765
You might also see this
referred to as kung pao chicken,

02:41:48.765 --> 02:41:50.100
with that A in the middle there,

02:41:50.100 --> 02:41:51.334
but it's the same dish.

02:41:51.334 --> 02:41:53.336
It's basically not too dissimilar

02:41:53.336 --> 02:41:54.838
to sweet and sour chicken
that you might find

02:41:54.838 --> 02:41:56.640
on the menu at a UK Chinese takeout.

02:41:56.640 --> 02:41:59.676
It's these dredged and
fried pieces of chicken

02:41:59.676 --> 02:42:01.478
in a sweet and spicy sauce.

02:42:01.478 --> 02:42:03.180
Kung pao chicken I think traditionally

02:42:03.180 --> 02:42:04.414
also might have some peanut in it.

02:42:04.414 --> 02:42:06.316
In the US, it's exactly the same.

02:42:06.316 --> 02:42:08.852
Our menus are categorized
by the protein type,

02:42:08.852 --> 02:42:11.188
but a lot of the dishes
are really different,

02:42:11.188 --> 02:42:12.823
so you don't always get the same sauce

02:42:12.823 --> 02:42:16.326
for, say, a seafood dish as
you would a chicken dish.

02:42:16.326 --> 02:42:17.494
Let's start with the chop suey,

02:42:17.494 --> 02:42:19.663
which I have never had before,

02:42:19.663 --> 02:42:21.198
but I'm very interested.

02:42:21.198 --> 02:42:22.933
You can get it with any protein.

02:42:22.933 --> 02:42:24.935
We got ours with chicken.

02:42:24.935 --> 02:42:26.002
And here's what that looks like.

02:42:26.002 --> 02:42:28.805
The chicken looks a little pale.

02:42:28.805 --> 02:42:31.842
I feel like this is a
surprisingly bland dish

02:42:31.842 --> 02:42:33.643
compared to a lot of other chicken dishes

02:42:33.643 --> 02:42:35.178
I've had from Chinese takeout.

02:42:35.178 --> 02:42:37.247
It's definitely not my favorite.

02:42:37.247 --> 02:42:38.949
Moving on to the sesame chicken.

02:42:38.949 --> 02:42:40.884
This was a fan favorite.

02:42:40.884 --> 02:42:42.619
I was told I had to get this.

02:42:42.619 --> 02:42:45.722
I'm not a sesame-chicken orderer,

02:42:45.722 --> 02:42:48.058
but my friends and coworkers are.

02:42:56.399 --> 02:42:57.868
Yeah, it's so good.

02:42:57.868 --> 02:42:59.603
Next we have orange chicken,

02:42:59.603 --> 02:43:01.338
which, as the name suggests,

02:43:01.338 --> 02:43:03.006
has orange in the sauce.

02:43:03.006 --> 02:43:07.110
And this particular place
uses the orange peel

02:43:07.110 --> 02:43:08.845
to season the chicken.

02:43:08.845 --> 02:43:11.281
I've definitely had a lot
of orange chicken in my day,

02:43:11.281 --> 02:43:14.518
particularly the frozen
kind from Trader Joe's.

02:43:14.518 --> 02:43:15.786
So it is pretty popular.

02:43:15.786 --> 02:43:16.987
It's not a spicy sauce.

02:43:16.987 --> 02:43:20.490
It's definitely just a sweet, tangy sauce,

02:43:20.490 --> 02:43:21.691
but it's very delicious.

02:43:21.691 --> 02:43:23.727
And last but not least, we
have General Tso's chicken,

02:43:23.727 --> 02:43:25.495
which is my personal favorite thing

02:43:25.495 --> 02:43:26.863
to order from Chinese takeout.

02:43:26.863 --> 02:43:29.132
You'll see it on a lot of
Chinese restaurant menus

02:43:29.132 --> 02:43:33.370
here in the US, but it's not
exactly authentic Chinese food.

02:43:33.370 --> 02:43:34.204
Shocker.

02:43:34.204 --> 02:43:36.840
General Tso was a real general
from the Hunan province

02:43:36.840 --> 02:43:38.842
who supposedly enjoyed eating a dish

02:43:38.842 --> 02:43:40.610
similar to General Tso's chicken,

02:43:40.610 --> 02:43:42.813
although this modern American version

02:43:42.813 --> 02:43:45.515
would probably be unrecognizable to him.

02:43:45.515 --> 02:43:47.818
The dish was actually created in the 1950s

02:43:47.818 --> 02:43:49.986
by a chef named Peng Chang-kuei.

02:43:49.986 --> 02:43:52.222
When he immigrated to New York in 1973,

02:43:52.222 --> 02:43:55.158
he altered his recipe to
cater to the American palate,

02:43:55.158 --> 02:43:57.027
which in this case means
he made it sweeter.

02:43:57.027 --> 02:43:59.029
Peng Chang-kuei called
it General Tso's chicken

02:43:59.029 --> 02:44:01.565
because he and General Tso
are from the same town.

02:44:01.565 --> 02:44:02.766
Now we're on to the beef options.

02:44:02.766 --> 02:44:04.434
Now, firstly, I have to shout out

02:44:04.434 --> 02:44:07.637
what might be my favorite
British Chinese takeaway dish,

02:44:07.637 --> 02:44:09.906
which is crispy shredded beef with chili.

02:44:09.906 --> 02:44:10.740
One thing I've got to say,

02:44:10.740 --> 02:44:12.576
talking a lot about textures
and crispiness here,

02:44:12.576 --> 02:44:15.078
what you will often find from
Chinese takeaways in the UK,

02:44:15.078 --> 02:44:16.913
they'll actually break a little hole

02:44:16.913 --> 02:44:17.981
in the plastic container.

02:44:17.981 --> 02:44:20.050
This is probably the first
thing that I'm ordering

02:44:20.050 --> 02:44:21.551
when I'm ordering a Chinese takeaway.

02:44:21.551 --> 02:44:25.689
What they do is they get
these little slivers of beef,

02:44:25.689 --> 02:44:28.091
dredge them usually in
something like corn flour,

02:44:28.091 --> 02:44:31.061
fry them up, and then
toss them in this lovely

02:44:31.061 --> 02:44:33.063
sweet and spicy chili sauce.

02:44:33.063 --> 02:44:34.197
I just love the variation you get.

02:44:34.197 --> 02:44:35.899
Like, some of them will
be slightly chewier,

02:44:35.899 --> 02:44:38.001
some of them will be crispier.

02:44:38.001 --> 02:44:39.669
Flavors are great.

02:44:39.669 --> 02:44:41.371
Next up we have some Szechuan beef.

02:44:41.371 --> 02:44:43.073
Now, I really enjoyed hearing Tianran

02:44:43.073 --> 02:44:44.074
talking about Szechuan flavors ...

02:44:44.074 --> 02:44:46.676
Tianran: The Szechuanese-style
cooking with the peppercorn.

02:44:46.676 --> 02:44:48.879
... when we were doing
the US vs China series.

02:44:48.879 --> 02:44:49.746
Go check that out.

02:44:49.746 --> 02:44:53.183
But I think Szechuan food
tends to be pretty spicy,

02:44:53.183 --> 02:44:54.718
and they also use Szechuan peppercorns,

02:44:54.718 --> 02:44:57.287
which give you this kind of
numbing effect in your mouth.

02:44:57.287 --> 02:44:58.321
The beef here also isn't fried.

02:44:58.321 --> 02:45:00.690
It's more just like
pieces of braised beef.

02:45:00.690 --> 02:45:02.158
I'm kind of intrigued to give this a try,

02:45:02.158 --> 02:45:04.194
although I'm worried
about my spice tolerance.

02:45:06.296 --> 02:45:07.130
Yeah, that's pretty spicy.

02:45:07.130 --> 02:45:08.665
It's very tasty, very flavorful.

02:45:08.665 --> 02:45:11.034
And then finally we've got
some beef in black bean sauce.

02:45:11.034 --> 02:45:12.636
I'd say this is a pretty
common one as well,

02:45:12.636 --> 02:45:13.803
and this is one that you can also get

02:45:13.803 --> 02:45:15.338
in different meat varieties,

02:45:15.338 --> 02:45:16.439
so you can get this with the chicken,

02:45:16.439 --> 02:45:17.674
with the pork, anything else.

02:45:17.674 --> 02:45:19.776
Comes with loads of peppers as well.

02:45:19.776 --> 02:45:21.611
Some chili in there, onions.

02:45:21.611 --> 02:45:24.047
It's a nice, very rich sauce.

02:45:24.047 --> 02:45:26.917
That's really good. That with
some rice, beautiful pairing.

02:45:26.917 --> 02:45:27.918
We'll often actually get this one.

02:45:27.918 --> 02:45:28.785
This is one of my favorites.

02:45:28.785 --> 02:45:30.020
I feel like I've said that
about a lot of things.

02:45:30.020 --> 02:45:31.721
I think I just order too
much Chinese takeaway.

02:45:31.721 --> 02:45:33.657
There's a variety of beef
dishes that you can get

02:45:33.657 --> 02:45:35.825
at Chinese food places here in America.

02:45:35.825 --> 02:45:37.727
Today, we got the moo shu beef.

02:45:37.727 --> 02:45:41.731
So it's got cabbage, some
mushrooms, obviously the beef.

02:45:41.731 --> 02:45:43.133
Let's take a taste.

02:45:43.133 --> 02:45:45.602
The beef in that is really,
really tender and delicious.

02:45:45.602 --> 02:45:47.304
I actually really like this!

02:45:47.304 --> 02:45:48.872
Seafood is also a pretty common option

02:45:48.872 --> 02:45:50.273
on UK Chinese takeaway menus.

02:45:50.273 --> 02:45:51.408
A lot of the options you will find

02:45:51.408 --> 02:45:54.411
have similar sauces to some
of the beef or pork options,

02:45:54.411 --> 02:45:56.179
but there are also a
few unique ones as well.

02:45:56.179 --> 02:45:58.014
Firstly, we have salt and pepper squid.

02:45:58.014 --> 02:45:59.883
You might remember the salt
and pepper chips from earlier.

02:45:59.883 --> 02:46:02.652
It's basically crispy squid
cooked in that same seasoning.

02:46:02.652 --> 02:46:04.554
Sometimes this might be
found under appetizers.

02:46:04.554 --> 02:46:05.989
Today, they had it in their main section,

02:46:05.989 --> 02:46:06.990
so we're going with that.

02:46:06.990 --> 02:46:08.725
I love crispy squid.

02:46:08.725 --> 02:46:10.293
The way that they kind of cut it

02:46:10.293 --> 02:46:11.561
with this crosshatch pattern,

02:46:11.561 --> 02:46:14.130
particularly in Chinese cuisine,

02:46:14.130 --> 02:46:15.732
gives it a really nice texture.

02:46:15.732 --> 02:46:16.866
It's not that chewy.

02:46:16.866 --> 02:46:19.269
It's really flavorful, not super fishy.

02:46:19.269 --> 02:46:20.370
Just to illustrate that sometimes

02:46:20.370 --> 02:46:23.073
the sauces are the same in
different parts of the menu,

02:46:23.073 --> 02:46:25.608
this is the pepper and
black bean sauce again,

02:46:25.608 --> 02:46:26.876
but this time with squid.

02:46:26.876 --> 02:46:28.645
Then we have a couple of
prawn options as well.

02:46:28.645 --> 02:46:30.981
Here we have the king
prawns in oyster sauce.

02:46:30.981 --> 02:46:32.983
And king prawns with cashew nuts.

02:46:32.983 --> 02:46:36.853
While we have the prawn
here in its un-balled form,

02:46:36.853 --> 02:46:38.154
let me get one of those out.

02:46:38.154 --> 02:46:40.824
Nico, how does that look?
Is that a prawn or a shrimp?

02:46:40.824 --> 02:46:41.658
Or neither?

02:46:41.658 --> 02:46:43.760
That prawn kind of looks shrimpy to me.

02:46:43.760 --> 02:46:46.296
It certainly does not
look like a king prawn.

02:46:46.296 --> 02:46:48.164
That's not the king of the prawns.

02:46:48.164 --> 02:46:51.534
In the US, shrimp is
definitely the most popular

02:46:51.534 --> 02:46:54.237
seafood option when you
get Chinese takeout.

02:46:54.237 --> 02:46:56.072
We have a couple of shrimp options here,

02:46:56.072 --> 02:46:58.675
the Szechuan shrimp and a seafood delight,

02:46:58.675 --> 02:47:00.110
which has shrimp in it

02:47:00.110 --> 02:47:02.679
and I'm guessing other
delightful sea creatures.

02:47:02.679 --> 02:47:03.847
That's just shrimp.

02:47:03.847 --> 02:47:05.415
Is that what is delightful?

02:47:05.415 --> 02:47:06.883
Or do they mean delight is, like,

02:47:06.883 --> 02:47:08.785
you get a bunch of veggies with it?

02:47:08.785 --> 02:47:11.154
Let's just try the Szechuan shrimp.

02:47:11.154 --> 02:47:14.290
It smells as spicy as I
think it's going to be.

02:47:14.290 --> 02:47:16.926
This is probably the biggest
shrimp I've seen today.

02:47:20.764 --> 02:47:22.899
I swear I've eaten with a fork before.

02:47:22.899 --> 02:47:24.634
Oh no, it's tail-on!

02:47:24.634 --> 02:47:27.237
It's spicy, not as spicy as
I thought it was going to be,

02:47:27.237 --> 02:47:29.539
but I'm sure the more you
eat it, the spicier it gets.

02:47:29.539 --> 02:47:31.708
But that sauce is really, really good,

02:47:31.708 --> 02:47:32.742
and the shrimp is awesome,

02:47:32.742 --> 02:47:35.045
even though it's got tails on it.

02:47:35.045 --> 02:47:36.179
Take the tails off.

02:47:36.179 --> 02:47:37.847
We also got a shrimp egg roll.

02:47:37.847 --> 02:47:39.616
Harry really wanted me to try this,

02:47:39.616 --> 02:47:40.683
and I'm glad he mentioned it,

02:47:40.683 --> 02:47:43.620
because I've never had a
shrimp egg roll before.

02:47:43.620 --> 02:47:45.922
Just pork. That's what I get every time.

02:47:45.922 --> 02:47:48.958
Do you see that one little
tiny piece of shrimp?

02:47:50.427 --> 02:47:52.896
I feel like it tastes slightly
different than the pork,

02:47:52.896 --> 02:47:54.764
not just because the meat is different.

02:47:54.764 --> 02:47:57.367
Another item you'll see in
a lot of Chinese restaurants

02:47:57.367 --> 02:47:59.169
in the US is egg foo young.

02:47:59.169 --> 02:48:01.004
It's an omelet made with vegetables,

02:48:01.004 --> 02:48:03.306
a protein, and a stir-fry sauce.

02:48:03.306 --> 02:48:04.641
Whoa, baby.

02:48:04.641 --> 02:48:06.176
This is a big omelet.

02:48:06.176 --> 02:48:07.510
It smells really good.

02:48:07.510 --> 02:48:08.611
It doesn't just smell like egg.

02:48:08.611 --> 02:48:10.747
It smells like a lot of good stuff.

02:48:10.747 --> 02:48:12.549
It needs a lot more salt, actually.

02:48:12.549 --> 02:48:14.484
I wonder if you're supposed to add sauce

02:48:14.484 --> 02:48:16.519
or something on top.

02:48:16.519 --> 02:48:18.288
I think we're actually supposed to add

02:48:18.288 --> 02:48:20.957
this gravy to it, so let me add that

02:48:20.957 --> 02:48:23.827
and see if it helps with the salt factor.

02:48:23.827 --> 02:48:26.396
This feels very UK-core right now,

02:48:26.396 --> 02:48:28.798
me pouring this gravy all over food.

02:48:30.667 --> 02:48:32.102
Better.

02:48:32.102 --> 02:48:34.337
Still not great. I don't
know if I like this.

02:48:34.337 --> 02:48:35.472
Yeah, egg foo young is interesting,

02:48:35.472 --> 02:48:36.573
but I'm not sure British people

02:48:36.573 --> 02:48:38.274
would really be going
in and kind of ordering

02:48:38.274 --> 02:48:39.909
just an omelet from a Chinese takeaway.

02:48:39.909 --> 02:48:41.678
I'm not sure how well that
would take off over here.

02:48:41.678 --> 02:48:45.849
It's time for s-s-s-s-s-s-sauce talk.

02:48:45.849 --> 02:48:47.350
Sauce talk.

02:48:47.350 --> 02:48:51.654
If there was ever a time
to do sauce talk, it's now.

02:48:51.654 --> 02:48:53.022
So, in front of me here,
I have all the sauces

02:48:53.022 --> 02:48:55.024
that were on offer at the
restaurant we ordered from.

02:48:55.024 --> 02:48:55.859
I'm going to go through them now,

02:48:55.859 --> 02:48:57.894
explain what they are, what
they might be paired with,

02:48:57.894 --> 02:48:58.728
and give them a try.

02:48:58.728 --> 02:49:00.196
We'll start with a slightly infamous one.

02:49:00.196 --> 02:49:01.764
It's British curry sauce.

02:49:01.764 --> 02:49:03.233
The British went round the world,

02:49:03.233 --> 02:49:04.501
took spices from everybody,

02:49:04.501 --> 02:49:06.703
and this is somehow the only
thing that we came up with.

02:49:06.703 --> 02:49:08.171
Brits love curry sauce,

02:49:08.171 --> 02:49:10.273
or at least this iteration of curry sauce.

02:49:12.742 --> 02:49:14.811
There's definitely some
heat there. Not a lot.

02:49:14.811 --> 02:49:16.946
It's quite a gentle spice flavor.

02:49:16.946 --> 02:49:18.047
There's something about the texture of it.

02:49:18.047 --> 02:49:19.382
It's just really satisfying.

02:49:19.382 --> 02:49:22.118
That really gloopy, cornstarch-y texture.

02:49:22.118 --> 02:49:24.354
Goes excellently with chicken balls.

02:49:24.354 --> 02:49:26.723
You'll often see Brits
drizzling this onto chips.

02:49:26.723 --> 02:49:28.525
Chips and curry sauce is
a big thing in the UK.

02:49:28.525 --> 02:49:29.726
Or you'll just see it drizzled

02:49:29.726 --> 02:49:32.195
across your entire succulent Chinese meal.

02:49:32.195 --> 02:49:33.396
I will say Joe really loved that

02:49:33.396 --> 02:49:35.765
when we did our fish-and-chip-shop
"Food Tours" episode.

02:49:35.765 --> 02:49:37.200
It's not just the Brits who can enjoy it.

02:49:37.200 --> 02:49:39.802
Then we have another iconic
UK Chinese takeout sauce,

02:49:39.802 --> 02:49:41.304
which is sweet and sour.

02:49:41.304 --> 02:49:42.539
Depending on where you get it from,

02:49:42.539 --> 02:49:43.873
the color again will vary.

02:49:43.873 --> 02:49:44.874
That's a pretty well-balanced one.

02:49:44.874 --> 02:49:46.643
The first thing that hits
your tongue is the sour,

02:49:46.643 --> 02:49:48.111
then it pretty quickly mellows out,

02:49:48.111 --> 02:49:49.546
and you get a nice sweet aftertaste.

02:49:49.546 --> 02:49:50.713
Again, I think this is kind of intended

02:49:50.713 --> 02:49:52.115
to be paired with the chicken balls

02:49:52.115 --> 02:49:54.484
or prawn balls or
something else like that.

02:49:54.484 --> 02:49:55.552
But I do think a lot of Brits

02:49:55.552 --> 02:49:57.487
will just kind of coat
their entire meal in it.

02:49:57.487 --> 02:49:59.122
While we're on the subject
of these iconic sauces,

02:49:59.122 --> 02:50:01.024
I also have to shout out
the polystyrene cups.

02:50:01.024 --> 02:50:02.725
I think Americans might
call that Styrofoam.

02:50:02.725 --> 02:50:03.626
But these are really iconic.

02:50:03.626 --> 02:50:06.763
This is the standard delivery
vessel for your sauces.

02:50:06.763 --> 02:50:07.697
The restaurant that we ordered from

02:50:07.697 --> 02:50:08.865
had two chili options.

02:50:08.865 --> 02:50:10.133
Firstly, we have sweet chili.

02:50:10.133 --> 02:50:11.768
Brits love sweet chili.

02:50:11.768 --> 02:50:14.003
This is on the menu at
a lot of restaurants.

02:50:14.003 --> 02:50:15.805
And then the next one
that we have is chili oil.

02:50:15.805 --> 02:50:17.707
I'd say this one isn't
super-commonly found

02:50:17.707 --> 02:50:19.342
on UK Chinese takeout menus.

02:50:19.342 --> 02:50:20.577
Next up we have the black bean sauce.

02:50:20.577 --> 02:50:21.411
Now, this is the same sauce

02:50:21.411 --> 02:50:23.313
that you might have seen in
some of the dishes earlier.

02:50:23.313 --> 02:50:24.414
Then we've got two more sauces.

02:50:24.414 --> 02:50:25.949
First up, we have hoisin sauce.

02:50:25.949 --> 02:50:28.251
Now, this is most commonly
paired with the crispy duck

02:50:28.251 --> 02:50:30.119
that you'll find on UK
Chinese takeout menus.

02:50:30.119 --> 02:50:32.855
It's a very thick dark brown sauce.

02:50:32.855 --> 02:50:34.958
Again, gloopy sauces. We love it.

02:50:34.958 --> 02:50:36.326
I think the main
ingredient in hoisin sauce,

02:50:36.326 --> 02:50:38.094
or one of the main ingredients, is plum.

02:50:38.094 --> 02:50:39.829
So you get this real sweetness from it,

02:50:39.829 --> 02:50:42.699
which works very well
with the savory duck meat

02:50:42.699 --> 02:50:43.700
and other things on the menu.

02:50:43.700 --> 02:50:45.235
And then finally we have barbecue sauce.

02:50:45.235 --> 02:50:46.302
Now, I was a little bit confused

02:50:46.302 --> 02:50:47.170
when I saw this on the menu,

02:50:47.170 --> 02:50:49.872
because barbecue sauce in
the kind of Western sense,

02:50:49.872 --> 02:50:52.108
so like a Texas barbecue
sauce, for example,

02:50:52.108 --> 02:50:53.176
does not look like this, and I think

02:50:53.176 --> 02:50:54.444
that's what I was expecting.

02:50:54.444 --> 02:50:55.678
But I think what's
actually being delivered

02:50:55.678 --> 02:50:57.680
is more of a Chinese-style barbecue sauce.

02:50:57.680 --> 02:50:59.015
What this one makes me think of is more

02:50:59.015 --> 02:51:01.351
when you eat, like, a char
siu pork bun, for example.

02:51:01.351 --> 02:51:03.119
Because while it is barbecuey,

02:51:03.119 --> 02:51:05.121
it's still got some sweetness to it.

02:51:05.121 --> 02:51:07.323
There's Chinese five spice in here.

02:51:07.323 --> 02:51:09.792
But if you're expecting,
like, Heinz barbecue sauce,

02:51:09.792 --> 02:51:10.627
that's not it.

02:51:10.627 --> 02:51:11.461
I think out of all of these ones,

02:51:11.461 --> 02:51:12.729
the one I would reach for the most

02:51:12.729 --> 02:51:14.163
would be the sweet and sour sauce.

02:51:14.163 --> 02:51:15.598
It's because I'll almost always order

02:51:15.598 --> 02:51:16.866
the chicken balls when I'm making

02:51:16.866 --> 02:51:18.334
a Chinese takeout order in the UK,

02:51:18.334 --> 02:51:19.636
and one of these will come with it.

02:51:19.636 --> 02:51:20.470
Yeah, I think in the UK,

02:51:20.470 --> 02:51:21.838
it's kind of just the definitive sauce,

02:51:21.838 --> 02:51:23.006
closely followed by curry.

02:51:23.006 --> 02:51:24.607
I think that's one of
the biggest differences

02:51:24.607 --> 02:51:27.844
between Chinese takeout
in the US and the UK.

02:51:27.844 --> 02:51:30.413
Our takeout comes with the
sauce already incorporated,

02:51:30.413 --> 02:51:31.481
for the most part.

02:51:31.481 --> 02:51:33.316
You do get a mix, though,

02:51:33.316 --> 02:51:35.251
of tiny little sauce packets like this.

02:51:35.251 --> 02:51:37.053
So, we've got soy sauce,

02:51:37.053 --> 02:51:38.321
duck sauce, which is so good.

02:51:38.321 --> 02:51:40.023
It's kind of just a sweet sauce.

02:51:40.023 --> 02:51:42.659
We have mustard usually,
which I'm not seeing here.

02:51:42.659 --> 02:51:44.060
But you do get mustard.

02:51:44.060 --> 02:51:45.895
And hot sauce.

02:51:45.895 --> 02:51:46.729
I'm pretty sure there are more,

02:51:46.729 --> 02:51:48.564
but that's just what we have right now.

02:51:48.564 --> 02:51:51.334
And then we have a couple little cups of,

02:51:51.334 --> 02:51:53.002
this looks like a paste.

02:51:53.002 --> 02:51:55.371
I think that this is a
little tiny container

02:51:55.371 --> 02:51:58.641
of oyster sauce, and this
is just a little container

02:51:58.641 --> 02:52:00.977
of extra soy sauce, probably for dipping.

02:52:00.977 --> 02:52:04.147
If you do not have a drawer
full of these sauces,

02:52:04.147 --> 02:52:05.415
I don't know what you're doing.

02:52:05.415 --> 02:52:07.850
I feel like everybody has
that drawer in their house

02:52:07.850 --> 02:52:10.586
that's filled with random
sauce packets like this.

02:52:13.022 --> 02:52:14.557
Now that we've covered
every item individually,

02:52:14.557 --> 02:52:16.559
what I wanted to do was
show you my ideal plate

02:52:16.559 --> 02:52:18.161
of British Chinese takeaway food.

02:52:18.161 --> 02:52:19.829
You might have seen these
on TikTok or elsewhere,

02:52:19.829 --> 02:52:21.464
as these kind of broke
the internet a while ago,

02:52:21.464 --> 02:52:23.232
but I'm going to show you my version.

02:52:23.232 --> 02:52:24.600
I need some chow mein.

02:52:25.535 --> 02:52:27.203
The amount of carbs that
might end up on this plate

02:52:27.203 --> 02:52:29.172
could be alarming to some,

02:52:29.172 --> 02:52:31.207
because we're going to go noodles.

02:52:31.207 --> 02:52:32.542
We're also going to go rice.

02:52:32.542 --> 02:52:34.177
You know we're going to get

02:52:34.177 --> 02:52:35.611
the salt and pepper chips involved.

02:52:35.611 --> 02:52:39.482
Then I want a healthy portion
of my crispy chili beef.

02:52:39.482 --> 02:52:41.284
I'm going to want some seaweed on here.

02:52:41.284 --> 02:52:43.086
Some of this prawn toast.

02:52:43.086 --> 02:52:45.188
Definitely a couple of spring rolls.

02:52:45.188 --> 02:52:48.124
And of course our prawn crackers as well.

02:52:48.124 --> 02:52:49.959
So generally speaking, this
is a pretty normal plate

02:52:49.959 --> 02:52:52.261
of Chinese takeaway food
for a British person.

02:52:52.261 --> 02:52:53.162
But we're not done there.

02:52:53.162 --> 02:52:55.365
The final step, of course,
is to add our sauces.

02:52:55.365 --> 02:52:56.632
Yes, that's sauces plural,

02:52:56.632 --> 02:52:59.235
because we're going to be
adding both the curry sauce

02:52:59.235 --> 02:53:00.937
and the sweet and sour sauce to this.

02:53:00.937 --> 02:53:02.605
And honestly, I'm not discriminating here.

02:53:02.605 --> 02:53:06.109
This is going pretty
much on the entire meal.

02:53:06.109 --> 02:53:07.910
And of course our sweet and sour sauce.

02:53:10.313 --> 02:53:12.115
Love it or hate it, that
is your typical plate

02:53:12.115 --> 02:53:14.050
of British Chinese takeaway food.

02:53:14.050 --> 02:53:15.218
I think I've done a pretty good job there.

02:53:15.218 --> 02:53:17.487
British people, please feel
to agree or disagree with me

02:53:17.487 --> 02:53:18.488
in the comments below.

02:53:18.488 --> 02:53:19.956
Nico, I've got to hear
your thoughts about this.

02:53:19.956 --> 02:53:20.957
Would you eat this?

02:53:20.957 --> 02:53:22.325
Let me stop you right there.

02:53:22.325 --> 02:53:26.195
Why do you guys sauce the whole plate?

02:53:26.195 --> 02:53:28.030
Like, doesn't the whole plate just then

02:53:28.030 --> 02:53:29.665
taste like that sauce?

02:53:29.665 --> 02:53:32.802
You want everything to taste
exactly like that sauce?

02:53:32.802 --> 02:53:34.670
Bone app the teeth.

02:53:34.670 --> 02:53:37.073
Now let's make up an American plate

02:53:37.073 --> 02:53:40.042
of Chinese takeout with
a bunch of staple foods

02:53:40.042 --> 02:53:44.480
that I think are pretty
nationally beloved, I would say.

02:53:44.480 --> 02:53:47.483
Let's start with our sides,
our little appetizers.

02:53:47.483 --> 02:53:50.753
So, we'd probably throw a
couple of wontons on there.

02:53:50.753 --> 02:53:52.855
I'd also do some type of roll.

02:53:52.855 --> 02:53:54.090
I would usually do an egg roll,

02:53:54.090 --> 02:53:56.159
but all we've got left are spring rolls.

02:53:56.159 --> 02:53:58.795
Next, you'd have your rice.

02:53:58.795 --> 02:54:01.164
I prefer white rice.

02:54:01.164 --> 02:54:02.999
I just think it's really easy to pair

02:54:02.999 --> 02:54:04.767
with any sort of entrée.

02:54:07.470 --> 02:54:10.973
OK, I wouldn't normally eat
the full container of rice,

02:54:10.973 --> 02:54:13.643
but we're going to keep it on the plate.

02:54:13.643 --> 02:54:15.645
We've got to get some chicken.

02:54:15.645 --> 02:54:17.947
You definitely need a side of veg,

02:54:17.947 --> 02:54:19.115
so we're going to do broccoli,

02:54:19.115 --> 02:54:21.083
because I feel like that's literally

02:54:21.083 --> 02:54:23.386
at every single Chinese restaurant,

02:54:23.386 --> 02:54:24.954
they have broccoli on the side.

02:54:24.954 --> 02:54:28.224
If you ignore the huge
mountain of white rice,

02:54:28.224 --> 02:54:30.226
I think this looks pretty appetizing.

02:54:30.226 --> 02:54:32.094
The last thing I would add to this plate

02:54:32.094 --> 02:54:34.630
is probably some lo
mein, so let's do that.

02:54:34.630 --> 02:54:36.032
I didn't mention these before,

02:54:36.032 --> 02:54:37.767
but these little fried,

02:54:37.767 --> 02:54:39.769
I think they're fried noodle pieces.

02:54:39.769 --> 02:54:42.205
They come with soups,

02:54:42.205 --> 02:54:44.640
so you can just throw them in your soup.

02:54:44.640 --> 02:54:46.409
Have them with your soup.

02:54:46.409 --> 02:54:48.144
They're just good to snack on.

02:54:48.144 --> 02:54:50.613
I think this looks like a
pretty appetizing plate.

02:54:50.613 --> 02:54:52.582
This is probably the point
where folks in the UK

02:54:52.582 --> 02:54:55.351
would douse their plate in sauce.

02:54:55.351 --> 02:54:57.153
I would do things a little differently.

02:54:57.153 --> 02:54:59.322
I really like dipping egg rolls

02:54:59.322 --> 02:55:01.624
into the duck sauce, which is this one.

02:55:01.624 --> 02:55:02.658
It's so good.

02:55:02.658 --> 02:55:04.227
And some people put sauce, I think,

02:55:04.227 --> 02:55:05.628
over their rice and stuff.

02:55:05.628 --> 02:55:08.965
So we have our white rice, our wontons,

02:55:08.965 --> 02:55:11.734
our fried noodles, some spring rolls,

02:55:11.734 --> 02:55:14.604
a side of broccoli, sesame chicken,

02:55:14.604 --> 02:55:18.574
and the lo mein, which I
think, this kind of sums up

02:55:18.574 --> 02:55:21.444
what a typical order
from a Chinese food place

02:55:21.444 --> 02:55:23.045
would look like here in America.

02:55:23.880 --> 02:55:27.450
If it doesn't look like what
you would order, sorry, babe.

02:55:27.450 --> 02:55:28.818
I don't know what to tell you.

02:55:28.818 --> 02:55:30.086
This is just what I think.

02:55:30.086 --> 02:55:31.821
I can't even believe I
didn't mention it yet.

02:55:31.821 --> 02:55:35.424
You have to get a can of Coke on the side.

02:55:35.424 --> 02:55:37.860
Any soft drink really will do.

02:55:37.860 --> 02:55:40.530
I just think you need a can of soda

02:55:40.530 --> 02:55:41.931
with your Chinese takeout.

02:55:41.931 --> 02:55:44.700
It's just, it's a sensation like no other.

02:55:44.700 --> 02:55:46.035
It goes together.

02:55:50.373 --> 02:55:52.074
In the UK when we order
food to be delivered,

02:55:52.074 --> 02:55:53.809
we say we're ordering "a takeaway"

02:55:53.809 --> 02:55:55.378
rather than ordering "takeout."

02:55:55.378 --> 02:55:56.812
Also in the UK, we tend not to bother

02:55:56.812 --> 02:55:58.614
adding with the words "food" or "takeaway"

02:55:58.614 --> 02:56:00.016
to the type of food we're eating.

02:56:00.016 --> 02:56:01.884
For example, if we're
ordering Chinese takeout,

02:56:01.884 --> 02:56:04.086
most Brits will just say that
we're having "a Chinese."

02:56:04.086 --> 02:56:05.688
Same applies for maybe Indian food,

02:56:05.688 --> 02:56:06.856
when you have "an Indian,"

02:56:06.856 --> 02:56:08.391
or even a full English breakfast.

02:56:08.391 --> 02:56:10.293
In the US we call it "takeout."

02:56:10.293 --> 02:56:11.894
They obviously both mean the same thing,

02:56:11.894 --> 02:56:13.829
but that's just our
preferred way of saying it.

02:56:13.829 --> 02:56:16.198
We also say "Chinese
food" or "Chinese takeout"

02:56:16.198 --> 02:56:18.301
rather than just "a Chinese."

02:56:18.301 --> 02:56:19.769
We also wanted to look at how each country

02:56:19.769 --> 02:56:20.903
packages its food.

02:56:20.903 --> 02:56:22.705
In the UK, you might
find your Chinese food

02:56:22.705 --> 02:56:25.508
in one of these foiled
boxes with a paper lid.

02:56:25.508 --> 02:56:26.909
The other most common form of packaging

02:56:26.909 --> 02:56:28.678
is one of these clear plastic tubs.

02:56:28.678 --> 02:56:30.379
And then finally for
your appetizer options,

02:56:30.379 --> 02:56:32.014
such as some spring rolls,
which we've got here,

02:56:32.014 --> 02:56:34.083
they generally will just
come in a paper bag.

02:56:34.083 --> 02:56:35.885
I've always seen those
folded cardboard boxes

02:56:35.885 --> 02:56:37.820
in American TV shows, and
they do look pretty cool,

02:56:37.820 --> 02:56:38.654
I have to say.

02:56:38.654 --> 02:56:40.523
Also the cute little takeout boxes

02:56:40.523 --> 02:56:42.625
are something that I
feel like we see a lot

02:56:42.625 --> 02:56:45.695
maybe in media when someone
orders Chinese food,

02:56:45.695 --> 02:56:47.997
but they're not super, super common.

02:56:47.997 --> 02:56:50.366
Mostly the food comes in
these kinds of containers,

02:56:50.366 --> 02:56:54.070
which is like a black
Tupperware with a clear top.

02:56:54.070 --> 02:56:56.706
These are usually reserved for the rice,

02:56:56.706 --> 02:56:59.408
maybe the lo mein, but it's not reserved

02:56:59.408 --> 02:57:01.978
for all of the food options.

02:57:01.978 --> 02:57:05.047
These boxes were actually
inspired by Japanese origami.

02:57:05.047 --> 02:57:07.283
Frederick Weeks Wilcox
was the first person

02:57:07.283 --> 02:57:10.853
to introduce the paper pail
box in the 19th century,

02:57:10.853 --> 02:57:12.288
to carry raw oysters.

02:57:12.288 --> 02:57:15.057
He created a patent for it
and called it a "paper pail,"

02:57:15.057 --> 02:57:17.727
which it basically is. It's
like a cute little pail.

02:57:20.363 --> 02:57:22.164
In the UK and the US, portion sizes

02:57:22.164 --> 02:57:23.599
and menu items will vary slightly

02:57:23.599 --> 02:57:25.568
depending on the different
restaurants that you go to.

02:57:25.568 --> 02:57:27.403
But we did want to compare a
couple of standard menu items

02:57:27.403 --> 02:57:28.537
in the US and the UK

02:57:28.537 --> 02:57:30.272
to see who might be
getting bigger portions.

02:57:30.272 --> 02:57:33.309
One iconic appetizer from a
Chinese takeaway in the UK

02:57:33.309 --> 02:57:34.143
is the spring roll.

02:57:34.143 --> 02:57:35.544
The restaurant that we ordered from

02:57:35.544 --> 02:57:37.880
serves these in a portion size of six.

02:57:37.880 --> 02:57:40.316
In the US, spring rolls are
made a little bit differently.

02:57:40.316 --> 02:57:41.784
They're made with rice paper,

02:57:41.784 --> 02:57:44.387
and they have thinly sliced
vegetables on the inside.

02:57:44.387 --> 02:57:46.822
This particular Chinese
restaurant only gave us two,

02:57:46.822 --> 02:57:49.191
but I have ordered from some
places that give you more.

02:57:49.191 --> 02:57:50.993
It just depends on where you order from.

02:57:50.993 --> 02:57:54.096
We do have egg rolls, which
are kind of similar to the UK.

02:57:54.096 --> 02:57:56.499
These come in only one
size, as far as I know,

02:57:56.499 --> 02:57:58.134
but depending on what
place you order from,

02:57:58.134 --> 02:57:59.468
they could be a little bit bigger,

02:57:59.468 --> 02:58:00.903
a little bit smaller than this.

02:58:00.903 --> 02:58:02.438
My local place, they're actually

02:58:02.438 --> 02:58:04.006
a little bit bigger than this size.

02:58:04.006 --> 02:58:06.809
In the UK, our egg fried
rice only came in one size.

02:58:06.809 --> 02:58:09.612
I don't want to weigh
this outside of the box,

02:58:09.612 --> 02:58:10.913
so I'm just going to weigh it in the box.

02:58:10.913 --> 02:58:12.682
This is a mountain.

02:58:12.682 --> 02:58:14.617
All of this rice just came out

02:58:14.617 --> 02:58:16.519
of this tiny little container.

02:58:16.519 --> 02:58:18.120
So, this is what our
fried rice looks like.

02:58:18.120 --> 02:58:19.555
At this particular restaurant,

02:58:19.555 --> 02:58:22.224
they only had one size of this fried rice,

02:58:22.224 --> 02:58:25.494
but I have seen places
do small and large sizes.

02:58:25.494 --> 02:58:27.963
All right, let's weigh
this mountain of rice.

02:58:29.665 --> 02:58:32.101
Harry: It's, like, just
maxed out the scales.

02:58:32.101 --> 02:58:33.502
Nico: That's a lot of rice.

02:58:33.502 --> 02:58:35.938
We do have chicken included in that.

02:58:35.938 --> 02:58:37.406
Just letting you know.

02:58:37.406 --> 02:58:39.208
And we did weigh the plate beforehand,

02:58:39.208 --> 02:58:41.610
so that's not part of this weight.

02:58:41.610 --> 02:58:43.312
The restaurant that we
ordered from had chow mein

02:58:43.312 --> 02:58:46.048
in two different sizes, regular and large,

02:58:46.048 --> 02:58:47.283
although the large was only available

02:58:47.283 --> 02:58:48.484
with certain varieties.

02:58:48.484 --> 02:58:50.553
So this is a chicken
chow mein regular size,

02:58:50.553 --> 02:58:53.389
and this is their house-special
chow mein in a large size.

02:58:53.389 --> 02:58:55.257
We do have chow mein here in America,

02:58:55.257 --> 02:58:58.594
but we didn't have it at
this particular restaurant.

02:58:58.594 --> 02:59:00.596
We do have pan-fried noodles,

02:59:00.596 --> 02:59:02.732
which I think are similar-ish,

02:59:02.732 --> 02:59:04.300
so we're going to weigh these.

02:59:04.300 --> 02:59:05.735
Harry: Although I feel
like it might once again

02:59:05.735 --> 02:59:07.236
be slightly too heavy for the scales.

02:59:07.236 --> 02:59:08.904
Moving on to sweet and sour chicken.

02:59:08.904 --> 02:59:12.141
That is a tried and true
classic with takeout.

02:59:12.141 --> 02:59:14.643
I think that I'm supposed
to sauce it myself,

02:59:14.643 --> 02:59:16.846
which isn't the norm for me.

02:59:16.846 --> 02:59:18.714
In my experience getting Chinese takeout,

02:59:18.714 --> 02:59:20.816
everything is already sauced.

02:59:20.816 --> 02:59:22.284
You don't need to add anything.

02:59:22.284 --> 02:59:24.587
This time, they came without sauce,

02:59:24.587 --> 02:59:26.722
so we're just going to add it ourselves.

02:59:26.722 --> 02:59:29.358
In the UK, our sweet and sour
chicken came in one size.

02:59:29.358 --> 02:59:32.027
In the UK, this consists of
little fried pieces of chicken,

02:59:32.027 --> 02:59:34.196
peppers, onions, and also pineapple.

02:59:34.196 --> 02:59:35.364
I'm getting worried that I think

02:59:35.364 --> 02:59:38.000
I was supposed to dip
the individual pieces

02:59:38.000 --> 02:59:39.702
into sauce and eat it like that.

02:59:39.702 --> 02:59:40.903
I've never ordered this.

02:59:40.903 --> 02:59:43.472
I just know it's a
common takeout item here.

02:59:43.472 --> 02:59:45.207
But I already poured
the sauce all over it,

02:59:45.207 --> 02:59:47.376
so let's just weigh it
and see how much it is.

02:59:50.846 --> 02:59:52.515
As we mentioned before,
each Chinese restaurant

02:59:52.515 --> 02:59:54.950
is unique as to how they
prepare and cook their food,

02:59:54.950 --> 02:59:56.018
so it's kind of impossible to know

02:59:56.018 --> 02:59:57.653
exactly what's going into each dish.

02:59:57.653 --> 02:59:59.321
One ingredient that we did
want to highlight, however,

02:59:59.321 --> 03:00:01.557
as it's commonly associated
with Chinese food,

03:00:01.557 --> 03:00:03.793
is MSG, or monosodium glutamate.

03:00:03.793 --> 03:00:07.062
In 1907, a Japanese
chemist named Kikunae Ikeda

03:00:07.062 --> 03:00:09.031
discovered a substance called glutamate,

03:00:09.031 --> 03:00:10.566
also known as umami.

03:00:10.566 --> 03:00:13.369
He later broke that
substance down into MSG.

03:00:13.369 --> 03:00:15.337
MSG is a seasoning and food enhancer

03:00:15.337 --> 03:00:16.939
that gives a rich, savory flavor.

03:00:16.939 --> 03:00:19.308
MSG is actually very
commonly found in fast foods

03:00:19.308 --> 03:00:22.044
including Chick-fil-A, snack
foods including Doritos,

03:00:22.044 --> 03:00:24.280
and naturally occurs in some cheeses.

03:00:24.280 --> 03:00:27.516
MSG has been recognized as
safe by the FDA several times,

03:00:27.516 --> 03:00:29.585
and only contains a third
of the sodium content

03:00:29.585 --> 03:00:31.887
of table salt. So why's
it got such a bad rap?

03:00:31.887 --> 03:00:34.223
In 1968, The New England
Journal of Medicine

03:00:34.223 --> 03:00:37.126
published a letter titled
"Chinese Restaurant Syndrome."

03:00:37.126 --> 03:00:39.228
In it, a doctor describes his experiences

03:00:39.228 --> 03:00:40.963
after eating at Chinese restaurants,

03:00:40.963 --> 03:00:43.232
citing symptoms like "numbness
in the back of the neck,"

03:00:43.232 --> 03:00:45.301
"general weakness," and "palpitations."

03:00:45.301 --> 03:00:46.669
The blame was placed on MSG,

03:00:46.669 --> 03:00:49.371
and the Chinese-restaurant-syndrome
myth quickly spread.

03:00:49.371 --> 03:00:51.907
The phrase even ended up in
the Merriam-Webster dictionary.

03:00:51.907 --> 03:00:54.210
Despite there being no evidence
that MSG was dangerous,

03:00:54.210 --> 03:00:56.011
there was still a public panic around it.

03:00:56.011 --> 03:00:58.514
Chinese restaurants in
particular were deemed unhealthy.

03:00:58.514 --> 03:01:01.050
Thankfully, the use of MSG is
much more normalized today,

03:01:01.050 --> 03:01:03.686
although the xenophobic
misinformation from the 1960s

03:01:03.686 --> 03:01:04.820
does still linger.

03:01:04.820 --> 03:01:07.223
If you Google "MSG," some of
the suggested search terms

03:01:07.223 --> 03:01:09.925
include things like, "Why
are Americans afraid of MSG?"

03:01:09.925 --> 03:01:12.161
and, "Is MSG a neurotoxin?"

03:01:12.161 --> 03:01:13.562
Short answer, no, it's not.

03:01:16.432 --> 03:01:18.267
The first Chinese restaurants
to appear in the UK

03:01:18.267 --> 03:01:19.668
were called eating houses.

03:01:19.668 --> 03:01:20.803
They would serve Chinese sailors

03:01:20.803 --> 03:01:22.371
who would dock in British ports.

03:01:22.371 --> 03:01:24.240
This goes as far back as the 19th century

03:01:24.240 --> 03:01:26.175
when Hong Kong was part
of the British empire.

03:01:26.175 --> 03:01:28.110
Notable locations for
this included Limehouse,

03:01:28.110 --> 03:01:29.845
which is part of the East End of London

03:01:29.845 --> 03:01:32.014
and was actually where the
original Chinatown was.

03:01:32.014 --> 03:01:33.549
The East End of London
was particularly hit

03:01:33.549 --> 03:01:35.184
by bombing raids in the Second World War,

03:01:35.184 --> 03:01:37.253
which caused Chinatown
to relocate to Soho,

03:01:37.253 --> 03:01:38.087
where it is today.

03:01:38.087 --> 03:01:40.222
The first recorded Cantonese
restaurant in London

03:01:40.222 --> 03:01:43.292
was a place called Cathay,
and it was opened in 1908.

03:01:43.292 --> 03:01:45.027
It was opened by a former ship's chef,

03:01:45.027 --> 03:01:46.195
a guy called Chung Koon.

03:01:46.195 --> 03:01:48.097
It was Chung Koon's son
who opened a restaurant

03:01:48.097 --> 03:01:49.899
in Bayswater decades later,

03:01:49.899 --> 03:01:51.433
which might have been the kind of origin

03:01:51.433 --> 03:01:53.102
of the Chinese takeaway in London.

03:01:53.102 --> 03:01:54.503
Apparently, the food was so popular

03:01:54.503 --> 03:01:55.771
that if customers couldn't get a table,

03:01:55.771 --> 03:01:57.473
they would simply ask for the food to go.

03:01:57.473 --> 03:01:58.807
Chung Koon's restaurant, Maxim,

03:01:58.807 --> 03:02:00.509
had a very popular dish consisting of pork

03:02:00.509 --> 03:02:02.811
and a sweet and sour sauce called jar jow.

03:02:02.811 --> 03:02:05.247
While the origins of the dish
are specifically Cantonese,

03:02:05.247 --> 03:02:07.583
it just became known as
Chinese food to Britons.

03:02:07.583 --> 03:02:09.385
The first known Chinese
restaurant in the US

03:02:09.385 --> 03:02:13.255
was called Canton and opened
in 1849 in San Francisco.

03:02:13.255 --> 03:02:15.357
The 1850s saw the appeal of the Gold Rush,

03:02:15.357 --> 03:02:17.293
so to escape worse conditions in China,

03:02:17.293 --> 03:02:19.495
there was a flood of Chinese
immigrants in this time.

03:02:19.495 --> 03:02:21.897
Restaurants began popping
up to feed Chinese workers

03:02:21.897 --> 03:02:23.365
who missed their cuisine from home,

03:02:23.365 --> 03:02:24.733
modifying their favorite dishes

03:02:24.733 --> 03:02:27.002
using ingredients that
they could find in the US.

03:02:27.002 --> 03:02:29.071
In 1882, the Chinese Exclusion Act

03:02:29.071 --> 03:02:31.674
forbade Chinese immigrants
from coming to America.

03:02:31.674 --> 03:02:33.542
An exception was made for restaurateurs,

03:02:33.542 --> 03:02:35.044
who could apply for merchant visas,

03:02:35.044 --> 03:02:37.112
which saw a boom in Chinese restaurants

03:02:37.112 --> 03:02:38.948
opening up in the 1910s.

03:02:38.948 --> 03:02:40.182
Any new restaurants that opened

03:02:40.182 --> 03:02:41.650
had to be considered "high-grade"

03:02:41.650 --> 03:02:43.585
to be within the 1882 act,

03:02:43.585 --> 03:02:45.955
so the owners lavishly
decorated the restaurants,

03:02:45.955 --> 03:02:48.524
and by 1943, the act was revoked.

03:02:48.524 --> 03:02:50.726
During this period,
dishes such as chop suey

03:02:50.726 --> 03:02:53.529
and egg foo young were
popularized across the country,

03:02:53.529 --> 03:02:56.131
especially the spread of chop suey houses.

03:02:56.131 --> 03:02:58.000
Chop suey was made from food scraps.

03:02:58.000 --> 03:03:00.669
Its name basically means leftovers.

03:03:00.669 --> 03:03:02.438
Male migrants who came over from China

03:03:02.438 --> 03:03:04.073
had little to no kitchen skills,

03:03:04.073 --> 03:03:06.442
and ingredients were different
from those back home,

03:03:06.442 --> 03:03:08.110
so they threw together
what they could find,

03:03:08.110 --> 03:03:10.379
creating a dish that
was very easy to make.

03:03:10.379 --> 03:03:12.982
Chop suey was a popular
Chinese American dish

03:03:12.982 --> 03:03:16.251
up until the 1960s, after
which General Tso's chicken

03:03:16.251 --> 03:03:19.054
kind of became the new popular thing.

03:03:19.054 --> 03:03:21.056
Also during this time,
Chinese food began to be

03:03:21.056 --> 03:03:23.325
preserved in cans for
pickling and freezing

03:03:23.325 --> 03:03:25.027
because the Great
Depression meant that people

03:03:25.027 --> 03:03:27.129
were looking for food
that was more affordable.

03:03:27.129 --> 03:03:29.798
Popularizing the chop suey
houses was a turning point

03:03:29.798 --> 03:03:32.701
for integrating Chinese
cuisine into American culture,

03:03:32.701 --> 03:03:35.471
even if the dish was
eventually less popular.

03:03:35.471 --> 03:03:37.339
Chinese food increased
in popularity in the UK

03:03:37.339 --> 03:03:38.874
during and after World War II.

03:03:38.874 --> 03:03:41.176
Recipes for Chinese cakes
as well as other simple

03:03:41.176 --> 03:03:43.712
Chinese recipes were
introduced to Brits by the BBC,

03:03:43.712 --> 03:03:45.414
and as some Brits returned
from being stationed

03:03:45.414 --> 03:03:46.949
in the Far East, they
brought back with them

03:03:46.949 --> 03:03:48.283
a taste for Chinese cuisine.

03:03:48.283 --> 03:03:49.551
An influx of Cantonese immigrants

03:03:49.551 --> 03:03:51.353
arriving from Hong Kong in the 1950s

03:03:51.353 --> 03:03:53.856
brought with it a wave of
Chinese restaurants to the UK.

03:03:53.856 --> 03:03:55.858
These restaurants really
blew up in popularity.

03:03:55.858 --> 03:03:57.326
The restaurants grew so popular, in fact,

03:03:57.326 --> 03:03:59.695
that Billy Butlin introduced
chop suey and chips

03:03:59.695 --> 03:04:01.764
to the restaurant menus
at his holiday camps.

03:04:01.764 --> 03:04:02.865
One trend that you also saw

03:04:02.865 --> 03:04:04.400
was Chinese families taking over

03:04:04.400 --> 03:04:05.768
old fish and chip restaurants.

03:04:05.768 --> 03:04:07.036
This is why you still see things

03:04:07.036 --> 03:04:09.471
like fish and chips or
battered sausage and chips

03:04:09.471 --> 03:04:11.540
on the menu at some Chinese restaurants.

03:04:11.540 --> 03:04:14.209
Around 1960, The Mandarin
opened in San Francisco,

03:04:14.209 --> 03:04:16.845
introducing Americans to
Chinese food beyond chop suey,

03:04:16.845 --> 03:04:19.214
like hot and sour soup and pot stickers.

03:04:19.214 --> 03:04:21.016
Cecilia Chiang, the
owner of the restaurant,

03:04:21.016 --> 03:04:22.985
wasn't sure what food
customers would want,

03:04:22.985 --> 03:04:26.021
so she added over 300 items to the menu.

03:04:26.021 --> 03:04:28.624
President Nixon's trip to China in 1972

03:04:28.624 --> 03:04:30.893
began to normalize relations
between the countries,

03:04:30.893 --> 03:04:33.562
and Americans were influenced
to try more Chinese food.

03:04:33.562 --> 03:04:36.065
In 1989, the National
Restaurant Association

03:04:36.065 --> 03:04:37.866
concluded that Chinese food was actually

03:04:37.866 --> 03:04:40.736
the most frequently consumed
cuisine across the country,

03:04:40.736 --> 03:04:42.037
and over the next few decades,

03:04:42.037 --> 03:04:44.440
the number of Chinese restaurants
went up by the thousands.

03:04:44.440 --> 03:04:46.975
In 2007, there were over 43,000.

03:04:46.975 --> 03:04:50.646
In the US today, there are over
23,000 Chinese restaurants.

03:04:50.646 --> 03:04:53.282
In the 1950s, there were
only 36 Chinese restaurants

03:04:53.282 --> 03:04:54.750
in the UK, but the next few decades

03:04:54.750 --> 03:04:56.485
saw that number rapidly ascend.

03:04:56.485 --> 03:04:59.855
By the late 1990s, that number
had risen to over 5,000.

03:04:59.855 --> 03:05:01.690
Today, there are a little over 7,000

03:05:01.690 --> 03:05:03.559
Chinese restaurants up and down the UK.

03:05:03.559 --> 03:05:05.160
According to a survey, Chinese food

03:05:05.160 --> 03:05:07.496
is the second-most-popular
cuisine in the US,

03:05:07.496 --> 03:05:10.199
right behind pizza, as of June 2023.

03:05:10.199 --> 03:05:12.301
A 2001 poll said that Chinese food

03:05:12.301 --> 03:05:14.336
was British people's
favorite foreign cuisine.

03:05:14.369 --> 03:05:16.271
Harry: From exclusive
items to portion sizes,

03:05:16.271 --> 03:05:17.639
we wanted to find out all the differences

03:05:17.639 --> 03:05:20.342
between Cadbury products
in the US and the UK.

03:05:20.342 --> 03:05:21.777
This is "Food Wars."

03:05:24.713 --> 03:05:26.348
In the UK, classic Dairy Milk chocolate

03:05:26.348 --> 03:05:28.050
comes in a bunch of sizes.

03:05:28.050 --> 03:05:30.652
The smallest one isn't sold individually,

03:05:30.652 --> 03:05:33.388
but it's one of these
selection-box bite-size pieces.

03:05:33.388 --> 03:05:36.158
Then we move up to one
of these little bars.

03:05:36.158 --> 03:05:38.060
These are sold mostly
as a snack for children,

03:05:38.060 --> 03:05:39.862
and they weigh 18 grams each.

03:05:39.862 --> 03:05:43.499
Then we have a multipack
bar, which weighs 33.5 grams.

03:05:43.499 --> 03:05:45.400
Then we have what I would
say is the standard bar,

03:05:45.400 --> 03:05:46.835
which is 45 grams.

03:05:46.835 --> 03:05:49.138
If you're feeling hungry,
you can have a duo bar.

03:05:49.138 --> 03:05:50.572
As you can see by the
crease in the middle,

03:05:50.572 --> 03:05:53.408
it's kind of separated
into two smaller bars,

03:05:53.408 --> 03:05:56.078
and in total it weighs 54.4 grams.

03:05:56.078 --> 03:05:57.312
In terms of standard Dairy Milk,

03:05:57.312 --> 03:06:00.048
you may also be able
to find a 95-gram bar.

03:06:00.048 --> 03:06:02.451
We actually found a
90-gram bar of Caramilk.

03:06:02.451 --> 03:06:04.086
The portion size varies slightly depending

03:06:04.086 --> 03:06:05.687
on what type of chocolate you're getting.

03:06:05.687 --> 03:06:08.123
Moving more in towards the larger sizes,

03:06:08.123 --> 03:06:10.559
we have a 110-gram bar of Dairy Milk.

03:06:10.559 --> 03:06:13.061
Up from that, we have a 180-gram bar.

03:06:13.061 --> 03:06:17.132
Our second-biggest size
is this 360-gram bar.

03:06:17.132 --> 03:06:20.636
And finally, our biggest size is this one.

03:06:22.304 --> 03:06:24.907
That's 850 grams of Dairy Milk.

03:06:24.907 --> 03:06:27.743
In the US, our Dairy Milk
bars come in one size,

03:06:27.743 --> 03:06:31.747
the 3.5-ounce, or 99.2-gram, bar.

03:06:31.747 --> 03:06:34.383
Now, this bar right next
to it, the Caramello,

03:06:34.383 --> 03:06:38.520
is actually 4 ounces, or 113.4 grams.

03:06:38.520 --> 03:06:40.656
Right here in the middle,
there's a fruit and nut size

03:06:40.656 --> 03:06:44.493
that's 5.4 ounces, or 153 grams.

03:06:44.493 --> 03:06:47.663
I couldn't get it in time.

03:06:47.663 --> 03:06:52.334
But our, I guess, biggest
size, or other size, is this,

03:06:52.334 --> 03:06:55.504
a bag of miniatures,
where it is an 8-ounce,

03:06:55.504 --> 03:06:59.775
or [227]-gram, bag, but it's
all these little guys in there.

03:06:59.775 --> 03:07:03.111
Our Cadbury bar, the 99.2-gram bar,

03:07:03.111 --> 03:07:05.948
comes in 18 squares per bar.

03:07:05.948 --> 03:07:09.318
If you divide it up,
it's 6 grams per square.

03:07:09.318 --> 03:07:11.587
For comparison, we'll take
a look at our 110-gram bar

03:07:11.587 --> 03:07:12.921
of Dairy Milk in the UK.

03:07:12.921 --> 03:07:15.090
One of these bars has
24 individual squares.

03:07:15.090 --> 03:07:17.092
We're going to find out how
much one of those weighs.

03:07:17.092 --> 03:07:19.528
So, according to our slightly
inaccurate analog scales,

03:07:19.528 --> 03:07:21.563
one square weighs around 6 grams.

03:07:21.563 --> 03:07:24.433
Although if we divide
the 110 figure by 24,

03:07:24.433 --> 03:07:27.302
we end up with around 4.5,
so somewhere between there.

03:07:27.302 --> 03:07:30.272
So there's no difference.
Good on you, Cadbury.

03:07:33.175 --> 03:07:34.743
Here are all the Cadbury
products from the UK

03:07:34.743 --> 03:07:36.278
that you won't find in the US.

03:07:36.278 --> 03:07:38.480
And here are all the
Cadbury products from the US

03:07:38.480 --> 03:07:40.115
you won't find in the UK.

03:07:40.115 --> 03:07:41.917
This is going to be an easy one for me.

03:07:41.917 --> 03:07:43.752
Now, I want to remind the audience

03:07:43.752 --> 03:07:45.754
that this is for Cadbury products produced

03:07:45.754 --> 03:07:47.089
in our respective countries.

03:07:47.089 --> 03:07:50.259
Yes, all this stuff is
available on Amazon. We know.

03:07:50.259 --> 03:07:51.226
Now, obviously we have a ton

03:07:51.226 --> 03:07:52.494
of exclusive products here in the UK,

03:07:52.494 --> 03:07:54.129
so we're going to break
them down into categories.

03:07:54.129 --> 03:07:56.031
We'll start with standard Dairy Milk bars

03:07:56.031 --> 03:07:57.199
but with different flavorings.

03:07:57.199 --> 03:07:58.367
We're going to start off with a bang here

03:07:58.367 --> 03:08:00.435
with what's possibly my
favorite just standard

03:08:00.435 --> 03:08:02.804
Dairy Milk bar, which is Cadbury wholenut.

03:08:02.804 --> 03:08:04.139
It's basically fruit and nut

03:08:04.139 --> 03:08:05.874
but with none of that
gross fruit in there.

03:08:05.874 --> 03:08:10.679
It's just the nuts.
Fruit and nut, wholenut.

03:08:10.679 --> 03:08:12.214
Now, depending on what
time of year you're looking

03:08:12.214 --> 03:08:13.582
for your Cadbury in the supermarket,

03:08:13.582 --> 03:08:15.717
you might be able to
find orange Dairy Milk,

03:08:15.717 --> 03:08:18.954
but because we're in the
buildup to the festive season,

03:08:18.954 --> 03:08:21.356
we got winter orange crisp instead.

03:08:21.356 --> 03:08:23.659
Chocolate and orange is kind
of a beloved flavor combination

03:08:23.659 --> 03:08:25.761
here in the UK. I think a
lot of people will think

03:08:25.761 --> 03:08:27.162
of Terry's chocolate oranges,

03:08:27.162 --> 03:08:29.031
but Cadbury's are getting
in on the act too.

03:08:29.031 --> 03:08:31.933
Next up, we have Cadbury Dairy Milk Oreo.

03:08:31.933 --> 03:08:33.502
You might see a few of
these as we go through.

03:08:33.502 --> 03:08:35.203
They seem to have some kind
of partnership going on

03:08:35.203 --> 03:08:37.239
with Oreo because there's
actually quite a few

03:08:37.239 --> 03:08:39.308
Dairy Milk x Oreo products.

03:08:39.308 --> 03:08:40.809
I'll open this one up
because it's actually

03:08:40.809 --> 03:08:42.311
quite cool what they've done, to be fair.

03:08:42.311 --> 03:08:43.745
From the outside, it looks

03:08:43.745 --> 03:08:46.448
just like a standard piece of Dairy Milk,

03:08:46.448 --> 03:08:50.085
but if you see, they've actually
filled it with Oreo cream.

03:08:51.687 --> 03:08:53.422
But they also put little
pieces of the biscuit in,

03:08:53.422 --> 03:08:54.956
so you get these crunchy bits as well.

03:08:54.956 --> 03:08:56.258
If the Cadbury Dairy Milk Oreo

03:08:56.258 --> 03:08:58.193
somehow isn't sweet enough for you,

03:08:58.193 --> 03:09:00.862
you can also get a white
chocolate Oreo bar.

03:09:00.862 --> 03:09:02.397
We're sticking with the classics here,

03:09:02.397 --> 03:09:04.499
and we're moving on to Dairy Milk caramel.

03:09:04.499 --> 03:09:06.435
Now, I know I said that this
might have been too sweet

03:09:06.435 --> 03:09:09.438
for me, but I actually do
really like the caramel bars.

03:09:09.438 --> 03:09:11.573
So, what you get is Dairy Milk chocolate,

03:09:11.573 --> 03:09:15.177
but then you have this quite
liquidy caramel on the inside.

03:09:17.479 --> 03:09:20.048
The caramel, it is
sweet, but it's not like,

03:09:20.048 --> 03:09:21.683
it's not like you're just
tasting sugar, you know?

03:09:21.683 --> 03:09:22.884
There is something else going on there.

03:09:22.884 --> 03:09:25.554
We did see that in the US,
you guys have Caramello.

03:09:25.554 --> 03:09:27.322
Is that kind of the same thing?

03:09:27.322 --> 03:09:28.190
Joe, what do you think?

03:09:28.190 --> 03:09:32.694
Hi, Harry, and, yes, we don't
have the Dairy Milk caramel.

03:09:32.694 --> 03:09:35.330
We have the Dairy Milk Caramello.

03:09:35.330 --> 03:09:38.033
I remember Caramello for
a while was a US brand.

03:09:38.033 --> 03:09:40.335
I remember the commercials. It went like,

03:09:40.335 --> 03:09:42.471
♪ Caramello ♪

03:09:42.471 --> 03:09:45.407
So that's yours. Let's
take a look at ours.

03:09:45.407 --> 03:09:48.143
Here's ours. It's already
kind of coming out there.

03:09:48.143 --> 03:09:50.779
It's caramel. Heavy, rich, I guess.

03:09:50.779 --> 03:09:52.114
Yeah, can't really get a straight answer,

03:09:52.114 --> 03:09:54.015
if Cadbury just bought Caramello in the US

03:09:54.015 --> 03:09:55.550
and repackaged it or what,

03:09:55.550 --> 03:09:58.754
but it looks like it's
probably relatively the same.

03:09:58.754 --> 03:10:00.088
Sticking with the caramel theme,

03:10:00.088 --> 03:10:02.724
we now have the new salted caramel bar.

03:10:02.724 --> 03:10:04.693
I feel like, I don't know if
this is the case in the US,

03:10:04.693 --> 03:10:07.596
but there's been a real
salted caramel takeover

03:10:07.596 --> 03:10:09.231
in the past couple of years, to the point

03:10:09.231 --> 03:10:12.734
where it's very hard to
find unsalted caramel now,

03:10:12.734 --> 03:10:16.405
and I suppose Cadbury
had to get on the trend.

03:10:17.639 --> 03:10:20.842
I really don't think I taste
much, if any, difference at all

03:10:20.842 --> 03:10:22.210
between that and the regular caramel.

03:10:22.210 --> 03:10:24.913
Since I have nothing else
to do for this episode,

03:10:24.913 --> 03:10:26.114
I'm going to go ahead and try some

03:10:26.114 --> 03:10:29.151
of this UK Cadbury I'm
hearing so much about,

03:10:29.151 --> 03:10:33.722
starting with the OG
Cadbury Dairy Milk bar.

03:10:37.492 --> 03:10:42.063
Mm, yeah. This is a much smoother, nicer,

03:10:42.063 --> 03:10:44.433
richer, creamier chocolate.

03:10:44.433 --> 03:10:46.701
I do really love this. It's fantastic.

03:10:46.701 --> 03:10:48.637
You brag a lot about your chocolate, UK,

03:10:48.637 --> 03:10:50.505
but another thing you
should be bragging about

03:10:50.505 --> 03:10:52.441
is your caramel. It's also really good

03:10:52.441 --> 03:10:54.376
and way better than what
we have here in America.

03:10:54.376 --> 03:10:56.244
So, the Caramellos from earlier,

03:10:56.244 --> 03:10:59.781
just not the biggest fan of those anymore.

03:10:59.781 --> 03:11:00.615
I liked them when I was a kid,

03:11:00.615 --> 03:11:03.218
but caramel in America is just too bleh.

03:11:03.218 --> 03:11:05.320
But yours, oh, yeah.

03:11:06.421 --> 03:11:08.557
I mean, pulls apart nicer.

03:11:08.557 --> 03:11:10.292
Now, this one got cheers of approval

03:11:10.292 --> 03:11:11.860
from the crew when I mentioned it.

03:11:11.860 --> 03:11:14.029
This is the Cadbury Dairy Milk Daim bar.

03:11:14.029 --> 03:11:16.531
Now, for those who don't
know, Diam is a chocolate bar

03:11:16.531 --> 03:11:18.066
in its own right, and it's,
like, a layer of chocolate

03:11:18.066 --> 03:11:20.268
on the outside with quite a hard layer

03:11:20.268 --> 03:11:22.170
of toffee caramel on the inside.

03:11:22.170 --> 03:11:24.172
Now, Daim bars themselves I
think have maybe fallen out

03:11:24.172 --> 03:11:26.708
of favor a little bit in the
UK. They were very popular

03:11:26.708 --> 03:11:28.743
with my granddad's generation,

03:11:28.743 --> 03:11:30.111
and I think people don't really buy them

03:11:30.111 --> 03:11:31.179
on their own anymore.

03:11:31.179 --> 03:11:33.081
However, I do say they are delicious.

03:11:33.081 --> 03:11:35.617
You can also still buy
them in places like Ikea.

03:11:35.617 --> 03:11:37.853
I think they're popular kind
of on continental Europe.

03:11:37.853 --> 03:11:39.354
Next up, we have another new bar.

03:11:39.354 --> 03:11:41.990
It's the Cadbury white confetti bar.

03:11:41.990 --> 03:11:43.158
I've never seen this before,

03:11:43.158 --> 03:11:45.327
before I went to do the
shopping for this episode,

03:11:45.327 --> 03:11:49.231
but it's white chocolate, and
it contains oat biscuit pieces

03:11:49.231 --> 03:11:51.433
as well as milk chocolate beans.

03:11:51.433 --> 03:11:54.202
But you do see quite a few
of these biscuity pieces.

03:11:55.604 --> 03:11:56.438
Oop.

03:11:56.438 --> 03:11:59.040
Yeah. I get a lot of the biscuit.

03:11:59.040 --> 03:12:01.176
Not much of the chocolate pieces.

03:12:01.176 --> 03:12:03.979
You may also be able to
find a mint Dairy Milk,

03:12:03.979 --> 03:12:05.714
a Crunchie pieces Dairy Milk,

03:12:05.714 --> 03:12:07.849
and also Dairy Milk with 30% less sugar,

03:12:07.849 --> 03:12:09.651
but I wasn't able to
track them down today.

03:12:09.651 --> 03:12:10.952
Then we have a couple of Dairy Milk bars

03:12:10.952 --> 03:12:13.655
with either slightly different
compositions or flavors.

03:12:13.655 --> 03:12:16.491
Firstly, we have Dairy
Milk marvelous creations.

03:12:16.491 --> 03:12:18.960
This one has jelly popping candy inside.

03:12:18.960 --> 03:12:20.996
Don't quote me on this,
but I feel like back

03:12:20.996 --> 03:12:24.165
in the early 2000s in the
UK, we had a Wonka series

03:12:24.165 --> 03:12:27.536
of chocolate bars, which
were inspired by Willy Wonka.

03:12:27.536 --> 03:12:29.404
Now, I think that was
actually a Nestlé product,

03:12:29.404 --> 03:12:31.072
but they had interesting flavors.

03:12:31.072 --> 03:12:32.440
They did things like putting jelly beans

03:12:32.440 --> 03:12:34.442
and popping candy into
their chocolate bars.

03:12:34.442 --> 03:12:36.645
They've disappeared. Don't
know what happened to them,

03:12:36.645 --> 03:12:38.146
but I feel like this is Cadbury's attempt

03:12:38.146 --> 03:12:40.582
to get in on that, bring
that back a little bit.

03:12:40.582 --> 03:12:41.583
Probably pretty well timed

03:12:41.583 --> 03:12:43.852
because, you know, the
"Wonka" movie's out soon.

03:12:43.852 --> 03:12:46.555
It's going to really date this
video, but we love Chalamet.

03:12:46.555 --> 03:12:47.389
These are pretty good.

03:12:47.389 --> 03:12:49.958
I do think it's an interesting
textural experience

03:12:49.958 --> 03:12:50.792
when you eat one of these,

03:12:50.792 --> 03:12:53.161
because it's the kind of
chewiness of the jelly bean

03:12:53.161 --> 03:12:55.597
mixed in with the melty milk chocolate.

03:12:55.597 --> 03:12:57.832
Not my favorite, but, hey,

03:12:57.832 --> 03:12:59.100
if you want something
a little bit different,

03:12:59.100 --> 03:13:00.335
then this is for you.

03:13:00.335 --> 03:13:02.470
Then we're on to the big taste bars.

03:13:02.470 --> 03:13:05.473
This is the Dairy Milk
big taste Oreo crunch.

03:13:05.473 --> 03:13:07.342
I did mention there was
a theme of Dairy Milk

03:13:07.342 --> 03:13:08.577
and Oreo crossover products,

03:13:08.577 --> 03:13:10.345
and this is the next one in that line.

03:13:10.345 --> 03:13:11.646
So, with the big taste bars,

03:13:11.646 --> 03:13:14.649
they are thicker than a
standard chocolate bar would be,

03:13:14.649 --> 03:13:16.918
and because of that, they
have layers going on.

03:13:16.918 --> 03:13:18.920
Basically double the height.

03:13:18.920 --> 03:13:20.822
So, here is the standard Dairy Milk Oreo,

03:13:20.822 --> 03:13:23.191
and here is the big taste Oreo.

03:13:23.191 --> 03:13:24.593
As you can see, you get much more

03:13:24.593 --> 03:13:26.328
distinct layers on this one.

03:13:29.331 --> 03:13:30.165
I'm going to be honest,

03:13:30.165 --> 03:13:32.334
I think I just prefer the standard one.

03:13:32.334 --> 03:13:34.569
One iconic Cadbury product is the Freddo.

03:13:34.569 --> 03:13:36.871
A Freddo is a small, individually
wrapped chocolate bar

03:13:36.871 --> 03:13:38.340
that's in the shape of a frog.

03:13:38.340 --> 03:13:39.507
You can get two versions of this.

03:13:39.507 --> 03:13:40.775
You can either get a standard one,

03:13:40.775 --> 03:13:42.377
which is just milk chocolate,

03:13:42.377 --> 03:13:44.045
or you can get a caramel Freddo.

03:13:44.045 --> 03:13:45.580
Much like a Dairy Milk caramel bar,

03:13:45.580 --> 03:13:47.349
it's a shell of milk chocolate

03:13:47.349 --> 03:13:49.417
with that kind of liquidy
caramel on the inside.

03:13:49.417 --> 03:13:51.086
I love Freddos. I feel
like a lot of Brits have

03:13:51.086 --> 03:13:52.587
a nostalgic attachment to them.

03:13:52.587 --> 03:13:54.389
I can't tell if they've
shrunk over the years

03:13:54.389 --> 03:13:56.691
or if I've just got
bigger, or possibly both,

03:13:56.691 --> 03:13:58.226
but I do feel like back in the day,

03:13:58.226 --> 03:13:59.060
when you're a little kid,

03:13:59.060 --> 03:14:01.296
these are a nice little snack-sized treat,

03:14:01.296 --> 03:14:02.964
whereas nowadays this is a mouthful.

03:14:02.964 --> 03:14:04.065
This is kind of tiny.

03:14:04.065 --> 03:14:05.600
They're also worth pointing
out because in the UK,

03:14:05.600 --> 03:14:08.003
they've become an unlikely
economic yardstick.

03:14:08.003 --> 03:14:09.771
When I was a kid, they cost just 10 pence,

03:14:09.771 --> 03:14:12.641
but the price has risen drastically
over the past few years.

03:14:12.641 --> 03:14:15.810
At one point in 2017, the
price rose as high as 30 p

03:14:15.810 --> 03:14:18.146
for one of these, whereas I
think it's now dropped back down

03:14:18.146 --> 03:14:20.982
to 25 after probably a
lot of public outcry.

03:14:20.982 --> 03:14:22.117
Because of the fluctuating price,

03:14:22.117 --> 03:14:23.752
they've kind of become
a measure of inflation

03:14:23.752 --> 03:14:25.220
and cost of living here in the UK.

03:14:25.220 --> 03:14:26.554
People's paychecks do not buy them

03:14:26.554 --> 03:14:29.457
as many Freddos as they used
to, and that is a sad thing.

03:14:29.457 --> 03:14:30.925
That's funny, Harry. In the US,

03:14:30.925 --> 03:14:33.995
Cadbury tried what I think
are similar shenanigans

03:14:33.995 --> 03:14:35.930
by decreasing the size of the products.

03:14:35.930 --> 03:14:38.333
Back in 2007, US comedian and actor

03:14:38.333 --> 03:14:41.736
B.J. Novak outed Cadbury
on the Conan O'Brien show

03:14:41.736 --> 03:14:44.172
for suddenly making Cadbury eggs smaller.

03:14:44.172 --> 03:14:45.473
In the UK, we also have a couple

03:14:45.473 --> 03:14:47.175
of plant-based-chocolate options.

03:14:47.175 --> 03:14:48.843
They're made with almonds instead of dairy

03:14:48.843 --> 03:14:51.179
in order to try and replicate
a milk chocolate feel,

03:14:51.179 --> 03:14:52.180
and they come in two forms,

03:14:52.180 --> 03:14:54.783
either as a classic bar
or as a salted caramel.

03:14:56.151 --> 03:14:57.318
It's actually not that bad.

03:14:57.318 --> 03:14:58.653
They've done really well, I think,

03:14:58.653 --> 03:15:00.922
to replicate the texture
of milk chocolate,

03:15:00.922 --> 03:15:03.625
because as it kind of
claims on the packaging,

03:15:03.625 --> 03:15:05.293
"smooth" I think is the word.

03:15:05.293 --> 03:15:06.828
It really is quite a smooth experience.

03:15:06.828 --> 03:15:07.796
It does kind of melt in your mouth,

03:15:07.796 --> 03:15:09.297
almost like a milk chocolate bar would.

03:15:09.297 --> 03:15:11.232
I think what's letting
it down really for me is

03:15:11.232 --> 03:15:13.268
still the taste of it
rather than the texture.

03:15:13.268 --> 03:15:16.137
It tastes almost more like
a sort of cooking chocolate,

03:15:16.137 --> 03:15:17.872
or like, you know when
you get those really cheap

03:15:17.872 --> 03:15:19.407
chocolate chips and it feels like

03:15:19.407 --> 03:15:22.477
there isn't enough cocoa
or creaminess to them?

03:15:22.477 --> 03:15:26.481
We have these four exclusive
bars here in the US.

03:15:26.481 --> 03:15:30.919
We got rock the road,
which is milk chocolate

03:15:30.919 --> 03:15:34.556
with roasted almonds,
marshmallows, and fudge.

03:15:36.057 --> 03:15:36.991
No, I don't like that.

03:15:36.991 --> 03:15:39.561
We have a royal dark dark chocolate.

03:15:39.561 --> 03:15:42.464
I'm sure you have several dark
chocolates. Ours is royal.

03:15:42.464 --> 03:15:43.965
Not a big dark chocolate guy.

03:15:45.633 --> 03:15:47.902
Not as bitter as I expect
from dark chocolate,

03:15:47.902 --> 03:15:51.272
so not bad, but, eh, I don't know.

03:15:51.272 --> 03:15:52.440
Again, just OK.

03:15:52.440 --> 03:15:53.775
Roasted almond.

03:15:55.176 --> 03:15:57.245
Hm, generous with the almonds, right?

03:15:57.245 --> 03:16:00.181
It's a pretty good almond haul.

03:16:00.181 --> 03:16:04.385
Last of our exclusive flavors
are Black Forest cake,

03:16:04.385 --> 03:16:08.423
which is dark chocolate
with cherry-flavored fudge

03:16:08.423 --> 03:16:09.324
and cookie pieces.

03:16:09.324 --> 03:16:12.694
Oh, man, OK. So, apparently, in the UK,

03:16:12.694 --> 03:16:15.430
you would call this a Black
Forest gâteau, which is funny

03:16:15.430 --> 03:16:17.866
because you didn't use the
French words for black or forest.

03:16:20.401 --> 03:16:22.070
Oh, yeah, that's right.

03:16:22.070 --> 03:16:24.539
And the Black Forest is in Germany.

03:16:24.539 --> 03:16:26.241
You guys don't know what's going on.

03:16:26.241 --> 03:16:28.843
Gonna be honest with you,
chocolate and cherry,

03:16:28.843 --> 03:16:30.545
not my favorite combo.

03:16:32.981 --> 03:16:35.483
Oh, God. Ugh! This is terrible.

03:16:35.483 --> 03:16:37.919
This is the worst one.
Oh, I hate this. Blegh.

03:16:37.919 --> 03:16:39.220
Next up, we're on to buttons.

03:16:39.220 --> 03:16:41.523
Now, Cadbury buttons are just
pieces of Dairy Milk chocolate

03:16:41.523 --> 03:16:43.892
shaped into little
buttons or little disks.

03:16:43.892 --> 03:16:46.261
Buttons come in a range
of sizes and flavors.

03:16:46.261 --> 03:16:49.230
So, the original ones are actually tiny.

03:16:49.230 --> 03:16:51.766
I feel like they've kind
of fallen out of favor,

03:16:51.766 --> 03:16:53.601
or maybe they're just only given to kids,

03:16:53.601 --> 03:16:57.505
but this is what, honestly,
the classic button looks like,

03:16:58.373 --> 03:17:01.476
and it's these really tiny
little chocolate disks.

03:17:01.476 --> 03:17:03.812
It's literally just plain
Dairy Milk chocolate.

03:17:04.679 --> 03:17:08.049
The ones that I would more
commonly go for in my adult life

03:17:08.049 --> 03:17:10.618
are the ones which are served
in these big sharing bags,

03:17:10.618 --> 03:17:12.620
and they're more like this size.

03:17:12.620 --> 03:17:13.888
I'd say that's, like,

03:17:13.888 --> 03:17:16.024
that's more than double
the size, I would say.

03:17:16.024 --> 03:17:17.192
And then if that button size

03:17:17.192 --> 03:17:18.960
is still not big enough for you,

03:17:18.960 --> 03:17:20.595
then we have mega buttons.

03:17:20.595 --> 03:17:23.698
So we have classic, giant, and mega.

03:17:23.698 --> 03:17:25.567
Just one more note on
the size of the buttons.

03:17:25.567 --> 03:17:27.769
We've observed that the classic ones,

03:17:27.769 --> 03:17:30.338
on the packet, are just called buttons,

03:17:30.338 --> 03:17:34.509
but what I think used to
be called the giant ones

03:17:34.509 --> 03:17:37.879
are also now just called
buttons, which you can see

03:17:37.879 --> 03:17:40.582
because they're still called giant buttons

03:17:40.582 --> 03:17:42.016
in the orange-flavored version.

03:17:42.016 --> 03:17:44.219
You can also get buttons
in a range of flavors.

03:17:44.219 --> 03:17:46.387
So, here we have some
mint chocolate buttons,

03:17:46.387 --> 03:17:47.822
some orange chocolate buttons.

03:17:47.822 --> 03:17:49.290
These ones are called caramel nibbles,

03:17:49.290 --> 03:17:51.059
but I always thought they
were just caramel buttons

03:17:51.059 --> 03:17:52.827
because, again, they're
kind of the disk shape

03:17:52.827 --> 03:17:54.562
but with a little pocket
of caramel in the middle.

03:17:54.562 --> 03:17:56.431
And you can also get Darkmilk buttons.

03:17:56.431 --> 03:17:58.700
Now, these are a Cadbury
invention which is somewhere

03:17:58.700 --> 03:18:00.401
between dark chocolate and milk chocolate.

03:18:00.401 --> 03:18:02.003
As well as Caramilk buttons.

03:18:02.003 --> 03:18:03.938
And if you want just
actual dark chocolate,

03:18:03.938 --> 03:18:05.607
Cadbury's calls that Bournville,

03:18:05.607 --> 03:18:08.443
and they have giant Bournville
dark chocolate buttons.

03:18:08.443 --> 03:18:11.212
Another Cadbury product in
the UK are Cadbury fingers.

03:18:11.212 --> 03:18:12.280
Now, these are a classic.

03:18:12.280 --> 03:18:14.148
I feel like these are a staple

03:18:14.148 --> 03:18:15.783
in a lot of British households.

03:18:15.783 --> 03:18:17.318
Basically what you get is a layer

03:18:17.318 --> 03:18:18.586
of biscuit along the bottom,

03:18:18.586 --> 03:18:20.688
which is then kind of
just completely dunked

03:18:20.688 --> 03:18:21.990
in Cadbury milk chocolate.

03:18:23.791 --> 03:18:25.460
Can't figure out exactly
what the biscuit is.

03:18:25.460 --> 03:18:27.328
I think it's kind of like shortbread.

03:18:27.328 --> 03:18:29.063
You can also get Cadbury
fingers in a range

03:18:29.063 --> 03:18:30.665
of sizes and flavors.

03:18:30.665 --> 03:18:33.201
For example, you can get these
little bite-sized pouches.

03:18:33.201 --> 03:18:35.203
We have the salted caramel fingers here.

03:18:35.203 --> 03:18:38.006
Then you can also find
white chocolate fingers,

03:18:38.006 --> 03:18:41.209
orange chocolate fingers, mint
chocolate fingers, and dark.

03:18:41.209 --> 03:18:42.710
One thing which has
just launched in the UK

03:18:42.710 --> 03:18:44.812
at the time of filming
is Cadbury delights.

03:18:44.812 --> 03:18:45.880
These are a new range of products

03:18:45.880 --> 03:18:48.082
which I believe are nougat
covered in chocolate.

03:18:48.082 --> 03:18:50.418
These come in the flavors
of hazelnut and caramel,

03:18:50.418 --> 03:18:52.654
orange and caramel, and salted caramel.

03:18:52.654 --> 03:18:54.622
Another exclusive we have in the US are

03:18:54.622 --> 03:18:57.792
the mini chocolate eggs, harvest eggs,

03:18:57.792 --> 03:18:59.827
but it's around the holidays right now,

03:18:59.827 --> 03:19:03.631
so we have instead mini snow balls.

03:19:03.631 --> 03:19:04.899
I feel like they're the same.

03:19:04.899 --> 03:19:06.301
These are the same. Come on.

03:19:08.102 --> 03:19:11.239
Yeah, just milk chocolate
with a crisp, sugary shell,

03:19:11.239 --> 03:19:12.473
as they say on here.

03:19:12.473 --> 03:19:14.208
Good for snacking, I think.

03:19:15.043 --> 03:19:16.311
I think these taste the best

03:19:16.311 --> 03:19:18.546
out of everything here, to be honest.

03:19:18.546 --> 03:19:20.949
Also have the Creme Eggs.
Couldn't find them anywhere!

03:19:20.949 --> 03:19:22.550
I thought they used to be
everywhere. Now they're gone.

03:19:22.550 --> 03:19:23.384
I don't know what happened to them,

03:19:23.384 --> 03:19:25.253
but you know what they look like.

03:19:25.253 --> 03:19:26.087
We have them.

03:19:26.087 --> 03:19:27.622
Tragically, we're
filming this in November,

03:19:27.622 --> 03:19:30.558
so we can't get our hands on
our seasonal egg exclusives.

03:19:30.558 --> 03:19:32.427
Cadbury's is obviously
known for Creme Eggs

03:19:32.427 --> 03:19:35.697
but also has mini eggs, which
I believe are eggcellent.

03:19:35.697 --> 03:19:37.532
In the UK, we can obviously
get the classic Creme Egg,

03:19:37.532 --> 03:19:40.501
but we can also get white
Creme Eggs and caramel eggs.

03:19:40.501 --> 03:19:41.703
We can also get classic mini eggs

03:19:41.703 --> 03:19:43.171
as well as a mini eggs bar.

03:19:43.171 --> 03:19:45.239
However, I will point out that
because it's in the run-up

03:19:45.239 --> 03:19:47.709
to Christmas, we are able
to get Heroes right now,

03:19:47.709 --> 03:19:49.143
which is a Cadbury selection box

03:19:49.143 --> 03:19:50.845
typically eaten around the holidays.

03:19:50.845 --> 03:19:52.280
As part of a Heroes box,

03:19:53.181 --> 03:19:56.150
you can get a little bite-sized
twisted Creme Egg bar.

03:19:56.150 --> 03:19:58.920
I get that you want to create
that artificial demand,

03:19:58.920 --> 03:20:00.888
but I feel like people are
going to eat Creme Eggs

03:20:00.888 --> 03:20:04.325
year-round regardless of
availability, so I feel like

03:20:04.325 --> 03:20:05.827
you should just give the
people what they want.

03:20:05.827 --> 03:20:07.128
Have Creme Eggs year-round.

03:20:07.128 --> 03:20:08.930
Same for mini eggs, please.

03:20:08.930 --> 03:20:10.631
I need those much more than Creme Eggs.

03:20:10.631 --> 03:20:12.500
Now we're on to some
exclusive chocolate bars

03:20:12.500 --> 03:20:14.335
which you'll find in
the UK but not the US.

03:20:14.335 --> 03:20:15.169
Going to go through them

03:20:15.169 --> 03:20:16.604
in a roughly alphabetical order here,

03:20:16.604 --> 03:20:18.306
starting with the Boost bar.

03:20:18.306 --> 03:20:20.208
Now, I believe when these
were first introduced,

03:20:20.208 --> 03:20:22.877
they were almost
advertised as an energy bar

03:20:22.877 --> 03:20:24.846
in chocolate form
because I think they had,

03:20:24.846 --> 03:20:27.749
like, mortifying levels of
sugar and glucose in them,

03:20:27.749 --> 03:20:29.417
whereas now I think they've
actually dialed back on that,

03:20:29.417 --> 03:20:31.219
and it's kind of just like a chocolate bar

03:20:31.219 --> 03:20:33.354
with some biscuit and
caramel in the middle.

03:20:33.354 --> 03:20:36.424
Got that Boost bar. Gimme the boost.

03:20:36.424 --> 03:20:38.760
Mm, everyone in the UK's watching me like,

03:20:38.760 --> 03:20:40.161
"This dummy's never had this before?"

03:20:40.161 --> 03:20:42.063
No, I haven't. Or if I had
it, I don't remember it.

03:20:42.063 --> 03:20:43.464
Now, you might remember from the buttons,

03:20:43.464 --> 03:20:46.200
but when you see dark
chocolate and Cadbury's,

03:20:46.200 --> 03:20:48.102
it's going to be marketed as Bournville.

03:20:48.102 --> 03:20:50.371
Comes in this iconic
red packaging as well,

03:20:50.371 --> 03:20:53.141
compared to the usual purple
colors used by Cadbury's.

03:20:53.141 --> 03:20:55.376
Fun fact, Bournville
is actually a real town

03:20:55.376 --> 03:20:56.978
which you can visit in the UK.

03:20:56.978 --> 03:20:59.013
It was created basically by Cadbury

03:20:59.013 --> 03:21:00.515
to house all of their workers,

03:21:00.515 --> 03:21:02.216
and apparently it's quite
a nice place to live.

03:21:02.216 --> 03:21:03.751
I'm not sure why they've
ended up using that name

03:21:03.751 --> 03:21:05.820
for their dark chocolate
range, but here it is.

03:21:05.820 --> 03:21:07.255
You can get it in a few different flavors.

03:21:07.255 --> 03:21:09.657
Here, for example, I have
the old Jamaica flavor,

03:21:09.657 --> 03:21:11.893
which is a dark chocolate
with rum and raisin.

03:21:11.893 --> 03:21:14.328
We also have the
Bournville mint crisp bar.

03:21:14.328 --> 03:21:15.163
You can also just get it

03:21:15.163 --> 03:21:16.998
in a standard plain dark chocolate one

03:21:16.998 --> 03:21:18.533
as well as an orange bar as well.

03:21:18.533 --> 03:21:19.967
Next up, we have Caramilk.

03:21:19.967 --> 03:21:21.169
So, as the name suggests,

03:21:21.169 --> 03:21:24.272
this is kind of like a
caramelly milk chocolate hybrid,

03:21:24.272 --> 03:21:26.340
which Cadbury's has released
over the past couple of years.

03:21:26.340 --> 03:21:27.809
What you end up with is this

03:21:27.809 --> 03:21:31.813
slightly weird yellowy golden color.

03:21:34.282 --> 03:21:35.583
Kind of weird because it's got the texture

03:21:35.583 --> 03:21:36.417
of a milk chocolate,

03:21:36.417 --> 03:21:40.221
but the flavor is much
closer to a buttery fudge.

03:21:40.221 --> 03:21:41.722
You can also get Caramilk in the form

03:21:41.722 --> 03:21:43.491
of a Caramilk crispy bar,

03:21:43.491 --> 03:21:47.061
which is Caramilk chocolate
with little crisped rice pieces.

03:21:47.061 --> 03:21:50.498
Speaking of crunch, next
up we have a Crunchie Bar.

03:21:50.498 --> 03:21:51.632
These are pretty iconic.

03:21:51.632 --> 03:21:54.102
Basically what you get is a honeycomb

03:21:54.102 --> 03:21:55.970
which is then dipped in milk chocolate

03:21:55.970 --> 03:21:57.438
to produce a Crunchie Bar.

03:21:57.438 --> 03:21:58.272
When I say honeycomb,

03:21:58.272 --> 03:22:00.141
obviously there's no
actual beehive in here.

03:22:00.141 --> 03:22:01.976
What you do is you mix melted sugar

03:22:01.976 --> 03:22:04.612
and, I believe, baking
soda, and it kind of aerates

03:22:04.612 --> 03:22:06.981
and puffs up and then
solidifies to leave you

03:22:06.981 --> 03:22:10.251
with what we know as, I guess,
confectioner's honeycomb.

03:22:10.251 --> 03:22:11.786
Again, slightly controversial take.

03:22:11.786 --> 03:22:13.621
I don't like Crunchie.
I don't like the texture

03:22:13.621 --> 03:22:15.022
of the honeycomb on my teeth.

03:22:15.022 --> 03:22:17.191
It feels really weird,
and I don't enjoy it,

03:22:17.191 --> 03:22:18.826
but I know it's a very popular bar,

03:22:18.826 --> 03:22:20.261
so you do you. That's fine.

03:22:20.261 --> 03:22:22.463
Next up is another
possible favorite of mine.

03:22:22.463 --> 03:22:23.831
It's a Curly Wurly.

03:22:23.831 --> 03:22:26.367
Now, I love these because it sounds like

03:22:26.367 --> 03:22:29.137
an American has kind
of improvised the name

03:22:29.137 --> 03:22:30.171
for a British chocolate bar.

03:22:30.171 --> 03:22:31.739
They're a little bit hard to describe.

03:22:31.739 --> 03:22:35.476
It's a combination of
chocolate and toffee,

03:22:36.310 --> 03:22:39.647
but it's in this very
distinctive, unique shape.

03:22:39.647 --> 03:22:42.383
I'm not really sure what the
logic is behind the shape, but.

03:22:44.285 --> 03:22:47.221
I'm not sure if the same
distinction exists in America,

03:22:47.221 --> 03:22:49.957
but here in the UK, if we say
something has toffee in it,

03:22:49.957 --> 03:22:52.493
that implies that it's
going to be very chewy.

03:22:52.493 --> 03:22:54.562
Kind of similar to caramel
in terms of the taste

03:22:54.562 --> 03:22:56.197
but a lot more chewy.

03:22:57.431 --> 03:22:59.934
Curly Wurly. Let's go.

03:22:59.934 --> 03:23:02.003
I have had these before,
and I do really like them.

03:23:02.003 --> 03:23:04.238
Oh, yeah.

03:23:04.238 --> 03:23:08.075
Mm. Mm! Yeah.

03:23:08.075 --> 03:23:10.444
Next up, we have some
more Darkmilk products.

03:23:10.444 --> 03:23:12.280
Again, this sits somewhere between dark

03:23:12.280 --> 03:23:15.049
and milk chocolate on the
kind of cocoa-content scale.

03:23:15.049 --> 03:23:18.019
This one is flavored with some
little salted caramel pieces.

03:23:18.019 --> 03:23:19.420
More of the salted caramel.

03:23:19.420 --> 03:23:20.621
Again, with the salted caramel.

03:23:20.621 --> 03:23:22.023
We get it. It's fine.

03:23:22.023 --> 03:23:23.691
You might also be able
to find the same bar

03:23:23.691 --> 03:23:26.060
but flavored with some
hazelnut praline pieces.

03:23:26.060 --> 03:23:28.429
Next up is another classic.
It's a Double Decker bar.

03:23:28.429 --> 03:23:29.730
Our buses aren't the only things

03:23:29.730 --> 03:23:31.132
that are double decker here in the UK.

03:23:31.132 --> 03:23:32.466
We also have these chocolate bars.

03:23:32.466 --> 03:23:34.802
There's quite a lot going
on in a Double Decker bar.

03:23:34.802 --> 03:23:36.304
I do love them. I'm going to be honest.

03:23:36.304 --> 03:23:37.872
But so what you get is, obviously,

03:23:37.872 --> 03:23:40.274
the Dairy Milk chocolate
coating on the outside,

03:23:42.376 --> 03:23:43.477
but then on the inside,

03:23:43.477 --> 03:23:45.513
you see you have this big layer of nougat,

03:23:45.513 --> 03:23:48.916
and on the other side you have
lots of these little crispy,

03:23:48.916 --> 03:23:51.118
I think kind of crispy rice pieces.

03:23:51.118 --> 03:23:53.521
Moving on from that, we have a Flake.

03:23:53.521 --> 03:23:55.790
So, I don't really understand
how Flakes are made,

03:23:55.790 --> 03:23:56.891
but they are quite impressive.

03:23:56.891 --> 03:23:59.026
Basically, I think what
they do is they get

03:23:59.026 --> 03:24:01.696
a kind of waterfall of chocolate,

03:24:01.696 --> 03:24:04.565
and the way that it kind of
hits the refrigerated surface,

03:24:04.565 --> 03:24:07.335
it kind of folds in on itself,

03:24:07.335 --> 03:24:10.137
and you get this really
layered, flaky texture.

03:24:10.137 --> 03:24:11.305
Flakes are pretty iconic.

03:24:11.305 --> 03:24:12.873
The main use of Flakes, arguably,

03:24:12.873 --> 03:24:15.076
in the UK is in ice-cream cones.

03:24:15.076 --> 03:24:18.279
What you will usually get
is a whipped ice-cream cone,

03:24:18.279 --> 03:24:20.381
Mr. Whippy, and then
they just stick a Flake

03:24:20.381 --> 03:24:21.315
in there as well.

03:24:21.315 --> 03:24:23.117
Flakes have also
apparently been the center

03:24:23.117 --> 03:24:26.220
of some controversy this year

03:24:26.220 --> 03:24:29.357
because, due to the
weird structure of them,

03:24:29.357 --> 03:24:30.725
they don't melt.

03:24:30.725 --> 03:24:31.892
Now, unlike most of the chocolate

03:24:31.892 --> 03:24:34.128
that I've been trying today,
I've kind of come away

03:24:34.128 --> 03:24:35.830
with little bits of melted
chocolate on my hand,

03:24:35.830 --> 03:24:37.765
but basically due to how thin the layers

03:24:37.765 --> 03:24:39.567
of the chocolate are, when it melts,

03:24:39.567 --> 03:24:42.470
the solids and the fats
don't really separate

03:24:42.470 --> 03:24:44.772
like they should do, and
therefore it just can't melt.

03:24:44.772 --> 03:24:46.407
You can put one of these in a bain-marie,

03:24:46.407 --> 03:24:47.742
and it will just not melt.

03:24:47.742 --> 03:24:50.278
You can microwave one,
and it will just burn

03:24:50.278 --> 03:24:51.612
before it starts to melt.

03:24:51.612 --> 03:24:52.913
The fun facts don't stop there.

03:24:52.913 --> 03:24:54.482
We're rolling straight in

03:24:54.482 --> 03:24:56.550
to the Fry's Chocolate Cream, whoo!

03:24:56.550 --> 03:25:00.021
Now, obviously, these do
not have the same packaging

03:25:01.088 --> 03:25:03.057
or branding as the rest of them,

03:25:03.057 --> 03:25:05.293
but they are indeed
manufactured by Cadbury's.

03:25:05.293 --> 03:25:06.861
So, these are bars of dark chocolate

03:25:06.861 --> 03:25:08.329
with a fondant cream center.

03:25:08.329 --> 03:25:10.364
They're worthy of special
mention because these are some

03:25:10.364 --> 03:25:13.501
of the first-ever
mass-produced chocolate bars.

03:25:13.501 --> 03:25:14.802
They took their name from the Fry family,

03:25:14.802 --> 03:25:17.271
who were a Quaker family
from Bristol in the UK.

03:25:17.271 --> 03:25:19.240
Now, the Frys were actually
the first people to figure out

03:25:19.240 --> 03:25:20.941
how to make mass-produced chocolate bars.

03:25:20.941 --> 03:25:23.377
We're going all the way back
to the 1840s here in the UK,

03:25:23.377 --> 03:25:25.880
but around that time, people
only really drank chocolate.

03:25:25.880 --> 03:25:28.316
It was quite rare to
get a piece of chocolate

03:25:28.316 --> 03:25:29.350
or a chocolate bar.

03:25:29.350 --> 03:25:30.918
The Frys were the first
ones who figured out how

03:25:30.918 --> 03:25:34.021
to press cacao beans to separate
the fats from the solids.

03:25:34.021 --> 03:25:35.456
Now, they were doing
this just to try and make

03:25:35.456 --> 03:25:38.092
a drinking powder that was
kind of easier to dissolve

03:25:38.092 --> 03:25:40.428
but inadvertently figured
out how to separate

03:25:40.428 --> 03:25:42.863
all the materials and
then reincorporate them

03:25:42.863 --> 03:25:45.232
in order to make a
shelf-stable chocolate bar.

03:25:47.435 --> 03:25:48.969
It's a taste of history right there.

03:25:48.969 --> 03:25:52.139
OK, so the packaging, it's
a Fry's Chocolate Cream.

03:25:52.139 --> 03:25:53.641
The packaging of this one is suspicious.

03:25:53.641 --> 03:25:55.643
It's, like, lowest effort of packaging.

03:25:55.643 --> 03:25:59.246
It's just this white text, no drop shadow,

03:25:59.246 --> 03:26:01.816
little bit of the bar right there.

03:26:04.251 --> 03:26:05.653
No, I do not like this.

03:26:05.653 --> 03:26:07.254
Now, while they're not
technically products themselves,

03:26:07.254 --> 03:26:09.056
rather an amalgamation of products,

03:26:09.056 --> 03:26:10.825
we have some selection
boxes here in the UK,

03:26:10.825 --> 03:26:11.992
which are worth pointing out.

03:26:11.992 --> 03:26:13.427
Now, these will start to pop up in stores

03:26:13.427 --> 03:26:15.463
around Christmastime, which
is when they're usually eaten.

03:26:15.463 --> 03:26:17.098
Now, these are what's
known as a selection box.

03:26:17.098 --> 03:26:19.533
So basically you have
small individual versions

03:26:19.533 --> 03:26:21.902
of quite a few of their
different chocolate bars,

03:26:21.902 --> 03:26:23.771
which you can then share with the family.

03:26:23.771 --> 03:26:26.841
So the box of Heroes will
contain Eclairs, Fudge, Wispa,

03:26:26.841 --> 03:26:29.744
Dairy Milk, Crunchie bits,
Twirl, Dairy Milk caramel,

03:26:29.744 --> 03:26:32.680
Double Decker, and the Creme Egg twisted.

03:26:32.680 --> 03:26:33.948
We also have Roses.

03:26:33.948 --> 03:26:36.417
And these aren't really
based on existing brands.

03:26:36.417 --> 03:26:37.585
These are more just like

03:26:37.585 --> 03:26:39.920
classic chocolate box
selections, I suppose.

03:26:39.920 --> 03:26:41.122
So we have things like the orange cream,

03:26:41.122 --> 03:26:42.490
the strawberry dream.

03:26:42.490 --> 03:26:45.659
We do actually have some mini
Dairy Milk bars in here now

03:26:45.659 --> 03:26:46.994
as well as a few other ones.

03:26:46.994 --> 03:26:48.696
I feel like in my power rankings

03:26:48.696 --> 03:26:51.399
of all the chocolate selection boxes,

03:26:51.399 --> 03:26:52.933
I don't know if the
Cadbury ones win for me.

03:26:52.933 --> 03:26:55.236
I do think Celebrations might win for me,

03:26:55.236 --> 03:26:58.272
which is like the Mars brand
equivalent of these ones.

03:26:58.272 --> 03:27:01.842
I'm thinking it's probably
Celebrations, Heroes, Roses.

03:27:01.842 --> 03:27:04.044
And Quality Street, of
course. The classics.

03:27:04.044 --> 03:27:06.847
Man. British Christmas,
what a wonderful time.

03:27:06.847 --> 03:27:08.315
While we're talking about selection boxes,

03:27:08.315 --> 03:27:10.851
I should also mention the milk tray.

03:27:10.851 --> 03:27:12.286
Now, these are a classic.

03:27:12.286 --> 03:27:15.189
These have been going back
years and years and years.

03:27:15.189 --> 03:27:16.457
I feel like it's less of a thing now,

03:27:16.457 --> 03:27:19.026
but these were very, very
popular to gift to people,

03:27:19.026 --> 03:27:21.128
whether someone's had you
around for a dinner party

03:27:21.128 --> 03:27:22.163
or it's a birthday.

03:27:22.163 --> 03:27:25.533
Give someone a milk tray
back in the '60s or '70s,

03:27:25.533 --> 03:27:27.034
it was just the done thing to do.

03:27:27.034 --> 03:27:30.037
Back to the chocolate
bars, this is a Picnic bar.

03:27:30.037 --> 03:27:32.273
I actually don't know if I've
ever had a Picnic bar before,

03:27:32.273 --> 03:27:34.141
mostly because they contain raisins.

03:27:34.141 --> 03:27:36.310
It's a milk chocolate
bar, but then inside,

03:27:36.310 --> 03:27:38.012
it's kind of just like
a mixture of everything.

03:27:38.012 --> 03:27:40.080
You get raisins. You get peanuts.

03:27:40.080 --> 03:27:41.682
There's crispy cereal in there.

03:27:41.682 --> 03:27:43.284
I think there's caramel as well.

03:27:43.284 --> 03:27:45.352
Next up is the Cadbury Timeout wafer.

03:27:45.352 --> 03:27:47.755
I'd say this is more of a
biscuit bar than a chocolate bar,

03:27:47.755 --> 03:27:50.090
but you have layers of
biscuit and chocolate,

03:27:50.090 --> 03:27:51.158
which are then obviously coated

03:27:51.158 --> 03:27:53.194
in more Cadbury Dairy Milk chocolate.

03:27:53.194 --> 03:27:54.562
Then we're on to a Twirl.

03:27:54.562 --> 03:27:56.430
These are kind of similar to the Flake

03:27:56.430 --> 03:27:57.531
in terms of the texture.

03:27:57.531 --> 03:27:59.934
It's kind of like Flake texture inside,

03:27:59.934 --> 03:28:02.770
but the actual individual bars
are coated with chocolate.

03:28:02.770 --> 03:28:04.305
Twirl. What is a Twirl?

03:28:05.940 --> 03:28:08.943
No, I don't like this. It's just OK.

03:28:08.943 --> 03:28:11.612
I don't like the airy texture of it.

03:28:11.612 --> 03:28:12.980
Man, these are a classic.

03:28:12.980 --> 03:28:14.882
I feel like these have
almost gone extinct.

03:28:14.882 --> 03:28:16.383
This is a Chomp bar.

03:28:16.383 --> 03:28:18.252
It's kind of the same as a Curly Wurly

03:28:18.252 --> 03:28:20.855
in that it's basically a
layer of toffee covered

03:28:20.855 --> 03:28:23.991
in milk chocolate, but instead
of that weird patterning

03:28:23.991 --> 03:28:25.826
with the gaps, this is just a solid bar.

03:28:25.826 --> 03:28:27.394
I feel like these have
always been served in this

03:28:27.394 --> 03:28:29.396
kind of small-size bar as more of a snack,

03:28:29.396 --> 03:28:31.732
maybe aimed towards
kids, but I love a Chomp.

03:28:31.732 --> 03:28:32.633
These are brilliant.

03:28:32.633 --> 03:28:34.635
Shh, it's a Wispa bar.

03:28:34.635 --> 03:28:36.971
I don't fully understand Wispa bars.

03:28:36.971 --> 03:28:40.307
I like them, don't get me
wrong, but it just kind of feels

03:28:40.307 --> 03:28:42.776
like you're getting less
chocolate for your money

03:28:42.776 --> 03:28:44.578
than you are with some other bars.

03:28:44.578 --> 03:28:46.280
Because basically they've
taken a milk chocolate bar,

03:28:46.280 --> 03:28:48.315
but they've figured out how to aerate it

03:28:48.315 --> 03:28:50.384
and kind of put little air bubbles in.

03:28:51.952 --> 03:28:54.121
What they've really gone for is a texture

03:28:54.121 --> 03:28:55.456
where when you bite into it,

03:28:55.456 --> 03:28:57.291
it almost melts on your tongue.

03:28:57.291 --> 03:28:58.926
We also have a Wispa gold bar.

03:28:58.926 --> 03:29:00.995
Wispa gold is the same aerated texture,

03:29:00.995 --> 03:29:03.564
but it has a layer of caramel
running along the top.

03:29:03.564 --> 03:29:05.199
Next up, we have a Starbar.

03:29:05.199 --> 03:29:06.033
Now, the way I see it,

03:29:06.033 --> 03:29:07.635
this is just like a better Picnic bar.

03:29:07.635 --> 03:29:09.503
So, a Starbar is basically
just milk chocolate

03:29:09.503 --> 03:29:12.339
on the outside, caramel, and peanuts.

03:29:12.339 --> 03:29:13.807
Next up we have the Fudge bar.

03:29:13.807 --> 03:29:14.875
It's a layer of fudge

03:29:14.875 --> 03:29:16.310
with some Cadbury
chocolate on the outside.

03:29:16.310 --> 03:29:18.979
They're still in the
Heroes box, which I like,

03:29:18.979 --> 03:29:21.081
but I feel like not that many
people like them anymore.

03:29:21.081 --> 03:29:23.951
And then finally, for
the time being, Eclairs.

03:29:23.951 --> 03:29:25.286
So, the Cadbury Eclair is very different

03:29:25.286 --> 03:29:27.021
to the pastry eclair.

03:29:27.021 --> 03:29:28.756
It's basically a ball of toffee

03:29:28.756 --> 03:29:30.624
with chocolate on the inside.

03:29:30.624 --> 03:29:32.059
I always liked these.

03:29:32.059 --> 03:29:33.193
There's always something really satisfying

03:29:33.193 --> 03:29:35.262
about the texture of toffee to me.

03:29:35.262 --> 03:29:36.830
I had braces as a little kid,

03:29:36.830 --> 03:29:39.700
and I really missed
toffee when I had braces,

03:29:39.700 --> 03:29:41.969
so I'll never take
toffee for granted again.

03:29:41.969 --> 03:29:44.538
Now, Cadbury in the US is a brand

03:29:44.538 --> 03:29:46.407
that's manufactured by Hershey,

03:29:46.407 --> 03:29:49.343
so there's no sub-brands under
Cadbury in the United States.

03:29:49.343 --> 03:29:52.913
However, Hershey does own about 29 brands.

03:29:52.913 --> 03:29:55.215
Here are a few that are
exclusive to the US.

03:29:55.215 --> 03:29:57.651
In no particular order,
first one, Milk Duds.

03:29:57.651 --> 03:29:59.019
If you've been to movie theater in the US,

03:29:59.019 --> 03:29:59.887
[rattling]

03:29:59.887 --> 03:30:01.789
recognize that sound?

03:30:01.789 --> 03:30:03.390
Some jerk sitting two rows behind.

03:30:03.390 --> 03:30:05.192
He doesn't understand he's making noise.

03:30:05.192 --> 03:30:06.927
He got Milk Duds!

03:30:06.927 --> 03:30:10.531
Milk Duds are like Curly
Wurlys in button form.

03:30:10.531 --> 03:30:12.232
Eh, that's actually pretty
good, what I just did there.

03:30:12.232 --> 03:30:15.669
It is just caramel covered in chocolate,

03:30:15.669 --> 03:30:18.005
but it is in button form
and not, like, drizzle form.

03:30:18.005 --> 03:30:21.008
These, guaranteed stuck to your teeth.

03:30:21.008 --> 03:30:23.410
Guaranteed you're digging
these out of your teeth.

03:30:23.410 --> 03:30:28.215
A Heath bar is, hang on,
"English toffee enrobed

03:30:28.215 --> 03:30:30.351
in a rich, chocolaty coating."

03:30:30.351 --> 03:30:31.952
They wrote that like they thought

03:30:31.952 --> 03:30:33.220
an American would read that and be like,

03:30:33.220 --> 03:30:34.254
"This is from England."

03:30:34.254 --> 03:30:37.091
Mounds is chocolate and coconut.

03:30:37.091 --> 03:30:38.525
There it is. It's a mound.

03:30:38.525 --> 03:30:40.160
Looks a little bit like a rabbit turd.

03:30:40.160 --> 03:30:41.595
It's just a chunk

03:30:41.595 --> 03:30:45.866
of coconut covered in dark chocolate.

03:30:45.866 --> 03:30:47.501
Now, if you're looking at
this and you're being like,

03:30:47.501 --> 03:30:49.970
"It needs something
else, Joe," yes, it does.

03:30:49.970 --> 03:30:53.240
How about almonds? Almond Joy.

03:30:53.240 --> 03:30:55.409
The exact same thing as a Mounds

03:30:55.409 --> 03:30:58.579
but if said rabbit ate

03:30:58.579 --> 03:31:00.147
a bunch of almonds whole.

03:31:00.147 --> 03:31:01.181
Coconut and chocolate.

03:31:01.181 --> 03:31:02.916
Coconut, almonds, and chocolate.

03:31:02.916 --> 03:31:04.685
Some of the bars are also
available in the form

03:31:04.685 --> 03:31:06.954
of these bite-sized
pieces. These come in bags.

03:31:06.954 --> 03:31:10.824
Notably, we have some Crunchie
rocks, we have bitsa Wispa,

03:31:10.824 --> 03:31:13.127
we have Curly Wurly squirlies,

03:31:13.127 --> 03:31:15.663
and the least fun name, Twirl bites.

03:31:15.663 --> 03:31:16.897
These are more designed to be shared

03:31:16.897 --> 03:31:19.199
than obviously a classic
chocolate bar would be.

03:31:19.199 --> 03:31:21.435
I think the idea is maybe
you get a bag of these,

03:31:21.435 --> 03:31:22.970
take it into the cinema or something,

03:31:22.970 --> 03:31:24.772
watch your film, got a little snack.

03:31:24.772 --> 03:31:25.839
Next up we have Brunch bars.

03:31:25.839 --> 03:31:28.008
These are Cadbury's take on cereal bars.

03:31:28.008 --> 03:31:29.810
We've got these Brunch bars
in three different flavors.

03:31:29.810 --> 03:31:32.713
We have a raisin one,
a milk choc chip one,

03:31:32.713 --> 03:31:34.281
and a dark choc chip one.

03:31:34.281 --> 03:31:36.083
I do actually quite like these.

03:31:36.083 --> 03:31:39.553
These were a occasional
packed-lunch inclusion

03:31:39.553 --> 03:31:40.387
when I was growing up.

03:31:40.387 --> 03:31:43.824
Basically, it's a cereal
bar made mostly with oats,

03:31:44.992 --> 03:31:48.328
but then you get this base
layer of chocolate as well.

03:31:48.328 --> 03:31:50.497
In this case, some choc chips as well.

03:31:52.066 --> 03:31:54.101
Yeah, they've got a nice chewy texture.

03:31:54.101 --> 03:31:55.502
And then, finally, it's
time for what we'll call

03:31:55.502 --> 03:31:56.403
the best of the rest.

03:31:56.403 --> 03:31:58.605
These are the kind of
miscellaneous Cadbury products

03:31:58.605 --> 03:32:00.441
which don't really fit into
any of the other categories

03:32:00.441 --> 03:32:01.809
but are still worth talking about.

03:32:01.809 --> 03:32:04.745
Firstly, we have animal biscuits.

03:32:04.745 --> 03:32:07.414
These were, again, a
classic packed-lunch staple

03:32:07.414 --> 03:32:08.682
when I was younger.

03:32:08.682 --> 03:32:11.485
You get a little biscuit,
which on one side

03:32:11.485 --> 03:32:14.154
is kind of decorated in
the shape of an animal

03:32:14.154 --> 03:32:15.589
and on the other side is just dipped

03:32:15.589 --> 03:32:16.890
in Dairy Milk chocolate.

03:32:16.890 --> 03:32:19.193
Gotta give a shout-out to mini rolls.

03:32:19.193 --> 03:32:22.429
These are another maybe
grandma-biscuit-tin staple.

03:32:22.429 --> 03:32:24.832
These are actually like a cake basically

03:32:24.832 --> 03:32:27.568
rather than a biscuit or a chocolate bar.

03:32:27.568 --> 03:32:30.704
It's a roll of chocolate sponge
cake, which is then, I say,

03:32:30.704 --> 03:32:33.073
rolled up with a little
layer of cream in the middle,

03:32:33.073 --> 03:32:34.208
and then the whole thing is dipped

03:32:34.208 --> 03:32:35.509
in milk chocolate as well.

03:32:37.344 --> 03:32:39.313
And then while we're
talking about Cadbury cakes,

03:32:39.313 --> 03:32:41.348
we also have Cadbury cake bars.

03:32:41.348 --> 03:32:42.382
Kind of similar to mini rolls

03:32:42.382 --> 03:32:43.550
in that you have the chocolate sponge,

03:32:43.550 --> 03:32:46.220
it's all coated in chocolate as well,

03:32:46.220 --> 03:32:48.622
but you have that caramel
layer along the top.

03:32:48.622 --> 03:32:50.991
Then we're on to to a
couple of fridge desserts.

03:32:50.991 --> 03:32:54.161
These are Cadbury's milk
chocolate sticky puds.

03:32:54.161 --> 03:32:55.829
So, these are great. I've had these

03:32:55.829 --> 03:32:59.433
or an iteration of these
many times in the past.

03:32:59.433 --> 03:33:02.136
Basically, they come in
these little plastic pots,

03:33:02.136 --> 03:33:04.438
which are microwavable,

03:33:04.438 --> 03:33:06.006
and the typical way to eat these is

03:33:06.006 --> 03:33:07.941
that you maybe pierce the film on this,

03:33:07.941 --> 03:33:10.444
put the whole thing in the
microwave for 30 seconds,

03:33:10.444 --> 03:33:14.782
a minute or so, take it
out, flip it onto a plate,

03:33:14.782 --> 03:33:16.250
and it comes out.

03:33:16.250 --> 03:33:19.119
Cooking it kind of steams the
sponge when it's in there.

03:33:19.119 --> 03:33:20.687
And then there's a chocolate sauce,

03:33:20.687 --> 03:33:22.556
as you can see on the packaging,

03:33:22.556 --> 03:33:24.191
which is kind of collected at this end,

03:33:24.191 --> 03:33:25.492
and then when you flip it,

03:33:25.492 --> 03:33:28.228
ends up kind of drizzling
down the side of the puds,

03:33:28.228 --> 03:33:31.732
and it's very nice, like a fun
kind of fancy-feeling dessert

03:33:31.732 --> 03:33:33.834
that you can just have
at home in 30 seconds.

03:33:33.834 --> 03:33:35.402
Pud, of course, is short for pudding,

03:33:35.402 --> 03:33:36.703
which is what we call dessert.

03:33:36.703 --> 03:33:39.807
And then we also have these
Dairy Milk pots of joy.

03:33:39.807 --> 03:33:42.309
These are apparently a
"smooth and creamy dessert

03:33:42.309 --> 03:33:44.311
made with melted Cadbury milk chocolate."

03:33:44.311 --> 03:33:45.712
I've not had one of these before.

03:33:45.712 --> 03:33:47.548
I think they're like a yogurt, basically,

03:33:47.548 --> 03:33:49.049
like a chocolate yogurt pot.

03:33:49.049 --> 03:33:50.384
I'm not sure what to make of this next one

03:33:50.384 --> 03:33:53.620
because it feels a little bit
like America is infiltrating

03:33:53.620 --> 03:33:56.356
our Cadbury products, but
this is Cadbury Dairy Milk

03:33:56.356 --> 03:33:59.526
fruitier and nuttier orange trail mix.

03:33:59.526 --> 03:34:01.328
These, I believe, are new.

03:34:01.328 --> 03:34:02.863
I don't think Cadbury's
been doing trail mix

03:34:02.863 --> 03:34:03.931
for a very long time.

03:34:03.931 --> 03:34:05.866
I also don't think trail
mix is very popular

03:34:05.866 --> 03:34:07.434
in the UK in general.

03:34:07.434 --> 03:34:08.902
It's just not that common.

03:34:08.902 --> 03:34:11.805
So, in a bag of this, apparently
you get sweetened cocoa,

03:34:11.805 --> 03:34:15.142
Dairy Milk orange buttons,
raisins and sultanas,

03:34:15.142 --> 03:34:17.110
roasted almonds, and hazelnuts.

03:34:17.110 --> 03:34:18.679
Speaking of things meant to sound English,

03:34:18.679 --> 03:34:20.247
it's a York Peppermint Pattie,

03:34:20.247 --> 03:34:22.182
all the way from the city of York.

03:34:22.182 --> 03:34:24.751
Is this, it is just, instead of coconut,

03:34:24.751 --> 03:34:27.754
it is peppermint.

03:34:27.754 --> 03:34:31.558
Mr. Goodbar, chocolate and peanuts.

03:34:31.558 --> 03:34:32.960
This bar, the packaging,

03:34:32.960 --> 03:34:34.294
you can tell this is an old-school bar.

03:34:34.294 --> 03:34:37.865
This bar is so old that its
main selling point was like,

03:34:37.865 --> 03:34:40.400
"We assure you there's
peanuts in this bar.

03:34:40.400 --> 03:34:42.502
We're not lying to you, all right?

03:34:42.502 --> 03:34:46.773
Yes. You won't believe how
packed with peanuts this bar is."

03:34:46.773 --> 03:34:48.008
Is this true

03:34:48.008 --> 03:34:52.346
to the Goodbar guarantee on the back?

03:34:52.346 --> 03:34:54.047
Possibly more.

03:34:54.047 --> 03:34:57.718
Wow, they overdelivered
on the promise of peanuts.

03:34:57.718 --> 03:35:00.087
5th Avenue bar. I don't know what this is.

03:35:00.087 --> 03:35:01.255
Ugh.

03:35:03.323 --> 03:35:05.692
Whatchamacallit, let's go.

03:35:05.692 --> 03:35:08.495
Chocolate, peanut-flavor
crisps, and caramel.

03:35:11.064 --> 03:35:14.001
Definitely very peanut
buttery. Eh, it's OK.

03:35:14.001 --> 03:35:15.102
We have two options when it comes

03:35:15.102 --> 03:35:16.770
to beverages for Cadbury's in the UK.

03:35:16.770 --> 03:35:19.673
Here we have original
Cadbury hot chocolate,

03:35:19.673 --> 03:35:23.110
and then we also have
Highlights milk choc drink.

03:35:23.110 --> 03:35:24.578
I'm not massively sure
what the difference is,

03:35:24.578 --> 03:35:26.813
but I think just judging
from kind of a look

03:35:26.813 --> 03:35:29.516
on the packaging,
Highlights might be designed

03:35:29.516 --> 03:35:32.319
as kind of a diet option,
just because it's described

03:35:32.319 --> 03:35:36.356
as an "instant low-calorie milk
chocolate flavor hot drink."

03:35:36.356 --> 03:35:39.059
Staying in the cupboard
section of the Cadbury's aisle,

03:35:39.059 --> 03:35:40.827
now we're on to the chocolate spread.

03:35:40.827 --> 03:35:42.562
This is effectively
kind of like a Nutella,

03:35:42.562 --> 03:35:44.264
but it doesn't have hazelnuts in it,

03:35:44.264 --> 03:35:46.266
so this is just like a
pure chocolate spread.

03:35:46.266 --> 03:35:47.868
Now we're moving on to the bakery section

03:35:47.868 --> 03:35:48.802
of your supermarket.

03:35:48.802 --> 03:35:51.271
I got most of these
from an Asda in London,

03:35:51.271 --> 03:35:53.840
so I do think the availability
will vary slightly depending

03:35:53.840 --> 03:35:56.076
on where you are and which
supermarket you're visiting,

03:35:56.076 --> 03:35:58.211
but I will say that most
supermarkets will have

03:35:58.211 --> 03:35:59.947
a bakery section, and you'll probably see

03:35:59.947 --> 03:36:01.581
some Cadbury branding in there somewhere.

03:36:01.581 --> 03:36:02.616
Here's what I was able to find.

03:36:02.616 --> 03:36:04.384
We have three of these bagged goods

03:36:04.384 --> 03:36:06.853
from the bakery-at-Asda range.

03:36:06.853 --> 03:36:09.122
Firstly, we have some oatie bakes,

03:36:09.122 --> 03:36:11.725
which are oat flapjacks
containing milk chocolate.

03:36:11.725 --> 03:36:15.262
Next up we have some Cadbury
Dairy Milk shortie squares.

03:36:15.262 --> 03:36:19.866
Shortie's like a melody in my square.

03:36:19.866 --> 03:36:23.170
Shortie squares are a
shortcake packed with, again,

03:36:23.170 --> 03:36:24.705
some Dairy Milk chocolate chunks.

03:36:24.705 --> 03:36:27.574
Then we have some Cadbury
Dairy Milk chocolate cookies.

03:36:27.574 --> 03:36:29.376
We're staying in the bakery aisle,

03:36:29.376 --> 03:36:33.046
and now we're on to Dairy
Milk caramel doughnuts.

03:36:33.046 --> 03:36:35.248
Now, I'm kind of intrigued
by these. I love doughnuts.

03:36:38.251 --> 03:36:39.453
It's just very dry.

03:36:39.453 --> 03:36:41.088
And then we have a few more baked goods,

03:36:41.088 --> 03:36:44.091
namely Dairy Milk caramel mini cookies,

03:36:44.091 --> 03:36:46.960
Dairy Milk cornflake cluster bites,

03:36:46.960 --> 03:36:49.463
and some Dairy Milk brownie bites.

03:36:49.463 --> 03:36:50.364
Then we're going to round things off

03:36:50.364 --> 03:36:51.732
with a few seasonal items.

03:36:51.732 --> 03:36:53.266
As I mentioned,
unfortunately we weren't able

03:36:53.266 --> 03:36:55.602
to get the Easter exclusives,
which are pretty iconic,

03:36:55.602 --> 03:36:57.504
namely the Creme Egg and the mini egg,

03:36:57.504 --> 03:37:00.407
but because we're kind of in
the Christmas/Halloween period,

03:37:00.407 --> 03:37:02.676
I was able to get some of
those exclusives instead.

03:37:02.676 --> 03:37:03.710
We'll start with the Halloween ones,

03:37:03.710 --> 03:37:06.079
which has just come and
gone here in the UK,

03:37:06.079 --> 03:37:09.616
but we were able to find
some mini bonfire logs,

03:37:09.616 --> 03:37:10.450
which are fun.

03:37:10.450 --> 03:37:11.885
I think these are like a mini roll,

03:37:11.885 --> 03:37:14.421
but instead of the
classic chocolate sponge,

03:37:14.421 --> 03:37:17.024
they've used a honeycomb sponge instead.

03:37:17.024 --> 03:37:19.893
And then another spooky
seasonal exclusive are

03:37:19.893 --> 03:37:21.561
the pumpkin patch cakes.

03:37:21.561 --> 03:37:22.629
These are chocolate cakes

03:37:22.629 --> 03:37:24.898
with an orange-colored cream filling.

03:37:24.898 --> 03:37:27.501
Then we have some Cadbury Christmas puds.

03:37:27.501 --> 03:37:29.603
These are Dairy Milk chocolate balls

03:37:29.603 --> 03:37:31.505
with a hazelnut truffle center,

03:37:31.505 --> 03:37:33.473
hazelnut pieces, and rice crisps.

03:37:35.542 --> 03:37:38.345
Again, it's that combination
of chocolate and nuts.

03:37:38.345 --> 03:37:41.014
Just can't go wrong for me.

03:37:41.014 --> 03:37:43.950
And of course, we can't talk
about seasonal exclusives

03:37:43.950 --> 03:37:45.685
without talking about Advent calendars.

03:37:45.685 --> 03:37:47.687
We love our Advent calendars over here.

03:37:47.687 --> 03:37:49.589
Cadbury's, of course, has to get involved,

03:37:49.589 --> 03:37:51.291
and they have a very simple

03:37:51.291 --> 03:37:53.360
but very effective
chocolate Advent calendar.

03:37:56.329 --> 03:37:58.665
This 110-gram bar of
Cadbury Dairy Milk costs

03:37:58.665 --> 03:38:00.434
£1.35 at Tesco.

03:38:00.434 --> 03:38:02.869
Breaking that down,
you're paying 1.22 pence

03:38:02.869 --> 03:38:04.004
per gram of chocolate.

03:38:04.004 --> 03:38:06.039
In the US, our bar is 99 grams,

03:38:06.039 --> 03:38:08.241
and it costs $2.79 cents,

03:38:08.241 --> 03:38:11.578
which is 2.8 cents per gram.

03:38:11.578 --> 03:38:14.948
So per gram, it's a
91% increase in the US.

03:38:14.948 --> 03:38:17.117
Ooh! Getting gouged, baby!

03:38:20.253 --> 03:38:22.055
Here's everything in a bar
of Dairy Milk chocolate

03:38:22.055 --> 03:38:26.593
in the UK: milk, sugar,
cocoa butter, cocoa mass,

03:38:26.593 --> 03:38:28.628
vegetable fats (palm and shea),

03:38:28.628 --> 03:38:31.932
emulsifiers (E442 (ammonium phosphatides),

03:38:31.932 --> 03:38:36.470
E476 (polyglycerol polyricinoleate)),

03:38:36.470 --> 03:38:37.671
and flavorings.

03:38:37.671 --> 03:38:40.874
Here's everything in a US
Dairy Milk chocolate bar.

03:38:40.874 --> 03:38:45.579
We got sugar, milk,
chocolate, cocoa butter,

03:38:45.579 --> 03:38:50.150
lactose, lecithin, PGPR,

03:38:50.150 --> 03:38:52.385
natural flavor, artificial flavor.

03:38:52.385 --> 03:38:55.555
UK chocolate is often
considered as superior by some,

03:38:55.555 --> 03:38:57.457
including myself, to US chocolate,

03:38:57.457 --> 03:38:59.025
and there are a few reasons for that.

03:38:59.025 --> 03:39:00.894
Firstly, the UK rules for
its chocolate production

03:39:00.894 --> 03:39:04.464
from the Cocoa and Chocolate
Products Regulations of 2003

03:39:04.464 --> 03:39:08.001
set minimums for milk
chocolate at 25% total fat

03:39:08.001 --> 03:39:10.770
(cocoa butter and milk fat), 5% milk fat,

03:39:10.770 --> 03:39:15.408
20% dry cocoa solids, 2.5%
dry nonfat cocoa solids,

03:39:15.408 --> 03:39:17.511
and 20% dry milk solids.

03:39:17.511 --> 03:39:19.379
In the US, our FDA rules

03:39:19.379 --> 03:39:22.015
for our milk chocolate differ quite a bit.

03:39:22.015 --> 03:39:25.085
10% chocolate liquor; 12% milk solids,

03:39:25.085 --> 03:39:27.521
which can be either
powder or condensed milk;

03:39:27.521 --> 03:39:30.290
at least 3.39% fat;

03:39:30.290 --> 03:39:32.559
and no more than 55% sugar.

03:39:32.559 --> 03:39:34.694
Secondly, those of us on
this side of the Atlantic

03:39:34.694 --> 03:39:36.696
can feel that American
chocolate has a sour,

03:39:36.696 --> 03:39:38.498
almost vomity taste to it.

03:39:38.498 --> 03:39:39.366
That's all very well and good

03:39:39.366 --> 03:39:41.268
for American-branded chocolate bars,

03:39:41.268 --> 03:39:42.736
but why doesn't US Cadbury taste

03:39:42.736 --> 03:39:44.404
like it does over here in the UK?

03:39:44.404 --> 03:39:46.006
It starts with The Hershey Company.

03:39:46.006 --> 03:39:47.207
Back in the early 1900s,

03:39:47.207 --> 03:39:49.309
the company introduced
cost-cutting measures,

03:39:49.309 --> 03:39:51.411
including a process to make its milk

03:39:51.411 --> 03:39:53.046
last longer in production.

03:39:53.046 --> 03:39:55.081
For this, they used a secret method

03:39:55.081 --> 03:39:58.051
called liposis? Liplosis?

03:39:58.051 --> 03:39:59.119
Yuelei: Lipolysis.

03:39:59.119 --> 03:40:00.954
Lipolysis!

03:40:00.954 --> 03:40:03.190
The details of Hershey's
chocolate-making process

03:40:03.190 --> 03:40:04.524
are a company secret,

03:40:04.524 --> 03:40:07.661
but part of the process
involves butyric acid.

03:40:07.661 --> 03:40:09.796
Butyric acid extends
the shelf life of milk,

03:40:09.796 --> 03:40:11.398
but it does partially sour it.

03:40:11.398 --> 03:40:13.300
It's also found in Parmesan cheese,

03:40:13.300 --> 03:40:16.570
fermented foods like sauerkraut,
and, of course, vomit.

03:40:16.570 --> 03:40:20.240
Hershey's became a powerful
chocolate company in the US,

03:40:20.240 --> 03:40:23.510
so much so they successfully
forced a ban on an import

03:40:23.510 --> 03:40:25.545
of Cadbury's chocolate into the US.

03:40:25.545 --> 03:40:28.782
In 1988, Hershey's acquired
the US Cadbury license

03:40:28.782 --> 03:40:30.817
and has been producing it in the US

03:40:30.817 --> 03:40:33.119
with their own Americanized formula,

03:40:33.119 --> 03:40:36.623
putting them in similar-looking
Cadbury packages ever since.

03:40:36.623 --> 03:40:38.158
Remember the price section from earlier?

03:40:38.158 --> 03:40:40.026
Well, it turns out that not
only are Americans paying

03:40:40.026 --> 03:40:42.162
basically double the price
for their chocolate as we are,

03:40:42.162 --> 03:40:43.930
but they're also getting
a worse-quality product.

03:40:43.930 --> 03:40:44.764
That's kind of sad.

03:40:47.767 --> 03:40:49.269
100 grams of Dairy Milk chocolate

03:40:49.269 --> 03:40:50.670
in the UK contains the following:

03:40:50.670 --> 03:40:53.506
534 calories, 30 grams of fat,

03:40:53.506 --> 03:40:56.876
of which 18 grams are
saturated, 57 grams of carbs,

03:40:56.876 --> 03:40:58.812
of which 56 grams of sugars,

03:40:58.812 --> 03:41:00.580
and 56 milligrams of sodium.

03:41:00.580 --> 03:41:03.583
Let's compare that to a
99-gram bar over here,

03:41:03.583 --> 03:41:06.253
seeing as there's only 1 gram difference.

03:41:06.253 --> 03:41:07.554
500 calories,

03:41:07.554 --> 03:41:11.691
57.5 grams of carbs,
27.5 grams of total fat,

03:41:11.691 --> 03:41:16.463
17.5 grams of saturated fat,
55 grams of total sugars,

03:41:16.463 --> 03:41:18.265
7.5 grams of protein,

03:41:18.265 --> 03:41:20.066
and 100 milligrams of sodium.

03:41:20.066 --> 03:41:21.968
If we compare the 100-gram stats in the UK

03:41:21.968 --> 03:41:23.903
to the 99-gram stats in the US,

03:41:23.903 --> 03:41:25.272
we'll find that the UK actually has

03:41:25.272 --> 03:41:27.007
slightly more calories and fat,

03:41:27.007 --> 03:41:29.409
although it has less carbs
and much less sodium.

03:41:31.978 --> 03:41:32.779
What?

03:41:32.779 --> 03:41:36.116
I've been watching the current
season of "Love Island" UK.

03:41:36.116 --> 03:41:38.818
So I'm like, "Are you
joking? Are you joking?

03:41:38.818 --> 03:41:41.488
You're having a laugh.
Are you having a laugh?"

03:41:41.488 --> 03:41:42.989
This is journalism, baby.

03:41:42.989 --> 03:41:44.624
This is why we went to journalism school.

03:41:44.624 --> 03:41:47.294
I didn't go to journalism
school, so I have an excuse.

